(25, 'bca-assay-calculator', 'biology', 'BCA Assay Calculator (standard curve)', 'BCA Assay Calculator (standard curve)', 'BCA Assay (standard curve) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The BCA assay returns a colour, not a concentration: you read absorbance, put it on a standard curve and interpolate. Free bca assay (standard curve) calculat', 'The BCA assay returns a colour, not a concentration: you read absorbance, put it on a standard curve and interpolate. It returns protein concentration and sample absorbance, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator takes the slope and intercept of your BSA standards and converts sample absorbance into protein concentration, including the dilution factor you used. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the BCA Assay (standard curve)\",\"html\":\"<p>Protein (mg/mL) = (A₅₆₂ − intercept) ÷ slope × dilution factor</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/bca-assay-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: BCA Assay Calculator (standard curve) — Absorbance minus the intercept, divided by the slope, times the dilution.\\\" title=\\\"BCA Assay Calculator (standard curve)\\\"><figcaption>Absorbance minus the intercept, divided by the slope, times the dilution.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A₅₆₂</code></td><td>blank-corrected absorbance of the sample</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>slope</code></td><td>absorbance per mg/mL from the standards</td><td>AU·mL/mg</td></tr><tr><td><code>intercept</code></td><td>fitted absorbance at zero protein</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>dilution</code></td><td>how much the sample was diluted before reading</td><td>×</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> BCA chemistry: Smith et al. (1985), Analytical Biochemistry 150:76–85.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the BCA Assay (standard curve)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: standard curve slope of <strong>1.35</strong> per mg/mL, standard curve intercept of <strong>0.02</strong>, sample dilution factor of <strong>1</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives protein concentration of <strong>0.4444</strong> mg/mL, sample absorbance of <strong>0.62</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.05 – 2 mg/mL</td><td><strong>Working range</strong></td><td>the linear part of a typical BCA curve</td></tr><tr><td>below 0.05</td><td><strong>Too dilute</strong></td><td>concentrate or use a micro-BCA protocol</td></tr><tr><td>above 2</td><td><strong>Too concentrated</strong></td><td>dilute and re-read; the curve flattens</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The BCA curve is linear over roughly 20–2000 µg/mL for BSA. Outside that you are reading the shoulder of the curve, where a small absorbance error becomes a large concentration error.</p></li><li><p>BCA is compatible with detergents such as 1% SDS but not with reducing agents above about 5 mM DTT, which drive the colour reaction on their own.</p></li><li><p>The response depends on the protein: BSA over-reads, lysozyme under-reads. Quote the standard you used.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Forgetting the dilution factor, which is the most common cause of a concentration that is half or a quarter of the true value.</li><li>Subtracting the blank from the standards but not from the samples.</li><li>Reading at the wrong wavelength — BCA is measured at 562 nm, Bradford at 595 nm.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/bradford-assay-calculator\\\">Bradford assay</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/beer-lambert-law\\\">Beer–Lambert law</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\\\">Protein molar conversions</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I make the standard curve?\",\"a\":\"Dilute BSA to 0, 0.125, 0.25, 0.5, 1 and 2 mg/mL, run them with the samples, and fit absorbance against concentration. The slope and intercept go in the two boxes above.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my sample reading above the top standard?\",\"a\":\"The assay has saturated. Dilute the sample and multiply by the new dilution factor.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the BCA Assay (standard curve) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns protein concentration and sample absorbance. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"a562\",\"label\":\"Sample absorbance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.62\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Read at 562 nm, blank subtracted\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"slope\",\"label\":\"Standard curve slope\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.35\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Absorbance units per mg/mL of protein\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"intercept\",\"label\":\"Standard curve intercept\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.02\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Absorbance at zero protein\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Sample dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"2 for a 1:1 dilution with buffer\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Protein concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"(a562-intercept)/slope*dilution\"},{\"l\":\"a562\",\"f\":\"conc*slope/dilution+intercept\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/bca-assay-calculator\",\"slug\":\"bca-assay-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"BCA Assay Calculator (standard curve)\",\"title\":\"BCA Assay (standard curve) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The BCA assay returns a colour, not a concentration: you read absorbance, put it on a standard curve and interpolate. Free bca assay (standard curve) calculat\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-bca-assay-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a562\",\"l\":\"Sample absorbance\",\"h\":\"Read at 562 nm, blank subtracted\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"slope\",\"l\":\"Standard curve slope\",\"h\":\"Absorbance units per mg/mL of protein\",\"suf\":\"per mg/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"intercept\",\"l\":\"Standard curve intercept\",\"h\":\"Absorbance at zero protein\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Sample dilution factor\",\"h\":\"2 for a 1:1 dilution with buffer\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Protein concentration\",\"suf\":\"mg/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"a562\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.62\",\"c\":null}]},\"slope\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.35\",\"c\":null}]},\"intercept\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.02\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"(a562-intercept)/slope*dilution\",\"r\":[\"a562\",\"intercept\",\"slope\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"a562\",\"f\":\"conc*slope/dilution+intercept\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"slope\",\"dilution\",\"intercept\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(26, 'bradford-assay-calculator', 'biology', 'Bradford Assay Calculator (standard curve)', 'Bradford Assay Calculator (standard curve)', 'Bradford Assay (standard curve) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Bradford is the quickest colorimetric protein assay and the one most sensitive to which protein you are measuring. Free bradford assay (standard curve) calcul', 'Bradford is the quickest colorimetric protein assay and the one most sensitive to which protein you are measuring. It returns protein concentration, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a 595 nm reading into mg/mL using your own standard curve, with the dilution factor applied. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Bradford Assay (standard curve)\",\"html\":\"<p>Protein (mg/mL) = (A₅₉₅ − intercept) ÷ slope × dilution factor</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/bradford-assay-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Bradford Assay Calculator (standard curve) — A 595 nm reading turned into a concentration through your own standards.\\\" title=\\\"Bradford Assay Calculator (standard curve)\\\"><figcaption>A 595 nm reading turned into a concentration through your own standards.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A₅₉₅</code></td><td>blank-corrected absorbance</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>slope</code></td><td>absorbance per mg/mL</td><td>AU·mL/mg</td></tr><tr><td><code>intercept</code></td><td>fitted absorbance at zero protein</td><td>AU</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Bradford (1976), Analytical Biochemistry 72:248–254.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Bradford Assay (standard curve)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: sample absorbance of <strong>0.48</strong>, standard curve slope of <strong>1.05</strong> per mg/mL, standard curve intercept of <strong>0.03</strong>, sample dilution factor of <strong>1</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives protein concentration of <strong>0.4286</strong> mg/mL.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.1 – 1.4 mg/mL</td><td><strong>Working range</strong></td><td>linear for most proteins</td></tr><tr><td>below 0.1</td><td><strong>Too dilute</strong></td><td>use a micro-Bradford cuvette</td></tr><tr><td>above 1.4</td><td><strong>Saturated</strong></td><td>dilute and re-read</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Bradford binds basic and aromatic residues, so the response is strongly protein-dependent: BSA is the usual standard, but a lysine-rich protein will over-read against it.</p></li><li><p>Detergents above 0.1% (especially SDS and Triton X-100) interfere; so do strong reducing agents.</p></li><li><p>The dye binds the cuvette, so use a fresh one or rinse with methanol between samples.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Reading Bradford at 562 nm, the BCA wavelength — the peak is 595 nm.</li><li>Using a BSA curve for a very different protein and quoting the number as absolute.</li><li>Leaving the dye–sample mix to stand for different times for standards and samples.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/bca-assay-calculator\\\">BCA assay</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\\\">Protein molar conversions</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Is Bradford or BCA better?\",\"a\":\"BCA is more tolerant of detergents and more linear; Bradford is faster and less sensitive to reducing agents. Both need a standard curve made with a protein similar to your sample.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Bradford Assay (standard curve) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns protein concentration. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"},{\"q\":\"Does it work in both directions?\",\"a\":\"Where the maths allows it, yes: the page states which values it solves for and which you supply. A field marked as a result is always computed, never typed into.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"a595\",\"label\":\"Sample absorbance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.48\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Read at 595 nm, blank subtracted\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"slope\",\"label\":\"Standard curve slope\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.05\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"intercept\",\"label\":\"Standard curve intercept\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.03\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Sample dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Protein concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"(a595-intercept)/slope*dilution\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/bradford-assay-calculator\",\"slug\":\"bradford-assay-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Bradford Assay Calculator (standard curve)\",\"title\":\"Bradford Assay (standard curve) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Bradford is the quickest colorimetric protein assay and the one most sensitive to which protein you are measuring. Free bradford assay (standard curve) calcul\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-bradford-assay-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a595\",\"l\":\"Sample absorbance\",\"h\":\"Read at 595 nm, blank subtracted\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"slope\",\"l\":\"Standard curve slope\",\"suf\":\"per mg/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"intercept\",\"l\":\"Standard curve intercept\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Sample dilution factor\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Protein concentration\",\"suf\":\"mg/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"a595\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.48\",\"c\":null}]},\"slope\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.05\",\"c\":null}]},\"intercept\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.03\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"(a595-intercept)/slope*dilution\",\"r\":[\"a595\",\"intercept\",\"slope\",\"dilution\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(27, 'beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration', 'biology', 'Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration', 'Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration', 'Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration Calculator', 'A280 is the fastest protein measurement there is: no reagents, no standard curve, and you get the sample back. Free beer-lambert a280 protein concentration ca', 'A280 is the fastest protein measurement there is: no reagents, no standard curve, and you get the sample back. It returns protein concentration and protein concentration, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator applies the Beer–Lambert law with an extinction coefficient to turn an absorbance into mg/mL. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration\",\"html\":\"<p>A = ε × c × l, so c (M) = A ÷ (ε × l); c (mg/mL) = c (M) × molecular weight</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration — Absorbance, coefficient and path length give concentration.\\\" title=\\\"Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration\\\"><figcaption>Absorbance, coefficient and path length give concentration.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A₂₈₀</code></td><td>absorbance at 280 nm</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>ε</code></td><td>molar extinction coefficient</td><td>M⁻¹cm⁻¹</td></tr><tr><td><code>l</code></td><td>path length</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>MW</code></td><td>molecular weight</td><td>Da</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Beer–Lambert law; extinction coefficients from the ExPASy ProtParam convention.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: absorbance at 280 nm of <strong>0.85</strong>, extinction coefficient of <strong>43,824</strong> M⁻¹cm⁻¹, molecular weight of <strong>55,000</strong> Da, path length of <strong>1</strong> cm.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives protein concentration of <strong>1.07</strong> mg/mL, protein concentration of <strong>19.4</strong> µM.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 AU ≈ 1 mg/mL</td><td><strong>Rule of thumb</strong></td><td>true only for proteins with ε ≈ MW</td></tr><tr><td>0.1 – 1.5 AU</td><td><strong>Reliable</strong></td><td>the linear range of most spectrophotometers</td></tr><tr><td>above 1.5</td><td><strong>Too concentrated</strong></td><td>dilute; the detector is saturating</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The rule that 1 mg/mL gives 1 AU at 280 nm is exactly true only when ε equals the molecular weight, which holds for a typical IgG and for few other proteins.</p></li><li><p>Nucleic acid contamination inflates A280 badly. Read A260 as well: a 260/280 ratio above 0.7 means the reading is mostly DNA or RNA.</p></li><li><p>For a 1 cm cuvette the path is 1; for a NanoDrop-style instrument it is 0.1–0.05 cm, which the instrument corrects for internally.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using a molar coefficient with the mg/mL formula (or the reverse) — the answer is out by a factor of the molecular weight.</li><li>Ignoring nucleic acid contamination.</li><li>Forgetting to blank against the actual buffer, which may absorb at 280 nm itself.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/protein-extinction-coefficient\\\">Protein extinction coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\\\">Protein molar conversions</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/beer-lambert-law\\\">Beer–Lambert law</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I get the extinction coefficient without a spectrophotometer?\",\"a\":\"Compute it from the sequence: 5500 × Trp + 1490 × Tyr + 125 × cystines, the ProtParam rule. The protein extinction coefficient calculator does that for you.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my A280 higher than expected?\",\"a\":\"Usually DNA, RNA or a buffer component such as DTT absorbing at 280 nm. Check the 260/280 ratio.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns protein concentration and protein concentration. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"a280\",\"label\":\"Absorbance at 280 nm\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.85\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Blanked against buffer\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ext\",\"label\":\"Extinction coefficient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"43824\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From the sequence, or 1.0 for 1 mg/mL = 1 AU\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"M⁻¹cm⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mw\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"55000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Used when the coefficient is molar\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"path\",\"label\":\"Path length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"1 cm cuvette, or the drop path of a NanoDrop\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"conc_mg\",\"label\":\"Protein concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"conc_um\",\"label\":\"Protein concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"conc_mg\",\"f\":\"a280*mw/(ext*path)\"},{\"l\":\"conc_um\",\"f\":\"a280/(ext*path)*1000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration\",\"slug\":\"beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration\",\"title\":\"Beer-Lambert A280 Protein Concentration Calculator\",\"desc\":\"A280 is the fastest protein measurement there is: no reagents, no standard curve, and you get the sample back. Free beer-lambert a280 protein concentration ca\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a280\",\"l\":\"Absorbance at 280 nm\",\"h\":\"Blanked against buffer\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ext\",\"l\":\"Extinction coefficient\",\"h\":\"From the sequence, or 1.0 for 1 mg/mL = 1 AU\",\"suf\":\"M⁻¹cm⁻¹\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight\",\"h\":\"Used when the coefficient is molar\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"path\",\"l\":\"Path length\",\"h\":\"1 cm cuvette, or the drop path of a NanoDrop\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc_mg\",\"l\":\"Protein concentration\",\"suf\":\"mg/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc_um\",\"l\":\"Protein concentration\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"a280\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.85\",\"c\":null}]},\"ext\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"43824\",\"c\":null}]},\"mw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"55000\",\"c\":null}]},\"path\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"conc_mg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"conc_um\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"conc_mg\",\"f\":\"a280*mw/(ext*path)\",\"r\":[\"a280\",\"mw\",\"ext\",\"path\"]},{\"l\":\"conc_um\",\"f\":\"a280/(ext*path)*1000000\",\"r\":[\"a280\",\"ext\",\"path\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'spectrophotometer', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(28, 'protein-extinction-coefficient', 'biology', 'Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) Calculator', 'Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) Calculator', 'Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) Calculator', 'A protein\'s extinction coefficient at 280 nm can be calculated from its amino acid composition: tryptophans and tyrosines do the absorbing, and cystines add a', 'A protein\'s extinction coefficient at 280 nm can be calculated from its amino acid composition: tryptophans and tyrosines do the absorbing, and cystines add a small amount. It returns extinction coefficient and A280 of a 1 mg/mL solution, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nEnter the counts and this returns ε in M⁻¹cm⁻¹ and the A280 of a 1 mg/mL solution. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280)\",\"html\":\"<p>ε₂₈₀ = 5500 × Trp + 1490 × Tyr + 125 × cystines</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/protein-extinction-coefficient.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) Calculator — Trp, Tyr and disulfides add up to the extinction coefficient.\\\" title=\\\"Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Trp, Tyr and disulfides add up to the extinction coefficient.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Trp</code></td><td>number of tryptophan residues</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tyr</code></td><td>number of tyrosine residues</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>cystine</code></td><td>number of disulfide bonds</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>ε</code></td><td>molar extinction coefficient</td><td>M⁻¹cm⁻¹</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Pace et al. (1995), Protein Science 4:2411–2423.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: tryptophan residues of <strong>8</strong>, tyrosine residues of <strong>12</strong>, cystine (disulfide) bonds of <strong>4</strong>, molecular weight of <strong>55,000</strong> Da.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives extinction coefficient of <strong>62,380</strong> M⁻¹cm⁻¹, a280 of a 1 mg/ml solution of <strong>1.13</strong> AU.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The coefficients come from Pace et al. (1995) and are what ExPASy ProtParam uses.</p></li><li><p>Count disulfide bonds, not free cysteines: reduced cysteines contribute nothing at 280 nm.</p></li><li><p>The A280 of a 1 mg/mL solution is the practical number for Beer–Lambert work, because it lets you skip the molecular weight.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Counting every cysteine residue instead of disulfide pairs.</li><li>Using the reduced-protein coefficient for a protein that is folded and disulfide-bonded.</li><li>Assuming the value holds in 6 M guanidine, where the spectrum shifts.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration\\\">A280 concentration</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\\\">Protein molar conversions</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What if my protein has no Trp or Tyr?\",\"a\":\"Then ε₂₈₀ is very small and A280 is useless. Use a colorimetric assay or measure at 205 nm, where the peptide backbone absorbs.\"},{\"q\":\"Does a His-tag change the coefficient?\",\"a\":\"Only slightly — histidine contributes about 20 M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 280 nm, which is negligible against a whole protein.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns extinction coefficient and A280 of a 1 mg/mL solution. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"trp\",\"label\":\"Tryptophan residues\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"W\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tyr\",\"label\":\"Tyrosine residues\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Y\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cystine\",\"label\":\"Cystine (disulfide) bonds\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"S–S pairs, not free cysteines\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mw\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"55000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ext\",\"label\":\"Extinction coefficient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"M⁻¹cm⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"a1mg\",\"label\":\"A280 of a 1 mg/mL solution\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"AU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Extinction\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"ext\",\"f\":\"trp*5500+tyr*1490+cystine*125\"},{\"l\":\"a1mg\",\"f\":\"(trp*5500+tyr*1490+cystine*125)/mw\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/protein-extinction-coefficient\",\"slug\":\"protein-extinction-coefficient\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) Calculator\",\"title\":\"Protein Extinction Coefficient (ε280) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"A protein\'s extinction coefficient at 280 nm can be calculated from its amino acid composition: tryptophans and tyrosines do the absorbing, and cystines add a\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-protein-extinction-coefficient\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"trp\",\"l\":\"Tryptophan residues\",\"h\":\"W\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tyr\",\"l\":\"Tyrosine residues\",\"h\":\"Y\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cystine\",\"l\":\"Cystine (disulfide) bonds\",\"h\":\"S–S pairs, not free cysteines\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ext\",\"l\":\"Extinction coefficient\",\"suf\":\"M⁻¹cm⁻¹\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a1mg\",\"l\":\"A280 of a 1 mg/mL solution\",\"suf\":\"AU\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"trp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8\",\"c\":null}]},\"tyr\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"cystine\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"mw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"55000\",\"c\":null}]},\"ext\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"a1mg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Extinction\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"ext\",\"f\":\"trp*5500+tyr*1490+cystine*125\",\"r\":[\"trp\",\"tyr\",\"cystine\"]},{\"l\":\"a1mg\",\"f\":\"(trp*5500+tyr*1490+cystine*125)/mw\",\"r\":[\"trp\",\"tyr\",\"cystine\",\"mw\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(29, 'protein-molar-conversions', 'biology', 'Protein Molar Conversions', 'Protein Molar Conversions', 'Protein Molar Conversions Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Protein concentrations get quoted in mg/mL, µM and nmol depending on who is asking. Free protein molar conversions calculator: enter your values and get molar', 'Protein concentrations get quoted in mg/mL, µM and nmol depending on who is asking. It returns molar concentration, molar concentration, amount per mL and mass in 1 mL, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts between mass concentration, molarity and amount using the molecular weight, so a protocol written in µM can be carried out from a stock measured in mg/mL. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Protein Molar Conversions\",\"html\":\"<p>Molarity (M) = mass concentration (g/L) ÷ molecular weight (g/mol)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/protein-molar-conversions.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Protein Molar Conversions — Mass concentration and molecular weight give molarity.\\\" title=\\\"Protein Molar Conversions\\\"><figcaption>Mass concentration and molecular weight give molarity.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>MW</code></td><td>molecular weight</td><td>Da = g/mol</td></tr><tr><td><code>c</code></td><td>mass concentration</td><td>mg/mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>M</code></td><td>molar concentration</td><td>µM</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Protein Molar Conversions?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: molecular weight of <strong>55,000</strong> Da.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives molar concentration of <strong>36.36</strong> µM, molar concentration of <strong>36,363.64</strong> nM, amount per ml of <strong>36.36</strong> nmol/mL, mass in 1 ml of <strong>2</strong> mg, mass concentration of <strong>2</strong> mg/mL.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>1 mg/mL of a 55 kDa protein is 18.2 µM; the same mass of a 5.5 kDa peptide is 182 µM. Molarity is where small proteins and peptides differ enormously from large ones.</p></li><li><p>Molecular weight in daltons is numerically the same as g/mol, which is why the unit conversion is so easy to get wrong by a factor of 1000.</p></li><li><p>For a protein complex, use the molecular weight of the whole assembly.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the molecular weight in kDa and forgetting the ×1000.</li><li>Converting a concentration of a protein solution that also contains carrier protein or BSA.</li><li>Quoting molarity for a heterogeneous sample such as a cell lysate, where it has no meaning.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration\\\">A280 concentration</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molecular-weight\\\">Protein molecular weight</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-extinction-coefficient\\\">Protein extinction coefficient</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I convert µg/mL to µM?\",\"a\":\"Divide by the molecular weight in Da and multiply by 1000: 100 µg/mL of a 50 kDa protein is 2 µM.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does 1 mg/mL give different molarities for different proteins?\",\"a\":\"Because molarity counts molecules, and heavier molecules are fewer per milligram.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Protein Molar Conversions actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns molar concentration, molar concentration, amount per mL and mass in 1 mL. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"mw\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"55000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"g/mol\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mg_ml\",\"label\":\"Mass concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"um\",\"label\":\"Molar concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"nm\",\"label\":\"Molar concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"nmol_ml\",\"label\":\"Amount per mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nmol/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mg_total\",\"label\":\"Mass in 1 mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"um\",\"f\":\"mg_ml/mw*1000000\"},{\"l\":\"nm\",\"f\":\"mg_ml/mw*1000000000\"},{\"l\":\"nmol_ml\",\"f\":\"mg_ml/mw*1000000\"},{\"l\":\"mg_total\",\"f\":\"mg_ml*1\"},{\"l\":\"mg_ml\",\"f\":\"um*mw/1000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\",\"slug\":\"protein-molar-conversions\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Protein Molar Conversions\",\"title\":\"Protein Molar Conversions Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Protein concentrations get quoted in mg/mL, µM and nmol depending on who is asking. Free protein molar conversions calculator: enter your values and get molar\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-protein-molar-conversions\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight\",\"h\":\"g/mol\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mg_ml\",\"l\":\"Mass concentration\",\"suf\":\"mg/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"um\",\"l\":\"Molar concentration\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nm\",\"l\":\"Molar concentration\",\"suf\":\"nM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nmol_ml\",\"l\":\"Amount per mL\",\"suf\":\"nmol/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mg_total\",\"l\":\"Mass in 1 mL\",\"suf\":\"mg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"mw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"55000\",\"c\":null}]},\"mg_ml\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"um\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"nm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"nmol_ml\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mg_total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"um\",\"f\":\"mg_ml/mw*1000000\",\"r\":[\"mg_ml\",\"mw\"]},{\"l\":\"nm\",\"f\":\"mg_ml/mw*1000000000\",\"r\":[\"mg_ml\",\"mw\"]},{\"l\":\"nmol_ml\",\"f\":\"mg_ml/mw*1000000\",\"r\":[\"mg_ml\",\"mw\"]},{\"l\":\"mg_total\",\"f\":\"mg_ml*1\",\"r\":[\"mg_ml\"]},{\"l\":\"mg_ml\",\"f\":\"um*mw/1000000\",\"r\":[\"um\",\"mw\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(30, 'enzyme-unit-converter', 'biology', 'Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat)', 'Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat)', 'Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat) Calculator', 'One unit of enzyme converts one micromole of substrate per minute; kcat is the same activity per active site per second. Free enzyme unit converter (u/mg, kca', 'One unit of enzyme converts one micromole of substrate per minute; kcat is the same activity per active site per second. It returns kcat (turnover number), molar activity, activity in katal per mg and catalytic efficiency (with Km), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts between specific activity (U/mg), molar activity, kcat and turnover number, which is how enzyme datasheets and papers are compared. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat)\",\"html\":\"<p>kcat (s⁻¹) = specific activity (U/mg) × 60 000 ÷ molecular weight (Da)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/enzyme-unit-converter.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat) — Specific activity and molecular weight give the turnover number.\\\" title=\\\"Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat)\\\"><figcaption>Specific activity and molecular weight give the turnover number.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>specific activity</code></td><td>µmol substrate per minute per mg enzyme</td><td>U/mg</td></tr><tr><td><code>MW</code></td><td>molecular weight of the enzyme subunit</td><td>Da</td></tr><tr><td><code>kcat</code></td><td>substrate molecules converted per active site per second</td><td>s⁻¹</td></tr><tr><td><code>Km</code></td><td>Michaelis constant</td><td>µM</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: enzyme molecular weight of <strong>48,000</strong> Da, km of <strong>250</strong> µM.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives kcat (turnover number) of <strong>150</strong> s⁻¹, molar activity of <strong>9,000</strong> min⁻¹, activity in katal per mg of <strong>2</strong> µkat/mg, catalytic efficiency (with km) of <strong>600,000</strong> M⁻¹s⁻¹, specific activity of <strong>120</strong> U/mg.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 – 10 s⁻¹</td><td><strong>Slow</strong></td><td>typical of many metabolic enzymes</td></tr><tr><td>10 – 1000 s⁻¹</td><td><strong>Efficient</strong></td><td>the usual range for well-characterised enzymes</td></tr><tr><td>above 10⁵ s⁻¹</td><td><strong>Diffusion-limited</strong></td><td>catalase, acetylcholinesterase territory</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>kcat assumes one active site per subunit. For a dimer with two active sites, divide the molar activity by two.</p></li><li><p>Catalytic efficiency is kcat/Km and approaches a diffusion limit of about 10⁸–10⁹ M⁻¹s⁻¹; values near that mean the enzyme is as fast as substrate arrival allows.</p></li><li><p>The 1 U = 1 µmol/min definition is the IUBMB one; 1 katal = 1 mol/s = 6 × 10⁷ U.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using the molecular weight of the whole multimer when the activity is quoted per subunit.</li><li>Confusing U/mg (specific activity) with U/mL (volumetric activity) — the second says nothing about purity.</li><li>Converting at 25 °C and comparing with a kcat measured at 37 °C.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/chemistry/michaelis-menten-equation\\\">Michaelis–Menten equation</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/enzyme-activity\\\">Enzyme activity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/lineweaver-burk-plot\\\">Lineweaver–Burk plot</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the difference between kcat and specific activity?\",\"a\":\"Specific activity is per milligram of protein; kcat is per molecule of enzyme per second. The molecular weight links them.\"},{\"q\":\"How do I go from U/mL to kcat?\",\"a\":\"Convert U/mL to U/mg using the protein concentration, then use the formula on this page.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns kcat (turnover number), molar activity, activity in katal per mg and catalytic efficiency (with Km). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"activity\",\"label\":\"Specific activity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"120\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Micromoles per minute per milligram\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U/mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mw\",\"label\":\"Enzyme molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"48000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"kcat\",\"label\":\"kcat (turnover number)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"s⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"molar_activity\",\"label\":\"Molar activity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"min⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ukat\",\"label\":\"Activity in katal per mg\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µkat/mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"efficiency\",\"label\":\"Catalytic efficiency (with Km)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"M⁻¹s⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"km\",\"label\":\"Km\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"250\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional — used for catalytic efficiency\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Enzyme units\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"kcat\",\"f\":\"activity*60000/mw\"},{\"l\":\"molar_activity\",\"f\":\"activity*60000/mw*60\"},{\"l\":\"ukat\",\"f\":\"activity/60\"},{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"activity*60000/mw/(km/1000000)\"},{\"l\":\"activity\",\"f\":\"kcat*mw/60000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/enzyme-unit-converter\",\"slug\":\"enzyme-unit-converter\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat)\",\"title\":\"Enzyme Unit Converter (U/mg, kcat) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"One unit of enzyme converts one micromole of substrate per minute; kcat is the same activity per active site per second. Free enzyme unit converter (u/mg, kca\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-enzyme-unit-converter\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"activity\",\"l\":\"Specific activity\",\"h\":\"Micromoles per minute per milligram\",\"suf\":\"U/mg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw\",\"l\":\"Enzyme molecular weight\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kcat\",\"l\":\"kcat (turnover number)\",\"suf\":\"s⁻¹\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"molar_activity\",\"l\":\"Molar activity\",\"suf\":\"min⁻¹\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ukat\",\"l\":\"Activity in katal per mg\",\"suf\":\"µkat/mg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"efficiency\",\"l\":\"Catalytic efficiency (with Km)\",\"suf\":\"M⁻¹s⁻¹\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"km\",\"l\":\"Km\",\"h\":\"Optional — used for catalytic efficiency\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"activity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"120\",\"c\":null}]},\"mw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"48000\",\"c\":null}]},\"kcat\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"molar_activity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ukat\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"efficiency\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"km\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"250\",\"c\":null}]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Enzyme units\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"kcat\",\"f\":\"activity*60000/mw\",\"r\":[\"activity\",\"mw\"]},{\"l\":\"molar_activity\",\"f\":\"activity*60000/mw*60\",\"r\":[\"activity\",\"mw\"]},{\"l\":\"ukat\",\"f\":\"activity/60\",\"r\":[\"activity\"]},{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"activity*60000/mw/(km/1000000)\",\"r\":[\"activity\",\"mw\",\"km\"]},{\"l\":\"activity\",\"f\":\"kcat*mw/60000\",\"r\":[\"kcat\",\"mw\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(31, 'atp-cellular-respiration-energy-yield', 'biology', 'ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield Calculator', 'ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield Calculator', 'ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield Calculator', 'How much ATP a fuel yields depends on the pathway it enters and on which shuttle carries its electrons into the mitochondrion. Free atp / cellular respiration', 'How much ATP a fuel yields depends on the pathway it enters and on which shuttle carries its electrons into the mitochondrion. It returns total ATP and energy captured, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator totals ATP, NADH and FADH₂ for glucose, fatty acids and amino acids using the modern P/O ratios of 2.5 and 1.5. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield\",\"html\":\"<p>ATP total = substrate-level ATP + 2.5 × NADH + 1.5 × FADH₂</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/atp-cellular-respiration-energy-yield.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield Calculator — Yield by pathway for the same starting material.\\\" title=\\\"ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Yield by pathway for the same starting material.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NADH</code></td><td>reducing equivalents delivered to complex I</td><td>mol/mol fuel</td></tr><tr><td><code>FADH₂</code></td><td>equivalents delivered via complex II</td><td>mol/mol fuel</td></tr><tr><td><code>P/O ratio</code></td><td>ATP per oxygen atom reduced (2.5 for NADH, 1.5 for FADH₂)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>energy</code></td><td>chemical energy captured at ~30.5 kJ/mol ATP</td><td>kJ/mol</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> P/O ratios from the current biochemistry consensus (2.5 for NADH, 1.5 for FADH₂).</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: fuel set to <strong>1.0</strong>, nadh produced of <strong>10</strong>, fadh₂ produced of <strong>2</strong>, atp (or gtp) made directly of <strong>4</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives total atp of <strong>32</strong> ATP, energy captured of <strong>976</strong> kJ/mol.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 ATP</td><td><strong>Anaerobic glycolysis</strong></td><td>glucose to lactate, no oxygen needed</td></tr><tr><td>30 – 32 ATP</td><td><strong>Aerobic glucose</strong></td><td>the textbook figure; 32 with the malate–aspartate shuttle</td></tr><tr><td>106 ATP</td><td><strong>Palmitate</strong></td><td>the classic fatty-acid figure, β-oxidation plus the cycle</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The older textbooks said 38 ATP for glucose, using P/O ratios of 3 and 2. The modern values 2.5 and 1.5 give 30–32.</p></li><li><p>The glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle costs one ATP per cytosolic NADH, which is why muscle tissue reports 30 and liver 32 for the same glucose.</p></li><li><p>Anaerobic yield is small but fast: 2 ATP per glucose, and lactate is regenerated to keep glycolysis running.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Counting the 2 ATP from glycolysis twice.</li><li>Using 3 and 2 as P/O ratios and then comparing with a modern paper.</li><li>Forgetting that amino-acid entry points differ — glutamate enters as α-ketoglutarate and yields far less than a fatty acid.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/chemistry/q10\\\">Q10 temperature coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/atp-cellular-respiration-energy-yield\\\">Respiratory quotient</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/enzyme-activity\\\">Enzyme activity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why 30 and 32 ATP for glucose?\",\"a\":\"The difference is the shuttle used to move cytosolic NADH into the mitochondrion: the malate–aspartate shuttle preserves it (32), the glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle costs 1 ATP (30).\"},{\"q\":\"How much ATP does a fatty acid give?\",\"a\":\"Palmitate yields 106 ATP by β-oxidation plus the cycle — more than three times glucose, per molecule, because it is far more reduced.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns total ATP and energy captured. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"fuel\",\"label\":\"Fuel\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"glucose-aerobic\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"glucose-aerobic\",\"Glucose (aerobic)\",\"1\"],[\"glucose-anaerobic\",\"Glucose (anaerobic)\",\"2\"],[\"palmitate-c16\",\"Palmitate (C16)\",\"3\"],[\"lactate\",\"Lactate\",\"4\"],[\"glutamate\",\"Glutamate\",\"5\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"nadh\",\"label\":\"NADH produced\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and the cycle\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"fadh2\",\"label\":\"FADH₂ produced\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"atp_sub\",\"label\":\"ATP (or GTP) made directly\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Substrate-level phosphorylation\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"atp_total\",\"label\":\"Total ATP\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ATP\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"energy\",\"label\":\"Energy captured\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kJ/mol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Yield\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"atp_total\",\"f\":\"if_else(fuel==2, atp_sub*2, atp_sub+nadh*2.5+fadh2*1.5)\"},{\"l\":\"energy\",\"f\":\"(atp_sub+nadh*2.5+fadh2*1.5)*30.5\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/atp-cellular-respiration-energy-yield\",\"slug\":\"atp-cellular-respiration-energy-yield\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield Calculator\",\"title\":\"ATP / Cellular Respiration Energy Yield Calculator\",\"desc\":\"How much ATP a fuel yields depends on the pathway it enters and on which shuttle carries its electrons into the mitochondrion. Free atp / cellular respiration\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-atp-cellular-respiration-energy-yield\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fuel\",\"l\":\"Fuel\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-fuel\",\"opts\":[[\"glucose-aerobic\",\"Glucose (aerobic)\",\"1\"],[\"glucose-anaerobic\",\"Glucose (anaerobic)\",\"2\"],[\"palmitate-c16\",\"Palmitate (C16)\",\"3\"],[\"lactate\",\"Lactate\",\"4\"],[\"glutamate\",\"Glutamate\",\"5\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nadh\",\"l\":\"NADH produced\",\"h\":\"From glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and the cycle\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fadh2\",\"l\":\"FADH₂ produced\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"atp_sub\",\"l\":\"ATP (or GTP) made directly\",\"h\":\"Substrate-level phosphorylation\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"atp_total\",\"l\":\"Total ATP\",\"suf\":\"ATP\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"energy\",\"l\":\"Energy captured\",\"suf\":\"kJ/mol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"fuel\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"glucose-aerobic\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-fuel\"},\"nadh\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"fadh2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"atp_sub\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"atp_total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"energy\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-fuel\":{\"name\":\"Fuel\",\"uiType\":\"SELECT\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"glucose-aerobic\",\"label\":\"Glucose (aerobic)\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"glucose-anaerobic\",\"label\":\"Glucose (anaerobic)\",\"value\":\"2\"},{\"uid\":\"palmitate-c16\",\"label\":\"Palmitate (C16)\",\"value\":\"3\"},{\"uid\":\"lactate\",\"label\":\"Lactate\",\"value\":\"4\"},{\"uid\":\"glutamate\",\"label\":\"Glutamate\",\"value\":\"5\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Yield\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"atp_total\",\"f\":\"if_else(fuel==2, atp_sub*2, atp_sub+nadh*2.5+fadh2*1.5)\",\"r\":[\"fuel\",\"atp_sub\",\"nadh\",\"fadh2\"]},{\"l\":\"energy\",\"f\":\"(atp_sub+nadh*2.5+fadh2*1.5)*30.5\",\"r\":[\"atp_sub\",\"nadh\",\"fadh2\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'mitochondria', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(32, 'enzyme-activity-units', 'biology', 'Enzyme Activity Calculator', 'Enzyme Activity Calculator', 'Enzyme Activity Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Enzyme activity is the rate at which a preparation converts substrate, expressed in units. Free enzyme activity calculator: enter your values and get substrat', 'Enzyme activity is the rate at which a preparation converts substrate, expressed in units. It returns substrate converted, activity and specific activity, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts between absorbance change per minute, units per mL and units per mg, using the extinction coefficient of the product and the light path. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Enzyme Activity\",\"html\":\"<p>Activity (µmol/min) = (ΔA/min ÷ (ε × l)) × volume;  1 U = 1 µmol/min</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/enzyme-activity-units.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Enzyme Activity Calculator — Take the slope of the linear phase, never the plateau.\\\" title=\\\"Enzyme Activity Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Take the slope of the linear phase, never the plateau.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ΔA/min</code></td><td>initial rate from the linear part of the curve</td><td>AU/min</td></tr><tr><td><code>ε</code></td><td>extinction coefficient of the absorbing species</td><td>M⁻¹cm⁻¹</td></tr><tr><td><code>l</code></td><td>path length</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume</code></td><td>total reaction volume</td><td>mL</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> IUBMB enzyme unit definition; ε₃₄₀ for NADH = 6220 M⁻¹cm⁻¹.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Enzyme Activity?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: absorbance change per minute of <strong>0.05</strong> AU/min, molar extinction coefficient of <strong>6,220</strong> M⁻¹cm⁻¹, path length of <strong>1</strong> cm, reaction volume of <strong>1</strong> mL, protein in the assay of <strong>0.1</strong> mg.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives substrate converted of <strong>0.008</strong> µmol/min, activity of <strong>8,038.59</strong> U/mL, specific activity of <strong>80.39</strong> U/mg.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>linear phase</td><td><strong>Use this</strong></td><td>the first 10–20% of substrate conversion</td></tr><tr><td>plateau</td><td><strong>Do not use</strong></td><td>substrate depletion or product inhibition</td></tr><tr><td>lag</td><td><strong>Do not use</strong></td><td>temperature equilibration</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Only the initial linear rate is a measure of activity. Once the curve bends, you are measuring the assay, not the enzyme.</p></li><li><p>NADH and NADPH absorb at 340 nm with ε = 6220 M⁻¹cm⁻¹, which is why so many coupled assays are read at that wavelength.</p></li><li><p>Units must be quoted with temperature, pH and substrate concentration, or they are not comparable.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the extinction coefficient of NAD⁺ instead of NADH (NAD⁺ barely absorbs at 340 nm).</li><li>Reading a plate without correcting for path length, which is much shorter than 1 cm.</li><li>Fitting the whole curve rather than the linear portion.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/enzyme-unit-converter\\\">Enzyme unit converter</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/michaelis-menten-equation\\\">Michaelis–Menten equation</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/lineweaver-burk-plot\\\">Lineweaver–Burk plot</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is 1 unit of enzyme?\",\"a\":\"One micromole of substrate converted per minute under the stated conditions.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my rate fall off?\",\"a\":\"Substrate depletion, product inhibition, or enzyme instability. Measure earlier in the curve.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Enzyme Activity actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns substrate converted, activity and specific activity. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"dabs\",\"label\":\"Absorbance change per minute\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.05\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Slope of the linear phase\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"AU/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ext\",\"label\":\"Molar extinction coefficient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6220\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10 000–15 000 for NADH at 340 nm is 6220\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"M⁻¹cm⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"path\",\"label\":\"Path length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Reaction volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"protein\",\"label\":\"Protein in the assay\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"u_ml\",\"label\":\"Activity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"u_mg\",\"label\":\"Specific activity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U/mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rate\",\"label\":\"Substrate converted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Activity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"dabs/(ext*path)*1000000*volume/1000\"},{\"l\":\"u_ml\",\"f\":\"dabs/(ext*path)*1000000*1000\"},{\"l\":\"u_mg\",\"f\":\"dabs/(ext*path)*1000000*volume/protein\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/enzyme-activity-units\",\"slug\":\"enzyme-activity-units\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Enzyme Activity Calculator\",\"title\":\"Enzyme Activity Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Enzyme activity is the rate at which a preparation converts substrate, expressed in units. Free enzyme activity calculator: enter your values and get substrat\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-enzyme-activity-units\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dabs\",\"l\":\"Absorbance change per minute\",\"h\":\"Slope of the linear phase\",\"suf\":\"AU/min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ext\",\"l\":\"Molar extinction coefficient\",\"h\":\"10 000–15 000 for NADH at 340 nm is 6220\",\"suf\":\"M⁻¹cm⁻¹\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"path\",\"l\":\"Path length\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Reaction volume\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"protein\",\"l\":\"Protein in the assay\",\"suf\":\"mg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"u_ml\",\"l\":\"Activity\",\"suf\":\"U/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"u_mg\",\"l\":\"Specific activity\",\"suf\":\"U/mg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate\",\"l\":\"Substrate converted\",\"suf\":\"µmol/min\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"dabs\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.05\",\"c\":null}]},\"ext\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6220\",\"c\":null}]},\"path\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"protein\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.1\",\"c\":null}]},\"u_ml\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"u_mg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Activity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"dabs/(ext*path)*1000000*volume/1000\",\"r\":[\"dabs\",\"ext\",\"path\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"u_ml\",\"f\":\"dabs/(ext*path)*1000000*1000\",\"r\":[\"dabs\",\"ext\",\"path\"]},{\"l\":\"u_mg\",\"f\":\"dabs/(ext*path)*1000000*volume/protein\",\"r\":[\"dabs\",\"ext\",\"path\",\"volume\",\"protein\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'spectrophotometer', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(33, 'lineweaver-burk-plot', 'biology', 'Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax Calculator', 'Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax Calculator', 'Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax Calculator — Formula & Result', 'The Lineweaver–Burk plot linearises Michaelis–Menten kinetics: 1/v against 1/[S] gives a straight line whose intercepts are 1/Vmax and −1/Km. Free lineweaver-', 'The Lineweaver–Burk plot linearises Michaelis–Menten kinetics: 1/v against 1/[S] gives a straight line whose intercepts are 1/Vmax and −1/Km. It returns vmax, km and vmax/Km, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nEnter two substrate/velocity pairs and the calculator returns Vmax, Km and catalytic efficiency. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax\",\"html\":\"<p>1/v = (Km/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/lineweaver-burk-plot.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km &amp; Vmax Calculator — The double-reciprocal line: x-intercept −1/Km, y-intercept 1/Vmax.\\\" title=\\\"Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km &amp; Vmax Calculator\\\"><figcaption>The double-reciprocal line: x-intercept −1/Km, y-intercept 1/Vmax.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>*S*</code></td><td>substrate concentration</td><td>mM</td></tr><tr><td><code>v</code></td><td>initial velocity</td><td>µmol/min</td></tr><tr><td><code>Vmax</code></td><td>velocity at saturating substrate</td><td>µmol/min</td></tr><tr><td><code>Km</code></td><td>substrate concentration at half Vmax</td><td>mM</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Lineweaver & Burk (1934); the cautions on the double-reciprocal fit follow Dowd & Riggs (1965).</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: substrate concentration 1 of <strong>0.5</strong> mM, initial velocity 1 of <strong>0.35</strong> µmol/min, substrate concentration 2 of <strong>2</strong> mM, initial velocity 2 of <strong>0.62</strong> µmol/min.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives vmax of <strong>0.8346</strong> µmol/min, km of <strong>0.6923</strong> mM, vmax/km of <strong>1.21</strong> µmol/min/mM.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>With two points, Vmax and Km are exact for a linear fit but very sensitive to a single bad measurement; four to six points from 0.2 to 5 × Km is the practical minimum.</p></li><li><p>The double-reciprocal plot weights the lowest substrate points most heavily, which is its well-known weakness. A non-linear fit to the same data is more reliable.</p></li><li><p>Km is an operational constant: it changes with pH, temperature and the second substrate.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using substrate concentrations in different units on one line.</li><li>Including points where the rate has already plateaued.</li><li>Reading Km off the y-intercept instead of the x-intercept (which is −1/Km).</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/chemistry/michaelis-menten-equation\\\">Michaelis–Menten equation</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/enzyme-unit-converter\\\">Enzyme unit converter</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cheng-prusoff-equation\\\">Cheng–Prusoff equation</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What does a high Km mean?\",\"a\":\"Low affinity: more substrate is needed to reach half of Vmax.\"},{\"q\":\"How many points do I need?\",\"a\":\"At least four, spread geometrically from about 0.2 to 5 × Km.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns vmax, km and vmax/Km. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"s1\",\"label\":\"Substrate concentration 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"v1\",\"label\":\"Initial velocity 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.35\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"s2\",\"label\":\"Substrate concentration 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"v2\",\"label\":\"Initial velocity 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.62\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"vmax\",\"label\":\"Vmax\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"km\",\"label\":\"Km\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"kcat_km\",\"label\":\"Vmax/Km\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol/min/mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Kinetics\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"vmax\",\"f\":\"1/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1))\"},{\"l\":\"km\",\"f\":\"(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1))\"},{\"l\":\"kcat_km\",\"f\":\"1/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1))/((((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1)))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/lineweaver-burk-plot\",\"slug\":\"lineweaver-burk-plot\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax Calculator\",\"title\":\"Lineweaver-Burk Plot / Km & Vmax Calculator — Formula & Result\",\"desc\":\"The Lineweaver–Burk plot linearises Michaelis–Menten kinetics: 1/v against 1/[S] gives a straight line whose intercepts are 1/Vmax and −1/Km. Free lineweaver-\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-lineweaver-burk-plot\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"s1\",\"l\":\"Substrate concentration 1\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"v1\",\"l\":\"Initial velocity 1\",\"suf\":\"µmol/min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"s2\",\"l\":\"Substrate concentration 2\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"v2\",\"l\":\"Initial velocity 2\",\"suf\":\"µmol/min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vmax\",\"l\":\"Vmax\",\"suf\":\"µmol/min\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"km\",\"l\":\"Km\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kcat_km\",\"l\":\"Vmax/Km\",\"suf\":\"µmol/min/mM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"s1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"v1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.35\",\"c\":null}]},\"s2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"v2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.62\",\"c\":null}]},\"vmax\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"km\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"kcat_km\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Kinetics\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"vmax\",\"f\":\"1/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1))\",\"r\":[\"v1\",\"v2\",\"s2\",\"s1\"]},{\"l\":\"km\",\"f\":\"(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1))\",\"r\":[\"v2\",\"v1\",\"s2\",\"s1\"]},{\"l\":\"kcat_km\",\"f\":\"1/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1))/((((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))/((1/v1)-(((1/v2)-(1/v1))/((1/s2)-(1/s1)))*(1/s1)))\",\"r\":[\"v1\",\"v2\",\"s2\",\"s1\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(34, 'cheng-prusoff-equation', 'biology', 'Cheng-Prusoff Equation Calculator', 'Cheng-Prusoff Equation Calculator', 'Cheng-Prusoff Equation Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The Cheng–Prusoff equation converts an assay IC50 into Ki, the true inhibition constant. Free cheng-prusoff equation calculator: enter your values and get ki', 'The Cheng–Prusoff equation converts an assay IC50 into Ki, the true inhibition constant. It returns ki and km apparent at this [S], and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe Cheng–Prusoff relationship converts one into the other using the substrate concentration and its Km, and it is the standard correction when comparing inhibitors across assays. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Cheng-Prusoff Equation\",\"html\":\"<p>Competitive: Ki = IC50 ÷ (1 + [S]/Km);  non-competitive: Ki = IC50;  uncompetitive: Ki = IC50 ÷ (1 + Km/[S])</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cheng-prusoff-equation.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Cheng-Prusoff Equation Calculator — IC50 corrected by the substrate-to-Km ratio gives Ki.\\\" title=\\\"Cheng-Prusoff Equation Calculator\\\"><figcaption>IC50 corrected by the substrate-to-Km ratio gives Ki.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>IC50</code></td><td>half-maximal inhibitory concentration in the assay</td><td>nM</td></tr><tr><td><code>[S]</code></td><td>substrate concentration</td><td>µM</td></tr><tr><td><code>Km</code></td><td>Michaelis constant of the substrate</td><td>µM</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ki</code></td><td>dissociation constant of the inhibitor</td><td>nM</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Cheng & Prusoff (1973), Biochemical Pharmacology 22:3099–3108.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Cheng-Prusoff Equation?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: ic50 of <strong>120</strong> nM, substrate concentration of <strong>1</strong> × Km, km of the substrate of <strong>1</strong> µM, inhibition type set to <strong>1.0</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives ki of <strong>60</strong> nM, km apparent at this [s] of <strong>2</strong> µM.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>competitive</td><td><strong>Ki &lt; IC50</strong></td><td>raising [S] makes inhibition look weaker</td></tr><tr><td>non-competitive</td><td><strong>Ki = IC50</strong></td><td>substrate concentration does not matter</td></tr><tr><td>uncompetitive</td><td><strong>Ki &lt; IC50</strong></td><td>the inhibitor prefers the ES complex</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The correction only holds for reversible inhibitors under rapid-equilibrium assumptions; tight-binding inhibitors need the Morrison equation.</p></li><li><p>Assay at a substrate concentration near Km: that is where the correction is most robust and where the signal is still workable.</p></li><li><p>Report Ki and the conditions: a Ki without Km and [S] cannot be reproduced.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Applying the competitive correction to a non-competitive inhibitor.</li><li>Using [S] in µM with Km in mM.</li><li>Treating IC50 from a cell-based assay as Ki — transport and protein binding intervene.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/lineweaver-burk-plot\\\">Lineweaver–Burk plot</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/michaelis-menten-equation\\\">Michaelis–Menten equation</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-inhibition\\\">Percent inhibition</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is IC50 different from Ki?\",\"a\":\"Because IC50 depends on how much substrate is present: more substrate competes with the inhibitor and raises the apparent IC50.\"},{\"q\":\"When can I skip the correction?\",\"a\":\"When [S] is far below Km, the competitive correction factor is close to 1.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Cheng-Prusoff Equation actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns ki and km apparent at this [S]. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"ic50\",\"label\":\"IC50\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"120\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From the competition assay\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"s\",\"label\":\"Substrate concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Radiometric assays often run at 1 × Km\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"× Km\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"km\",\"label\":\"Km of the substrate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Not needed when the substrate box is in Km units\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mode\",\"label\":\"Inhibition type\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"competitive\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"competitive\",\"Competitive\",\"1\"],[\"non-competitive\",\"Non-competitive\",\"2\"],[\"uncompetitive\",\"Uncompetitive\",\"3\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ki\",\"label\":\"Ki\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"km_app\",\"label\":\"Km apparent at this [S]\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Ki\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"ki\",\"f\":\"if_else(mode==1, ic50/(1+s), if_else(mode==2, ic50, ic50/(1+1/s)))\"},{\"l\":\"km_app\",\"f\":\"km*(1+s)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cheng-prusoff-equation\",\"slug\":\"cheng-prusoff-equation\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Cheng-Prusoff Equation Calculator\",\"title\":\"Cheng-Prusoff Equation Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The Cheng–Prusoff equation converts an assay IC50 into Ki, the true inhibition constant. Free cheng-prusoff equation calculator: enter your values and get ki\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cheng-prusoff-equation\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ic50\",\"l\":\"IC50\",\"h\":\"From the competition assay\",\"suf\":\"nM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"s\",\"l\":\"Substrate concentration\",\"h\":\"Radiometric assays often run at 1 × Km\",\"suf\":\"× Km\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"km\",\"l\":\"Km of the substrate\",\"h\":\"Not needed when the substrate box is in Km units\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mode\",\"l\":\"Inhibition type\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-mode\",\"opts\":[[\"competitive\",\"Competitive\",\"1\"],[\"non-competitive\",\"Non-competitive\",\"2\"],[\"uncompetitive\",\"Uncompetitive\",\"3\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ki\",\"l\":\"Ki\",\"suf\":\"nM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"km_app\",\"l\":\"Km apparent at this [S]\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"ic50\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"120\",\"c\":null}]},\"s\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"km\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"mode\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"competitive\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-mode\"},\"ki\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"km_app\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-mode\":{\"name\":\"Inhibition type\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"competitive\",\"label\":\"Competitive\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"non-competitive\",\"label\":\"Non-competitive\",\"value\":\"2\"},{\"uid\":\"uncompetitive\",\"label\":\"Uncompetitive\",\"value\":\"3\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Ki\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"ki\",\"f\":\"if_else(mode==1, ic50/(1+s), if_else(mode==2, ic50, ic50/(1+1/s)))\",\"r\":[\"mode\",\"ic50\",\"s\"]},{\"l\":\"km_app\",\"f\":\"km*(1+s)\",\"r\":[\"km\",\"s\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(35, 'percent-inhibition', 'biology', 'Percent Inhibition Calculator', 'Percent Inhibition Calculator', 'Percent Inhibition Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Percent inhibition compares a treated sample with an untreated control, and there are two conventions in use: relative to the control signal, and relative to', 'Percent inhibition compares a treated sample with an untreated control, and there are two conventions in use: relative to the control signal, and relative to the full signal window. It returns percent inhibition, percent of control remaining and percent of signal window, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator reports both, plus the IC50-style fraction remaining. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Percent Inhibition\",\"html\":\"<p>Inhibition (%) = (control − sample) ÷ (control − blank) × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/percent-inhibition.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Percent Inhibition Calculator — Control, sample and blank, and the inhibition they imply.\\\" title=\\\"Percent Inhibition Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Control, sample and blank, and the inhibition they imply.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>control</code></td><td>mean of untreated wells</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>sample</code></td><td>mean of treated wells</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>blank</code></td><td>background with no enzyme or no cells</td><td>AU</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Percent Inhibition?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: control signal of <strong>1.2</strong> AU, sample signal of <strong>0.45</strong> AU, blank / background of <strong>0.05</strong> AU.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives percent inhibition of <strong>65.22</strong>%, percent of control remaining of <strong>37.5</strong>%, percent of signal window of <strong>34.78</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 – 20%</td><td><strong>Weak</strong></td><td>within assay noise for many systems</td></tr><tr><td>20 – 80%</td><td><strong>Dose-dependent</strong></td><td>the useful part of a dose–response curve</td></tr><tr><td>80 – 100%</td><td><strong>Near-complete</strong></td><td>check for cytotoxicity or assay interference</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Blank-correcting both arms matters: a raw comparison against an uncorrected control understates inhibition whenever the background is a significant fraction of the signal.</p></li><li><p>Percent inhibition is concentration-dependent; on its own, without the concentration, it is not a property of the compound.</p></li><li><p>Aggregators and fluorescence quenchers show up as 100% inhibition at every concentration — a flat curve is a warning sign, not a hit.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Averaging wells with very different cell numbers and calling it a mean.</li><li>Comparing a treated plate read on one day with a control plate read on another.</li><li>Reporting inhibition without the concentration and the incubation time.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cheng-prusoff-equation\\\">Cheng–Prusoff equation</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-viability\\\">Percent viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/alamarblue-cell-viability\\\">AlamarBlue viability</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Should I blank-correct?\",\"a\":\"Yes, both the control and the sample. Otherwise the background dilutes the apparent effect.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a good percent inhibition?\",\"a\":\"There is no universal threshold; what matters is the shape of the dose–response curve and the reproducibility of the control.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Percent Inhibition actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns percent inhibition, percent of control remaining and percent of signal window. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"control\",\"label\":\"Control signal\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Untreated wells\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"AU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"sample\",\"label\":\"Sample signal\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.45\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Treated wells\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"AU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"blank\",\"label\":\"Blank / background\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.05\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"No-enzyme or no-cell wells\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"AU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"inhibition\",\"label\":\"Percent inhibition\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"remaining\",\"label\":\"Percent of control remaining\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"window\",\"label\":\"Percent of signal window\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Inhibition\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"inhibition\",\"f\":\"(control-sample)/(control-blank)*100\"},{\"l\":\"remaining\",\"f\":\"sample/control*100\"},{\"l\":\"window\",\"f\":\"(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/percent-inhibition\",\"slug\":\"percent-inhibition\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Percent Inhibition Calculator\",\"title\":\"Percent Inhibition Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Percent inhibition compares a treated sample with an untreated control, and there are two conventions in use: relative to the control signal, and relative to\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-percent-inhibition\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"control\",\"l\":\"Control signal\",\"h\":\"Untreated wells\",\"suf\":\"AU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sample\",\"l\":\"Sample signal\",\"h\":\"Treated wells\",\"suf\":\"AU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"blank\",\"l\":\"Blank / background\",\"h\":\"No-enzyme or no-cell wells\",\"suf\":\"AU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"inhibition\",\"l\":\"Percent inhibition\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"remaining\",\"l\":\"Percent of control remaining\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"window\",\"l\":\"Percent of signal window\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"control\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"sample\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.45\",\"c\":null}]},\"blank\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.05\",\"c\":null}]},\"inhibition\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"remaining\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"window\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Inhibition\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"inhibition\",\"f\":\"(control-sample)/(control-blank)*100\",\"r\":[\"control\",\"sample\",\"blank\"]},{\"l\":\"remaining\",\"f\":\"sample/control*100\",\"r\":[\"sample\",\"control\"]},{\"l\":\"window\",\"f\":\"(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\",\"r\":[\"sample\",\"blank\",\"control\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'plate', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(36, 'protein-hydrodynamic-radius', 'biology', 'Protein Hydrodynamic Radius Calculator', 'Protein Hydrodynamic Radius Calculator', 'Protein Hydrodynamic Radius Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The hydrodynamic radius is the effective size a protein presents to the solvent — what size-exclusion chromatography and dynamic light scattering actually mea', 'The hydrodynamic radius is the effective size a protein presents to the solvent — what size-exclusion chromatography and dynamic light scattering actually measure. It returns hydrodynamic radius, diffusion coefficient and relative elution volume (SEC), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator estimates it from molecular weight, and converts between radius, diffusion coefficient and elution behaviour for globular and disordered proteins. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Protein Hydrodynamic Radius\",\"html\":\"<p>Globular: Rh ≈ 0.066 × MW^⅓ nm;  D = kT ÷ (6πηR)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/protein-hydrodynamic-radius.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Protein Hydrodynamic Radius Calculator — Mass and fold give a radius, and the radius gives the diffusion coefficient.\\\" title=\\\"Protein Hydrodynamic Radius Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Mass and fold give a radius, and the radius gives the diffusion coefficient.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>MW</code></td><td>molecular weight</td><td>Da</td></tr><tr><td><code>Rh</code></td><td>hydrodynamic radius</td><td>nm</td></tr><tr><td><code>η</code></td><td>solvent viscosity</td><td>Pa·s</td></tr><tr><td><code>D</code></td><td>translational diffusion coefficient</td><td>µm²/s</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Protein Hydrodynamic Radius?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: molecular weight of <strong>55,000</strong> Da, fold set to <strong>1.0</strong>, solvent viscosity of <strong>0.89</strong> cP, temperature of <strong>25</strong>°C.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives hydrodynamic radius of <strong>2.5</strong> nm, diffusion coefficient of <strong>98.07</strong> µm²/s, relative elution volume (sec) of <strong>1.35</strong> Ve/V0.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 – 3 nm</td><td><strong>Small globular</strong></td><td>lysozyme, cytochrome c</td></tr><tr><td>3 – 6 nm</td><td><strong>Typical globular</strong></td><td>BSA, IgG fragments</td></tr><tr><td>above 8 nm</td><td><strong>Large or disordered</strong></td><td>multimers, complexes, IDPs</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>A disordered protein of the same molecular weight is two to three times larger than a globular one; the difference is what makes SEC a shape measurement, not just a mass one.</p></li><li><p>The Stokes–Einstein relation assumes a sphere, which is why a radius derived this way is \'hydrodynamic\' rather than geometric.</p></li><li><p>Glycosylation adds mass without adding much radius per dalton, so heavily modified proteins sit below the globular line.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using viscosity in poise where Pa·s is needed (a factor of 10).</li><li>Treating the estimate as a measurement: DLS gives the real number.</li><li>Comparing Rh across temperatures without correcting the viscosity of water.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\\\">Protein molar conversions</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration\\\">A280 concentration</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal hydrodynamic radius for a 50 kDa protein?\",\"a\":\"About 3 nm if globular, 6–8 nm if partly disordered.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my SEC elution suggest a much larger protein?\",\"a\":\"Because SEC separates by size, and an elongated or disordered protein behaves like a larger sphere.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Protein Hydrodynamic Radius actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns hydrodynamic radius, diffusion coefficient and relative elution volume (SEC). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"mw\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"55000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"shape\",\"label\":\"Fold\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"globular\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"globular\",\"Globular\",\"1\"],[\"disordered-coil\",\"Disordered / coil\",\"2\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"viscosity\",\"label\":\"Solvent viscosity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.89\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Water at 25 °C = 0.89 cP\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cP\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"temp\",\"label\":\"Temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rh\",\"label\":\"Hydrodynamic radius\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Diffusion coefficient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µm²/s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ve\",\"label\":\"Relative elution volume (SEC)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Ve/V0\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Size\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rh\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1, 0.066*pow(mw,0.333), 0.192*pow(mw,0.5))\"},{\"l\":\"d\",\"f\":\"0.0000000000000000000000138*(temp+273.15)/(6*3.14159265358979*viscosity*0.001*(0.066*pow(mw,0.333)*0.000000001))*1000000000000\"},{\"l\":\"ve\",\"f\":\"1+0.35*pow(0.066*pow(mw,0.333)/2.5,3)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/protein-hydrodynamic-radius\",\"slug\":\"protein-hydrodynamic-radius\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Protein Hydrodynamic Radius Calculator\",\"title\":\"Protein Hydrodynamic Radius Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The hydrodynamic radius is the effective size a protein presents to the solvent — what size-exclusion chromatography and dynamic light scattering actually mea\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-protein-hydrodynamic-radius\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"shape\",\"l\":\"Fold\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-shape\",\"opts\":[[\"globular\",\"Globular\",\"1\"],[\"disordered-coil\",\"Disordered / coil\",\"2\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"viscosity\",\"l\":\"Solvent viscosity\",\"h\":\"Water at 25 °C = 0.89 cP\",\"suf\":\"cP\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"temp\",\"l\":\"Temperature\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rh\",\"l\":\"Hydrodynamic radius\",\"suf\":\"nm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"d\",\"l\":\"Diffusion coefficient\",\"suf\":\"µm²/s\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ve\",\"l\":\"Relative elution volume (SEC)\",\"suf\":\"Ve/V0\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"mw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"55000\",\"c\":null}]},\"shape\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"globular\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-shape\"},\"viscosity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.89\",\"c\":null}]},\"temp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"rh\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"d\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ve\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-shape\":{\"name\":\"Fold\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"globular\",\"label\":\"Globular\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"disordered-coil\",\"label\":\"Disordered / coil\",\"value\":\"2\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Size\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rh\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1, 0.066*pow(mw,0.333), 0.192*pow(mw,0.5))\",\"r\":[\"shape\",\"mw\"]},{\"l\":\"d\",\"f\":\"0.0000000000000000000000138*(temp+273.15)/(6*3.14159265358979*viscosity*0.001*(0.066*pow(mw,0.333)*0.000000001))*1000000000000\",\"r\":[\"temp\",\"viscosity\",\"mw\"]},{\"l\":\"ve\",\"f\":\"1+0.35*pow(0.066*pow(mw,0.333)/2.5,3)\",\"r\":[\"mw\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:41', '2026-10-29 14:08:41'),
(37, 'protein-gravy-calculator', 'biology', 'Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) Calculator', 'Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) Calculator', 'Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'GRAVY — grand average of hydropathicity — is the mean Kyte–Doolittle hydropathy of a protein\'s residues. Free protein gravy (hydropathy) calculator: enter you', 'GRAVY — grand average of hydropathicity — is the mean Kyte–Doolittle hydropathy of a protein\'s residues. It returns GRAVY and hydrophobic fraction, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nPositive values mean the protein is hydrophobic, negative means hydrophilic, and the number is a quick read on solubility. This calculator computes it from residue counts. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy)\",\"html\":\"<p>GRAVY = Σ (hydropathy value of each residue) ÷ number of residues</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/protein-gravy-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) Calculator — GRAVY on the Kyte–Doolittle scale.\\\" title=\\\"Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) Calculator\\\"><figcaption>GRAVY on the Kyte–Doolittle scale.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>hydrophobic</code></td><td>A, V, I, L, M, F, W, Y — positive Kyte–Doolittle values</td><td>count</td></tr><tr><td><code>neutral</code></td><td>G, P, S, T, C, N, Q — near zero</td><td>count</td></tr><tr><td><code>hydrophilic</code></td><td>D, E, K, R, H — strongly negative</td><td>count</td></tr><tr><td><code>GRAVY</code></td><td>mean hydropathy, roughly −2 to +2</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Kyte & Doolittle (1982), Journal of Molecular Biology 157:105–132.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: hydrophobic residues (a v i l m f w y) of <strong>120</strong>, neutral residues (g p s t c n q) of <strong>80</strong>, hydrophilic residues (d e k r h) of <strong>100</strong>, total residues of <strong>300</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives gravy of <strong>-0.3867</strong>, hydrophobic fraction of <strong>40</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below −1.0</td><td><strong>Hydrophilic</strong></td><td>soluble cytoplasmic protein</td></tr><tr><td>−1.0 to 0</td><td><strong>Mixed</strong></td><td>soluble, often with a hydrophobic core</td></tr><tr><td>0 to +0.5</td><td><strong>Hydrophobic</strong></td><td>membrane-associated or aggregation-prone</td></tr><tr><td>above +0.5</td><td><strong>Strongly hydrophobic</strong></td><td>membrane protein or a fragment of one</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The grouped average uses representative values; residue-exact GRAVY needs the real sequence and a lookup table, which is what ProtParam does.</p></li><li><p>GRAVY is a bulk property: a soluble protein can contain a strongly hydrophobic stretch (a transmembrane helix) and still average out hydrophilic.</p></li><li><p>For aggregation risk, look at the hydrophobic fraction inside a sliding window, not the whole-protein average.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Counting residues twice or using the sequence length instead of the counted total.</li><li>Reading GRAVY as a solubility test — it correlates with, but does not determine, solubility.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\\\">Protein molar conversions</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-hydrodynamic-radius\\\">Protein hydrodynamic radius</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What GRAVY value means a protein is soluble?\",\"a\":\"Usually below 0, and comfortably below −0.5. Overexpression constructs often carry a fusion tag partly to push GRAVY down.\"},{\"q\":\"Is GRAVY the same as the hydropathy plot?\",\"a\":\"No. GRAVY is one number for the whole protein; a hydropathy plot slides a window along the sequence to find transmembrane segments.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns GRAVY and hydrophobic fraction. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"hydrophobic\",\"label\":\"Hydrophobic residues (A V I L M F W Y)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"120\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Counted in the sequence\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"neutral\",\"label\":\"Neutral residues (G P S T C N Q)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"80\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"hydrophilic\",\"label\":\"Hydrophilic residues (D E K R H)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total residues\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"300\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gravy\",\"label\":\"GRAVY\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hydrophobic_pct\",\"label\":\"Hydrophobic fraction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Hydropathy\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"gravy\",\"f\":\"(1.8*hydrophobic+(-0.9)*neutral+(-2.6)*hydrophilic)/total\"},{\"l\":\"hydrophobic_pct\",\"f\":\"hydrophobic/total*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/protein-gravy-calculator\",\"slug\":\"protein-gravy-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) Calculator\",\"title\":\"Protein GRAVY (Hydropathy) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"GRAVY — grand average of hydropathicity — is the mean Kyte–Doolittle hydropathy of a protein\'s residues. Free protein gravy (hydropathy) calculator: enter you\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-protein-gravy-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hydrophobic\",\"l\":\"Hydrophobic residues (A V I L M F W Y)\",\"h\":\"Counted in the sequence\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"neutral\",\"l\":\"Neutral residues (G P S T C N Q)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hydrophilic\",\"l\":\"Hydrophilic residues (D E K R H)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total residues\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gravy\",\"l\":\"GRAVY\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hydrophobic_pct\",\"l\":\"Hydrophobic fraction\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"hydrophobic\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"120\",\"c\":null}]},\"neutral\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"80\",\"c\":null}]},\"hydrophilic\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"300\",\"c\":null}]},\"gravy\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hydrophobic_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Hydropathy\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"gravy\",\"f\":\"(1.8*hydrophobic+(-0.9)*neutral+(-2.6)*hydrophilic)/total\",\"r\":[\"hydrophobic\",\"neutral\",\"hydrophilic\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"hydrophobic_pct\",\"f\":\"hydrophobic/total*100\",\"r\":[\"hydrophobic\",\"total\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(38, 'enzyme-specific-activity-yield', 'biology', 'Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield', 'Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield', 'Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield Calculator', 'Purification tables report specific activity and fold purification, and both come from the same three numbers: total protein, total activity and the starting', 'Purification tables report specific activity and fold purification, and both come from the same three numbers: total protein, total activity and the starting values. It returns specific activity, starting specific activity, fold purification and activity yield, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator builds the two summary figures for any step and shows what the recovery cost. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield\",\"html\":\"<p>Specific activity = total activity ÷ total protein;  fold = specific ÷ starting specific;  yield = activity recovered ÷ activity started with × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/enzyme-specific-activity-yield.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield — Activity over protein gives specific activity; compare it with the start.\\\" title=\\\"Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield\\\"><figcaption>Activity over protein gives specific activity; compare it with the start.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>total protein</code></td><td>protein in the fraction</td><td>mg</td></tr><tr><td><code>total activity</code></td><td>units in the fraction</td><td>U</td></tr><tr><td><code>fold</code></td><td>purification factor</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>yield</code></td><td>recovery of activity</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: total protein in this step of <strong>45</strong> mg, total activity in this step of <strong>5,400</strong> U, total protein in the starting material of <strong>900</strong> mg, total activity in the starting material of <strong>7,200</strong> U.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives specific activity of <strong>120</strong> U/mg, starting specific activity of <strong>8</strong> U/mg, fold purification of <strong>15</strong> ×, activity yield of <strong>75</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Fold purification and yield always trade off: the step that purifies most is usually the one that loses the most activity.</p></li><li><p>A step with a fold below 1 has removed protein and activity in the same proportion — it is a concentration step, not a purification step.</p></li><li><p>Specific activity is only comparable if it is measured at the same temperature, pH and substrate concentration in every row of the table.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using the protein concentration of the fraction instead of the total.</li><li>Adding the yields of successive steps — they multiply, not add.</li><li>Comparing specific activities measured with different substrates.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/enzyme-unit-converter\\\">Enzyme unit converter</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/enzyme-activity\\\">Enzyme activity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/bca-assay-calculator\\\">BCA assay</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good fold purification?\",\"a\":\"There is no target; what matters is that specific activity rises and enough activity survives for the next step.\"},{\"q\":\"Why did my yield rise?\",\"a\":\"Usually activation or removal of an inhibitor — or an assay artefact. Re-measure before believing it.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns specific activity, starting specific activity, fold purification and activity yield. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"total_protein\",\"label\":\"Total protein in this step\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"45\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total_activity\",\"label\":\"Total activity in this step\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5400\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"start_protein\",\"label\":\"Total protein in the starting material\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"900\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"start_activity\",\"label\":\"Total activity in the starting material\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"7200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"specific\",\"label\":\"Specific activity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U/mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"start_specific\",\"label\":\"Starting specific activity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U/mg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fold\",\"label\":\"Fold purification\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"yield\",\"label\":\"Activity yield\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Purification\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"specific\",\"f\":\"total_activity/total_protein\"},{\"l\":\"start_specific\",\"f\":\"start_activity/start_protein\"},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"(total_activity/total_protein)/(start_activity/start_protein)\"},{\"l\":\"yield\",\"f\":\"total_activity/start_activity*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/enzyme-specific-activity-yield\",\"slug\":\"enzyme-specific-activity-yield\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield\",\"title\":\"Enzyme Kinetics: Specific Activity and Yield Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Purification tables report specific activity and fold purification, and both come from the same three numbers: total protein, total activity and the starting\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-enzyme-specific-activity-yield\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_protein\",\"l\":\"Total protein in this step\",\"suf\":\"mg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_activity\",\"l\":\"Total activity in this step\",\"suf\":\"U\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"start_protein\",\"l\":\"Total protein in the starting material\",\"suf\":\"mg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"start_activity\",\"l\":\"Total activity in the starting material\",\"suf\":\"U\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"specific\",\"l\":\"Specific activity\",\"suf\":\"U/mg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"start_specific\",\"l\":\"Starting specific activity\",\"suf\":\"U/mg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fold\",\"l\":\"Fold purification\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"yield\",\"l\":\"Activity yield\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"total_protein\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"45\",\"c\":null}]},\"total_activity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5400\",\"c\":null}]},\"start_protein\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"900\",\"c\":null}]},\"start_activity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"7200\",\"c\":null}]},\"specific\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"start_specific\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fold\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"yield\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Purification\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"specific\",\"f\":\"total_activity/total_protein\",\"r\":[\"total_activity\",\"total_protein\"]},{\"l\":\"start_specific\",\"f\":\"start_activity/start_protein\",\"r\":[\"start_activity\",\"start_protein\"]},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"(total_activity/total_protein)/(start_activity/start_protein)\",\"r\":[\"total_activity\",\"total_protein\",\"start_activity\",\"start_protein\"]},{\"l\":\"yield\",\"f\":\"total_activity/start_activity*100\",\"r\":[\"total_activity\",\"start_activity\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(39, 'osmolarity-osmotic-pressure', 'biology', 'Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure Calculator', 'Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure Calculator', 'Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Osmolarity counts the particles a solute produces in solution, which is why 150 mM NaCl is 300 mOsm. Free osmolarity / osmotic pressure calculator: enter your', 'Osmolarity counts the particles a solute produces in solution, which is why 150 mM NaCl is 300 mOsm. It returns osmolarity, osmotic pressure and osmotic pressure, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts concentration, osmolality and osmotic pressure, and it handles the dissociation factor that makes salt and sugars behave differently. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure\",\"html\":\"<p>Osmolarity = M × i;  π = iMRT</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure Calculator — Concentration × particles, then the gas law, gives osmotic pressure.\\\" title=\\\"Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Concentration × particles, then the gas law, gives osmotic pressure.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>M</code></td><td>molar concentration</td><td>mol/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>i</code></td><td>van \'t Hoff factor, particles per formula unit</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>R</code></td><td>gas constant</td><td>8.314 J/mol·K</td></tr><tr><td><code>π</code></td><td>osmotic pressure</td><td>kPa</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> van \'t Hoff relation; physiological ranges from standard clinical chemistry references.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: molar concentration of <strong>150</strong> mM, dissociation factor (i) of <strong>2</strong>, temperature of <strong>37</strong>°C.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives osmolarity of <strong>300</strong> mOsm/L, osmotic pressure of <strong>773.58</strong> kPa, osmotic pressure of <strong>7.63</strong> atm.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>275 – 295 mOsm/L</td><td><strong>Isotonic</strong></td><td>plasma-like; safe for cells</td></tr><tr><td>below 275</td><td><strong>Hypotonic</strong></td><td>cells swell and can lyse</td></tr><tr><td>above 295</td><td><strong>Hypertonic</strong></td><td>cells shrink; used for cryopreservation</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Osmolarity is per litre of solution; osmolality is per kilogram of water. In dilute solutions they are the same; in plasma they differ by about 1%.</p></li><li><p>Osmotic pressure is a colligative property: it depends on particle count, not identity. That is why 150 mM NaCl and 300 mM glucose are both about 300 mOsm.</p></li><li><p>Cell culture media are formulated to 260–320 mOsm/L, and adding a drug stock can push a medium out of range if the stock is concentrated in salt.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Forgetting the dissociation factor for salts.</li><li>Entering concentration in mmol/L and reading the result as molar.</li><li>Using 8.314 with pressure in atm.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/chemistry/molarity\\\">Molarity</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/percent-solution\\\">Percent solution</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\\\">Dilution (C1V1 = C2V2)</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the osmolarity of normal saline?\",\"a\":\"154 mM NaCl × 2 = 308 mOsm/L, close to plasma at about 290.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is osmolality used in clinical work?\",\"a\":\"Because it is measured per kilogram of water, which does not change with temperature or with the volume the solute displaces.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns osmolarity, osmotic pressure and osmotic pressure. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Molar concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"150\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Total solute molarity\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"i\",\"label\":\"Dissociation factor (i)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"2 for NaCl, 1 for glucose, 1.8 for a weak electrolyte\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"temp\",\"label\":\"Temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"37\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"osmolarity\",\"label\":\"Osmolarity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mOsm/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pressure\",\"label\":\"Osmotic pressure\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kPa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"atm\",\"label\":\"Osmotic pressure\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"atm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Osmotic pressure\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"osmolarity\",\"f\":\"conc*i\"},{\"l\":\"pressure\",\"f\":\"conc*i*8.314*(temp+273.15)/1000\"},{\"l\":\"atm\",\"f\":\"conc*i*0.082057*(temp+273.15)/1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\",\"slug\":\"osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure Calculator\",\"title\":\"Osmolarity / Osmotic Pressure Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Osmolarity counts the particles a solute produces in solution, which is why 150 mM NaCl is 300 mOsm. Free osmolarity / osmotic pressure calculator: enter your\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Molar concentration\",\"h\":\"Total solute molarity\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"i\",\"l\":\"Dissociation factor (i)\",\"h\":\"2 for NaCl, 1 for glucose, 1.8 for a weak electrolyte\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"temp\",\"l\":\"Temperature\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"osmolarity\",\"l\":\"Osmolarity\",\"suf\":\"mOsm/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pressure\",\"l\":\"Osmotic pressure\",\"suf\":\"kPa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"atm\",\"l\":\"Osmotic pressure\",\"suf\":\"atm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"150\",\"c\":null}]},\"i\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"temp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"37\",\"c\":null}]},\"osmolarity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pressure\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"atm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Osmotic pressure\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"osmolarity\",\"f\":\"conc*i\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"i\"]},{\"l\":\"pressure\",\"f\":\"conc*i*8.314*(temp+273.15)/1000\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"i\",\"temp\"]},{\"l\":\"atm\",\"f\":\"conc*i*0.082057*(temp+273.15)/1000\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"i\",\"temp\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'drop', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(40, 'hemocytometer-cell-counter', 'biology', 'Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL)', 'Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL)', 'Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL) Calculator', 'A hemocytometer turns a counted number of cells into a concentration using one physical fact: each large square holds 0.1 µL. Free hemocytometer cell counter', 'A hemocytometer turns a counted number of cells into a concentration using one physical fact: each large square holds 0.1 µL. It returns cell concentration, cell concentration, total cells in the suspension and concentration, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator averages your counts, applies the dilution factor — including the trypan blue step — and returns cells/mL, cells/µL and total cells. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL)\",\"html\":\"<p>Cells/mL = (cells counted ÷ squares counted) × dilution factor × 10⁴</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL) — Counts per square, times dilution, times the chamber constant.\\\" title=\\\"Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL)\\\"><figcaption>Counts per square, times dilution, times the chamber constant.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>cells_counted</code></td><td>cells counted across all squares</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>squares</code></td><td>number of large squares counted</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>dilution</code></td><td>total dilution before counting</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>10⁴</code></td><td>reciprocal of the 0.1 µL volume of one large square</td><td>mL⁻¹</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Improved Neubauer chamber geometry; the counting convention is the standard haematology one.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: total cells counted of <strong>320</strong> cells, squares counted of <strong>4</strong>, dilution factor of <strong>2</strong> ×, suspension volume of <strong>10</strong> mL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cell concentration of <strong>1,600,000</strong> cells/mL, cell concentration of <strong>1,600</strong> cells/µL, total cells in the suspension of <strong>16,000,000</strong> cells, concentration of <strong>1.6</strong> ×10⁶/mL.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>20 – 200 per square</td><td><strong>Countable</strong></td><td>high enough for precision, low enough to resolve</td></tr><tr><td>below 20</td><td><strong>Too few</strong></td><td>sampling error dominates; count more squares</td></tr><tr><td>above 200</td><td><strong>Too many</strong></td><td>clumps and overlapping cells; dilute</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The 10⁴ is geometry, not a correction: 1 mm × 1 mm × 0.1 mm = 0.1 mm³ = 10⁻⁴ mL.</p></li><li><p>Count the cells touching the top and left lines and skip the bottom and right ones, so border cells are counted exactly once.</p></li><li><p>Aim for 100–200 cells per large square. Below about 20, the Poisson counting error alone is 20% or worse.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Forgetting the trypan blue dilution — the single most common cause of a concentration that is exactly half the truth.</li><li>Counting clumps as single cells without disaggregating and re-counting.</li><li>Loading the chamber without letting it fill by capillary action, which leaves an uneven distribution.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cell-counting-calculator\\\">Cell counting calculator</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/trypan-blue-cell-viability\\\">Trypan blue viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-density\\\">Cell density</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why do I multiply by 10 000?\",\"a\":\"Because each large square holds 0.0001 mL, and dividing by such a small volume is the same as multiplying by 10 000.\"},{\"q\":\"How many squares should I count?\",\"a\":\"Four large corner squares is standard; count more if the cells are sparse.\"},{\"q\":\"Does this work for the Fuchs–Rosenthal chamber?\",\"a\":\"Not directly — that chamber is 0.2 mm deep, so the factor is 5 000 rather than 10 000.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns cell concentration, cell concentration, total cells in the suspension and concentration. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"cells_counted\",\"label\":\"Total cells counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"320\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Live plus dead, over all squares counted\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"squares\",\"label\":\"Squares counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Usually the four corner squares\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"2 for a 1:1 mix with trypan blue\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Suspension volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Used for the total yield\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Cell concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_ul\",\"label\":\"Cell concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_cells\",\"label\":\"Total cells in the suspension\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"million\",\"label\":\"Concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×10⁶/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000\"},{\"l\":\"per_ul\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10\"},{\"l\":\"total_cells\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000*volume\"},{\"l\":\"million\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000/1000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter\",\"slug\":\"hemocytometer-cell-counter\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL)\",\"title\":\"Hemocytometer Cell Counter (cells/mL) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"A hemocytometer turns a counted number of cells into a concentration using one physical fact: each large square holds 0.1 µL. Free hemocytometer cell counter\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-hemocytometer-cell-counter\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells_counted\",\"l\":\"Total cells counted\",\"h\":\"Live plus dead, over all squares counted\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"squares\",\"l\":\"Squares counted\",\"h\":\"Usually the four corner squares\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor\",\"h\":\"2 for a 1:1 mix with trypan blue\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Suspension volume\",\"h\":\"Used for the total yield\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Cell concentration\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_ul\",\"l\":\"Cell concentration\",\"suf\":\"cells/µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_cells\",\"l\":\"Total cells in the suspension\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"million\",\"l\":\"Concentration\",\"suf\":\"×10⁶/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"cells_counted\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"320\",\"c\":null}]},\"squares\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_ul\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_cells\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"million\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"per_ul\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"total_cells\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000*volume\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"million\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000/1000000\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(41, 'trypan-blue-cell-viability', 'biology', 'Trypan Blue Cell Viability Calculator', 'Trypan Blue Cell Viability Calculator', 'Trypan Blue Cell Viability Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Trypan blue viability is live cells divided by live plus dead cells, as a percentage. Free trypan blue cell viability calculator: enter your values and get vi', 'Trypan blue viability is live cells divided by live plus dead cells, as a percentage. It returns viability, live cell concentration and live cells in 10 mL, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe same count, corrected for the squares counted and the dilution, gives the live cell concentration and the live cells in a fixed volume. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Trypan Blue Cell Viability\",\"html\":\"<p>Viability (%) = live ÷ (live + dead) × 100;  live cells/mL = (live ÷ squares) × dilution × 10⁴</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/trypan-blue-cell-viability.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Trypan Blue Cell Viability Calculator — Live and dead counts give the percentage and the density.\\\" title=\\\"Trypan Blue Cell Viability Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Live and dead counts give the percentage and the density.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>live</code></td><td>unstained, refractile cells</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>dead</code></td><td>blue-stained cells</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>dilution</code></td><td>total dilution including the dye</td><td>×</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Trypan Blue Cell Viability?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: live cells counted of <strong>280</strong> cells, dead cells counted of <strong>40</strong> cells, squares counted of <strong>4</strong>, dilution factor of <strong>2</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives viability of <strong>87.5</strong>%, live cell concentration of <strong>1,400,000</strong> cells/mL, live cells in 10 ml of <strong>14,000,000</strong> cells.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>above 95%</td><td><strong>Healthy</strong></td><td>typical of a log-phase culture</td></tr><tr><td>85 – 95%</td><td><strong>Acceptable</strong></td><td>fine for most assays</td></tr><tr><td>below 85%</td><td><strong>Poor</strong></td><td>check handling, medium and time since harvest</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Count within five minutes of adding the dye: longer and live cells start taking it up.</p></li><li><p>Viability by dye exclusion overestimates health — a stressed cell that still excludes dye counts as live. Pair it with growth or an ATP assay for anything critical.</p></li><li><p>Report live-cell density, not total, whenever you seed a plate: dead cells do not divide.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Forgetting the dye dilution in the concentration calculation.</li><li>Counting too few cells, which makes the percentage jump by several points per cell.</li><li>Leaving the haemocytometer to dry between samples, which concentrates the cells.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter\\\">Hemocytometer counter</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-viability\\\">Percent viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-seeding-density\\\">Cell seeding density</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why do my live cells look blue?\",\"a\":\"They have been in the dye too long, or the suspension is acidic. Recount within five minutes.\"},{\"q\":\"Is trypan blue toxic to cells?\",\"a\":\"It is excluded by intact membranes but is cytotoxic over time, so count before plating.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Trypan Blue Cell Viability actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns viability, live cell concentration and live cells in 10 mL. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"live\",\"label\":\"Live cells counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"280\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dead\",\"label\":\"Dead cells counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"squares\",\"label\":\"Squares counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Includes the trypan blue mix\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"viability\",\"label\":\"Viability\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"live_conc\",\"label\":\"Live cell concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_live\",\"label\":\"Live cells in 10 mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Viability\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"live/(live+dead)*100\"},{\"l\":\"live_conc\",\"f\":\"live/squares*dilution*10000\"},{\"l\":\"total_live\",\"f\":\"live/squares*dilution*10000*10\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/trypan-blue-cell-viability\",\"slug\":\"trypan-blue-cell-viability\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Trypan Blue Cell Viability Calculator\",\"title\":\"Trypan Blue Cell Viability Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Trypan blue viability is live cells divided by live plus dead cells, as a percentage. Free trypan blue cell viability calculator: enter your values and get vi\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-trypan-blue-cell-viability\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"live\",\"l\":\"Live cells counted\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dead\",\"l\":\"Dead cells counted\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"squares\",\"l\":\"Squares counted\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor\",\"h\":\"Includes the trypan blue mix\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"viability\",\"l\":\"Viability\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"live_conc\",\"l\":\"Live cell concentration\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_live\",\"l\":\"Live cells in 10 mL\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"live\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"280\",\"c\":null}]},\"dead\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"squares\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"viability\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"live_conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_live\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Viability\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"live/(live+dead)*100\",\"r\":[\"live\",\"dead\"]},{\"l\":\"live_conc\",\"f\":\"live/squares*dilution*10000\",\"r\":[\"live\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"total_live\",\"f\":\"live/squares*dilution*10000*10\",\"r\":[\"live\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(42, 'cell-counting-calculator', 'biology', 'Cell Counting Calculator', 'Cell Counting Calculator', 'Cell Counting Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Cell counting turns a haemocytometer count, a dilution and a suspension volume into cells per mL. Free cell counting calculator: enter your values and get con', 'Cell counting turns a haemocytometer count, a dilution and a suspension volume into cells per mL. It returns concentration, total cells available, volume to take and wells at 100 000 per well, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt also returns the total cells in the suspension and the volume to take for a target number of cells. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Cell Counting\",\"html\":\"<p>Cells/mL = (count ÷ squares) × dilution × 10⁴;  volume to take = cells needed ÷ cells/mL</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cell-counting-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Cell Counting Calculator — Count, dilution and volume give concentration and yield.\\\" title=\\\"Cell Counting Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Count, dilution and volume give concentration and yield.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>cells_counted</code></td><td>cells counted</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>squares</code></td><td>large squares counted</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>conc</code></td><td>cell density</td><td>cells/mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>take</code></td><td>volume of suspension to pipette</td><td>mL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Cell Counting?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: cells counted of <strong>340</strong> cells, squares counted of <strong>4</strong>, dilution factor of <strong>1</strong> ×, suspension volume of <strong>12</strong> mL, cells needed of <strong>2,000,000</strong> cells.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives concentration of <strong>850,000</strong> cells/mL, total cells available of <strong>10,200,000</strong> cells, volume to take of <strong>2.35</strong> mL, wells at 100 000 per well of <strong>102</strong> wells.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Count twice, from two separate loadings, and average: the difference between loadings is a better error estimate than any single count.</p></li><li><p>Take the volume with a pipette that is appropriate for it — 20 µL out of 12 mL carries a much larger relative error than 2 mL.</p></li><li><p>If the count varies by more than 15% between loadings, the suspension is not homogeneous: triturate and start again.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using the average of counts taken from the same loading as if they were independent.</li><li>Forgetting that the dilution factor applies to every downstream number.</li><li>Seeding by volume rather than by cell number, which drifts as the culture grows.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter\\\">Hemocytometer counter</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-seeding-density\\\">Cell seeding density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-doubling-time\\\">Cell doubling time</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How precise is a haemocytometer count?\",\"a\":\"About 10–15% between operators when 100–200 cells are counted per square.\"},{\"q\":\"Should I count before or after dilution?\",\"a\":\"Count after all dilutions and record the total factor; that is what the calculation needs.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Cell Counting actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns concentration, total cells available, volume to take and wells at 100 000 per well. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"cells_counted\",\"label\":\"Cells counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"340\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"squares\",\"label\":\"Squares counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Suspension volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Cells needed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2000000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"For the next flask or plate\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total cells available\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"take\",\"label\":\"Volume to take\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"vessels\",\"label\":\"Wells at 100 000 per well\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"wells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Counting\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000\"},{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000*volume\"},{\"l\":\"take\",\"f\":\"target/(cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000)\"},{\"l\":\"vessels\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000*volume/100000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cell-counting-calculator\",\"slug\":\"cell-counting-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Cell Counting Calculator\",\"title\":\"Cell Counting Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Cell counting turns a haemocytometer count, a dilution and a suspension volume into cells per mL. Free cell counting calculator: enter your values and get con\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cell-counting-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells_counted\",\"l\":\"Cells counted\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"squares\",\"l\":\"Squares counted\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Suspension volume\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Cells needed\",\"h\":\"For the next flask or plate\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Concentration\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total cells available\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"take\",\"l\":\"Volume to take\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vessels\",\"l\":\"Wells at 100 000 per well\",\"suf\":\"wells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"cells_counted\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"340\",\"c\":null}]},\"squares\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2000000\",\"c\":null}]},\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"take\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"vessels\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Counting\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000*volume\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"take\",\"f\":\"target/(cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000)\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"vessels\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000*volume/100000\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\",\"volume\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(43, 'cell-density', 'biology', 'Cell Density Calculator', 'Cell Density Calculator', 'Cell Density Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Cell density is cells per millilitre of culture, and it decides when to split, how to seed and whether a culture is in log phase. Free cell density calculator', 'Cell density is cells per millilitre of culture, and it decides when to split, how to seed and whether a culture is in log phase. It returns cell density, density per area and cells/mL from OD600, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts counts, haemocytometer readings and OD600 values into cells/mL and cells/cm². Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Cell Density\",\"html\":\"<p>Cells/mL = (count ÷ squares) × dilution × 10⁴;  for E. coli, 1 OD600 ≈ 8 × 10⁸ cells/mL</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cell-density.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Cell Density Calculator — Counts, dilution and growth area give density per mL and per cm².\\\" title=\\\"Cell Density Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Counts, dilution and growth area give density per mL and per cm².</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>cells_counted</code></td><td>cells counted</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>dilution</code></td><td>dilution before counting</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>OD600</code></td><td>optical density at 600 nm</td><td>AU</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Cell Density?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: cells counted of <strong>300</strong> cells, squares counted of <strong>4</strong>, dilution factor of <strong>1</strong> ×, growth area of <strong>25</strong> cm², od600 of <strong>0.6</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cell density of <strong>750,000</strong> cells/mL, density per area of <strong>30,000</strong> cells/cm², cells/ml from od600 of <strong>480,000,000</strong> cells/mL.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.6 – 1.0 ×10⁶/mL</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>sparse; split less or seed more</td></tr><tr><td>1 – 5 ×10⁶/mL</td><td><strong>Log phase</strong></td><td>healthy adherent culture ready to passage</td></tr><tr><td>above 10⁷/mL</td><td><strong>Confluent / stationary</strong></td><td>split now</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The OD600 conversion is species-specific: 8 × 10⁸ cells/mL per OD unit is a working figure for E. coli, and yeast is roughly 3 × 10⁷.</p></li><li><p>Adherent cultures are usually quoted per cm², suspension cultures per mL; the growth area box converts between them.</p></li><li><p>Confluence is a visual estimate; density is the measurement. They disagree most in heterogeneous cultures.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using OD600 for eukaryotic cells, where it means almost nothing.</li><li>Reading OD600 above 1.0 without diluting, where the relationship stops being linear.</li><li>Quoting cells/mL for an adherent culture without the area.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter\\\">Hemocytometer counter</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/od600-to-cell-density\\\">OD600 to cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-seeding-density\\\">Cell seeding density</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal cell density for a T75 flask?\",\"a\":\"About 1–2 × 10⁶ cells/mL at 80% confluence, or roughly 8 × 10⁴ cells/cm².\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my OD600 reading not linear?\",\"a\":\"Above about 1.0 the detector saturates; dilute tenfold and multiply.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Cell Density actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns cell density, density per area and cells/mL from OD600. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"cells_counted\",\"label\":\"Cells counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"300\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"squares\",\"label\":\"Squares counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Growth area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"T25 = 25, T75 = 75, 6-well ≈ 9.6 per well\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"density\",\"label\":\"Cell density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_cm2\",\"label\":\"Density per area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"od\",\"label\":\"OD600\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"For bacterial cultures only\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"od_cells\",\"label\":\"Cells/mL from OD600\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Density\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000\"},{\"l\":\"per_cm2\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000/area\"},{\"l\":\"od_cells\",\"f\":\"od*800000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cell-density\",\"slug\":\"cell-density\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Cell Density Calculator\",\"title\":\"Cell Density Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Cell density is cells per millilitre of culture, and it decides when to split, how to seed and whether a culture is in log phase. Free cell density calculator\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cell-density\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells_counted\",\"l\":\"Cells counted\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"squares\",\"l\":\"Squares counted\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Growth area\",\"h\":\"T25 = 25, T75 = 75, 6-well ≈ 9.6 per well\",\"suf\":\"cm²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"density\",\"l\":\"Cell density\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_cm2\",\"l\":\"Density per area\",\"suf\":\"cells/cm²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od\",\"l\":\"OD600\",\"h\":\"For bacterial cultures only\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od_cells\",\"l\":\"Cells/mL from OD600\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"cells_counted\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"300\",\"c\":null}]},\"squares\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"density\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_cm2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"od\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.6\",\"c\":null}]},\"od_cells\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Density\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"per_cm2\",\"f\":\"cells_counted/squares*dilution*10000/area\",\"r\":[\"cells_counted\",\"squares\",\"dilution\",\"area\"]},{\"l\":\"od_cells\",\"f\":\"od*800000000\",\"r\":[\"od\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(44, 'od600-to-cell-density', 'biology', 'OD600 to Cell Density Calculator', 'OD600 to Cell Density Calculator', 'OD600 to Cell Density Calculator — Formula & Results', 'OD600 is the fastest way to follow a bacterial culture, but it is an optical reading, not a cell count. Free od600 to cell density calculator: enter your valu', 'OD600 is the fastest way to follow a bacterial culture, but it is an optical reading, not a cell count. It returns corrected OD600, cell density and dry cell weight, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts absorbance to cells/mL and to dry cell weight using the standard factors, with a dilution correction for readings above 1. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the OD600 to Cell Density\",\"html\":\"<p>Cells/mL = OD600 × dilution × (cells/mL per OD unit)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/od600-to-cell-density.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: OD600 to Cell Density Calculator — Absorbance times dilution times the calibration factor.\\\" title=\\\"OD600 to Cell Density Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Absorbance times dilution times the calibration factor.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>OD600</code></td><td>absorbance at 600 nm against a medium blank</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>dilution</code></td><td>how much the sample was diluted before reading</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>factor</code></td><td>strain-specific conversion</td><td>cells/mL per OD</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the OD600 to Cell Density?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: od600 reading of <strong>0.45</strong>, dilution before reading of <strong>1</strong> ×, cells/ml per od unit of <strong>800,000,000</strong> cells/mL, dry cell weight per od unit of <strong>0.4</strong> g/L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives corrected od600 of <strong>0.45</strong>, cell density of <strong>360,000,000</strong> cells/mL, dry cell weight of <strong>0.18</strong> g/L.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.1 – 1.0</td><td><strong>Linear</strong></td><td>the reliable range of the spectrophotometer</td></tr><tr><td>1.0 – 1.5</td><td><strong>Edge</strong></td><td>dilute to be safe</td></tr><tr><td>above 1.5</td><td><strong>Non-linear</strong></td><td>always dilute and correct</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The factor depends on the strain, the medium and the instrument (path length). Calibrate it once against a haemocytometer count or a plate count for your own system.</p></li><li><p>The relationship is only linear up to about 1.0; above that, dilution is mandatory rather than optional.</p></li><li><p>OD600 counts dead cells as well as live ones, so it is a biomass measure, not a viability one.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Reading against water rather than against fresh medium.</li><li>Forgetting to multiply by the dilution factor after diluting.</li><li>Using a cuvette with a different path length without correcting.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\\\">Bacterial growth curve</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-density\\\">Cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/specific-growth-rate-from-od\\\">Specific growth rate from OD readings</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many cells is OD600 = 1?\",\"a\":\"About 8 × 10⁸ E. coli per mL, though this varies two-fold between strains and media.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use OD600 to measure growth rate?\",\"a\":\"Yes — in the linear range, ln(OD) against time gives the growth rate, which is what the growth curve calculator does.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the OD600 to Cell Density actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns corrected OD600, cell density and dry cell weight. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"od\",\"label\":\"OD600 reading\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.45\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution before reading\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10 if you diluted 1:10\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"factor\",\"label\":\"Cells/mL per OD unit\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"800000000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"8×10⁸ for E. coli, 3×10⁷ for yeast\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dcw_factor\",\"label\":\"Dry cell weight per OD unit\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"About 0.4–0.5 g/L per OD for E. coli\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cells\",\"label\":\"Cell density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"dcw\",\"label\":\"Dry cell weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"od_corrected\",\"label\":\"Corrected OD600\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"od_corrected\",\"f\":\"od*dilution\"},{\"l\":\"cells\",\"f\":\"od*dilution*factor\"},{\"l\":\"dcw\",\"f\":\"od*dilution*dcw_factor\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/od600-to-cell-density\",\"slug\":\"od600-to-cell-density\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"OD600 to Cell Density Calculator\",\"title\":\"OD600 to Cell Density Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"OD600 is the fastest way to follow a bacterial culture, but it is an optical reading, not a cell count. Free od600 to cell density calculator: enter your valu\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-od600-to-cell-density\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od\",\"l\":\"OD600 reading\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution before reading\",\"h\":\"10 if you diluted 1:10\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"factor\",\"l\":\"Cells/mL per OD unit\",\"h\":\"8×10⁸ for E. coli, 3×10⁷ for yeast\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dcw_factor\",\"l\":\"Dry cell weight per OD unit\",\"h\":\"About 0.4–0.5 g/L per OD for E. coli\",\"suf\":\"g/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells\",\"l\":\"Cell density\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dcw\",\"l\":\"Dry cell weight\",\"suf\":\"g/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od_corrected\",\"l\":\"Corrected OD600\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"od\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.45\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"factor\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"800000000\",\"c\":null}]},\"dcw_factor\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"cells\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"dcw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"od_corrected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"od_corrected\",\"f\":\"od*dilution\",\"r\":[\"od\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"cells\",\"f\":\"od*dilution*factor\",\"r\":[\"od\",\"dilution\",\"factor\"]},{\"l\":\"dcw\",\"f\":\"od*dilution*dcw_factor\",\"r\":[\"od\",\"dilution\",\"dcw_factor\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'spectrophotometer', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(45, 'cell-seeding-density', 'biology', 'Cell Seeding Density Calculator', 'Cell Seeding Density Calculator', 'Cell Seeding Density Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Seeding density is the number of cells per unit area or volume you start a culture with. Free cell seeding density calculator: enter your values and get cells', 'Seeding density is the number of cells per unit area or volume you start a culture with. It returns cells per vessel, suspension to pipette per vessel, cells needed in total and medium to add per vessel, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nGet it wrong and the experiment either takes a week to reach confluence or is confluent the next morning. This calculator turns a target density into the volume of suspension to pipette and the medium to add. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Cell Seeding Density\",\"html\":\"<p>Cells per vessel = target density × growth area;  volume = cells per vessel ÷ stock concentration</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cell-seeding-density.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Cell Seeding Density Calculator — Target density × area gives cells; divide by stock concentration for the volume.\\\" title=\\\"Cell Seeding Density Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Target density × area gives cells; divide by stock concentration for the volume.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>target</code></td><td>cells per cm² you want to start with</td><td>cells/cm²</td></tr><tr><td><code>area</code></td><td>growth area of one flask or well</td><td>cm²</td></tr><tr><td><code>conc</code></td><td>stock concentration</td><td>cells/mL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Cell Seeding Density?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: stock concentration of <strong>1,500,000</strong> cells/mL, target density of <strong>20,000</strong> cells/cm², growth area per vessel of <strong>25</strong> cm², number of vessels of <strong>1</strong>, final volume per vessel of <strong>5</strong> mL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cells per vessel of <strong>500,000</strong> cells, suspension to pipette per vessel of <strong>0.3333</strong> mL, cells needed in total of <strong>500,000</strong> cells, medium to add per vessel of <strong>4.67</strong> mL.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5 000 – 20 000 /cm²</td><td><strong>Sparse</strong></td><td>cloning, single-cell work</td></tr><tr><td>20 000 – 50 000 /cm²</td><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>routine passage of most lines</td></tr><tr><td>above 50 000 /cm²</td><td><strong>Dense</strong></td><td>fast experiments, or a line that needs contact</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Seeding density sets the time to confluence: doubling the density roughly halves it, which is often easier than changing the medium.</p></li><li><p>Growth area is the number that matters for adherent cells; the flask volume is just what fits over it.</p></li><li><p>For a first passage of a new line, seed two densities and keep the one that looks right on day two.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the flask\'s nominal volume instead of its growth area.</li><li>Forgetting that the medium added dilutes the density — you seed cells into the final volume.</li><li>Seeding dead cells: always work from the live-cell count, not the total.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cell-counting-calculator\\\">Cell counting calculator</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-doubling-time\\\">Cell doubling time</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-culture-vessel-reference\\\">Culture vessel reference</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many cells should I seed in a 6-well plate?\",\"a\":\"A well is about 9.6 cm², so 20 000 cells/cm² is roughly 190 000 cells per well.\"},{\"q\":\"Does seeding density affect the experiment?\",\"a\":\"It changes growth rate, confluency timing and sometimes differentiation, so keep it constant across arms.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Cell Seeding Density actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns cells per vessel, suspension to pipette per vessel, cells needed in total and medium to add per vessel. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Stock concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1500000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From your count\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Target density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Growth area per vessel\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"T25 = 25, T75 = 75, 6-well = 9.6\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"vessels\",\"label\":\"Number of vessels\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"medium\",\"label\":\"Final volume per vessel\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"per_vessel\",\"label\":\"Cells per vessel\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Suspension to pipette per vessel\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_cells\",\"label\":\"Cells needed in total\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"medium_add\",\"label\":\"Medium to add per vessel\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Seeding\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"per_vessel\",\"f\":\"target*area\"},{\"l\":\"volume\",\"f\":\"target*area/conc\"},{\"l\":\"total_cells\",\"f\":\"target*area*vessels\"},{\"l\":\"medium_add\",\"f\":\"medium-target*area/conc\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cell-seeding-density\",\"slug\":\"cell-seeding-density\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Cell Seeding Density Calculator\",\"title\":\"Cell Seeding Density Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Seeding density is the number of cells per unit area or volume you start a culture with. Free cell seeding density calculator: enter your values and get cells\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cell-seeding-density\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Stock concentration\",\"h\":\"From your count\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Target density\",\"suf\":\"cells/cm²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Growth area per vessel\",\"h\":\"T25 = 25, T75 = 75, 6-well = 9.6\",\"suf\":\"cm²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vessels\",\"l\":\"Number of vessels\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"medium\",\"l\":\"Final volume per vessel\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_vessel\",\"l\":\"Cells per vessel\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Suspension to pipette per vessel\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_cells\",\"l\":\"Cells needed in total\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"medium_add\",\"l\":\"Medium to add per vessel\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1500000\",\"c\":null}]},\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20000\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"vessels\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"medium\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5\",\"c\":null}]},\"per_vessel\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_cells\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"medium_add\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Seeding\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"per_vessel\",\"f\":\"target*area\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"area\"]},{\"l\":\"volume\",\"f\":\"target*area/conc\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"area\",\"conc\"]},{\"l\":\"total_cells\",\"f\":\"target*area*vessels\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"area\",\"vessels\"]},{\"l\":\"medium_add\",\"f\":\"medium-target*area/conc\",\"r\":[\"medium\",\"target\",\"area\",\"conc\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(46, 'cell-culture-vessel-reference', 'biology', 'Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference', 'Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference', 'Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference Calculator', 'Every plasticware format has a growth area, a working volume and a recommended cell number, and mixing them up is a classic source of inconsistent experiments', 'Every plasticware format has a growth area, a working volume and a recommended cell number, and mixing them up is a classic source of inconsistent experiments. It returns growth area, medium per vessel, cells per vessel at that density and total growth area, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis reference converts between formats and tells you how much medium and how many cells a vessel takes. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference\",\"html\":\"<p>Medium (mL) ≈ 0.2 × growth area (cm²);  cells = growth area × target density</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cell-culture-vessel-reference.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference — Growth area, medium and cells for the format you picked.\\\" title=\\\"Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference\\\"><figcaption>Growth area, medium and cells for the format you picked.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>vessel</code></td><td>growth area of one vessel</td><td>cm²</td></tr><tr><td><code>medium</code></td><td>working volume</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>cells</code></td><td>cells to seed</td><td>cells</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: format set to <strong>75.0</strong>, number of vessels of <strong>1</strong>, medium per cm² of <strong>0.2</strong> mL/cm², target seeding density of <strong>20,000</strong> cells/cm².</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives growth area of <strong>75</strong> cm², medium per vessel of <strong>15</strong> mL, cells per vessel at that density of <strong>1,500,000</strong> cells, total growth area of <strong>75</strong> cm².</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The 0.2 mL/cm² rule keeps the depth of medium at about 2 mm, which is what most protocols assume for gas exchange and autocrine signalling.</p></li><li><p>Multi-well plates are quoted per well: a 6-well plate is 9.6 cm² per well, about 58 cm² in total.</p></li><li><p>Transwell and low-attachment formats have their own areas and should not be substituted.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using the plate\'s total area when the protocol is per well.</li><li>Filling a T175 with the T25 volume (or the reverse) and calling the difference a medium effect.</li><li>Forgetting that medium volume changes the drug concentration in a dose–response assay.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cell-seeding-density\\\">Cell seeding density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-density\\\">Cell density</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How much medium does a T75 flask need?\",\"a\":\"About 15 mL at the usual 0.2 mL/cm², with a range of 10–20 mL depending on the protocol.\"},{\"q\":\"How many cells in a confluent T75?\",\"a\":\"Roughly 7–8 million for most adherent lines, which is about 10⁵ cells/cm².\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns growth area, medium per vessel, cells per vessel at that density and total growth area. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"vessel\",\"label\":\"Format\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"t75-flask\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"t25-flask\",\"T25 flask\",\"25\"],[\"t75-flask\",\"T75 flask\",\"75\"],[\"t175-flask\",\"T175 flask\",\"175\"],[\"35-mm-dish\",\"35 mm dish\",\"8\"],[\"60-mm-dish\",\"60 mm dish\",\"21\"],[\"100-mm-dish\",\"100 mm dish\",\"55\"],[\"6-well-plate-per-well\",\"6-well plate (per well)\",\"9.6\"],[\"12-well-plate-per-well\",\"12-well plate (per well)\",\"3.8\"],[\"24-well-plate-per-well\",\"24-well plate (per well)\",\"1.9\"],[\"96-well-plate-per-well\",\"96-well plate (per well)\",\"0.32\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"factor\",\"label\":\"Number of vessels\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"medium_per_cm2\",\"label\":\"Medium per cm²\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.15–0.25 mL/cm² is the usual range\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL/cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Target seeding density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Growth area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"medium\",\"label\":\"Medium per vessel\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cells\",\"label\":\"Cells per vessel at that density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_area\",\"label\":\"Total growth area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Vessel\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"vessel\"},{\"l\":\"medium\",\"f\":\"vessel*medium_per_cm2\"},{\"l\":\"cells\",\"f\":\"vessel*target\"},{\"l\":\"total_area\",\"f\":\"vessel*factor\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cell-culture-vessel-reference\",\"slug\":\"cell-culture-vessel-reference\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference\",\"title\":\"Cell Culture Dish / Plate / Flask Reference Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Every plasticware format has a growth area, a working volume and a recommended cell number, and mixing them up is a classic source of inconsistent experiments\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cell-culture-vessel-reference\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vessel\",\"l\":\"Format\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-vessel\",\"opts\":[[\"t25-flask\",\"T25 flask\",\"25\"],[\"t75-flask\",\"T75 flask\",\"75\"],[\"t175-flask\",\"T175 flask\",\"175\"],[\"35-mm-dish\",\"35 mm dish\",\"8\"],[\"60-mm-dish\",\"60 mm dish\",\"21\"],[\"100-mm-dish\",\"100 mm dish\",\"55\"],[\"6-well-plate-per-well\",\"6-well plate (per well)\",\"9.6\"],[\"12-well-plate-per-well\",\"12-well plate (per well)\",\"3.8\"],[\"24-well-plate-per-well\",\"24-well plate (per well)\",\"1.9\"],[\"96-well-plate-per-well\",\"96-well plate (per well)\",\"0.32\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"factor\",\"l\":\"Number of vessels\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"medium_per_cm2\",\"l\":\"Medium per cm²\",\"h\":\"0.15–0.25 mL/cm² is the usual range\",\"suf\":\"mL/cm²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Target seeding density\",\"suf\":\"cells/cm²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Growth area\",\"suf\":\"cm²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"medium\",\"l\":\"Medium per vessel\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells\",\"l\":\"Cells per vessel at that density\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_area\",\"l\":\"Total growth area\",\"suf\":\"cm²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"vessel\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"t75-flask\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-vessel\"},\"factor\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"medium_per_cm2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20000\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"medium\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cells\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-vessel\":{\"name\":\"Format\",\"uiType\":\"SELECT\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"t25-flask\",\"label\":\"T25 flask\",\"value\":\"25\"},{\"uid\":\"t75-flask\",\"label\":\"T75 flask\",\"value\":\"75\"},{\"uid\":\"t175-flask\",\"label\":\"T175 flask\",\"value\":\"175\"},{\"uid\":\"35-mm-dish\",\"label\":\"35 mm dish\",\"value\":\"8\"},{\"uid\":\"60-mm-dish\",\"label\":\"60 mm dish\",\"value\":\"21\"},{\"uid\":\"100-mm-dish\",\"label\":\"100 mm dish\",\"value\":\"55\"},{\"uid\":\"6-well-plate-per-well\",\"label\":\"6-well plate (per well)\",\"value\":\"9.6\"},{\"uid\":\"12-well-plate-per-well\",\"label\":\"12-well plate (per well)\",\"value\":\"3.8\"},{\"uid\":\"24-well-plate-per-well\",\"label\":\"24-well plate (per well)\",\"value\":\"1.9\"},{\"uid\":\"96-well-plate-per-well\",\"label\":\"96-well plate (per well)\",\"value\":\"0.32\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Vessel\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"vessel\",\"r\":[\"vessel\"]},{\"l\":\"medium\",\"f\":\"vessel*medium_per_cm2\",\"r\":[\"vessel\",\"medium_per_cm2\"]},{\"l\":\"cells\",\"f\":\"vessel*target\",\"r\":[\"vessel\",\"target\"]},{\"l\":\"total_area\",\"f\":\"vessel*factor\",\"r\":[\"vessel\",\"factor\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'plate', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(47, 'cell-density-unit-converter', 'biology', 'Cell Density Unit Converter', 'Cell Density Unit Converter', 'Cell Density Unit Converter Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Cell counts get written as cells/mL, cells/µL, ×10⁶/mL or ×10⁵/mL depending on the lab, and a factor of a thousand between them is easy to lose. Free cell den', 'Cell counts get written as cells/mL, cells/µL, ×10⁶/mL or ×10⁵/mL depending on the lab, and a factor of a thousand between them is easy to lose. It returns cells per mL, cells per µL, millions per mL and cells per litre, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis converter moves between all of them, and to and from a haemocytometer count. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Cell Density Unit Converter\",\"html\":\"<p>1 ×10⁶ cells/mL = 1 000 000 cells/mL = 1 000 cells/µL</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cell-density-unit-converter.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Cell Density Unit Converter — One concentration, every unit.\\\" title=\\\"Cell Density Unit Converter\\\"><figcaption>One concentration, every unit.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>cells/mL</code></td><td>the base unit</td><td>cells/mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>cells/µL</code></td><td>a thousandth of cells/mL</td><td>cells/µL</td></tr><tr><td><code>×10⁶/mL</code></td><td>millions per mL</td><td>10⁶ cells/mL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Cell Density Unit Converter?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: cell concentration of <strong>1.5</strong> ×10⁶/mL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cells per ml of <strong>1,500,000</strong> cells/mL, cells per µl of <strong>1,500</strong> cells/µL, millions per ml of <strong>1.5</strong> ×10⁶/mL, cells per litre of <strong>1,500,000,000</strong> cells/L, log₁₀(cells/ml) of <strong>6.18</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>A log₁₀ value is how cell densities are plotted in growth curves, because it turns exponential growth into a straight line.</p></li><li><p>The ×10⁶/mL convention is common in haematology and in cell culture; the underlying unit is still cells per millilitre.</p></li><li><p>Mixing cells/µL and cells/mL between a count and a protocol is the most common unit error in the lab.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Reading 1.5 as 1.5 × 10⁵ rather than 1.5 × 10⁶.</li><li>Converting a bacterial count (10⁸ scale) with the same factor as a mammalian one (10⁶ scale).</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cell-density\\\">Cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter\\\">Hemocytometer counter</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many cells/µL is 1 × 10⁶ cells/mL?\",\"a\":\"Exactly 1 000 cells/µL.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Cell Density Unit Converter actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns cells per mL, cells per µL, millions per mL and cells per litre. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"},{\"q\":\"Does it work in both directions?\",\"a\":\"Where the maths allows it, yes: the page states which values it solves for and which you supply. A field marked as a result is always computed, never typed into.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"value\",\"label\":\"Cell concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×10⁶/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"per_ml\",\"label\":\"Cells per mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_ul\",\"label\":\"Cells per µL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"million\",\"label\":\"Millions per mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×10⁶/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_l\",\"label\":\"Cells per litre\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"log_value\",\"label\":\"log₁₀(cells/mL)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"per_ml\",\"f\":\"value*1000000\"},{\"l\":\"per_ul\",\"f\":\"value*1000\"},{\"l\":\"million\",\"f\":\"value\"},{\"l\":\"per_l\",\"f\":\"value*1000000000\"},{\"l\":\"log_value\",\"f\":\"log10(value*1000000)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cell-density-unit-converter\",\"slug\":\"cell-density-unit-converter\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Cell Density Unit Converter\",\"title\":\"Cell Density Unit Converter Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Cell counts get written as cells/mL, cells/µL, ×10⁶/mL or ×10⁵/mL depending on the lab, and a factor of a thousand between them is easy to lose. Free cell den\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cell-density-unit-converter\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"value\",\"l\":\"Cell concentration\",\"suf\":\"×10⁶/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_ml\",\"l\":\"Cells per mL\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_ul\",\"l\":\"Cells per µL\",\"suf\":\"cells/µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"million\",\"l\":\"Millions per mL\",\"suf\":\"×10⁶/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_l\",\"l\":\"Cells per litre\",\"suf\":\"cells/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"log_value\",\"l\":\"log₁₀(cells/mL)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"per_ml\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_ul\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"million\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_l\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"log_value\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"per_ml\",\"f\":\"value*1000000\",\"r\":[\"value\"]},{\"l\":\"per_ul\",\"f\":\"value*1000\",\"r\":[\"value\"]},{\"l\":\"million\",\"f\":\"value\",\"r\":[\"value\"]},{\"l\":\"per_l\",\"f\":\"value*1000000000\",\"r\":[\"value\"]},{\"l\":\"log_value\",\"f\":\"log10(value*1000000)\",\"r\":[\"value\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(48, 'alamarblue-cell-viability', 'biology', 'AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction Calculator', 'AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction Calculator', 'AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction Calculator', 'AlamarBlue (resazurin) is reduced by living cells to resorufin, which is fluorescent and pink. Free alamarblue cell viability / percent reduction calculator:', 'AlamarBlue (resazurin) is reduced by living cells to resorufin, which is fluorescent and pink. It returns percent reduction, viability relative to control and cytotoxicity, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nViability is the reduction relative to a control, corrected for the background of medium alone. This calculator does that three-point correction. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction\",\"html\":\"<p>Viability (%) = (sample − blank) ÷ (control − blank) × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/alamarblue-cell-viability.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction Calculator — Sample, control and blank, and the reduction between them.\\\" title=\\\"AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Sample, control and blank, and the reduction between them.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sample</code></td><td>fluorescence of treated wells</td><td>RFU</td></tr><tr><td><code>control</code></td><td>fluorescence of untreated wells</td><td>RFU</td></tr><tr><td><code>blank</code></td><td>medium plus reagent, no cells</td><td>RFU</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> AlamarBlue/resazurin reduction chemistry; 70% viability threshold from ISO 10993-5.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: sample fluorescence of <strong>32,000</strong> RFU, control fluorescence of <strong>48,000</strong> RFU, medium-only blank of <strong>2,500</strong> RFU.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives percent reduction of <strong>64.84</strong>%, viability relative to control of <strong>64.84</strong>%, cytotoxicity of <strong>35.16</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>above 90%</td><td><strong>Non-cytotoxic</strong></td><td>within normal variation</td></tr><tr><td>70 – 90%</td><td><strong>Mild</strong></td><td>watch for dose-dependent effects</td></tr><tr><td>below 70%</td><td><strong>Cytotoxic</strong></td><td>the usual threshold in ISO 10993 testing</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>AlamarBlue measures metabolic activity, not cell number. A cytostatic compound lowers the signal without killing anything.</p></li><li><p>The blank matters: resazurin is partly reduced by medium components, and an uncorrected blank inflates weak signals.</p></li><li><p>Incubation time should be identical for all wells; reduction is enzymatic and keeps going.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Reading in the wrong channel — resorufin fluoresces at 590 nm with excitation around 560 nm.</li><li>Comparing plates incubated for different times.</li><li>Leaving the reagent in the wells during imaging, where it bleaches.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/percent-viability\\\">Percent viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/trypan-blue-cell-viability\\\">Trypan blue viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-inhibition\\\">Percent inhibition</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is percent reduction?\",\"a\":\"The share of the dye that the cells have reduced, corrected for the medium-only blank — the same number as viability when the control is fully viable.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use absorbance instead of fluorescence?\",\"a\":\"Yes, at 570 nm against a 600 nm reference; the calculation is identical.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns percent reduction, viability relative to control and cytotoxicity. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sample\",\"label\":\"Sample fluorescence\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"32000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Treated wells\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"RFU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"control\",\"label\":\"Control fluorescence\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"48000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Untreated wells\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"RFU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"blank\",\"label\":\"Medium-only blank\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"No cells\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"RFU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"reduction\",\"label\":\"Percent reduction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"viability\",\"label\":\"Viability relative to control\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ic\",\"label\":\"Cytotoxicity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Viability\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"reduction\",\"f\":\"(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\"},{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\"},{\"l\":\"ic\",\"f\":\"100-(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/alamarblue-cell-viability\",\"slug\":\"alamarblue-cell-viability\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction Calculator\",\"title\":\"AlamarBlue Cell Viability / Percent Reduction Calculator\",\"desc\":\"AlamarBlue (resazurin) is reduced by living cells to resorufin, which is fluorescent and pink. Free alamarblue cell viability / percent reduction calculator:\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-alamarblue-cell-viability\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sample\",\"l\":\"Sample fluorescence\",\"h\":\"Treated wells\",\"suf\":\"RFU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"control\",\"l\":\"Control fluorescence\",\"h\":\"Untreated wells\",\"suf\":\"RFU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"blank\",\"l\":\"Medium-only blank\",\"h\":\"No cells\",\"suf\":\"RFU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"reduction\",\"l\":\"Percent reduction\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"viability\",\"l\":\"Viability relative to control\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ic\",\"l\":\"Cytotoxicity\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sample\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"32000\",\"c\":null}]},\"control\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"48000\",\"c\":null}]},\"blank\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2500\",\"c\":null}]},\"reduction\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"viability\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ic\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Viability\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"reduction\",\"f\":\"(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\",\"r\":[\"sample\",\"blank\",\"control\"]},{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\",\"r\":[\"sample\",\"blank\",\"control\"]},{\"l\":\"ic\",\"f\":\"100-(sample-blank)/(control-blank)*100\",\"r\":[\"sample\",\"blank\",\"control\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'plate', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(49, 'percent-viability', 'biology', 'Percent Viability Calculator', 'Percent Viability Calculator', 'Percent Viability Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Percent viability is live cells as a share of all cells, and there are three ways to measure it: dye exclusion, metabolic reduction and membrane integrity. Fr', 'Percent viability is live cells as a share of all cells, and there are three ways to measure it: dye exclusion, metabolic reduction and membrane integrity. It returns viability, non-viable and live to dead ratio, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator handles the live/dead form and the control-relative form, and reports the cytotoxicity that goes with it. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Percent Viability\",\"html\":\"<p>Viability (%) = live ÷ (live + dead) × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/percent-viability.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Percent Viability Calculator — Live and dead cells as a share of the population.\\\" title=\\\"Percent Viability Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Live and dead cells as a share of the population.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>live</code></td><td>cells excluding the dye or reducing the substrate</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>dead</code></td><td>cells taking up the dye</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>viability</code></td><td>live share of the population</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Percent Viability?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: live cells of <strong>920</strong> cells, dead cells of <strong>80</strong> cells.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives viability of <strong>92</strong>%, non-viable of <strong>8</strong>%, live to dead ratio of <strong>11.5</strong> :1.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>95 – 100%</td><td><strong>Healthy</strong></td><td>normal for a fresh culture</td></tr><tr><td>80 – 95%</td><td><strong>Reduced</strong></td><td>harvest stress or a mild treatment</td></tr><tr><td>below 80%</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>check conditions before the experiment</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>A viability of 50% can mean 50% of the cells are dead, or that every cell is half as metabolically active — the two methods answer different questions.</p></li><li><p>Always quote the method: trypan blue, PI/FACS and ATP assays give different numbers on the same population.</p></li><li><p>For cytotoxicity reporting, the complement of viability assumes the untreated control was 100% viable, which is rarely exactly true.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Counting fewer than 100 cells, where a single cell is a whole percentage point.</li><li>Using total cells (live + dead) as the denominator in one place and live cells in another.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/trypan-blue-cell-viability\\\">Trypan blue viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/alamarblue-cell-viability\\\">AlamarBlue viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-inhibition\\\">Percent inhibition</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What viability is acceptable for an experiment?\",\"a\":\"90% or better at the start of most assays; treatments that drop it below 70% usually confound the readout with cytotoxicity.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Percent Viability actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns viability, non-viable and live to dead ratio. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"},{\"q\":\"Does it work in both directions?\",\"a\":\"Where the maths allows it, yes: the page states which values it solves for and which you supply. A field marked as a result is always computed, never typed into.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"live\",\"label\":\"Live cells\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"920\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dead\",\"label\":\"Dead cells\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"80\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"viability\",\"label\":\"Viability\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"dead_pct\",\"label\":\"Non-viable\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Live to dead ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Viability\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"live/(live+dead)*100\"},{\"l\":\"dead_pct\",\"f\":\"dead/(live+dead)*100\"},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"live/dead\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/percent-viability\",\"slug\":\"percent-viability\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Percent Viability Calculator\",\"title\":\"Percent Viability Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Percent viability is live cells as a share of all cells, and there are three ways to measure it: dye exclusion, metabolic reduction and membrane integrity. Fr\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-percent-viability\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"live\",\"l\":\"Live cells\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dead\",\"l\":\"Dead cells\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"viability\",\"l\":\"Viability\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dead_pct\",\"l\":\"Non-viable\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Live to dead ratio\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"live\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"920\",\"c\":null}]},\"dead\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"80\",\"c\":null}]},\"viability\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"dead_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Viability\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"live/(live+dead)*100\",\"r\":[\"live\",\"dead\"]},{\"l\":\"dead_pct\",\"f\":\"dead/(live+dead)*100\",\"r\":[\"dead\",\"live\"]},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"live/dead\",\"r\":[\"live\",\"dead\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(50, 'mitotic-index', 'biology', 'Mitotic Index Calculator', 'Mitotic Index Calculator', 'Mitotic Index Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The mitotic index is the percentage of cells caught in mitosis on a slide, the classic measure of proliferation in plant roots, tumour tissue and any treated', 'The mitotic index is the percentage of cells caught in mitosis on a slide, the classic measure of proliferation in plant roots, tumour tissue and any treated culture. It returns mitotic index, mitotic cells per 1000 and phases add up to, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator returns the index, the phase breakdown and the total counted. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Mitotic Index\",\"html\":\"<p>Mitotic index (%) = mitotic cells ÷ total cells × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/mitotic-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Mitotic Index Calculator — Mitotic phases as a share of the cells counted.\\\" title=\\\"Mitotic Index Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Mitotic phases as a share of the cells counted.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>mitotic</code></td><td>cells showing condensed chromosomes</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>total</code></td><td>all cells counted in the field</td><td>cells</td></tr><tr><td><code>index</code></td><td>share of cells in mitosis</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Mitotic Index?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: total cells counted of <strong>1,200</strong> cells, cells in mitosis of <strong>96</strong> cells, prophase of <strong>40</strong> cells, metaphase of <strong>28</strong> cells, anaphase of <strong>18</strong> cells, telophase of <strong>10</strong> cells.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives mitotic index of <strong>8</strong>%, mitotic cells per 1000 of <strong>80</strong>, phases add up to of <strong>96</strong> cells.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 – 2%</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>quiescent tissue or an inhibited treatment</td></tr><tr><td>2 – 6%</td><td><strong>Normal</strong></td><td>typical of healthy root meristems</td></tr><tr><td>above 6%</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>rapidly dividing tissue, or a block in mitosis</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The index depends on how long mitosis lasts as well as on how often it starts: a drug that arrests cells in metaphase raises the index without raising proliferation.</p></li><li><p>Count at least 1000 cells, ideally from several fields, and score blind to the treatment.</p></li><li><p>Colchicine or similar spindle poisons are used to accumulate metaphases; say whether you used one.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Counting only fields with obvious mitoses, which inflates the index.</li><li>Confusing apoptotic bodies with mitotic figures.</li><li>Counting cells that overlap, which makes the denominator unreliable.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cell-doubling-time\\\">Cell doubling time</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-viability\\\">Percent viability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/microscope-magnification\\\">Microscope magnification</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal mitotic index?\",\"a\":\"About 2–6% in plant root tips and lower in most adult animal tissues.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my mitotic index so high after treatment?\",\"a\":\"Spindle poisons and DNA damage can arrest cells in mitosis, so the index rises even as proliferation falls.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Mitotic Index actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns mitotic index, mitotic cells per 1000 and phases add up to. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total cells counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mitotic\",\"label\":\"Cells in mitosis\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"96\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Prophase to telophase\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"prophase\",\"label\":\"Prophase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional phase breakdown\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"metaphase\",\"label\":\"Metaphase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"28\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"anaphase\",\"label\":\"Anaphase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"18\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"telophase\",\"label\":\"Telophase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"index\",\"label\":\"Mitotic index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_1000\",\"label\":\"Mitotic cells per 1000\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"phase_check\",\"label\":\"Phases add up to\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Index\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"index\",\"f\":\"mitotic/total*100\"},{\"l\":\"per_1000\",\"f\":\"mitotic/total*1000\"},{\"l\":\"phase_check\",\"f\":\"prophase+metaphase+anaphase+telophase\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/mitotic-index\",\"slug\":\"mitotic-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Mitotic Index Calculator\",\"title\":\"Mitotic Index Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The mitotic index is the percentage of cells caught in mitosis on a slide, the classic measure of proliferation in plant roots, tumour tissue and any treated\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-mitotic-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total cells counted\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mitotic\",\"l\":\"Cells in mitosis\",\"h\":\"Prophase to telophase\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"prophase\",\"l\":\"Prophase\",\"h\":\"Optional phase breakdown\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"metaphase\",\"l\":\"Metaphase\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"anaphase\",\"l\":\"Anaphase\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"telophase\",\"l\":\"Telophase\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"index\",\"l\":\"Mitotic index\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_1000\",\"l\":\"Mitotic cells per 1000\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"phase_check\",\"l\":\"Phases add up to\",\"suf\":\"cells\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1200\",\"c\":null}]},\"mitotic\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"96\",\"c\":null}]},\"prophase\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"metaphase\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"28\",\"c\":null}]},\"anaphase\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"18\",\"c\":null}]},\"telophase\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"index\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_1000\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"phase_check\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Index\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"index\",\"f\":\"mitotic/total*100\",\"r\":[\"mitotic\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"per_1000\",\"f\":\"mitotic/total*1000\",\"r\":[\"mitotic\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"phase_check\",\"f\":\"prophase+metaphase+anaphase+telophase\",\"r\":[\"prophase\",\"metaphase\",\"anaphase\",\"telophase\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'microscope', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(51, 'fold-change', 'biology', 'Fold Change Calculator', 'Fold Change Calculator', 'Fold Change Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Fold change compares a treated value with a control: 2 means doubled, 0.5 means halved. Free fold change calculator: enter your values and get fold change, lo', 'Fold change compares a treated value with a control: 2 means doubled, 0.5 means halved. It returns fold change, log₂ fold change, percent change and reciprocal fold change, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator handles the direction properly, works with averages and replicates, and warns when the two conventions (treated/control versus control/treated) give different answers. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Fold Change\",\"html\":\"<p>Fold change = treated ÷ control;  log₂ fold change = log₂(treated/control)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/fold-change.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Fold Change Calculator — Control and treated, and the ratio between them.\\\" title=\\\"Fold Change Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Control and treated, and the ratio between them.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>treated</code></td><td>mean of the treated group</td><td>any</td></tr><tr><td><code>control</code></td><td>mean of the control group</td><td>any</td></tr><tr><td><code>log₂ FC</code></td><td>symmetric measure: 1 is up twofold, −1 is down twofold</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Fold Change?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: treated value of <strong>240</strong>, control value of <strong>100</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives fold change of <strong>2.4</strong> ×, log₂ fold change of <strong>1.26</strong>, percent change of <strong>140</strong>%, reciprocal fold change of <strong>0.4167</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 and above</td><td><strong>Upregulated</strong></td><td>at least doubled</td></tr><tr><td>0.5 – 2</td><td><strong>Unchanged</strong></td><td>within the usual biological noise</td></tr><tr><td>0.5 and below</td><td><strong>Downregulated</strong></td><td>at least halved</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Fold change on a linear scale is asymmetric: up twofold is 2, down twofold is 0.5. log₂ makes the two symmetric, which is why it is used in expression analysis.</p></li><li><p>A fold change without a measure of variance is nearly meaningless — a 3× change from n=2 can be noise.</p></li><li><p>With qPCR, fold change is usually computed as 2^−ΔΔCt, which is the same thing expressed through cycle thresholds.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Comparing a treated sample with a different baseline than the one the graph uses.</li><li>Averaging fold changes across replicates instead of computing them from the means, or the reverse.</li><li>Going from a negative control value close to zero, where small absolute differences become enormous ratios.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/percent-inhibition\\\">Percent inhibition</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cfu-ml-calculator\\\">CFU/mL</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Is a 2-fold change significant?\",\"a\":\"Not by itself. Significance needs the variance of the replicates; fold change is an effect size.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do some papers report log₂ fold change?\",\"a\":\"Because it is symmetric and averages sensibly across genes and conditions.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Fold Change actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns fold change, log₂ fold change, percent change and reciprocal fold change. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"treated\",\"label\":\"Treated value\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"240\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"control\",\"label\":\"Control value\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"fold\",\"label\":\"Fold change\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"log2fc\",\"label\":\"log₂ fold change\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pct\",\"label\":\"Percent change\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"inverse\",\"label\":\"Reciprocal fold change\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Fold change\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"treated/control\"},{\"l\":\"log2fc\",\"f\":\"log2(treated/control)\"},{\"l\":\"pct\",\"f\":\"(treated/control-1)*100\"},{\"l\":\"inverse\",\"f\":\"control/treated\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/fold-change\",\"slug\":\"fold-change\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Fold Change Calculator\",\"title\":\"Fold Change Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Fold change compares a treated value with a control: 2 means doubled, 0.5 means halved. Free fold change calculator: enter your values and get fold change, lo\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-fold-change\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"treated\",\"l\":\"Treated value\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"control\",\"l\":\"Control value\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fold\",\"l\":\"Fold change\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"log2fc\",\"l\":\"log₂ fold change\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pct\",\"l\":\"Percent change\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"inverse\",\"l\":\"Reciprocal fold change\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"treated\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"240\",\"c\":null}]},\"control\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"fold\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"log2fc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"inverse\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Fold change\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"treated/control\",\"r\":[\"treated\",\"control\"]},{\"l\":\"log2fc\",\"f\":\"log2(treated/control)\",\"r\":[\"treated\",\"control\"]},{\"l\":\"pct\",\"f\":\"(treated/control-1)*100\",\"r\":[\"treated\",\"control\"]},{\"l\":\"inverse\",\"f\":\"control/treated\",\"r\":[\"control\",\"treated\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'chart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(52, 'cfu-ml-calculator', 'biology', 'CFU/mL Calculator', 'CFU/mL Calculator', 'CFU/mL Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A CFU/mL count comes from one plate: clumps form a single colony, so the count is colony-forming units, not cells. Free cfu/ml calculator: enter your values a', 'A CFU/mL count comes from one plate: clumps form a single colony, so the count is colony-forming units, not cells. It returns CFU per mL, log₁₀(CFU/mL), CFU per 100 mL and dilution factor of the plated sample, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a colony count and a plating volume into CFU/mL, choosing the countable dilution and applying the volume factor correctly. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the CFU/mL\",\"html\":\"<p>CFU/mL = colonies ÷ volume plated (mL) × dilution factor</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cfu-ml-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: CFU/mL Calculator — Colonies, volume and dilution give CFU/mL.\\\" title=\\\"CFU/mL Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Colonies, volume and dilution give CFU/mL.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>colonies</code></td><td>colonies on the countable plate</td><td>CFU</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume</code></td><td>volume of diluted sample plated</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>dilution</code></td><td>the reciprocal of the dilution</td><td>×</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the CFU/mL?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: colonies counted of <strong>148</strong> CFU, volume plated of <strong>0.1</strong> mL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cfu per ml of <strong>1,480,000,000</strong> CFU/mL, log₁₀(cfu/ml) of <strong>9.17</strong>, cfu per 100 ml of <strong>148,000,000,000</strong> CFU/100 mL, dilution factor of the plated sample of <strong>1,000,000</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>30 – 300 colonies</td><td><strong>Countable</strong></td><td>statistically reliable window</td></tr><tr><td>below 30</td><td><strong>Too few</strong></td><td>the counting error exceeds 10%</td></tr><tr><td>above 300</td><td><strong>Too many</strong></td><td>crowding merges colonies</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>CFU undercounts cells: a pair of attached cells forms one colony, so the result is a lower bound on cell number.</p></li><li><p>Plate at least two dilutions: you cannot know in advance which one will land in 30–300.</p></li><li><p>For drinking water, results are usually reported per 100 mL; the calculator gives that too.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Forgetting the plating volume, which changes the answer by a factor of ten.</li><li>Using a dilution factor of 10⁻⁶ instead of 10⁶.</li><li>Counting a plate with 500 colonies and reporting it as precise.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cfu-g-calculator\\\">CFU/g</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-concentration-calculator\\\">Bacterial concentration</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/serial-dilution-planner\\\">Serial dilution planner</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why CFU and not cells?\",\"a\":\"Because the method counts colonies, and two cells that stick together produce one colony.\"},{\"q\":\"What if my plates have 20 and 280 colonies?\",\"a\":\"Use the 280 plate and quote it as within range; average both only if they give the same CFU/mL estimate.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the CFU/mL actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns CFU per mL, log₁₀(CFU/mL), CFU per 100 mL and dilution factor of the plated sample. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"colonies\",\"label\":\"Colonies counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"148\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Countable range is 30–300\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Volume plated\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.1 mL for a spread plate\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor of the plated sample\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10⁶ for a 10⁻⁶ dilution\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cfu_ml\",\"label\":\"CFU per mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cfu_log\",\"label\":\"log₁₀(CFU/mL)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cfu_100\",\"label\":\"CFU per 100 mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU/100 mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Count\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cfu_ml\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution\"},{\"l\":\"cfu_log\",\"f\":\"log10(colonies/volume*dilution)\"},{\"l\":\"cfu_100\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution*100\"},{\"l\":\"dilution\",\"f\":\"cfu_ml*volume/colonies\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cfu-ml-calculator\",\"slug\":\"cfu-ml-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"CFU/mL Calculator\",\"title\":\"CFU/mL Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A CFU/mL count comes from one plate: clumps form a single colony, so the count is colony-forming units, not cells. Free cfu/ml calculator: enter your values a\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cfu-ml-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"colonies\",\"l\":\"Colonies counted\",\"h\":\"Countable range is 30–300\",\"suf\":\"CFU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Volume plated\",\"h\":\"0.1 mL for a spread plate\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor of the plated sample\",\"h\":\"10⁶ for a 10⁻⁶ dilution\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cfu_ml\",\"l\":\"CFU per mL\",\"suf\":\"CFU/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cfu_log\",\"l\":\"log₁₀(CFU/mL)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cfu_100\",\"l\":\"CFU per 100 mL\",\"suf\":\"CFU/100 mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"colonies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"148\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.1\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000000\",\"c\":null}]},\"cfu_ml\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cfu_log\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cfu_100\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Count\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cfu_ml\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"cfu_log\",\"f\":\"log10(colonies/volume*dilution)\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"cfu_100\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution*100\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"dilution\",\"f\":\"cfu_ml*volume/colonies\",\"r\":[\"cfu_ml\",\"volume\",\"colonies\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'petri', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(53, 'cfu-g-calculator', 'biology', 'CFU/g Calculator', 'CFU/g Calculator', 'CFU/g Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Food, soil and tissue microbiology counts per gram, not per millilitre, and getting from a plate count to CFU/g needs the dilution and the wet weight. Free cf', 'Food, soil and tissue microbiology counts per gram, not per millilitre, and getting from a plate count to CFU/g needs the dilution and the wet weight. It returns CFU per gram, log₁₀(CFU/g) and CFU per kg, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator does it, and converts to log CFU/g, the scale used in food safety limits. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the CFU/g\",\"html\":\"<p>CFU/g = colonies ÷ volume plated × dilution factor ÷ sample weight</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cfu-g-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: CFU/g Calculator — Plate count, dilution and sample weight give CFU/g.\\\" title=\\\"CFU/g Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Plate count, dilution and sample weight give CFU/g.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>colonies</code></td><td>colonies on the countable plate</td><td>CFU</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume</code></td><td>volume plated</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>dilution</code></td><td>total dilution</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>weight</code></td><td>mass of sample in the first dilution</td><td>g</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the CFU/g?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: colonies counted of <strong>126</strong> CFU, volume plated of <strong>0.1</strong> mL, dilution factor of <strong>10,000</strong> ×, sample weight of <strong>1</strong> g.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cfu per gram of <strong>12,600,000</strong> CFU/g, log₁₀(cfu/g) of <strong>7.1</strong>, cfu per kg of <strong>12,600,000,000</strong> CFU/kg.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 4 log CFU/g</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>below most spoilage thresholds</td></tr><tr><td>4 – 6 log</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>typical of raw foods</td></tr><tr><td>above 6 log</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>spoilage or a process failure</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Log CFU/g is the industry language: 6 log CFU/g is a million per gram.</p></li><li><p>If the sample was frozen or dried, report whether the weight was taken before or after.</p></li><li><p>Homogenisation must be included in the first dilution volume, not after it.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Dividing by the weight twice.</li><li>Treating a 25 g sample in 225 mL as a dilution factor of 225 instead of 10.</li><li>Reporting CFU/mL from a solid sample without stating the weight used.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cfu-ml-calculator\\\">CFU/mL</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/serial-dilution-planner\\\">Serial dilution planner</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I convert CFU/g to log CFU/g?\",\"a\":\"Take the base-10 logarithm: 1 × 10⁶ CFU/g is 6 log CFU/g.\"},{\"q\":\"Does this work for swabs?\",\"a\":\"Use CFU per swab or per cm² instead; a swab has no meaningful gram weight.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the CFU/g actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns CFU per gram, log₁₀(CFU/g) and CFU per kg. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"colonies\",\"label\":\"Colonies counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"126\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Volume plated\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10⁴ for a 1 g in 9 mL then 10⁻³ series\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"weight\",\"label\":\"Sample weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Weight that went into the first dilution\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cfu_g\",\"label\":\"CFU per gram\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU/g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cfu_log\",\"label\":\"log₁₀(CFU/g)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_kg\",\"label\":\"CFU per kg\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU/kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Count\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cfu_g\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution/weight\"},{\"l\":\"cfu_log\",\"f\":\"log10(colonies/volume*dilution/weight)\"},{\"l\":\"per_kg\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution/weight*1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cfu-g-calculator\",\"slug\":\"cfu-g-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"CFU/g Calculator\",\"title\":\"CFU/g Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Food, soil and tissue microbiology counts per gram, not per millilitre, and getting from a plate count to CFU/g needs the dilution and the wet weight. Free cf\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cfu-g-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"colonies\",\"l\":\"Colonies counted\",\"suf\":\"CFU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Volume plated\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor\",\"h\":\"10⁴ for a 1 g in 9 mL then 10⁻³ series\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"weight\",\"l\":\"Sample weight\",\"h\":\"Weight that went into the first dilution\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cfu_g\",\"l\":\"CFU per gram\",\"suf\":\"CFU/g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cfu_log\",\"l\":\"log₁₀(CFU/g)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_kg\",\"l\":\"CFU per kg\",\"suf\":\"CFU/kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"colonies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"126\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.1\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10000\",\"c\":null}]},\"weight\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"cfu_g\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cfu_log\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_kg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Count\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cfu_g\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution/weight\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume\",\"dilution\",\"weight\"]},{\"l\":\"cfu_log\",\"f\":\"log10(colonies/volume*dilution/weight)\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume\",\"dilution\",\"weight\"]},{\"l\":\"per_kg\",\"f\":\"colonies/volume*dilution/weight*1000\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume\",\"dilution\",\"weight\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'petri', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(54, 'bacterial-concentration-calculator', 'biology', 'Bacterial Concentration Calculator', 'Bacterial Concentration Calculator', 'Bacterial Concentration Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Bacterial concentration can come from a plate count, an OD reading or a dilution series, and the three disagree. Free bacterial concentration calculator: ente', 'Bacterial concentration can come from a plate count, an OD reading or a dilution series, and the three disagree. It returns cells per mL (from OD), log₁₀ CFU/mL and cells per CFU, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts between them and reports the concentration in cells/mL, CFU/mL and log units, with the doubling time it implies. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Bacterial Concentration\",\"html\":\"<p>Cells/mL ≈ OD600 × (cells/mL per OD unit);  log₁₀ CFU/mL = log₁₀(CFU/mL)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/bacterial-concentration-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Bacterial Concentration Calculator — Plate count against the OD estimate, side by side.\\\" title=\\\"Bacterial Concentration Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Plate count against the OD estimate, side by side.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>CFU/mL</code></td><td>colony-forming units from a plate</td><td>CFU/mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>OD600</code></td><td>absorbance of the culture</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>ratio</code></td><td>how many cells make up one colony — the clumping factor</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Bacterial Concentration?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: cfu per ml of <strong>5,000,000</strong> CFU/mL, od600 of <strong>0.6</strong>, cells per ml per od unit of <strong>800,000,000</strong> cells/mL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cells per ml (from od) of <strong>480,000,000</strong> cells/mL, log₁₀ cfu/ml of <strong>6.7</strong>, cells per cfu of <strong>96</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Cells per CFU is usually 1–3 for well-dispersed cultures and much higher for clumping strains; it is the size of the gap between the two methods.</p></li><li><p>Both methods count dead cells differently: OD600 counts everything, plating counts only what can grow.</p></li><li><p>For a standard curve, calibrate OD against CFU for your own strain, medium and spectrophotometer once, and reuse the factor.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Comparing OD-derived cell counts with CFU directly and reporting a discrepancy as a result.</li><li>Using the E. coli factor for a Gram-positive organism.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cfu-ml-calculator\\\">CFU/mL</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/od600-to-cell-density\\\">OD600 to cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\\\">Bacterial growth curve</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Which number should I report?\",\"a\":\"Both, with the method: OD-based cells/mL for mass, CFU/mL for viable count.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do they differ by a factor of two?\",\"a\":\"Clumping, dead cells and chains of cells. For streptococci the gap can be tenfold.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Bacterial Concentration actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns cells per mL (from OD), log₁₀ CFU/mL and cells per CFU. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"cfu_ml\",\"label\":\"CFU per mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5000000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From a plate count\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"od\",\"label\":\"OD600\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional cross-check\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"factor\",\"label\":\"Cells per mL per OD unit\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"800000000.0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cells\",\"label\":\"Cells per mL (from OD)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"log_cfu\",\"label\":\"log₁₀ CFU/mL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Cells per CFU\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cells\",\"f\":\"od*factor\"},{\"l\":\"log_cfu\",\"f\":\"log10(cfu_ml)\"},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"od*factor/cfu_ml\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/bacterial-concentration-calculator\",\"slug\":\"bacterial-concentration-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Bacterial Concentration Calculator\",\"title\":\"Bacterial Concentration Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Bacterial concentration can come from a plate count, an OD reading or a dilution series, and the three disagree. Free bacterial concentration calculator: ente\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-bacterial-concentration-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cfu_ml\",\"l\":\"CFU per mL\",\"h\":\"From a plate count\",\"suf\":\"CFU/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od\",\"l\":\"OD600\",\"h\":\"Optional cross-check\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"factor\",\"l\":\"Cells per mL per OD unit\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells\",\"l\":\"Cells per mL (from OD)\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"log_cfu\",\"l\":\"log₁₀ CFU/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Cells per CFU\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"cfu_ml\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5000000\",\"c\":null}]},\"od\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.6\",\"c\":null}]},\"factor\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"800000000.0\",\"c\":null}]},\"cells\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"log_cfu\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cells\",\"f\":\"od*factor\",\"r\":[\"od\",\"factor\"]},{\"l\":\"log_cfu\",\"f\":\"log10(cfu_ml)\",\"r\":[\"cfu_ml\"]},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"od*factor/cfu_ml\",\"r\":[\"od\",\"factor\",\"cfu_ml\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'bacteria', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(55, 'transformation-efficiency', 'biology', 'Transformation Efficiency Calculator', 'Transformation Efficiency Calculator', 'Transformation Efficiency Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Transformation efficiency is the number of transformants per microgram of plasmid — the number that tells you whether your competent cells are any good. Free', 'Transformation efficiency is the number of transformants per microgram of plasmid — the number that tells you whether your competent cells are any good. It returns transformation efficiency, total transformants and transformants per ng, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator computes it from colonies, plated volume and plasmid mass, and flags the values that mean something went wrong. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Transformation Efficiency\",\"html\":\"<p>Efficiency (CFU/µg) = colonies × (recovery volume ÷ plated volume) × dilution ÷ µg DNA</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/transformation-efficiency.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Transformation Efficiency Calculator — Colonies scaled to the whole recovery, divided by the DNA mass.\\\" title=\\\"Transformation Efficiency Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Colonies scaled to the whole recovery, divided by the DNA mass.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>colonies</code></td><td>colonies on the countable plate</td><td>CFU</td></tr><tr><td><code>recovery</code></td><td>volume the cells were resuspended in</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume plated</code></td><td>fraction of the recovery that was plated</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>dna</code></td><td>mass of plasmid added</td><td>ng</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Transformation Efficiency?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: colonies on the plate of <strong>320</strong> CFU, volume plated of <strong>0.1</strong> mL, recovery volume of <strong>1</strong> mL, plasmid dna transformed of <strong>10</strong> ng, dilution plated of <strong>1</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives transformation efficiency of <strong>320,000</strong> CFU/µg, total transformants of <strong>3,200</strong> CFU, transformants per ng of <strong>320</strong> CFU/ng.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>above 1 × 10⁸</td><td><strong>Excellent</strong></td><td>chemically competent commercial-grade cells</td></tr><tr><td>1 × 10⁷ – 1 × 10⁸</td><td><strong>Good</strong></td><td>fine for cloning and library work</td></tr><tr><td>1 × 10⁶ – 1 × 10⁷</td><td><strong>Adequate</strong></td><td>check heat shock, antibiotics and DNA quality</td></tr><tr><td>below 1 × 10⁶</td><td><strong>Poor</strong></td><td>usually competent-cell preparation or a salt problem</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Efficiency drops sharply with plasmid size: a 10 kb construct transforms several times less well than a 3 kb one, and that is not a competent-cell problem.</p></li><li><p>Plate a range of volumes (10 µL and 100 µL of the recovery) so at least one plate is countable.</p></li><li><p>The antibiotic must be at the right concentration and the plates fresh; old ampicillin plates give satellite colonies that look like transformants.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using the plated volume as if it were the whole recovery.</li><li>Reporting CFU/µg using the mass of DNA in the ligation rather than the plasmid transformed.</li><li>Reading efficiency from a plate with 2000 colonies, where the count is meaningless.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/plasmid-copy-number\\\">Plasmid copy number</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cfu-ml-calculator\\\">CFU/mL</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/transformation-efficiency\\\">Transformation efficiency</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good transformation efficiency?\",\"a\":\"10⁸ CFU/µg or better for chemically competent cells; 10⁹–10¹⁰ for electroporation.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my efficiency low for a ligation?\",\"a\":\"Ligations contain nicked and linear DNA; efficiency is quoted for supercoiled plasmid.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Transformation Efficiency actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns transformation efficiency, total transformants and transformants per ng. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"colonies\",\"label\":\"Colonies on the plate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"320\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume_plated\",\"label\":\"Volume plated\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"recovery\",\"label\":\"Recovery volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"SOC or LB used to resuspend\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dna\",\"label\":\"Plasmid DNA transformed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution plated\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10 if you plated 1/10 of the recovery\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"efficiency\",\"label\":\"Transformation efficiency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU/µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_colonies\",\"label\":\"Total transformants\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_ng\",\"label\":\"Transformants per ng\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"CFU/ng\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Efficiency\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"colonies/(volume_plated/recovery)/dna*1000*dilution\"},{\"l\":\"total_colonies\",\"f\":\"colonies/(volume_plated/recovery)*dilution\"},{\"l\":\"per_ng\",\"f\":\"colonies/(volume_plated/recovery)/dna*dilution\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/transformation-efficiency\",\"slug\":\"transformation-efficiency\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Transformation Efficiency Calculator\",\"title\":\"Transformation Efficiency Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Transformation efficiency is the number of transformants per microgram of plasmid — the number that tells you whether your competent cells are any good. Free\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-transformation-efficiency\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"colonies\",\"l\":\"Colonies on the plate\",\"suf\":\"CFU\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume_plated\",\"l\":\"Volume plated\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"recovery\",\"l\":\"Recovery volume\",\"h\":\"SOC or LB used to resuspend\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dna\",\"l\":\"Plasmid DNA transformed\",\"suf\":\"ng\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution plated\",\"h\":\"10 if you plated 1/10 of the recovery\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"efficiency\",\"l\":\"Transformation efficiency\",\"suf\":\"CFU/µg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_colonies\",\"l\":\"Total transformants\",\"suf\":\"CFU\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_ng\",\"l\":\"Transformants per ng\",\"suf\":\"CFU/ng\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"colonies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"320\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume_plated\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.1\",\"c\":null}]},\"recovery\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"dna\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"efficiency\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_colonies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_ng\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Efficiency\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"colonies/(volume_plated/recovery)/dna*1000*dilution\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume_plated\",\"recovery\",\"dna\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"total_colonies\",\"f\":\"colonies/(volume_plated/recovery)*dilution\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume_plated\",\"recovery\",\"dilution\"]},{\"l\":\"per_ng\",\"f\":\"colonies/(volume_plated/recovery)/dna*dilution\",\"r\":[\"colonies\",\"volume_plated\",\"recovery\",\"dna\",\"dilution\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'bacteria', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:42', '2026-10-29 14:08:42'),
(56, 'pcr-master-mix-calculator', 'biology', 'PCR Master Mix Calculator', 'PCR Master Mix Calculator', 'PCR Master Mix Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A master mix is one bulk solution split across every tube in the run. Free pcr master mix calculator: enter your values and get total mix volume, master mix t', 'A master mix is one bulk solution split across every tube in the run. It returns total mix volume, master mix to add, each primer to add and water to add, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator sizes the mix, adds a pipetting allowance and returns the water volume to top up. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the PCR Master Mix\",\"html\":\"<p>Reactions prepared = reactions + extra;  master mix = total ÷ stock fold;  water = total − master mix − primers − template</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/pcr-master-mix-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: PCR Master Mix Calculator — Reactions × volume, then each component\'s share, then the water.\\\" title=\\\"PCR Master Mix Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Reactions × volume, then each component\'s share, then the water.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>total</code></td><td>number of tubes × volume per tube</td><td>µL</td></tr><tr><td><code>mm_vol</code></td><td>2× master mix, used at half the final volume</td><td>µL</td></tr><tr><td><code>primer_vol</code></td><td>each primer, from C1V1 = C2V2</td><td>µL</td></tr><tr><td><code>water_vol</code></td><td>the balance</td><td>µL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the PCR Master Mix?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: reactions of <strong>24</strong>, extra reactions of <strong>2</strong>, volume per reaction of <strong>25</strong> µL, master mix stock concentration of <strong>2</strong> ×, primer stock of <strong>10</strong> µM, primer final concentration of <strong>0.5</strong> µM, template volume per reaction of <strong>1</strong> µL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives total mix volume of <strong>650</strong> µL, master mix to add of <strong>325</strong> µL, each primer to add of <strong>32.5</strong> µL, water to add of <strong>234</strong> µL.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Prepare for two more reactions than you have tubes. Pipetting loss of 5–10% per component is normal, and running out halfway through a plate is unrecoverable.</p></li><li><p>Add the master mix to the plate first and the template last: template in a well without mix is the classic cause of one empty lane.</p></li><li><p>Primer volume is C1V1 = C2V2 per reaction, then multiplied by the number of reactions — 0.5 µM final from a 10 µM stock is 1.25 µL in a 25 µL reaction.</p></li><li><p>Keep the mix on ice and add polymerase last if it is a hot-start enzyme supplied separately.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Making exactly the number of reactions needed and coming up short.</li><li>Forgetting that the template volume is part of the 25 µL, not additional to it.</li><li>Pipetting the viscous master mix without pre-wetting the tip, which loses several microlitres.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/pcr-annealing-temperature\\\">Annealing temperature</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/primer-resuspension-working-stock\\\">Primer resuspension</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\\\">C1V1 dilution</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many extra reactions should I make?\",\"a\":\"Two, or 10% of the plate — more for a full 96-well plate.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my water volume negative?\",\"a\":\"The components already add up to more than the reaction volume: check the master mix concentration and the template volume.\"},{\"q\":\"Does this work for a 20 µL reaction?\",\"a\":\"Yes — set the volume per reaction to 20 and every component rescales.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the PCR Master Mix actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns total mix volume, master mix to add, each primer to add and water to add. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"reactions\",\"label\":\"Reactions\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"24\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Samples plus controls\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"extra\",\"label\":\"Extra reactions\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Allowance for pipetting loss\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rxn_vol\",\"label\":\"Volume per reaction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mm_stock\",\"label\":\"Master mix stock concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"2× mix is used at half the final volume\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"primer_stock\",\"label\":\"Primer stock\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Each primer, from a 10 µM working stock\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"primer_final\",\"label\":\"Primer final concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"template\",\"label\":\"Template volume per reaction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total mix volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mm_vol\",\"label\":\"Master mix to add\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"primer_vol\",\"label\":\"Each primer to add\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"water_vol\",\"label\":\"Water to add\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Master mix\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol\"},{\"l\":\"mm_vol\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol/mm_stock\"},{\"l\":\"primer_vol\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol*primer_final/primer_stock\"},{\"l\":\"water_vol\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol-(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol/mm_stock-(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol*primer_final/primer_stock*2-(reactions+extra)*template\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/pcr-master-mix-calculator\",\"slug\":\"pcr-master-mix-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"PCR Master Mix Calculator\",\"title\":\"PCR Master Mix Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A master mix is one bulk solution split across every tube in the run. Free pcr master mix calculator: enter your values and get total mix volume, master mix t\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-pcr-master-mix-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"reactions\",\"l\":\"Reactions\",\"h\":\"Samples plus controls\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"extra\",\"l\":\"Extra reactions\",\"h\":\"Allowance for pipetting loss\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rxn_vol\",\"l\":\"Volume per reaction\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mm_stock\",\"l\":\"Master mix stock concentration\",\"h\":\"2× mix is used at half the final volume\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"primer_stock\",\"l\":\"Primer stock\",\"h\":\"Each primer, from a 10 µM working stock\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"primer_final\",\"l\":\"Primer final concentration\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"template\",\"l\":\"Template volume per reaction\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total mix volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mm_vol\",\"l\":\"Master mix to add\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"primer_vol\",\"l\":\"Each primer to add\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"water_vol\",\"l\":\"Water to add\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"reactions\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"24\",\"c\":null}]},\"extra\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"rxn_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"mm_stock\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"primer_stock\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"primer_final\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"template\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mm_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"primer_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"water_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Master mix\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol\",\"r\":[\"reactions\",\"extra\",\"rxn_vol\"]},{\"l\":\"mm_vol\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol/mm_stock\",\"r\":[\"reactions\",\"extra\",\"rxn_vol\",\"mm_stock\"]},{\"l\":\"primer_vol\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol*primer_final/primer_stock\",\"r\":[\"reactions\",\"extra\",\"rxn_vol\",\"primer_final\",\"primer_stock\"]},{\"l\":\"water_vol\",\"f\":\"(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol-(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol/mm_stock-(reactions+extra)*rxn_vol*primer_final/primer_stock*2-(reactions+extra)*template\",\"r\":[\"reactions\",\"extra\",\"rxn_vol\",\"mm_stock\",\"primer_final\",\"primer_stock\",\"template\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'pcr', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(57, 'primer-resuspension-working-stock', 'biology', 'Primer Resuspension and Working Stock Calculator', 'Primer Resuspension and Working Stock Calculator', 'Primer Resuspension and Working Stock Calculator', 'A primer arrives as a dried pellet measured in nanomoles. Free primer resuspension and working stock calculator: enter your values and get volume to add to th', 'A primer arrives as a dried pellet measured in nanomoles. It returns volume to add to the pellet, stock to take for the working stock, diluent for the working stock and working stocks from one tube, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nDissolve it to a 100 µM stock, then dilute to a 10 µM working stock — this calculator gives the exact volume of TE or water for each step and how much working stock one tube of stock will make. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Primer Resuspension and Working Stock\",\"html\":\"<p>Volume (µL) = nmol ÷ concentration (µM) × 1000;  stock volume = C2V2 ÷ C1</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/primer-resuspension-working-stock.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Primer Resuspension and Working Stock Calculator — Nanonmoles in the tube to microlitres of diluent, twice over.\\\" title=\\\"Primer Resuspension and Working Stock Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Nanonmoles in the tube to microlitres of diluent, twice over.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>nmol</code></td><td>amount in the vial, per the spec sheet</td><td>nmol</td></tr><tr><td><code>stock</code></td><td>master stock concentration</td><td>µM</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume</code></td><td>diluent to add</td><td>µL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Primer Resuspension and Working Stock?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: primer in the tube of <strong>33.4</strong> nmol, stock concentration of <strong>100</strong> µM, working concentration of <strong>10</strong> µM, working stock to make of <strong>500</strong> µL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives volume to add to the pellet of <strong>334</strong> µL, stock to take for the working stock of <strong>50</strong> µL, diluent for the working stock of <strong>450</strong> µL, working stocks from one tube of <strong>167</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>1 nmol in 1 mL is 1 µM, so the arithmetic is friendly: 33.4 nmol in 334 µL gives 100 µM.</p></li><li><p>Vortex for 30 seconds and leave at room temperature for 10 minutes; pellets of long oligos dissolve slowly and a half-dissolved stock reads low.</p></li><li><p>Use TE rather than water for long-term storage: the Tris buffer holds the pH and the EDTA protects against nucleases.</p></li><li><p>Store the 100 µM stock at −20 °C in aliquots and keep the 10 µM working stock at 4 °C for up to a few weeks.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Assuming the vial contains exactly the nominal amount instead of reading the spec sheet.</li><li>Freezing and thawing the working stock repeatedly, which is a common source of failed PCR.</li><li>Recording concentrations as \'100×\' rather than in µM, which cannot be checked later.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/pcr-master-mix-calculator\\\">PCR master mix</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\\\">C1V1 dilution</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\\\">Oligo Tm</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How much water do I add to a 33.4 nmol primer?\",\"a\":\"334 µL for a 100 µM stock, or 3.34 mL for 10 µM.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use water instead of TE?\",\"a\":\"For short-term use yes, but TE keeps the oligo stable for years.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Primer Resuspension and Working Stock actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns volume to add to the pellet, stock to take for the working stock, diluent for the working stock and working stocks from one tube. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"nmol\",\"label\":\"Primer in the tube\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"33.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Printed on the vial or the spec sheet\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"stock\",\"label\":\"Stock concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"100 µM is the usual master stock\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"working\",\"label\":\"Working concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"working_vol\",\"label\":\"Working stock to make\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"water_stock\",\"label\":\"Volume to add to the pellet\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"take\",\"label\":\"Stock to take for the working stock\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"water_working\",\"label\":\"Diluent for the working stock\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"aliquots\",\"label\":\"Working stocks from one tube\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Resuspension\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"water_stock\",\"f\":\"nmol/stock*1000\"},{\"l\":\"take\",\"f\":\"working*working_vol/stock\"},{\"l\":\"water_working\",\"f\":\"working_vol-working*working_vol/stock\"},{\"l\":\"aliquots\",\"f\":\"nmol/stock*1000/working*working_vol/stock\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/primer-resuspension-working-stock\",\"slug\":\"primer-resuspension-working-stock\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Primer Resuspension and Working Stock Calculator\",\"title\":\"Primer Resuspension and Working Stock Calculator\",\"desc\":\"A primer arrives as a dried pellet measured in nanomoles. Free primer resuspension and working stock calculator: enter your values and get volume to add to th\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-primer-resuspension-working-stock\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nmol\",\"l\":\"Primer in the tube\",\"h\":\"Printed on the vial or the spec sheet\",\"suf\":\"nmol\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stock\",\"l\":\"Stock concentration\",\"h\":\"100 µM is the usual master stock\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"working\",\"l\":\"Working concentration\",\"suf\":\"µM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"working_vol\",\"l\":\"Working stock to make\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"water_stock\",\"l\":\"Volume to add to the pellet\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"take\",\"l\":\"Stock to take for the working stock\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"water_working\",\"l\":\"Diluent for the working stock\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"aliquots\",\"l\":\"Working stocks from one tube\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"nmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"33.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"stock\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"working\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"working_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"500\",\"c\":null}]},\"water_stock\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"take\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"water_working\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"aliquots\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Resuspension\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"water_stock\",\"f\":\"nmol/stock*1000\",\"r\":[\"nmol\",\"stock\"]},{\"l\":\"take\",\"f\":\"working*working_vol/stock\",\"r\":[\"working\",\"working_vol\",\"stock\"]},{\"l\":\"water_working\",\"f\":\"working_vol-working*working_vol/stock\",\"r\":[\"working_vol\",\"working\",\"stock\"]},{\"l\":\"aliquots\",\"f\":\"nmol/stock*1000/working*working_vol/stock\",\"r\":[\"nmol\",\"stock\",\"working\",\"working_vol\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'pipette', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(58, 'serial-dilution-planner', 'biology', 'Serial Dilution Planner', 'Serial Dilution Planner', 'Serial Dilution Planner Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A serial dilution builds a concentration series by transferring a fixed volume from tube to tube. Free serial dilution planner calculator: enter your values a', 'A serial dilution builds a concentration series by transferring a fixed volume from tube to tube. It returns transfer volume, diluent per tube, final concentration and diluent needed in total, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis planner gives the transfer volume, the diluent volume, every concentration in the series and the total volume of diluent you need to prepare. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Serial Dilution Planner\",\"html\":\"<p>Transfer = tube volume ÷ dilution factor;  C(n) = C₀ ÷ factorⁿ</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/serial-dilution-planner.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Serial Dilution Planner — Every step divides the concentration by the same factor.\\\" title=\\\"Serial Dilution Planner\\\"><figcaption>Every step divides the concentration by the same factor.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>factor</code></td><td>how much each step dilutes</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>steps</code></td><td>number of transfers</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>transfer</code></td><td>volume moved from the previous tube</td><td>µL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Serial Dilution Planner?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: starting concentration of <strong>1,000</strong>, dilution factor per step of <strong>10</strong> ×, number of steps of <strong>6</strong>, volume in each tube of <strong>1,000</strong> µL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives transfer volume of <strong>100</strong> µL, diluent per tube of <strong>900</strong> µL, final concentration of <strong>0.001</strong>, diluent needed in total of <strong>5,400</strong> µL.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The transfer volume must not exceed a tenth of the tube volume to keep the previous tube\'s contents from being disturbed — that is the point of the factor.</p></li><li><p>The last tube is a disposal tube: the series has one more tube than transfers.</p></li><li><p>Each step multiplies the error of the previous one, so keep the numbers exact rather than rounding the intermediate concentrations.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Changing tips for the transfer but not for the mixing, which carries over the previous tube.</li><li>Diluting into the same volume you took from (which leaves the tube short).</li><li>Assuming a tenfold series over six steps gives 10⁻⁶ when it gives 10⁻⁶ from a starting concentration of 1 — write the series out.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cfu-ml-calculator\\\">CFU/mL</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\\\">C1V1 dilution</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter\\\">Hemocytometer counter</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many tubes do I need for a 10⁻⁶ dilution?\",\"a\":\"Six transfers, so seven tubes including the starting material.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my transfer volume so small?\",\"a\":\"Because the tube volume is small or the factor is large; increase the tube volume or use a two-step dilution.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Serial Dilution Planner actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns transfer volume, diluent per tube, final concentration and diluent needed in total. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"stock\",\"label\":\"Starting concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Any unit — mg/mL, µM, CFU/mL\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"factor\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor per step\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10 for a tenfold series\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"steps\",\"label\":\"Number of steps\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tube_vol\",\"label\":\"Volume in each tube\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"transfer\",\"label\":\"Transfer volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"diluent\",\"label\":\"Diluent per tube\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"final\",\"label\":\"Final concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_diluent\",\"label\":\"Diluent needed in total\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Series\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"transfer\",\"f\":\"tube_vol/factor\"},{\"l\":\"diluent\",\"f\":\"tube_vol-tube_vol/factor\"},{\"l\":\"final\",\"f\":\"stock/pow(factor,steps)\"},{\"l\":\"total_diluent\",\"f\":\"(tube_vol-tube_vol/factor)*steps\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/serial-dilution-planner\",\"slug\":\"serial-dilution-planner\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Serial Dilution Planner\",\"title\":\"Serial Dilution Planner Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A serial dilution builds a concentration series by transferring a fixed volume from tube to tube. Free serial dilution planner calculator: enter your values a\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-serial-dilution-planner\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stock\",\"l\":\"Starting concentration\",\"h\":\"Any unit — mg/mL, µM, CFU/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"factor\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor per step\",\"h\":\"10 for a tenfold series\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"steps\",\"l\":\"Number of steps\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tube_vol\",\"l\":\"Volume in each tube\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"transfer\",\"l\":\"Transfer volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"diluent\",\"l\":\"Diluent per tube\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"final\",\"l\":\"Final concentration\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_diluent\",\"l\":\"Diluent needed in total\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"stock\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"factor\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"steps\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"tube_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"transfer\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"diluent\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"final\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_diluent\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Series\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"transfer\",\"f\":\"tube_vol/factor\",\"r\":[\"tube_vol\",\"factor\"]},{\"l\":\"diluent\",\"f\":\"tube_vol-tube_vol/factor\",\"r\":[\"tube_vol\",\"factor\"]},{\"l\":\"final\",\"f\":\"stock/pow(factor,steps)\",\"r\":[\"stock\",\"factor\",\"steps\"]},{\"l\":\"total_diluent\",\"f\":\"(tube_vol-tube_vol/factor)*steps\",\"r\":[\"tube_vol\",\"factor\",\"steps\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'pipette', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(59, 'dilution-calculator-c1v1', 'biology', 'Dilution Calculator (C1V1 = C2V2)', 'Dilution Calculator (C1V1 = C2V2)', 'Dilution (C1V1 = C2V2) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Every dilution question is the same equation: the amount of solute before equals the amount after. Free dilution (c1v1 = c2v2) calculator: enter your values a', 'Every dilution question is the same equation: the amount of solute before equals the amount after. It returns stock volume, diluent to add and dilution factor, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator solves C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ for whichever value you are missing and tells you how much diluent to add. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Dilution (C1V1 = C2V2)\",\"html\":\"<p>C₁ × V₁ = C₂ × V₂</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Dilution Calculator (C1V1 = C2V2) — Concentration before and after, and the volume that bridges them.\\\" title=\\\"Dilution Calculator (C1V1 = C2V2)\\\"><figcaption>Concentration before and after, and the volume that bridges them.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>C₁</code></td><td>stock concentration</td><td>any</td></tr><tr><td><code>V₁</code></td><td>volume of stock to take</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>C₂</code></td><td>final concentration</td><td>any</td></tr><tr><td><code>V₂</code></td><td>final volume</td><td>mL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Dilution (C1V1 = C2V2)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: stock concentration of <strong>10</strong> M, final concentration of <strong>0.5</strong> M, final volume of <strong>100</strong> mL.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives stock volume of <strong>5</strong> mL, diluent to add of <strong>95</strong> mL, dilution factor of <strong>20</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The confidence check is units: C₁ and C₂ must be in the same unit and V₁ and V₂ in the same unit, or the equation quietly gives a wrong answer.</p></li><li><p>Add the stock to about 80% of the final volume of diluent, then top up to the mark: mixing volumes is not strictly additive.</p></li><li><p>For a dilution series, do it stepwise; a single 1000× dilution is less accurate than three 10× steps because of pipetting error.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Mixing molarity with mass concentration in the same equation.</li><li>Forgetting that V₂ includes the stock volume.</li><li>Using a volumetric flask for a small volume and a beaker for the rest.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/serial-dilution-planner\\\">Serial dilution</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/molarity\\\">Molarity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/primer-resuspension-working-stock\\\">Primer resuspension</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What if I want a dilution factor, not a concentration?\",\"a\":\"Dilution factor is C₁/C₂; a 1:10 dilution has a factor of 10.\"},{\"q\":\"Can V₁ be larger than V₂?\",\"a\":\"No — that would mean concentrating the solution, which a dilution cannot do.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Dilution (C1V1 = C2V2) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns stock volume, diluent to add and dilution factor. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"c1\",\"label\":\"Stock concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"M\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"v1\",\"label\":\"Stock volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"c2\",\"label\":\"Final concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"M\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"v2\",\"label\":\"Final volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"diluent\",\"label\":\"Diluent to add\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fold\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Dilution\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"v1\",\"f\":\"c2*v2/c1\"},{\"l\":\"diluent\",\"f\":\"v2-c2*v2/c1\"},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"c1/c2\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\",\"slug\":\"dilution-calculator-c1v1\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Dilution Calculator (C1V1 = C2V2)\",\"title\":\"Dilution (C1V1 = C2V2) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Every dilution question is the same equation: the amount of solute before equals the amount after. Free dilution (c1v1 = c2v2) calculator: enter your values a\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-dilution-calculator-c1v1\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"c1\",\"l\":\"Stock concentration\",\"suf\":\"M\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"v1\",\"l\":\"Stock volume\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"c2\",\"l\":\"Final concentration\",\"suf\":\"M\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"v2\",\"l\":\"Final volume\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"diluent\",\"l\":\"Diluent to add\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fold\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"c1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"v1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"c2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"v2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"diluent\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fold\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Dilution\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"v1\",\"f\":\"c2*v2/c1\",\"r\":[\"c2\",\"v2\",\"c1\"]},{\"l\":\"diluent\",\"f\":\"v2-c2*v2/c1\",\"r\":[\"v2\",\"c2\",\"c1\"]},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"c1/c2\",\"r\":[\"c1\",\"c2\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(60, 'od260-nucleic-acid-concentration', 'biology', 'OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration Calculator', 'OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration Calculator', 'OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration Calculator', 'A260 converts to concentration with a factor that depends on what you are measuring: 50 for double-stranded DNA, 40 for RNA, 33 for single-stranded DNA. Free', 'A260 converts to concentration with a factor that depends on what you are measuring: 50 for double-stranded DNA, 40 for RNA, 33 for single-stranded DNA. It returns concentration, A260/A280 and total yield in 100 µL, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator applies the right factor, the dilution, and the A260/A280 ratio that tells you whether the sample is clean. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration\",\"html\":\"<p>Concentration (ng/µL) = A260 × dilution × factor, with factor 50 (dsDNA), 40 (RNA), 33 (ssDNA)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/od260-nucleic-acid-concentration.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration Calculator — Absorbance, dilution and a factor that depends on the molecule.\\\" title=\\\"OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Absorbance, dilution and a factor that depends on the molecule.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A260</code></td><td>absorbance at 260 nm</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>factor</code></td><td>50 ng/µL per A260 for dsDNA</td><td>ng/µL</td></tr><tr><td><code>purity</code></td><td>A260/A280</td><td>ratio</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Factors of 50, 40 and 33 ng/µL per A260 unit are the standard convention for dsDNA, RNA and ssDNA.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: nucleic acid set to <strong>50.0</strong>, a260 reading of <strong>0.42</strong>, dilution factor of <strong>1</strong> ×, a280 reading of <strong>0.23</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives concentration of <strong>21,000</strong> ng/µL, a260/a280 of <strong>1.83</strong>, total yield in 100 µl of <strong>2,100</strong> µg.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1.8</td><td><strong>Pure DNA</strong></td><td>the usual target for dsDNA</td></tr><tr><td>2.0</td><td><strong>Pure RNA</strong></td><td>the usual target for RNA</td></tr><tr><td>below 1.6</td><td><strong>Protein or phenol carry-over</strong></td><td>re-purify before sequencing</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The factors assume a 1 cm path length. A Nanodrop normalises to 1 cm automatically; a microplate reader does not, so check the path length.</p></li><li><p>A260 measures everything that absorbs at 260 nm, including free nucleotides and primers, so a high concentration with a poor 260/280 can still fail downstream.</p></li><li><p>Below about 0.1 AU the reading is dominated by noise; use a fluorescent assay instead.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using the dsDNA factor for RNA (50 instead of 40, a 25% overestimate).</li><li>Forgetting the dilution when the sample was diluted for the reading.</li><li>Reading a sample that still has ethanol in it — ethanol absorbs strongly in the UV.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter\\\">dsDNA µg to pmol</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/genome-copy-number\\\">Genome copy number</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/beer-lambert-law\\\">Beer–Lambert law</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why 50 for dsDNA?\",\"a\":\"A 50 µg/mL solution of double-stranded DNA in a 1 cm cuvette has an A260 of 1.0.\"},{\"q\":\"My A260/A280 is 1.9 but the sequencing failed — why?\",\"a\":\"The ratio is insensitive to some inhibitors; check A260/A230 and run a gel.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns concentration, A260/A280 and total yield in 100 µL. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"type\",\"label\":\"Nucleic acid\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"dsdna\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"dsdna\",\"dsDNA\",\"50\"],[\"ssdna-oligo\",\"ssDNA / oligo\",\"33\"],[\"rna\",\"RNA\",\"40\"],[\"protein\",\"Protein\",\"1.5\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"a260\",\"label\":\"A260 reading\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.42\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Absorbance in a 1 cm cuvette\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dilution\",\"label\":\"Dilution factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10 if you diluted 1:10 for the Nanodrop\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"a280\",\"label\":\"A280 reading\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.23\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional — gives the purity ratio\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"purity\",\"label\":\"A260/A280\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"yield_ug\",\"label\":\"Total yield in 100 µL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"a260*dilution*type*1000\"},{\"l\":\"purity\",\"f\":\"a260/a280\"},{\"l\":\"yield_ug\",\"f\":\"a260*dilution*type*1000*100/1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/od260-nucleic-acid-concentration\",\"slug\":\"od260-nucleic-acid-concentration\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration Calculator\",\"title\":\"OD260 to Nucleic Acid Concentration Calculator\",\"desc\":\"A260 converts to concentration with a factor that depends on what you are measuring: 50 for double-stranded DNA, 40 for RNA, 33 for single-stranded DNA. Free\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-od260-nucleic-acid-concentration\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"type\",\"l\":\"Nucleic acid\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-type\",\"opts\":[[\"dsdna\",\"dsDNA\",\"50\"],[\"ssdna-oligo\",\"ssDNA / oligo\",\"33\"],[\"rna\",\"RNA\",\"40\"],[\"protein\",\"Protein\",\"1.5\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a260\",\"l\":\"A260 reading\",\"h\":\"Absorbance in a 1 cm cuvette\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dilution\",\"l\":\"Dilution factor\",\"h\":\"10 if you diluted 1:10 for the Nanodrop\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a280\",\"l\":\"A280 reading\",\"h\":\"Optional — gives the purity ratio\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Concentration\",\"suf\":\"ng/µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"purity\",\"l\":\"A260/A280\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"yield_ug\",\"l\":\"Total yield in 100 µL\",\"suf\":\"µg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"type\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"dsdna\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-type\"},\"a260\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.42\",\"c\":null}]},\"dilution\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"a280\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.23\",\"c\":null}]},\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"purity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"yield_ug\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-type\":{\"name\":\"Nucleic acid\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"dsdna\",\"label\":\"dsDNA\",\"value\":\"50\"},{\"uid\":\"ssdna-oligo\",\"label\":\"ssDNA / oligo\",\"value\":\"33\"},{\"uid\":\"rna\",\"label\":\"RNA\",\"value\":\"40\"},{\"uid\":\"protein\",\"label\":\"Protein\",\"value\":\"1.5\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Concentration\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"conc\",\"f\":\"a260*dilution*type*1000\",\"r\":[\"a260\",\"dilution\",\"type\"]},{\"l\":\"purity\",\"f\":\"a260/a280\",\"r\":[\"a260\",\"a280\"]},{\"l\":\"yield_ug\",\"f\":\"a260*dilution*type*1000*100/1000\",\"r\":[\"a260\",\"dilution\",\"type\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'spectrophotometer', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(61, 'dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter', 'biology', 'dsDNA µg to pmol Converter', 'dsDNA µg to pmol Converter', 'dsDNA µg to pmol Converter Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Molecular biology protocols ask for DNA in pmol, and the freezer gives you µg. Free dsdna µg to pmol converter calculator: enter your values and get amount, m', 'Molecular biology protocols ask for DNA in pmol, and the freezer gives you µg. It returns amount, mass for 1 pmol, concentration if in 50 µL and molecules, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe bridge is the length of the molecule: one base pair of double-stranded DNA weighs 660 g/mol, so the conversion needs the size as well as the mass. This calculator handles both directions. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the dsDNA µg to pmol Converter\",\"html\":\"<p>pmol = µg × 10⁶ ÷ (length in bp × 660);  1 pmol of 1 000 bp = 0.66 µg</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: dsDNA µg to pmol Converter — Mass divided by the molar mass of the fragment.\\\" title=\\\"dsDNA µg to pmol Converter\\\"><figcaption>Mass divided by the molar mass of the fragment.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>µg</code></td><td>mass of DNA</td><td>µg</td></tr><tr><td><code>bp</code></td><td>length in base pairs</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>660</code></td><td>average molar mass of one base pair, g/mol</td><td>g/mol</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the dsDNA µg to pmol Converter?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: mass of dna of <strong>1</strong> µg, fragment length of <strong>3,000</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives amount of <strong>0.5051</strong> pmol, mass for 1 pmol of <strong>1.98</strong> µg, concentration if in 50 µl of <strong>20</strong> ng/µL, molecules of <strong>304,141</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>660 g/mol per bp is the standard average; a specific fragment can differ by a few percent depending on GC content, which does not matter for ligation ratios.</p></li><li><p>The same arithmetic works in reverse: pmol × bp × 660 ÷ 10⁶ = µg.</p></li><li><p>For single-stranded oligos the molar mass is roughly 330 g/mol per nucleotide, which is why primers are quoted in nmol rather than µg.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the plasmid length when the fragment of interest is much shorter.</li><li>Dropping the 10⁶ that converts grams to micrograms.</li><li>Treating nmol and pmol as interchangeable — they differ by a thousand.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/od260-nucleic-acid-concentration\\\">OD260 concentration</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/ligation-molar-ratio\\\">Ligation molar ratio</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/genome-copy-number\\\">Genome copy number</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many pmol is 1 µg of a 3 kb plasmid?\",\"a\":\"About 0.505 pmol — 1 000 ng ÷ (3 000 × 0.66 ng/pmol).\"},{\"q\":\"Why does length matter so much?\",\"a\":\"Because the amount of substance is the mass divided by the molar mass, and the molar mass scales with length.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the dsDNA µg to pmol Converter actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns amount, mass for 1 pmol, concentration if in 50 µL and molecules. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"ug\",\"label\":\"Mass of DNA\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"bp\",\"label\":\"Fragment length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"pmol\",\"label\":\"Amount\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ug_from_pmol\",\"label\":\"Mass for 1 pmol\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ng_ul\",\"label\":\"Concentration if in 50 µL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"molecules\",\"label\":\"Molecules\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"pmol\",\"f\":\"ug*1000000/(bp*660)\"},{\"l\":\"ug_from_pmol\",\"f\":\"bp*660/1000000\"},{\"l\":\"ng_ul\",\"f\":\"ug*1000/50\"},{\"l\":\"molecules\",\"f\":\"ug*0.000000000001/660/bp*602200000000000000000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter\",\"slug\":\"dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"dsDNA µg to pmol Converter\",\"title\":\"dsDNA µg to pmol Converter Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Molecular biology protocols ask for DNA in pmol, and the freezer gives you µg. Free dsdna µg to pmol converter calculator: enter your values and get amount, m\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ug\",\"l\":\"Mass of DNA\",\"suf\":\"µg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bp\",\"l\":\"Fragment length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pmol\",\"l\":\"Amount\",\"suf\":\"pmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ug_from_pmol\",\"l\":\"Mass for 1 pmol\",\"suf\":\"µg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ng_ul\",\"l\":\"Concentration if in 50 µL\",\"suf\":\"ng/µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"molecules\",\"l\":\"Molecules\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"ug\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"bp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3000\",\"c\":null}]},\"pmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ug_from_pmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ng_ul\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"molecules\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"pmol\",\"f\":\"ug*1000000/(bp*660)\",\"r\":[\"ug\",\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"ug_from_pmol\",\"f\":\"bp*660/1000000\",\"r\":[\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"ng_ul\",\"f\":\"ug*1000/50\",\"r\":[\"ug\"]},{\"l\":\"molecules\",\"f\":\"ug*0.000000000001/660/bp*602200000000000000000000\",\"r\":[\"ug\",\"bp\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(62, 'dna-molarity-from-mass', 'biology', 'DNA Molarity from Mass or A260', 'DNA Molarity from Mass or A260', 'DNA Molarity from Mass or A260 Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Concentration in ng/µL tells you how much DNA you have; molarity tells you how many molecules. Free dna molarity from mass or a260 calculator: enter your valu', 'Concentration in ng/µL tells you how much DNA you have; molarity tells you how many molecules. It returns molar concentration, pmol per µL, copies per µL and equivalent A260, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nSequencing, ligation and qPCR all need the second. This calculator converts between mass concentration, molar concentration and copy number. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the DNA Molarity from Mass or A260\",\"html\":\"<p>nM = (ng/µL × 10⁶) ÷ (bp × 660);  copies/µL = (ng/µL × 6.022×10¹⁴) ÷ (bp × 660)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/dna-molarity-from-mass.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: DNA Molarity from Mass or A260 — Mass concentration, length and Avogadro\'s number.\\\" title=\\\"DNA Molarity from Mass or A260\\\"><figcaption>Mass concentration, length and Avogadro\'s number.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ng/µL</code></td><td>mass concentration</td><td>ng/µL</td></tr><tr><td><code>bp</code></td><td>molecule length</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>nM</code></td><td>molar concentration</td><td>nM</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the DNA Molarity from Mass or A260?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: dna concentration of <strong>50</strong> ng/µL, fragment length of <strong>5,000</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives molar concentration of <strong>15.15</strong> nM, pmol per µl of <strong>0.0152</strong> pmol/µL, copies per µl of <strong>9,124,242,424</strong>, equivalent a260 of <strong>1</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The copy-number form is the one qPCR standard curves are built on: a 10-fold dilution series is a 10-fold drop in copies per microlitre.</p></li><li><p>Doubling the length halves the molarity at the same mass concentration — long constructs need much more mass for the same number of molecules.</p></li><li><p>For a plasmid, use the full plasmid length, not the insert.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Mixing ng/µL and µg/mL by a factor of a thousand.</li><li>Using a size in kilobases in a formula that expects base pairs.</li><li>Quoting molarity from a concentration measured with the wrong UV factor.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter\\\">dsDNA µg to pmol</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/genome-copy-number\\\">Genome copy number</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/od260-nucleic-acid-concentration\\\">OD260 concentration</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many copies are in 1 ng of a 5 kb plasmid?\",\"a\":\"About 1.8 × 10⁸ copies.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do I need molarity for sequencing?\",\"a\":\"Because the reaction consumes molecules, not micrograms, and library molarity determines cluster density.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the DNA Molarity from Mass or A260 actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns molar concentration, pmol per µL, copies per µL and equivalent A260. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"ng_ul\",\"label\":\"DNA concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"bp\",\"label\":\"Fragment length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"nm\",\"label\":\"Molar concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pmol_ul\",\"label\":\"pmol per µL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pmol/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"copies_ul\",\"label\":\"Copies per µL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"a260\",\"label\":\"Equivalent A260\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Molarity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"nm\",\"f\":\"ng_ul*1000000/(bp*660)\"},{\"l\":\"pmol_ul\",\"f\":\"ng_ul*1000000/(bp*660)/1000\"},{\"l\":\"copies_ul\",\"f\":\"ng_ul*0.000000001/660/bp*602200000000000000000000\"},{\"l\":\"a260\",\"f\":\"ng_ul/50\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/dna-molarity-from-mass\",\"slug\":\"dna-molarity-from-mass\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"DNA Molarity from Mass or A260\",\"title\":\"DNA Molarity from Mass or A260 Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Concentration in ng/µL tells you how much DNA you have; molarity tells you how many molecules. Free dna molarity from mass or a260 calculator: enter your valu\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-dna-molarity-from-mass\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ng_ul\",\"l\":\"DNA concentration\",\"suf\":\"ng/µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bp\",\"l\":\"Fragment length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nm\",\"l\":\"Molar concentration\",\"suf\":\"nM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pmol_ul\",\"l\":\"pmol per µL\",\"suf\":\"pmol/µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"copies_ul\",\"l\":\"Copies per µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a260\",\"l\":\"Equivalent A260\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"ng_ul\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"bp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5000\",\"c\":null}]},\"nm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pmol_ul\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"copies_ul\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"a260\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Molarity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"nm\",\"f\":\"ng_ul*1000000/(bp*660)\",\"r\":[\"ng_ul\",\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"pmol_ul\",\"f\":\"ng_ul*1000000/(bp*660)/1000\",\"r\":[\"ng_ul\",\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"copies_ul\",\"f\":\"ng_ul*0.000000001/660/bp*602200000000000000000000\",\"r\":[\"ng_ul\",\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"a260\",\"f\":\"ng_ul/50\",\"r\":[\"ng_ul\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(63, 'ligation-molar-ratio', 'biology', 'Ligation Molar Ratio Calculator (Insert : Vector)', 'Ligation Molar Ratio Calculator (Insert : Vector)', 'Ligation Molar Ratio (Insert : Vector) Calculator', 'A ligation works when the insert outnumbers the vector enough to find it, but not so much that it ligates to itself. Free ligation molar ratio (insert : vecto', 'A ligation works when the insert outnumbers the vector enough to find it, but not so much that it ligates to itself. It returns insert mass, vector amount, insert amount and total DNA in the reaction, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe standard is a 3:1 molar ratio, and the calculation converts that ratio into nanograms using the two lengths. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Ligation Molar Ratio (Insert : Vector)\",\"html\":\"<p>Insert (ng) = vector (ng) × (insert bp ÷ vector bp) × molar ratio</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/ligation-molar-ratio.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Ligation Molar Ratio Calculator (Insert : Vector) — Vector and insert masses that give the chosen molar ratio.\\\" title=\\\"Ligation Molar Ratio Calculator (Insert : Vector)\\\"><figcaption>Vector and insert masses that give the chosen molar ratio.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>vector_ng</code></td><td>mass of cut vector</td><td>ng</td></tr><tr><td><code>vector_bp</code></td><td>vector length</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>insert_bp</code></td><td>insert length</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>ratio</code></td><td>moles of insert per mole of vector</td><td>:1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Ligation Molar Ratio (Insert : Vector)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: vector mass of <strong>100</strong> ng, vector length of <strong>4,000</strong> bp, insert length of <strong>800</strong> bp, insert : vector molar ratio of <strong>3</strong> :1.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives insert mass of <strong>60</strong> ng, vector amount of <strong>37.88</strong> pmol, insert amount of <strong>113.64</strong> pmol, total dna in the reaction of <strong>160</strong> ng.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>3:1 favours the desired product without starving the vector; 1:1 reduces tandem inserts, 10:1 drives short-insert ligations that otherwise fail.</p></li><li><p>Total DNA in a 20 µL ligation should stay around 100–200 ng: more DNA favours self-ligation and concatemers.</p></li><li><p>Both fragments should be clean — a ligation is exquisitely sensitive to residual phosphatase, salt and EDTA from a gel-purification kit.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the insert:vector mass ratio when the two fragments are different lengths.</li><li>Phosphatasing the insert as well as the vector, which leaves nothing to ligate to.</li><li>Leaving the ligation for hours at room temperature, which favours rearrangement.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter\\\">dsDNA µg to pmol</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/transformation-efficiency\\\">Transformation efficiency</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/restriction-digest-setup\\\">Restriction digest</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why 3:1?\",\"a\":\"It is the ratio that maximises the chance of a vector meeting an insert while limiting vector–vector and insert–insert products.\"},{\"q\":\"How much insert for 100 ng of a 4 kb vector and an 800 bp insert?\",\"a\":\"60 ng — 100 × (800/4000) × 3.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Ligation Molar Ratio (Insert : Vector) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns insert mass, vector amount, insert amount and total DNA in the reaction. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"vector_ng\",\"label\":\"Vector mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"vector_bp\",\"label\":\"Vector length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"insert_bp\",\"label\":\"Insert length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"800\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Insert : vector molar ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"3:1 is the usual starting point\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"insert_ng\",\"label\":\"Insert mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"vector_pmol\",\"label\":\"Vector amount\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"insert_pmol\",\"label\":\"Insert amount\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_ng\",\"label\":\"Total DNA in the reaction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Ligation\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"insert_ng\",\"f\":\"vector_ng*insert_bp/vector_bp*ratio\"},{\"l\":\"vector_pmol\",\"f\":\"vector_ng*1000000/(vector_bp*660)\"},{\"l\":\"insert_pmol\",\"f\":\"vector_ng*1000000/(vector_bp*660)*ratio\"},{\"l\":\"total_ng\",\"f\":\"vector_ng+vector_ng*insert_bp/vector_bp*ratio\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/ligation-molar-ratio\",\"slug\":\"ligation-molar-ratio\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Ligation Molar Ratio Calculator (Insert : Vector)\",\"title\":\"Ligation Molar Ratio (Insert : Vector) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"A ligation works when the insert outnumbers the vector enough to find it, but not so much that it ligates to itself. Free ligation molar ratio (insert : vecto\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-ligation-molar-ratio\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vector_ng\",\"l\":\"Vector mass\",\"suf\":\"ng\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vector_bp\",\"l\":\"Vector length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"insert_bp\",\"l\":\"Insert length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Insert : vector molar ratio\",\"h\":\"3:1 is the usual starting point\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"insert_ng\",\"l\":\"Insert mass\",\"suf\":\"ng\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vector_pmol\",\"l\":\"Vector amount\",\"suf\":\"pmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"insert_pmol\",\"l\":\"Insert amount\",\"suf\":\"pmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_ng\",\"l\":\"Total DNA in the reaction\",\"suf\":\"ng\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"vector_ng\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"vector_bp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4000\",\"c\":null}]},\"insert_bp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"800\",\"c\":null}]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3\",\"c\":null}]},\"insert_ng\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"vector_pmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"insert_pmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_ng\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Ligation\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"insert_ng\",\"f\":\"vector_ng*insert_bp/vector_bp*ratio\",\"r\":[\"vector_ng\",\"insert_bp\",\"vector_bp\",\"ratio\"]},{\"l\":\"vector_pmol\",\"f\":\"vector_ng*1000000/(vector_bp*660)\",\"r\":[\"vector_ng\",\"vector_bp\"]},{\"l\":\"insert_pmol\",\"f\":\"vector_ng*1000000/(vector_bp*660)*ratio\",\"r\":[\"vector_ng\",\"vector_bp\",\"ratio\"]},{\"l\":\"total_ng\",\"f\":\"vector_ng+vector_ng*insert_bp/vector_bp*ratio\",\"r\":[\"vector_ng\",\"insert_bp\",\"vector_bp\",\"ratio\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(64, 'restriction-digest-setup', 'biology', 'Restriction Digest Reaction Setup', 'Restriction Digest Reaction Setup', 'Restriction Digest Reaction Setup Calculator — Formula & Resul', 'A restriction digest is a small reaction with a strict recipe: enzyme units, buffer strength and volume. Free restriction digest reaction setup calculator: en', 'A restriction digest is a small reaction with a strict recipe: enzyme units, buffer strength and volume. It returns DNA volume, enzyme volume, buffer volume and water volume, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator builds the 20 µL reaction. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Restriction Digest Reaction Setup\",\"html\":\"<p>Enzyme units = DNA (µg) × units per µg;  buffer = reaction volume ÷ stock fold;  water = the balance</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/restriction-digest-setup.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Restriction Digest Reaction Setup — A 20 µL digest: DNA, enzyme, buffer and water.\\\" title=\\\"Restriction Digest Reaction Setup\\\"><figcaption>A 20 µL digest: DNA, enzyme, buffer and water.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>dna_ng</code></td><td>mass of DNA digested</td><td>ng</td></tr><tr><td><code>units_per_ug</code></td><td>enzyme excess over the minimum</td><td>U/µg</td></tr><tr><td><code>buffer_vol</code></td><td>1× final concentration</td><td>µL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Restriction Digest Reaction Setup?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: dna mass of <strong>1,000</strong> ng, dna concentration of <strong>200</strong> ng/µL, reaction volume of <strong>20</strong> µL, enzyme units per µg dna of <strong>10</strong> U/µg, enzyme concentration of <strong>20,000</strong> U/mL, buffer stock of <strong>10</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives dna volume of <strong>5</strong> µL, enzyme volume of <strong>0.5</strong> µL, buffer volume of <strong>2</strong> µL, water volume of <strong>12.5</strong> µL, total enzyme units of <strong>10</strong> U.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Glycerol carry-over above 5% of the reaction volume causes star activity; keep the enzyme volume at or below 1/10 of the total.</p></li><li><p>One unit is defined against a specific substrate DNA, so a new lot or a new template may need more; 10 U/µg is the safe working excess.</p></li><li><p>Most enzymes are active for 15 minutes to an hour at 37 °C; longer incubations mostly add star activity, not completeness.</p></li><li><p>Heat-inactivate or column-purify before ligation — residual enzyme will re-cut the product.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Adding 5 µL of enzyme to a 20 µL reaction for a \'fast\' digest.</li><li>Using a 10× buffer at 1× in a reaction that already contains a large volume of DNA in TE.</li><li>Assuming complete digestion without running an aliquot on a gel.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/ligation-molar-ratio\\\">Ligation molar ratio</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/agarose-gel-calculator\\\">Agarose gel</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\\\">C1V1 dilution</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What if the DNA volume exceeds the reaction volume?\",\"a\":\"Concentrate the DNA or scale the reaction up; the calculator returns a negative water volume when that happens.\"},{\"q\":\"Does doubled enzyme mean double the reaction volume?\",\"a\":\"No — doubling units per µg means more enzyme in the same volume, until the glycerol limit.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Restriction Digest Reaction Setup actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns DNA volume, enzyme volume, buffer volume and water volume. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"dna_ng\",\"label\":\"DNA mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dna_conc\",\"label\":\"DNA concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total_vol\",\"label\":\"Reaction volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"units_per_ug\",\"label\":\"Enzyme units per µg DNA\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"5–10 is typical\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U/µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"enzyme_conc\",\"label\":\"Enzyme concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"20 U/µL is typical\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"buffer_x\",\"label\":\"Buffer stock\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"CutSmart is 10×\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dna_vol\",\"label\":\"DNA volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"enzyme_vol\",\"label\":\"Enzyme volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"buffer_vol\",\"label\":\"Buffer volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"water_vol\",\"label\":\"Water volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"units\",\"label\":\"Total enzyme units\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"U\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Digest\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"dna_vol\",\"f\":\"dna_ng/dna_conc\"},{\"l\":\"enzyme_vol\",\"f\":\"dna_ng/1000*units_per_ug/enzyme_conc*1000\"},{\"l\":\"buffer_vol\",\"f\":\"total_vol/buffer_x\"},{\"l\":\"water_vol\",\"f\":\"total_vol-dna_ng/dna_conc-dna_ng/1000*units_per_ug/enzyme_conc*1000-total_vol/buffer_x\"},{\"l\":\"units\",\"f\":\"dna_ng/1000*units_per_ug\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/restriction-digest-setup\",\"slug\":\"restriction-digest-setup\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Restriction Digest Reaction Setup\",\"title\":\"Restriction Digest Reaction Setup Calculator — Formula & Resul\",\"desc\":\"A restriction digest is a small reaction with a strict recipe: enzyme units, buffer strength and volume. Free restriction digest reaction setup calculator: en\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-restriction-digest-setup\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dna_ng\",\"l\":\"DNA mass\",\"suf\":\"ng\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dna_conc\",\"l\":\"DNA concentration\",\"suf\":\"ng/µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_vol\",\"l\":\"Reaction volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"units_per_ug\",\"l\":\"Enzyme units per µg DNA\",\"h\":\"5–10 is typical\",\"suf\":\"U/µg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"enzyme_conc\",\"l\":\"Enzyme concentration\",\"h\":\"20 U/µL is typical\",\"suf\":\"U/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"buffer_x\",\"l\":\"Buffer stock\",\"h\":\"CutSmart is 10×\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dna_vol\",\"l\":\"DNA volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"enzyme_vol\",\"l\":\"Enzyme volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"buffer_vol\",\"l\":\"Buffer volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"water_vol\",\"l\":\"Water volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"units\",\"l\":\"Total enzyme units\",\"suf\":\"U\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"dna_ng\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"dna_conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"200\",\"c\":null}]},\"total_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"units_per_ug\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"enzyme_conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20000\",\"c\":null}]},\"buffer_x\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"dna_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"enzyme_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"buffer_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"water_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"units\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Digest\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"dna_vol\",\"f\":\"dna_ng/dna_conc\",\"r\":[\"dna_ng\",\"dna_conc\"]},{\"l\":\"enzyme_vol\",\"f\":\"dna_ng/1000*units_per_ug/enzyme_conc*1000\",\"r\":[\"dna_ng\",\"units_per_ug\",\"enzyme_conc\"]},{\"l\":\"buffer_vol\",\"f\":\"total_vol/buffer_x\",\"r\":[\"total_vol\",\"buffer_x\"]},{\"l\":\"water_vol\",\"f\":\"total_vol-dna_ng/dna_conc-dna_ng/1000*units_per_ug/enzyme_conc*1000-total_vol/buffer_x\",\"r\":[\"total_vol\",\"dna_ng\",\"dna_conc\",\"units_per_ug\",\"enzyme_conc\",\"buffer_x\"]},{\"l\":\"units\",\"f\":\"dna_ng/1000*units_per_ug\",\"r\":[\"dna_ng\",\"units_per_ug\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(65, 'pcr-annealing-temperature', 'biology', 'PCR Annealing Temperature Calculator', 'PCR Annealing Temperature Calculator', 'PCR Annealing Temperature Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Annealing temperature is the one PCR variable worth calculating: too low and you get primer dimers and non-specific bands, too high and you get nothing. Free', 'Annealing temperature is the one PCR variable worth calculating: too low and you get primer dimers and non-specific bands, too high and you get nothing. It returns annealing temperature, gradient low, gradient high and tm difference, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator uses the primer pair\'s melting temperatures, the salt and the product length to suggest a starting temperature and a gradient to test. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the PCR Annealing Temperature\",\"html\":\"<p>Ta = min(Tm) − 5 °C; test a gradient of Ta ± 3 °C</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/pcr-annealing-temperature.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: PCR Annealing Temperature Calculator — Annealing temperature against the primers\' melting temperatures.\\\" title=\\\"PCR Annealing Temperature Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Annealing temperature against the primers\' melting temperatures.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Tm</code></td><td>melting temperature of each primer</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>offset</code></td><td>how far below Tm to anneal</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ta</code></td><td>annealing temperature</td><td>°C</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the PCR Annealing Temperature?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: forward primer tm of <strong>60</strong>°C, reverse primer tm of <strong>60</strong>°C, offset below the lower tm of <strong>5</strong>°C, product length of <strong>400</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives annealing temperature of <strong>55</strong>°C, gradient low of <strong>52</strong>°C, gradient high of <strong>58</strong>°C, tm difference of <strong>0</strong>°C, extension time (taq, 1 min per kb) of <strong>24</strong> s, cycling time for 30 cycles of <strong>12</strong> min.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tm gap under 2 °C</td><td><strong>Good pair</strong></td><td>both primers anneal at the same temperature</td></tr><tr><td>2 – 5 °C</td><td><strong>Acceptable</strong></td><td>use the lower Tm and expect reduced efficiency</td></tr><tr><td>above 5 °C</td><td><strong>Poor pair</strong></td><td>redesign; a long product will not amplify efficiently</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Use the lower of the two Tms, and 5 °C below it as a starting point. The first cycles matter more than the rest, so a two-step or touchdown protocol can rescue a difficult pair.</p></li><li><p>Salt matters: Tm rises about 16.6 °C per log unit of monovalent cation concentration, so a primer quoted in 50 mM Na⁺ is not the same in a 1× Q5 buffer.</p></li><li><p>Add 3 °C if the primers are longer than 22 nt or the product is GC-rich; subtract it for AT-rich templates.</p></li><li><p>Two-step PCR (denature, then anneal/extend at 68–72 °C) is becoming standard for high-fidelity polymerases that extend quickly.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Averaging the two Tms instead of using the lower one.</li><li>Using the manufacturer\'s default 60 °C for every pair — it works often enough to hide the pairs it fails.</li><li>Changing several variables at once when a single gradient would identify the problem.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\\\">Oligo Tm</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/pcr-master-mix-calculator\\\">PCR master mix</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-calculator\\\">GC content</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Should annealing temperature be higher or lower than Tm?\",\"a\":\"Lower, usually 3–5 °C below the lower primer\'s Tm.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my product vanish at higher annealing temperatures?\",\"a\":\"Because the primers no longer bind; the temperature is above their Tm in your buffer.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a touchdown PCR?\",\"a\":\"A program that starts 10 °C above the annealing temperature and steps down, which favours the specific product early and prevents primer dimers later.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the PCR Annealing Temperature actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns annealing temperature, gradient low, gradient high and tm difference. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"tm_f\",\"label\":\"Forward primer Tm\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"60\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tm_r\",\"label\":\"Reverse primer Tm\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"60\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"offset\",\"label\":\"Offset below the lower Tm\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"3–5 °C is standard\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"product\",\"label\":\"Product length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"400\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ta\",\"label\":\"Annealing temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gradient_low\",\"label\":\"Gradient low\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gradient_high\",\"label\":\"Gradient high\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tm_gap\",\"label\":\"Tm difference\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"extension_s\",\"label\":\"Extension time (Taq, 1 min per kb)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cycles_time\",\"label\":\"Cycling time for 30 cycles\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Annealing\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"ta\",\"f\":\"min(tm_f,tm_r)-offset\"},{\"l\":\"gradient_low\",\"f\":\"min(tm_f,tm_r)-offset-3\"},{\"l\":\"gradient_high\",\"f\":\"min(tm_f,tm_r)-offset+3\"},{\"l\":\"tm_gap\",\"f\":\"max(tm_f,tm_r)-min(tm_f,tm_r)\"},{\"l\":\"extension_s\",\"f\":\"max(15,product/1000*60)\"},{\"l\":\"cycles_time\",\"f\":\"30*(max(tm_f,tm_r)-min(tm_f,tm_r)+max(15,product/1000*60))/60\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/pcr-annealing-temperature\",\"slug\":\"pcr-annealing-temperature\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"PCR Annealing Temperature Calculator\",\"title\":\"PCR Annealing Temperature Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Annealing temperature is the one PCR variable worth calculating: too low and you get primer dimers and non-specific bands, too high and you get nothing. Free\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-pcr-annealing-temperature\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm_f\",\"l\":\"Forward primer Tm\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm_r\",\"l\":\"Reverse primer Tm\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"offset\",\"l\":\"Offset below the lower Tm\",\"h\":\"3–5 °C is standard\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"product\",\"l\":\"Product length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ta\",\"l\":\"Annealing temperature\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gradient_low\",\"l\":\"Gradient low\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gradient_high\",\"l\":\"Gradient high\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm_gap\",\"l\":\"Tm difference\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"extension_s\",\"l\":\"Extension time (Taq, 1 min per kb)\",\"suf\":\"s\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cycles_time\",\"l\":\"Cycling time for 30 cycles\",\"suf\":\"min\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"tm_f\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"60\",\"c\":null}]},\"tm_r\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"60\",\"c\":null}]},\"offset\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5\",\"c\":null}]},\"product\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"400\",\"c\":null}]},\"ta\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gradient_low\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gradient_high\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tm_gap\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"extension_s\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cycles_time\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Annealing\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"ta\",\"f\":\"min(tm_f,tm_r)-offset\",\"r\":[\"tm_f\",\"tm_r\",\"offset\"]},{\"l\":\"gradient_low\",\"f\":\"min(tm_f,tm_r)-offset-3\",\"r\":[\"tm_f\",\"tm_r\",\"offset\"]},{\"l\":\"gradient_high\",\"f\":\"min(tm_f,tm_r)-offset+3\",\"r\":[\"tm_f\",\"tm_r\",\"offset\"]},{\"l\":\"tm_gap\",\"f\":\"max(tm_f,tm_r)-min(tm_f,tm_r)\",\"r\":[\"tm_f\",\"tm_r\"]},{\"l\":\"extension_s\",\"f\":\"max(15,product/1000*60)\",\"r\":[\"product\"]},{\"l\":\"cycles_time\",\"f\":\"30*(max(tm_f,tm_r)-min(tm_f,tm_r)+max(15,product/1000*60))/60\",\"r\":[\"tm_f\",\"tm_r\",\"product\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'thermometer', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(66, 'oligo-tm-calculator', 'biology', 'Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) Calculator', 'Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) Calculator', 'Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Melting temperature is where half the oligo is unpaired, and it decides annealing temperatures, hybridisation conditions and whether a primer pair will work t', 'Melting temperature is where half the oligo is unpaired, and it decides annealing temperatures, hybridisation conditions and whether a primer pair will work together. It returns GC content, tm, Wallace, tm, GC rule and tm, GC rule with salt correction, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator offers the simple Wallace rule, the GC-based rule and the nearest-neighbour model that real protocols use. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature)\",\"html\":\"<p>Wallace: Tm = 2(A+T) + 4(G+C);  GC rule: Tm = 64.9 + 41(G+C − 16.4)/N;  salt correction adds 16.6·log₁₀[Na⁺]</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/oligo-tm-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) Calculator — Three models, one oligo, a spread worth knowing about.\\\" title=\\\"Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Three models, one oligo, a spread worth knowing about.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A, T, G, C</code></td><td>base counts</td><td>nt</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>oligo length</td><td>nt</td></tr><tr><td><code>[Na⁺]</code></td><td>monovalent salt concentration</td><td>M</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: oligo length of <strong>20</strong> nt, number of g and c bases of <strong>11</strong>, monovalent salt of <strong>50</strong> mM.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives gc content of <strong>55</strong>%, tm, wallace of <strong>62</strong>°C, tm, gc rule of <strong>53.83</strong>°C, tm, gc rule with salt correction of <strong>32.23</strong>°C.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The Wallace rule is only valid for oligos of 14 nt or shorter; for longer primers it overestimates Tm badly.</p></li><li><p>The nearest-neighbour model is the one to trust: it accounts for stacking interactions between adjacent bases, which is why GC content alone is not enough.</p></li><li><p>The salt correction is large: going from 50 mM to 1 M Na⁺ adds about 16 °C to Tm.</p></li><li><p>The value shown here is the GC-based estimate with a salt correction; treat it as a strong starting point and confirm with a gradient.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using the Wallace rule for a 25-mer.</li><li>Comparing Tm values computed by different methods and expecting them to agree.</li><li>Ignoring the magnesium concentration, which raises Tm further.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/pcr-annealing-temperature\\\">Annealing temperature</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-calculator\\\">GC content</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/primer-resuspension-working-stock\\\">Primer resuspension</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Which Tm method should I use?\",\"a\":\"Nearest-neighbour for anything you will actually order or anneal; Wallace for quick estimates of short oligos.\"},{\"q\":\"Does Tm depend on concentration?\",\"a\":\"Yes — higher oligo concentration raises Tm, which is why 250 nM is quoted as a standard.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns GC content, tm, Wallace, tm, GC rule and tm, GC rule with salt correction. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Oligo length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nt\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gc\",\"label\":\"Number of G and C bases\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"11\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"salt\",\"label\":\"Monovalent salt\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tm_salt\",\"label\":\"Tm, GC rule with salt correction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tm_wallace\",\"label\":\"Tm, Wallace\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tm_gc\",\"label\":\"Tm, GC rule\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gc_pct\",\"label\":\"GC content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Melting\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"gc_pct\",\"f\":\"gc/length*100\"},{\"l\":\"tm_wallace\",\"f\":\"2*(length-gc)+4*gc\"},{\"l\":\"tm_gc\",\"f\":\"64.9+41*(gc-16.4)/length\"},{\"l\":\"tm_salt\",\"f\":\"64.9+41*(gc-16.4)/length+16.6*log10(salt/1000)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\",\"slug\":\"oligo-tm-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) Calculator\",\"title\":\"Oligo Tm (Melting Temperature) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Melting temperature is where half the oligo is unpaired, and it decides annealing temperatures, hybridisation conditions and whether a primer pair will work t\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-oligo-tm-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Oligo length\",\"suf\":\"nt\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc\",\"l\":\"Number of G and C bases\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"salt\",\"l\":\"Monovalent salt\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm_salt\",\"l\":\"Tm, GC rule with salt correction\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm_wallace\",\"l\":\"Tm, Wallace\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm_gc\",\"l\":\"Tm, GC rule\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc_pct\",\"l\":\"GC content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"gc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"11\",\"c\":null}]},\"salt\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"tm_salt\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tm_wallace\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tm_gc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gc_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Melting\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"gc_pct\",\"f\":\"gc/length*100\",\"r\":[\"gc\",\"length\"]},{\"l\":\"tm_wallace\",\"f\":\"2*(length-gc)+4*gc\",\"r\":[\"length\",\"gc\"]},{\"l\":\"tm_gc\",\"f\":\"64.9+41*(gc-16.4)/length\",\"r\":[\"gc\",\"length\"]},{\"l\":\"tm_salt\",\"f\":\"64.9+41*(gc-16.4)/length+16.6*log10(salt/1000)\",\"r\":[\"gc\",\"length\",\"salt\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'thermometer', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(67, 'gc-content-calculator', 'biology', 'GC Content Calculator', 'GC Content Calculator', 'GC Content Calculator — Formula & Results', 'GC content is the share of guanine and cytosine in a sequence, and it predicts melting temperature, PCR difficulty, codon bias and even the organism a sequenc', 'GC content is the share of guanine and cytosine in a sequence, and it predicts melting temperature, PCR difficulty, codon bias and even the organism a sequence came from. It returns GC content, AT content, sequence length and estimated Tm (GC rule), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator takes counts or a raw sequence and returns GC content, AT content and the expected Tm. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the GC Content\",\"html\":\"<p>GC content (%) = (G + C) ÷ (A + T + G + C) × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/gc-content-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: GC Content Calculator — Each base as a share of the sequence, GC highlighted.\\\" title=\\\"GC Content Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Each base as a share of the sequence, GC highlighted.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>G, C, A, T</code></td><td>base counts</td><td>nt</td></tr><tr><td><code>GC content</code></td><td>share of G and C</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the GC Content?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: guanine (g) of <strong>220</strong>, cytosine (c) of <strong>180</strong>, adenine (a) of <strong>300</strong>, thymine (t) of <strong>300</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives gc content of <strong>40</strong>%, at content of <strong>60</strong>%, sequence length of <strong>1,000</strong> nt, estimated tm (gc rule) of <strong>80.63</strong>°C.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>30 – 50%</td><td><strong>AT-rich</strong></td><td>lower Tm; may need additives or a lower annealing temperature</td></tr><tr><td>50 – 60%</td><td><strong>Typical</strong></td><td>most genomes and most successful primers</td></tr><tr><td>above 60%</td><td><strong>GC-rich</strong></td><td>secondary structure and hairpins; consider DMSO or betaine</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Human DNA averages about 41% GC; bacterial genomes range from 25% to 75%, and the number is often used to identify an organism from a genome assembly.</p></li><li><p>GC-rich templates form secondary structure that blocks polymerase processivity, which is why they need higher denaturation temperatures or cosolvents.</p></li><li><p>For primers, aim for 40–60% GC and check that the 3′ end is not a GC clamp of more than three bases.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Counting Ns or ambiguous bases as AT.</li><li>Computing GC content on the coding strand with a counting error introduced by line breaks.</li><li>Assuming 50% GC means an average primer — a 30-mer and a 15-mer at 50% have very different Tms.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\\\">Oligo Tm</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/pcr-annealing-temperature\\\">Annealing temperature</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dna-molarity-from-mass\\\">DNA molarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good GC content for a primer?\",\"a\":\"40–60%, with a Tm matched to its partner.\"},{\"q\":\"How does GC content affect sequencing?\",\"a\":\"GC-rich regions sequence poorly because of secondary structure and polymerase stalling.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the GC Content actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns GC content, AT content, sequence length and estimated Tm (GC rule). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"g\",\"label\":\"Guanine (G)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"220\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Cytosine (C)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"180\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Adenine (A)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"300\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"t\",\"label\":\"Thymine (T)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"300\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gc\",\"label\":\"GC content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"at\",\"label\":\"AT content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Sequence length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nt\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tm\",\"label\":\"Estimated Tm (GC rule)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Content\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"gc\",\"f\":\"(g+c)/(g+c+a+t)*100\"},{\"l\":\"at\",\"f\":\"(a+t)/(g+c+a+t)*100\"},{\"l\":\"length\",\"f\":\"g+c+a+t\"},{\"l\":\"tm\",\"f\":\"64.9+41*((g+c)-16.4)/(g+c+a+t)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/gc-content-calculator\",\"slug\":\"gc-content-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"GC Content Calculator\",\"title\":\"GC Content Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"GC content is the share of guanine and cytosine in a sequence, and it predicts melting temperature, PCR difficulty, codon bias and even the organism a sequenc\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-gc-content-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"g\",\"l\":\"Guanine (G)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"c\",\"l\":\"Cytosine (C)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a\",\"l\":\"Adenine (A)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t\",\"l\":\"Thymine (T)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc\",\"l\":\"GC content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"at\",\"l\":\"AT content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Sequence length\",\"suf\":\"nt\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm\",\"l\":\"Estimated Tm (GC rule)\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"g\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"220\",\"c\":null}]},\"c\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"180\",\"c\":null}]},\"a\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"300\",\"c\":null}]},\"t\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"300\",\"c\":null}]},\"gc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"at\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Content\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"gc\",\"f\":\"(g+c)/(g+c+a+t)*100\",\"r\":[\"g\",\"c\",\"a\",\"t\"]},{\"l\":\"at\",\"f\":\"(a+t)/(g+c+a+t)*100\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"t\",\"g\",\"c\"]},{\"l\":\"length\",\"f\":\"g+c+a+t\",\"r\":[\"g\",\"c\",\"a\",\"t\"]},{\"l\":\"tm\",\"f\":\"64.9+41*((g+c)-16.4)/(g+c+a+t)\",\"r\":[\"g\",\"c\",\"a\",\"t\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(68, 'genome-copy-number', 'biology', 'Genome Copy Number Calculator', 'Genome Copy Number Calculator', 'Genome Copy Number Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The genome copy number in a nanogram of DNA depends only on the genome size. Free genome copy number calculator: enter your values and get genome copies, copi', 'The genome copy number in a nanogram of DNA depends only on the genome size. It returns genome copies, copies per µL in 50 µL, log₁₀ copies and mass of one genome, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe answer depends only on genome size, and it is the number that sets qPCR standard curves, sequencing input and the limit of detection. This calculator converts mass to copies for any genome size. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Genome Copy Number\",\"html\":\"<p>Copies = mass (g) ÷ (genome length × 660 g/mol) × 6.022 × 10²³</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/genome-copy-number.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Genome Copy Number Calculator — Mass, genome length and Avogadro\'s number.\\\" title=\\\"Genome Copy Number Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Mass, genome length and Avogadro\'s number.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>mass</code></td><td>mass of DNA</td><td>ng</td></tr><tr><td><code>genome size</code></td><td>length of one genome</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>copies</code></td><td>number of genomes</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Genome Copy Number?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: dna mass of <strong>10</strong> ng, genome size of <strong>3.2</strong> Mb.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives genome copies of <strong>2,851,391</strong>, copies per µl in 50 µl of <strong>57,027.82</strong> copies/µL, log₁₀ copies of <strong>6.46</strong>, mass of one genome of <strong>3.51</strong> fg.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>A human diploid genome weighs about 6.6 pg; a haploid one 3.3 pg. That is the number to remember: one cell, one copy.</p></li><li><p>For qPCR standards, diluting to 10⁶ copies per µL and then serially tenfold gives a clean seven-point curve.</p></li><li><p>Long-read sequencing needs far more mass per copy because the molecules are not amplified — the copy number, not the mass, is what the flow cell sees.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using genome size in base pairs where the formula expects megabases.</li><li>Forgetting that a diploid cell contains two copies per genome equivalent.</li><li>Treating mitochondrial or plasmid DNA with the nuclear genome size.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/dna-molarity-from-mass\\\">DNA molarity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/od260-nucleic-acid-concentration\\\">OD260 concentration</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/plasmid-copy-number\\\">Plasmid copy number</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many copies of a genome are in 1 ng of human DNA?\",\"a\":\"About 303 copies of the 3.2 Gb genome.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the mass of one copy of the E. coli genome?\",\"a\":\"About 5.0 fg for 4.6 Mb.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Genome Copy Number actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns genome copies, copies per µL in 50 µL, log₁₀ copies and mass of one genome. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"mass_ng\",\"label\":\"DNA mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ng\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"genome_mb\",\"label\":\"Genome size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"3 200 Mb for human, 4.6 Mb for E. coli\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Mb\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"copies\",\"label\":\"Genome copies\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"copies_ul\",\"label\":\"Copies per µL in 50 µL\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"copies/µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"log_copies\",\"label\":\"log₁₀ copies\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mass_per_copy\",\"label\":\"Mass of one genome\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"fg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Copies\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"copies\",\"f\":\"mass_ng*0.000000001/(genome_mb*1000000*660)/1.66054e-24\"},{\"l\":\"copies_ul\",\"f\":\"mass_ng*0.000000001/(genome_mb*1000000*660)/1.66054e-24/50\"},{\"l\":\"log_copies\",\"f\":\"log10(mass_ng*0.000000001/(genome_mb*1000000*660)/1.66054e-24)\"},{\"l\":\"mass_per_copy\",\"f\":\"genome_mb*1000000*660/602200000000000000000000*1000000000000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/genome-copy-number\",\"slug\":\"genome-copy-number\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Genome Copy Number Calculator\",\"title\":\"Genome Copy Number Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The genome copy number in a nanogram of DNA depends only on the genome size. Free genome copy number calculator: enter your values and get genome copies, copi\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-genome-copy-number\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mass_ng\",\"l\":\"DNA mass\",\"suf\":\"ng\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"genome_mb\",\"l\":\"Genome size\",\"h\":\"3 200 Mb for human, 4.6 Mb for E. coli\",\"suf\":\"Mb\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"copies\",\"l\":\"Genome copies\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"copies_ul\",\"l\":\"Copies per µL in 50 µL\",\"suf\":\"copies/µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"log_copies\",\"l\":\"log₁₀ copies\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mass_per_copy\",\"l\":\"Mass of one genome\",\"suf\":\"fg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"mass_ng\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"genome_mb\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"copies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"copies_ul\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"log_copies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mass_per_copy\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Copies\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"copies\",\"f\":\"mass_ng*0.000000001/(genome_mb*1000000*660)/1.66054e-24\",\"r\":[\"mass_ng\",\"genome_mb\"]},{\"l\":\"copies_ul\",\"f\":\"mass_ng*0.000000001/(genome_mb*1000000*660)/1.66054e-24/50\",\"r\":[\"mass_ng\",\"genome_mb\"]},{\"l\":\"log_copies\",\"f\":\"log10(mass_ng*0.000000001/(genome_mb*1000000*660)/1.66054e-24)\",\"r\":[\"mass_ng\",\"genome_mb\"]},{\"l\":\"mass_per_copy\",\"f\":\"genome_mb*1000000*660/602200000000000000000000*1000000000000000\",\"r\":[\"genome_mb\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(69, 'plasmid-copy-number', 'biology', 'Plasmid Copy Number Calculator', 'Plasmid Copy Number Calculator', 'Plasmid Copy Number Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Copy number is how many plasmids each cell carries, and it decides how much a culture yields and how strongly a gene is expressed. Free plasmid copy number ca', 'Copy number is how many plasmids each cell carries, and it decides how much a culture yields and how strongly a gene is expressed. It returns cells harvested, plasmid recovered and plasmids per cell, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator derives it from a miniprep yield, the plasmid size and the number of cells harvested. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Plasmid Copy Number\",\"html\":\"<p>Copy number = plasmids recovered ÷ cells harvested</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/plasmid-copy-number.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Plasmid Copy Number Calculator — Yield per cell decides the copy number class.\\\" title=\\\"Plasmid Copy Number Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Yield per cell decides the copy number class.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>yield</code></td><td>plasmid mass from the prep</td><td>µg</td></tr><tr><td><code>plasmid size</code></td><td>length in kb</td><td>kb</td></tr><tr><td><code>cells</code></td><td>culture volume × OD600 × 8×10⁸</td><td>cells</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Plasmid Copy Number?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: plasmid yield of <strong>8</strong> µg, plasmid size of <strong>5</strong> kb, culture volume of <strong>3</strong> mL, culture od600 of <strong>2</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives cells harvested of <strong>4,800,000,000</strong>, plasmid recovered of <strong>2.42</strong> pmol, plasmids per cell of <strong>304.14</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 – 20</td><td><strong>Low copy</strong></td><td>pBR322-style, stringent replication</td></tr><tr><td>20 – 100</td><td><strong>Medium copy</strong></td><td>pUC relatives with a weakened origin</td></tr><tr><td>above 100</td><td><strong>High copy</strong></td><td>pUC19 and pET-style plasmids, relaxed replication</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Culture OD600 × 8 × 10⁸ cells/mL is a working estimate for E. coli; the answer moves with the strain and the medium, so compare copy numbers measured the same way.</p></li><li><p>Copy number rises with chloramphenicol amplification in relaxed plasmids and falls with large inserts, which are a replication burden.</p></li><li><p>A miniprep yield is not a purity measurement: contaminating genomic DNA and RNA inflate the apparent copy number.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Forgetting that the harvested culture volume sets the cell number.</li><li>Using the insert size instead of the whole plasmid length.</li><li>Comparing a maxiprep yield per litre with a miniprep yield per millilitre.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/genome-copy-number\\\">Genome copy number</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/transformation-efficiency\\\">Transformation efficiency</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dna-molarity-from-mass\\\">DNA molarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal plasmid copy number?\",\"a\":\"About 15–20 for pBR322 and 500–700 for pUC-based high-copy plasmids.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my measured copy number low?\",\"a\":\"Large inserts, a low-copy origin, or growth at 37 °C to stationary phase, which reduces plasmid yield per cell.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Plasmid Copy Number actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns cells harvested, plasmid recovered and plasmids per cell. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"yield_ug\",\"label\":\"Plasmid yield\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From a miniprep of known culture volume\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"plasmid_kb\",\"label\":\"Plasmid size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kb\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"culture_ml\",\"label\":\"Culture volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"od\",\"label\":\"Culture OD600\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"At harvest\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"copy_number\",\"label\":\"Plasmids per cell\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cells_total\",\"label\":\"Cells harvested\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"plasmid_pmol\",\"label\":\"Plasmid recovered\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Copy number\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cells_total\",\"f\":\"culture_ml*od*800000000\"},{\"l\":\"plasmid_pmol\",\"f\":\"yield_ug*1000000/(plasmid_kb*1000*660)\"},{\"l\":\"copy_number\",\"f\":\"yield_ug*0.000001/(plasmid_kb*1000*660)*602200000000000000000000/(culture_ml*od*800000000)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/plasmid-copy-number\",\"slug\":\"plasmid-copy-number\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Plasmid Copy Number Calculator\",\"title\":\"Plasmid Copy Number Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Copy number is how many plasmids each cell carries, and it decides how much a culture yields and how strongly a gene is expressed. Free plasmid copy number ca\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-plasmid-copy-number\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"yield_ug\",\"l\":\"Plasmid yield\",\"h\":\"From a miniprep of known culture volume\",\"suf\":\"µg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"plasmid_kb\",\"l\":\"Plasmid size\",\"suf\":\"kb\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"culture_ml\",\"l\":\"Culture volume\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od\",\"l\":\"Culture OD600\",\"h\":\"At harvest\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"copy_number\",\"l\":\"Plasmids per cell\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells_total\",\"l\":\"Cells harvested\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"plasmid_pmol\",\"l\":\"Plasmid recovered\",\"suf\":\"pmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"yield_ug\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8\",\"c\":null}]},\"plasmid_kb\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5\",\"c\":null}]},\"culture_ml\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3\",\"c\":null}]},\"od\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"copy_number\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cells_total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"plasmid_pmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Copy number\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cells_total\",\"f\":\"culture_ml*od*800000000\",\"r\":[\"culture_ml\",\"od\"]},{\"l\":\"plasmid_pmol\",\"f\":\"yield_ug*1000000/(plasmid_kb*1000*660)\",\"r\":[\"yield_ug\",\"plasmid_kb\"]},{\"l\":\"copy_number\",\"f\":\"yield_ug*0.000001/(plasmid_kb*1000*660)*602200000000000000000000/(culture_ml*od*800000000)\",\"r\":[\"yield_ug\",\"plasmid_kb\",\"culture_ml\",\"od\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(70, 'bacterial-growth-curve', 'biology', 'Bacterial Growth Curve Calculator (generation time)', 'Bacterial Growth Curve Calculator (generation time)', 'Bacterial Growth Curve (generation time) Calculator', 'Two OD readings taken at two times contain everything needed to describe exponential growth: the specific growth rate, the doubling time and the number of gen', 'Two OD readings taken at two times contain everything needed to describe exponential growth: the specific growth rate, the doubling time and the number of generations. It returns specific growth rate, doubling time, generations elapsed and fold increase, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator extracts them and shows the projected curve. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Bacterial Growth Curve (generation time)\",\"html\":\"<p>µ = (ln OD₂ − ln OD₁) ÷ (t₂ − t₁);  doubling time = ln2 ÷ µ</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/bacterial-growth-curve.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Bacterial Growth Curve Calculator (generation time) — A straight line on a log scale, and the doubling time it implies.\\\" title=\\\"Bacterial Growth Curve Calculator (generation time)\\\"><figcaption>A straight line on a log scale, and the doubling time it implies.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>OD</code></td><td>optical density in the linear range</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>time of the reading</td><td>h</td></tr><tr><td><code>µ</code></td><td>specific growth rate</td><td>h⁻¹</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Bacterial Growth Curve (generation time)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: first od600 of <strong>0.1</strong>, second od600 of <strong>0.8</strong>, first reading time of <strong>0</strong> h, second reading time of <strong>4</strong> h.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives specific growth rate of <strong>0.5199</strong> h⁻¹, doubling time of <strong>80</strong> min, generations elapsed of <strong>3</strong>, fold increase of <strong>8</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 0.05</td><td><strong>Lag</strong></td><td>the culture has not started dividing</td></tr><tr><td>0.1 – 1.0</td><td><strong>Exponential</strong></td><td>the window the maths assumes</td></tr><tr><td>above 1.0</td><td><strong>Stationary approach</strong></td><td>OD is no longer linear with cell number</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The formula assumes exponential growth with no lag and no death. Use readings inside the linear OD range (0.1–1.0), taken during log phase.</p></li><li><p>µ has units of reciprocal time, so a rate of 0.7 h⁻¹ is a doubling time of about an hour.</p></li><li><p>Dilute and read again if your second OD is above 1.0: saturation bends the curve and makes µ look smaller than it is.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using readings that span the lag phase, which gives a nonsense negative rate.</li><li>Mixing OD values from different path lengths or instruments.</li><li>Reporting a doubling time without the medium and temperature it was measured in.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/specific-growth-rate-from-od\\\">Specific growth rate from OD</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/od600-to-cell-density\\\">OD600 to cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-doubling-time\\\">Cell doubling time</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a typical doubling time for E. coli?\",\"a\":\"About 20 minutes at 37 °C in rich medium; 60 minutes or more in minimal medium.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is my growth rate negative?\",\"a\":\"The second OD is lower than the first — the culture is dying, or the readings were entered in the wrong order.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Bacterial Growth Curve (generation time) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns specific growth rate, doubling time, generations elapsed and fold increase. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"od1\",\"label\":\"First OD600\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"od2\",\"label\":\"Second OD600\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"t1\",\"label\":\"First reading time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"t2\",\"label\":\"Second reading time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mu\",\"label\":\"Specific growth rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"doubling\",\"label\":\"Doubling time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"generations\",\"label\":\"Generations elapsed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fold\",\"label\":\"Fold increase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Growth\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"mu\",\"f\":\"(log(od2)-log(od1))/(t2-t1)\"},{\"l\":\"doubling\",\"f\":\"0.6931471805599453/((log(od2)-log(od1))/(t2-t1))*60\"},{\"l\":\"generations\",\"f\":\"(log(od2)-log(od1))/0.6931471805599453\"},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"od2/od1\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\",\"slug\":\"bacterial-growth-curve\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Bacterial Growth Curve Calculator (generation time)\",\"title\":\"Bacterial Growth Curve (generation time) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Two OD readings taken at two times contain everything needed to describe exponential growth: the specific growth rate, the doubling time and the number of gen\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-bacterial-growth-curve\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od1\",\"l\":\"First OD600\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od2\",\"l\":\"Second OD600\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t1\",\"l\":\"First reading time\",\"suf\":\"h\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t2\",\"l\":\"Second reading time\",\"suf\":\"h\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mu\",\"l\":\"Specific growth rate\",\"suf\":\"h⁻¹\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"doubling\",\"l\":\"Doubling time\",\"suf\":\"min\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"generations\",\"l\":\"Generations elapsed\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fold\",\"l\":\"Fold increase\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"od1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.1\",\"c\":null}]},\"od2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.8\",\"c\":null}]},\"t1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0\",\"c\":null}]},\"t2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"mu\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"doubling\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"generations\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fold\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Growth\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"mu\",\"f\":\"(log(od2)-log(od1))/(t2-t1)\",\"r\":[\"od2\",\"od1\",\"t2\",\"t1\"]},{\"l\":\"doubling\",\"f\":\"0.6931471805599453/((log(od2)-log(od1))/(t2-t1))*60\",\"r\":[\"od2\",\"od1\",\"t2\",\"t1\"]},{\"l\":\"generations\",\"f\":\"(log(od2)-log(od1))/0.6931471805599453\",\"r\":[\"od2\",\"od1\"]},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"od2/od1\",\"r\":[\"od2\",\"od1\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'bacteria', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(71, 'specific-growth-rate-from-od', 'biology', 'Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings', 'Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings', 'Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings Calculator', 'The specific growth rate µ is the slope of ln(OD) against time, and it is the standard way to compare growth conditions. Free specific growth rate from od rea', 'The specific growth rate µ is the slope of ln(OD) against time, and it is the standard way to compare growth conditions. It returns specific growth rate, doubling time, generations and cell density at the end, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator computes it from a series of OD readings, with the doubling time and the generation count that follow from it. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings\",\"html\":\"<p>µ = ln(OD_end ÷ OD_start) ÷ Δt</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/specific-growth-rate-from-od.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings — ln(OD) against time: the slope is the growth rate.\\\" title=\\\"Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings\\\"><figcaption>ln(OD) against time: the slope is the growth rate.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>OD</code></td><td>absorbance at 600 nm</td><td>AU</td></tr><tr><td><code>µ</code></td><td>specific growth rate</td><td>h⁻¹</td></tr><tr><td><code>Δt</code></td><td>time between readings</td><td>h</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: od at start of <strong>0.05</strong>, od at end of <strong>0.9</strong>, time between readings of <strong>3</strong> h.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives specific growth rate of <strong>0.9635</strong> h⁻¹, doubling time of <strong>0.7194</strong> h, generations of <strong>4.17</strong>, cell density at the end of <strong>720,000,000</strong> cells/mL.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>µ above 0.6 h⁻¹</td><td><strong>Fast</strong></td><td>rich medium, optimal temperature</td></tr><tr><td>µ 0.3 – 0.6 h⁻¹</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>typical of minimal medium with a good carbon source</td></tr><tr><td>µ below 0.3 h⁻¹</td><td><strong>Slow</strong></td><td>stress, a poor carbon source, or a suboptimal temperature</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Doubling time is ln2/µ — 0.693 divided by the rate — not 1/µ. Mixing the two is the most common error in growth-rate reporting.</p></li><li><p>Take at least three readings across log phase and fit a line rather than using two points; two points hide the moment the culture leaves exponential growth.</p></li><li><p>µ is temperature- and medium-dependent; a Q10-style comparison only makes sense when nothing else changed.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using OD values below 0.05, where the blank error dominates.</li><li>Fitting a line through lag, log and stationary points together.</li><li>Reporting 1/µ as the doubling time.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\\\">Bacterial growth curve</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/od600-to-cell-density\\\">OD600 to cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-doubling-time\\\">Cell doubling time</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the difference between µ and the doubling time?\",\"a\":\"µ is the rate of exponential increase per unit time; doubling time is the time to double, ln2/µ.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use this for mammalian cells?\",\"a\":\"Yes, provided the readings are in the linear range or replaced by cell counts.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns specific growth rate, doubling time, generations and cell density at the end. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"od_start\",\"label\":\"OD at start\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.05\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"od_end\",\"label\":\"OD at end\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.9\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"hours\",\"label\":\"Time between readings\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mu\",\"label\":\"Specific growth rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h⁻¹\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"doubling_h\",\"label\":\"Doubling time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"generations\",\"label\":\"Generations\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cells_end\",\"label\":\"Cell density at the end\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cells/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Rate\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"mu\",\"f\":\"(log(od_end)-log(od_start))/hours\"},{\"l\":\"doubling_h\",\"f\":\"0.6931471805599453/((log(od_end)-log(od_start))/hours)\"},{\"l\":\"generations\",\"f\":\"(log(od_end)-log(od_start))/0.6931471805599453\"},{\"l\":\"cells_end\",\"f\":\"od_end*800000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/specific-growth-rate-from-od\",\"slug\":\"specific-growth-rate-from-od\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings\",\"title\":\"Specific Growth Rate from OD Readings Calculator\",\"desc\":\"The specific growth rate µ is the slope of ln(OD) against time, and it is the standard way to compare growth conditions. Free specific growth rate from od rea\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-specific-growth-rate-from-od\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od_start\",\"l\":\"OD at start\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"od_end\",\"l\":\"OD at end\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hours\",\"l\":\"Time between readings\",\"suf\":\"h\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mu\",\"l\":\"Specific growth rate\",\"suf\":\"h⁻¹\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"doubling_h\",\"l\":\"Doubling time\",\"suf\":\"h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"generations\",\"l\":\"Generations\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cells_end\",\"l\":\"Cell density at the end\",\"suf\":\"cells/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"od_start\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.05\",\"c\":null}]},\"od_end\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.9\",\"c\":null}]},\"hours\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3\",\"c\":null}]},\"mu\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"doubling_h\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"generations\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cells_end\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Rate\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"mu\",\"f\":\"(log(od_end)-log(od_start))/hours\",\"r\":[\"od_end\",\"od_start\",\"hours\"]},{\"l\":\"doubling_h\",\"f\":\"0.6931471805599453/((log(od_end)-log(od_start))/hours)\",\"r\":[\"od_end\",\"od_start\",\"hours\"]},{\"l\":\"generations\",\"f\":\"(log(od_end)-log(od_start))/0.6931471805599453\",\"r\":[\"od_end\",\"od_start\"]},{\"l\":\"cells_end\",\"f\":\"od_end*800000000\",\"r\":[\"od_end\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'bacteria', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(72, 'agarose-gel-calculator', 'biology', 'Agarose Gel Calculator (gel percentage and loading)', 'Agarose Gel Calculator (gel percentage and loading)', 'Agarose Gel (gel percentage and loading) Calculator', 'The agarose percentage decides which fragment sizes resolve, and the number of lanes decides how much gel and buffer you need. Free agarose gel (gel percentag', 'The agarose percentage decides which fragment sizes resolve, and the number of lanes decides how much gel and buffer you need. It returns agarose to weigh out, loading dye per sample, running buffer and total volume needed, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives the grams of agarose, the buffer volume, the loading dye and the ladder volume for your gel. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Agarose Gel (gel percentage and loading)\",\"html\":\"<p>Agarose (g) = % × gel volume (mL) ÷ 100;  dye volume = sample volume ÷ dye fold</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/agarose-gel-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Agarose Gel Calculator (gel percentage and loading) — Agarose, buffer, dye and sample for one gel.\\\" title=\\\"Agarose Gel Calculator (gel percentage and loading)\\\"><figcaption>Agarose, buffer, dye and sample for one gel.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>percent</code></td><td>agarose concentration</td><td>% w/v</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume</code></td><td>gel volume</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>agarose</code></td><td>mass to weigh</td><td>g</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Agarose Gel (gel percentage and loading)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: agarose percentage of <strong>1</strong>%, gel volume of <strong>100</strong> mL, number of lanes of <strong>16</strong>, sample volume per lane of <strong>10</strong> µL, loading dye stock of <strong>6</strong> ×.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives agarose to weigh out of <strong>1</strong> g, loading dye per sample of <strong>1.67</strong> µL, running buffer of <strong>500</strong> mL, total volume needed of <strong>160</strong> µL.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.5 – 0.8%</td><td><strong>Large fragments</strong></td><td>1–20 kb; resolves genomic digests</td></tr><tr><td>1 – 1.5%</td><td><strong>Most PCR products</strong></td><td>200 bp – 5 kb</td></tr><tr><td>2 – 3%</td><td><strong>Small fragments</strong></td><td>20–200 bp; needs high-resolution agarose</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Migration is roughly logarithmic in size: a 1% gel resolves 0.5–10 kb well, and every increase in percentage shifts the useful window downward.</p></li><li><p>Use the same buffer for the gel and the tank. TAE gives better resolution on large fragments, TBE better buffering capacity for long runs.</p></li><li><p>Let the gel cool to about 55 °C before pouring — hotter warps the tray, cooler sets it before you pour.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Weighing agarose into a wet flask and forgetting the water already there.</li><li>Overloading lanes so the bands merge into a smear.</li><li>Running at a voltage that heats the gel enough to distort the bands.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/restriction-digest-setup\\\">Restriction digest</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dsdna-ug-to-pmol-converter\\\">dsDNA µg to pmol</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What percentage gel do I need?\",\"a\":\"1% for most PCR products, 2% for fragments under 300 bp, 0.7% for genomic digests.\"},{\"q\":\"How much dye do I add to a 10 µL sample with 6× dye?\",\"a\":\"About 1.7 µL, or adjust the sample so the final volume is convenient.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Agarose Gel (gel percentage and loading) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns agarose to weigh out, loading dye per sample, running buffer and total volume needed. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"percent\",\"label\":\"Agarose percentage\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"1% for 0.5–10 kb\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Gel volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Depends on the tray\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"lanes\",\"label\":\"Number of lanes\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"16\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"sample_vol\",\"label\":\"Sample volume per lane\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dye_x\",\"label\":\"Loading dye stock\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"6× is standard\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"agarose_g\",\"label\":\"Agarose to weigh out\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"dye_vol\",\"label\":\"Loading dye per sample\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"buffer_vol\",\"label\":\"Running buffer\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_sample\",\"label\":\"Total volume needed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Gel\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"agarose_g\",\"f\":\"volume*percent/100\"},{\"l\":\"dye_vol\",\"f\":\"sample_vol/dye_x\"},{\"l\":\"buffer_vol\",\"f\":\"volume*5\"},{\"l\":\"total_sample\",\"f\":\"lanes*sample_vol\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/agarose-gel-calculator\",\"slug\":\"agarose-gel-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Agarose Gel Calculator (gel percentage and loading)\",\"title\":\"Agarose Gel (gel percentage and loading) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"The agarose percentage decides which fragment sizes resolve, and the number of lanes decides how much gel and buffer you need. Free agarose gel (gel percentag\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-agarose-gel-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"percent\",\"l\":\"Agarose percentage\",\"h\":\"1% for 0.5–10 kb\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Gel volume\",\"h\":\"Depends on the tray\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"lanes\",\"l\":\"Number of lanes\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sample_vol\",\"l\":\"Sample volume per lane\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dye_x\",\"l\":\"Loading dye stock\",\"h\":\"6× is standard\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"agarose_g\",\"l\":\"Agarose to weigh out\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dye_vol\",\"l\":\"Loading dye per sample\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"buffer_vol\",\"l\":\"Running buffer\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_sample\",\"l\":\"Total volume needed\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"percent\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"lanes\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"16\",\"c\":null}]},\"sample_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"dye_x\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"agarose_g\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"dye_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"buffer_vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_sample\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Gel\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"agarose_g\",\"f\":\"volume*percent/100\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"percent\"]},{\"l\":\"dye_vol\",\"f\":\"sample_vol/dye_x\",\"r\":[\"sample_vol\",\"dye_x\"]},{\"l\":\"buffer_vol\",\"f\":\"volume*5\",\"r\":[\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"total_sample\",\"f\":\"lanes*sample_vol\",\"r\":[\"lanes\",\"sample_vol\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'gel', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(73, 'buffer-preparation-calculator', 'biology', 'Buffer Preparation Calculator', 'Buffer Preparation Calculator', 'Buffer Preparation Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A buffer is a weak acid and its conjugate base, and the ratio between them sets the pH. Free buffer preparation calculator: enter your values and get pKa used', 'A buffer is a weak acid and its conjugate base, and the ratio between them sets the pH. It returns pKa used, base : acid ratio, fraction as base and lower end of the useful pH range, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe Henderson–Hasselbalch equation turns a target pH into a ratio, and this calculator turns the ratio into the volumes or masses you pipette. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Buffer Preparation\",\"html\":\"<p>pH = pKa + log₁₀([A⁻] ÷ [HA]),  so  [A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/buffer-preparation-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Buffer Preparation Calculator — The base-to-acid ratio that produces the target pH.\\\" title=\\\"Buffer Preparation Calculator\\\"><figcaption>The base-to-acid ratio that produces the target pH.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>pKa</code></td><td>acid dissociation constant of the buffer</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>pH</code></td><td>target pH</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>ratio</code></td><td>base to acid</td><td>:1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Buffer Preparation?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: buffer system set to <strong>HEPES (pKa 7.55)</strong>, use my own pka instead (temperature-corrected, another buffer) of <strong>0</strong>, my pka of <strong>7.2</strong>, target ph of <strong>7.4</strong>, buffer concentration of <strong>0.05</strong> M, final volume of <strong>1</strong> L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives pka used of <strong>7.55</strong>, base : acid ratio of <strong>0.7079</strong> :1, fraction as base of <strong>41.45</strong>%, lower end of the useful ph range of <strong>6.55</strong>, buffer substance in the batch of <strong>50</strong> mmol, basic form needed of <strong>20.73</strong> mmol, acidic form needed of <strong>29.27</strong> mmol.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>A buffer works within about one pH unit of its pKa; outside that the ratio runs away from 1:1 and the buffering capacity collapses.</p></li><li><p>Tris has a large temperature coefficient: a solution at pH 8.06 at 25 °C reads about 7.7 at 4 °C, so pH it at the temperature you will use it.</p></li><li><p>Prepare a concentrated stock and dilute — weighing 0.05 mol into a litre is imprecise, and the pH will need adjusting anyway.</p></li><li><p>Phosphate buffers precipitate divalent cations; if your assay needs Mg²⁺, use HEPES or Tris.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Choosing a buffer whose pKa is two units from the target pH.</li><li>Adjusting pH with strong acid without noting the final volume, which changes concentration.</li><li>Assuming pH paper is precise enough for an enzyme assay.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/chemistry/ph\\\">pH calculator</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/molarity\\\">Molarity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\\\">Osmolarity and osmotic pressure</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Which buffer should I use for pH 7.4?\",\"a\":\"Phosphate or HEPES — both have pKa values within half a unit of 7.4. HEPES is preferred when metal ions are involved.\"},{\"q\":\"Does temperature matter?\",\"a\":\"For Tris, strongly. For phosphate and HEPES, much less.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Buffer Preparation actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns pKa used, base : acid ratio, fraction as base and lower end of the useful pH range. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"acid\",\"label\":\"Buffer system\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"hepes-pka-7-55\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"acetate-pka-4-76\",\"Acetate (pKa 4.76)\",\"4.76\"],[\"mes-pka-6-10\",\"MES (pKa 6.10)\",\"6.10\"],[\"phosphate-pka-7-20\",\"Phosphate (pKa 7.20)\",\"7.20\"],[\"hepes-pka-7-55\",\"HEPES (pKa 7.55)\",\"7.55\"],[\"tris-pka-8-06-at-25-c\",\"Tris (pKa 8.06 at 25 °C)\",\"8.06\"],[\"bicarbonate-pka-6-35\",\"Bicarbonate (pKa 6.35)\",\"6.35\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"custom\",\"label\":\"Use my own pKa instead (temperature-corrected, another buffer)\",\"type\":\"checkbox\",\"default\":\"0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"pka\",\"label\":\"My pKa\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"7.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Only used when the box above is ticked\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ph\",\"label\":\"Target pH\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"7.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Buffer concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.05\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"M\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Final volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Base : acid ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"base_frac\",\"label\":\"Fraction as base\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pka_used\",\"label\":\"pKa used\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Working pH range starts here minus 1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"headroom\",\"label\":\"Lower end of the useful pH range\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_mol\",\"label\":\"Buffer substance in the batch\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"base_mol\",\"label\":\"Basic form needed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"acid_mol\",\"label\":\"Acidic form needed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Buffer\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"pka_used\",\"f\":\"(1-custom)*acid+custom*pka\"},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka))\"},{\"l\":\"base_frac\",\"f\":\"pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka))/(1+pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)))*100\"},{\"l\":\"headroom\",\"f\":\"((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)-1\"},{\"l\":\"total_mol\",\"f\":\"conc*volume*1000\"},{\"l\":\"base_mol\",\"f\":\"conc*volume*1000*pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka))/(1+pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)))\"},{\"l\":\"acid_mol\",\"f\":\"conc*volume*1000/(1+pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/buffer-preparation-calculator\",\"slug\":\"buffer-preparation-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Buffer Preparation Calculator\",\"title\":\"Buffer Preparation Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A buffer is a weak acid and its conjugate base, and the ratio between them sets the pH. Free buffer preparation calculator: enter your values and get pKa used\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-buffer-preparation-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"acid\",\"l\":\"Buffer system\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-acid\",\"opts\":[[\"acetate-pka-4-76\",\"Acetate (pKa 4.76)\",\"4.76\"],[\"mes-pka-6-10\",\"MES (pKa 6.10)\",\"6.10\"],[\"phosphate-pka-7-20\",\"Phosphate (pKa 7.20)\",\"7.20\"],[\"hepes-pka-7-55\",\"HEPES (pKa 7.55)\",\"7.55\"],[\"tris-pka-8-06-at-25-c\",\"Tris (pKa 8.06 at 25 °C)\",\"8.06\"],[\"bicarbonate-pka-6-35\",\"Bicarbonate (pKa 6.35)\",\"6.35\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"custom\",\"l\":\"Use my own pKa instead (temperature-corrected, another buffer)\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-custom\",\"opts\":[[\"yes\",\"Yes\",\"1\"],[\"no\",\"No\",\"0\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pka\",\"l\":\"My pKa\",\"h\":\"Only used when the box above is ticked\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ph\",\"l\":\"Target pH\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Buffer concentration\",\"suf\":\"M\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Final volume\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Base : acid ratio\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"base_frac\",\"l\":\"Fraction as base\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pka_used\",\"l\":\"pKa used\",\"h\":\"Working pH range starts here minus 1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"headroom\",\"l\":\"Lower end of the useful pH range\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_mol\",\"l\":\"Buffer substance in the batch\",\"suf\":\"mmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"base_mol\",\"l\":\"Basic form needed\",\"suf\":\"mmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"acid_mol\",\"l\":\"Acidic form needed\",\"suf\":\"mmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"acid\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"hepes-pka-7-55\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-acid\"},\"custom\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"no\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-custom\"},\"pka\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"7.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"ph\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"7.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.05\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"base_frac\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pka_used\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"headroom\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_mol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"base_mol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"acid_mol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-acid\":{\"name\":\"Buffer system\",\"uiType\":\"SELECT\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"acetate-pka-4-76\",\"label\":\"Acetate (pKa 4.76)\",\"value\":\"4.76\"},{\"uid\":\"mes-pka-6-10\",\"label\":\"MES (pKa 6.10)\",\"value\":\"6.10\"},{\"uid\":\"phosphate-pka-7-20\",\"label\":\"Phosphate (pKa 7.20)\",\"value\":\"7.20\"},{\"uid\":\"hepes-pka-7-55\",\"label\":\"HEPES (pKa 7.55)\",\"value\":\"7.55\"},{\"uid\":\"tris-pka-8-06-at-25-c\",\"label\":\"Tris (pKa 8.06 at 25 °C)\",\"value\":\"8.06\"},{\"uid\":\"bicarbonate-pka-6-35\",\"label\":\"Bicarbonate (pKa 6.35)\",\"value\":\"6.35\"}]},\"ds-custom\":{\"name\":\"Use my own pKa instead (temperature-corrected, another buffer)\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"yes\",\"label\":\"Yes\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"no\",\"label\":\"No\",\"value\":\"0\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Buffer\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"pka_used\",\"f\":\"(1-custom)*acid+custom*pka\",\"r\":[\"custom\",\"acid\",\"pka\"]},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka))\",\"r\":[\"ph\",\"custom\",\"acid\",\"pka\"]},{\"l\":\"base_frac\",\"f\":\"pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka))/(1+pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)))*100\",\"r\":[\"ph\",\"custom\",\"acid\",\"pka\"]},{\"l\":\"headroom\",\"f\":\"((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)-1\",\"r\":[\"custom\",\"acid\",\"pka\"]},{\"l\":\"total_mol\",\"f\":\"conc*volume*1000\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"base_mol\",\"f\":\"conc*volume*1000*pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka))/(1+pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)))\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"volume\",\"ph\",\"custom\",\"acid\",\"pka\"]},{\"l\":\"acid_mol\",\"f\":\"conc*volume*1000/(1+pow(10,ph-((1-custom)*acid+custom*pka)))\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"volume\",\"ph\",\"custom\",\"acid\",\"pka\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:43', '2026-10-29 14:08:43'),
(74, 'microscope-magnification', 'biology', 'Microscope Magnification Calculator', 'Microscope Magnification Calculator', 'Microscope Magnification Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Total magnification is the objective times the eyepiece, but what people usually need is the reverse: how big the object actually is. Free microscope magnific', 'Total magnification is the objective times the eyepiece, but what people usually need is the reverse: how big the object actually is. It returns total magnification, field of view and actual specimen size, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a measured image size into a real size, and a real size into the field of view. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Microscope Magnification\",\"html\":\"<p>Total magnification = objective × eyepiece;  field of view = field number ÷ objective</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/microscope-magnification.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Microscope Magnification Calculator — Objective, eyepiece, and the size of the real object.\\\" title=\\\"Microscope Magnification Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Objective, eyepiece, and the size of the real object.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>objective</code></td><td>objective lens magnification</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>eyepiece</code></td><td>ocular magnification</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>field number</code></td><td>the eyepiece\'s field-of-view diameter in mm</td><td>mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Microscope Magnification?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: objective magnification of <strong>40</strong> ×, eyepiece magnification of <strong>10</strong> ×, field number of <strong>20</strong>, measured image size of <strong>25</strong> mm.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives total magnification of <strong>400</strong> ×, field of view of <strong>0.5</strong> mm, actual specimen size of <strong>62.5</strong> µm.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Resolution, not magnification, is the limit: 0.2 µm is about the best a light microscope can do, and empty magnification beyond about 1000× adds nothing.</p></li><li><p>Use a stage micrometer to calibrate a camera or an eyepiece graticule — the printed magnification of a screen image is almost never the optical one.</p></li><li><p>Field of view shrinks in proportion to the objective magnification: a 40× objective on a 20 mm field number sees 0.5 mm.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Multiplying by the camera\'s digital zoom and calling it magnification.</li><li>Measuring an image printed at a different size than assumed.</li><li>Confusing field of view (diameter) with field area.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/microscope-magnification\\\">Microscope magnification</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/mitotic-index\\\">Mitotic index</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the magnification of a 40× objective with a 10× eyepiece?\",\"a\":\"400×.\"},{\"q\":\"How do I measure a cell from a photograph?\",\"a\":\"Calibrate with a stage micrometer, then measure pixels and convert; the optical magnification alone does not tell you the scale of a resized image.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Microscope Magnification actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns total magnification, field of view and actual specimen size. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"objective\",\"label\":\"Objective magnification\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"eyepiece\",\"label\":\"Eyepiece magnification\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"field_number\",\"label\":\"Field number\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Eyepiece field number, usually 18–22\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"measured\",\"label\":\"Measured image size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"On the printed or on-screen image\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total magnification\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fov\",\"label\":\"Field of view\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"actual\",\"label\":\"Actual specimen size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Magnification\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"objective*eyepiece\"},{\"l\":\"fov\",\"f\":\"field_number/objective\"},{\"l\":\"actual\",\"f\":\"measured/(objective*eyepiece)*1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/microscope-magnification\",\"slug\":\"microscope-magnification\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Microscope Magnification Calculator\",\"title\":\"Microscope Magnification Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Total magnification is the objective times the eyepiece, but what people usually need is the reverse: how big the object actually is. Free microscope magnific\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-microscope-magnification\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"objective\",\"l\":\"Objective magnification\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"eyepiece\",\"l\":\"Eyepiece magnification\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"field_number\",\"l\":\"Field number\",\"h\":\"Eyepiece field number, usually 18–22\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"measured\",\"l\":\"Measured image size\",\"h\":\"On the printed or on-screen image\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total magnification\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fov\",\"l\":\"Field of view\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"actual\",\"l\":\"Actual specimen size\",\"suf\":\"µm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"objective\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"eyepiece\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"field_number\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"measured\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fov\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"actual\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Magnification\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"objective*eyepiece\",\"r\":[\"objective\",\"eyepiece\"]},{\"l\":\"fov\",\"f\":\"field_number/objective\",\"r\":[\"field_number\",\"objective\"]},{\"l\":\"actual\",\"f\":\"measured/(objective*eyepiece)*1000\",\"r\":[\"measured\",\"objective\",\"eyepiece\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'microscope', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(75, 'hardy-weinberg-recessive-allele', 'biology', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency Calculator', 'If you already know q, the recessive allele\'s frequency, the rest follows directly: p is 1 − q, and the genotype frequencies are p², 2pq and q². Free hardy–we', 'If you already know q, the recessive allele\'s frequency, the rest follows directly: p is 1 − q, and the genotype frequencies are p², 2pq and q². It returns dominant allele frequency (p), AA frequency, aa frequency (carriers) and aa frequency (affected), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator spells out the three genotypes, the carrier rate and the ratio of carriers to affected individuals. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency\",\"html\":\"<p>p = 1 − q;  AA = p²;  Aa = 2pq;  aa = q²</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-allele.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency — Carrier frequency peaks at q = 0.5 and stays high as q falls.\\\" title=\\\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency\\\"><figcaption>Carrier frequency peaks at q = 0.5 and stays high as q falls.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>recessive allele frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>dominant allele frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>2pq</code></td><td>heterozygote frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: recessive allele frequency (q) of <strong>0.2</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives dominant allele frequency (p) of <strong>0.8</strong>, aa frequency of <strong>0.64</strong>, aa frequency (carriers) of <strong>0.32</strong>, aa frequency (affected) of <strong>0.04</strong>, carriers per affected individual of <strong>8</strong> :1, affected share of the population of <strong>4</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The ratio of carriers to affected people is 2p/q, which shows how sharply carriers outnumber patients as q falls.</p></li><li><p>These are expected frequencies for a large randomly mating population, not counts for a particular family.</p></li><li><p>For a sex-linked trait the arithmetic differs: males carry one X, so their frequency of the recessive phenotype equals q rather than q².</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using q² for an X-linked recessive trait in males.</li><li>Rounding p and q so that they no longer sum to exactly 1 before squaring.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote\\\">Hardy–Weinberg from q²</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/autosomal-recessive-risk\\\">Recessive inheritance risk</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Is the carrier frequency 2pq or q?\",\"a\":\"2pq — the heterozygote frequency. q alone is the allele frequency.\"},{\"q\":\"What if q is 0.5?\",\"a\":\"Then p is 0.5 too and the three genotype frequencies are 0.25, 0.5 and 0.25.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns dominant allele frequency (p), AA frequency, aa frequency (carriers) and aa frequency (affected). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"q\",\"label\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Between 0 and 1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"p\",\"label\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"aa\",\"label\":\"AA frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ab\",\"label\":\"Aa frequency (carriers)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"bb\",\"label\":\"aa frequency (affected)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"carrier_ratio\",\"label\":\"Carriers per affected individual\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pct\",\"label\":\"Affected share of the population\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Genotypes\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"p\",\"f\":\"1-q\"},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"(1-q)*(1-q)\"},{\"l\":\"ab\",\"f\":\"2*(1-q)*q\"},{\"l\":\"bb\",\"f\":\"q*q\"},{\"l\":\"carrier_ratio\",\"f\":\"2*(1-q)*q/(q*q)\"},{\"l\":\"pct\",\"f\":\"q*q*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-allele\",\"slug\":\"hardy-weinberg-recessive-allele\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency\",\"title\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Allele Frequency Calculator\",\"desc\":\"If you already know q, the recessive allele\'s frequency, the rest follows directly: p is 1 − q, and the genotype frequencies are p², 2pq and q². Free hardy–we\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-hardy-weinberg-recessive-allele\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"q\",\"l\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"h\":\"Between 0 and 1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p\",\"l\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"aa\",\"l\":\"AA frequency\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ab\",\"l\":\"Aa frequency (carriers)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bb\",\"l\":\"aa frequency (affected)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"carrier_ratio\",\"l\":\"Carriers per affected individual\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pct\",\"l\":\"Affected share of the population\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"q\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"p\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"aa\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ab\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"bb\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"carrier_ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Genotypes\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"p\",\"f\":\"1-q\",\"r\":[\"q\"]},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"(1-q)*(1-q)\",\"r\":[\"q\"]},{\"l\":\"ab\",\"f\":\"2*(1-q)*q\",\"r\":[\"q\"]},{\"l\":\"bb\",\"f\":\"q*q\",\"r\":[\"q\"]},{\"l\":\"carrier_ratio\",\"f\":\"2*(1-q)*q/(q*q)\",\"r\":[\"q\"]},{\"l\":\"pct\",\"f\":\"q*q*100\",\"r\":[\"q\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(76, 'hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote', 'biology', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency Calcula', 'The recessive phenotype frequency is q², so the allele frequency is its square root. Free hardy–weinberg from the recessive homozygote frequency calculator: e', 'The recessive phenotype frequency is q², so the allele frequency is its square root. It returns recessive allele frequency (q), dominant allele frequency (p), heterozygote frequency and homozygous dominant frequency, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator carries it through to every genotype frequency and the carrier rate. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency\",\"html\":\"<p>q = √(q²);  p = 1 − q;  Aa = 2pq</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency — From the visible phenotype back to the allele frequency.\\\" title=\\\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency\\\"><figcaption>From the visible phenotype back to the allele frequency.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>q²</code></td><td>frequency of the recessive phenotype</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>recessive allele frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>2pq</code></td><td>carrier frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: frequency of the recessive phenotype of <strong>0.04</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives recessive allele frequency (q) of <strong>0.2</strong>, dominant allele frequency (p) of <strong>0.8</strong>, heterozygote frequency of <strong>0.32</strong>, homozygous dominant frequency of <strong>0.64</strong>, carriers per affected individual of <strong>8</strong> :1.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>This square root is only valid when the recessive phenotype is fully penetrant and every affected individual really is a homozygote.</p></li><li><p>At low frequencies the square root is numerically sensitive: 0.0001 and 0.0004 give q of 0.01 and 0.02, a doubling, so record the observed frequency with care.</p></li><li><p>For a dominant trait the same arithmetic cannot recover the allele frequency from phenotypes, because homozygotes and heterozygotes look identical.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Taking the square root of a percentage (0.4%) instead of a proportion (0.004).</li><li>Assuming the trait is at equilibrium when it is under strong selection.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-allele\\\">Hardy–Weinberg from q</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-heterozygote\\\">Hardy–Weinberg from 2pq</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is the allele frequency the square root of the affected frequency?\",\"a\":\"Because an affected individual must inherit a recessive allele from both parents, and the two draws are independent: q × q = q².\"},{\"q\":\"What if 4% of a population is affected?\",\"a\":\"q = 0.2, p = 0.8, and 32% of the population are carriers.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns recessive allele frequency (q), dominant allele frequency (p), heterozygote frequency and homozygous dominant frequency. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"affected\",\"label\":\"Frequency of the recessive phenotype\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.04\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"q², as a decimal\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"q\",\"label\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"p\",\"label\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hetero\",\"label\":\"Heterozygote frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"homo_dom\",\"label\":\"Homozygous dominant frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"carriers_per_case\",\"label\":\"Carriers per affected individual\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Alleles\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"q\",\"f\":\"sqrt(affected)\"},{\"l\":\"p\",\"f\":\"1-sqrt(affected)\"},{\"l\":\"hetero\",\"f\":\"2*(1-sqrt(affected))*sqrt(affected)\"},{\"l\":\"homo_dom\",\"f\":\"(1-sqrt(affected))*(1-sqrt(affected))\"},{\"l\":\"carriers_per_case\",\"f\":\"2*(1-sqrt(affected))*sqrt(affected)/affected\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote\",\"slug\":\"hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency\",\"title\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Recessive Homozygote Frequency Calcula\",\"desc\":\"The recessive phenotype frequency is q², so the allele frequency is its square root. Free hardy–weinberg from the recessive homozygote frequency calculator: e\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"affected\",\"l\":\"Frequency of the recessive phenotype\",\"h\":\"q², as a decimal\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"q\",\"l\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p\",\"l\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hetero\",\"l\":\"Heterozygote frequency\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"homo_dom\",\"l\":\"Homozygous dominant frequency\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"carriers_per_case\",\"l\":\"Carriers per affected individual\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"affected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.04\",\"c\":null}]},\"q\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"p\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hetero\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"homo_dom\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"carriers_per_case\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Alleles\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"q\",\"f\":\"sqrt(affected)\",\"r\":[\"affected\"]},{\"l\":\"p\",\"f\":\"1-sqrt(affected)\",\"r\":[\"affected\"]},{\"l\":\"hetero\",\"f\":\"2*(1-sqrt(affected))*sqrt(affected)\",\"r\":[\"affected\"]},{\"l\":\"homo_dom\",\"f\":\"(1-sqrt(affected))*(1-sqrt(affected))\",\"r\":[\"affected\"]},{\"l\":\"carriers_per_case\",\"f\":\"2*(1-sqrt(affected))*sqrt(affected)/affected\",\"r\":[\"affected\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(77, 'hardy-weinberg-heterozygote', 'biology', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency Calculator', 'Heterozygote frequency 2pq is a quadratic in q, so it has two solutions — a fact that bites anyone converting a carrier rate back into an allele frequency. Fr', 'Heterozygote frequency 2pq is a quadratic in q, so it has two solutions — a fact that bites anyone converting a carrier rate back into an allele frequency. It returns q (lower root), q (upper root), p for the lower root and homozygote frequency, lower root, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator solves both branches and reports which one applies to your population. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency\",\"html\":\"<p>2q² − 2q + h = 0,  so  q = [1 ± √(1 − 2h)] ÷ 2</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/hardy-weinberg-heterozygote.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency — 2pq peaks at 0.5 and gives two allele frequencies for every value below it.\\\" title=\\\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency\\\"><figcaption>2pq peaks at 0.5 and gives two allele frequencies for every value below it.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>h</code></td><td>observed heterozygote frequency</td><td>0–0.5</td></tr><tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>recessive allele frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>1 − 2h</code></td><td>the discriminant; negative means no solution</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: heterozygote frequency (2pq) of <strong>0.42</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives q (lower root) of <strong>0.3</strong>, q (upper root) of <strong>0.7</strong>, p for the lower root of <strong>0.7</strong>, homozygote frequency, lower root of <strong>0.58</strong>, maximum possible 2pq of <strong>0.5</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>2pq never exceeds 0.5, so a reported heterozygote frequency above 50% cannot come from a two-allele locus at equilibrium — check the data before trusting the roots.</p></li><li><p>The two roots are mirror images: q and 1 − q give the same heterozygote frequency, so the data alone cannot tell you which allele is common.</p></li><li><p>Real populations often have fewer heterozygotes than 2pq predicts (a Wahlund effect from population structure), which is one reason the observed value may be the more useful input.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Entering 42 for 42% instead of 0.42.</li><li>Taking the lower root without checking which allele is the rare one.</li><li>Using this inversion when the locus has more than two alleles.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote\\\">Hardy–Weinberg from q²</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-dominant-homozygote\\\">Hardy–Weinberg from p²</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Can 2pq be greater than 0.5?\",\"a\":\"No — its maximum is 0.5 at p = q = 0.5. A larger value means the population is not at equilibrium or the data are wrong.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are there two answers?\",\"a\":\"Because 2q(1 − q) is symmetric about q = 0.5; both allele frequencies produce the same heterozygote share.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns q (lower root), q (upper root), p for the lower root and homozygote frequency, lower root. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"hetero\",\"label\":\"Heterozygote frequency (2pq)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.42\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Between 0 and 0.5\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"q_low\",\"label\":\"q (lower root)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"q_high\",\"label\":\"q (upper root)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"p_low\",\"label\":\"p for the lower root\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"homo_low\",\"label\":\"Homozygote frequency, lower root\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"max_note\",\"label\":\"Maximum possible 2pq\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.5\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Roots\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"q_low\",\"f\":\"(1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2\"},{\"l\":\"q_high\",\"f\":\"(1+sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2\"},{\"l\":\"p_low\",\"f\":\"1-(1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2\"},{\"l\":\"homo_low\",\"f\":\"pow(1-(1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2,2)+pow((1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2,2)\"},{\"l\":\"max_note\",\"f\":\"0.5\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-heterozygote\",\"slug\":\"hardy-weinberg-heterozygote\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency\",\"title\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Heterozygote Frequency Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Heterozygote frequency 2pq is a quadratic in q, so it has two solutions — a fact that bites anyone converting a carrier rate back into an allele frequency. Fr\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-hardy-weinberg-heterozygote\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hetero\",\"l\":\"Heterozygote frequency (2pq)\",\"h\":\"Between 0 and 0.5\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"q_low\",\"l\":\"q (lower root)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"q_high\",\"l\":\"q (upper root)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p_low\",\"l\":\"p for the lower root\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"homo_low\",\"l\":\"Homozygote frequency, lower root\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"max_note\",\"l\":\"Maximum possible 2pq\",\"h\":\"0.5\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"hetero\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.42\",\"c\":null}]},\"q_low\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"q_high\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"p_low\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"homo_low\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"max_note\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Roots\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"q_low\",\"f\":\"(1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2\",\"r\":[\"hetero\"]},{\"l\":\"q_high\",\"f\":\"(1+sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2\",\"r\":[\"hetero\"]},{\"l\":\"p_low\",\"f\":\"1-(1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2\",\"r\":[\"hetero\"]},{\"l\":\"homo_low\",\"f\":\"pow(1-(1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2,2)+pow((1-sqrt(1-2*hetero))/2,2)\",\"r\":[\"hetero\"]},{\"l\":\"max_note\",\"f\":\"0.5\",\"r\":[]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(78, 'hardy-weinberg-dominant-homozygote', 'biology', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency Calculat', 'Homozygous dominant frequency is p². Free hardy–weinberg from the dominant homozygote frequency calculator: enter your values and get dominant allele frequenc', 'Homozygous dominant frequency is p². It returns dominant allele frequency (p), recessive allele frequency (q), heterozygote frequency and recessive homozygote frequency, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nUnless you have genotype-level data — sequencing, a codominant marker, a biochemical test — you cannot see this class, so it is usually used the other way round: to predict how many homozygotes a population should contain. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency\",\"html\":\"<p>p = √(p²);  q = 1 − p;  2pq = 2p(1 − p)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/hardy-weinberg-dominant-homozygote.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency — p² back to p, then q, then the heterozygotes.\\\" title=\\\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency\\\"><figcaption>p² back to p, then q, then the heterozygotes.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>p²</code></td><td>homozygous dominant frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>dominant allele frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>recessive allele frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: homozygous dominant frequency (p²) of <strong>0.49</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives dominant allele frequency (p) of <strong>0.7</strong>, recessive allele frequency (q) of <strong>0.3</strong>, heterozygote frequency of <strong>0.42</strong>, recessive homozygote frequency of <strong>0.09</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>This direction needs genotype data, not phenotypes: a dominant phenotype pools AA and Aa and hides the split.</p></li><li><p>Codominant markers such as microsatellites or SNP arrays give p² directly, which is why population studies use them.</p></li><li><p>The square root is exact only at equilibrium; selection and drift both distort it.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using the dominant phenotype frequency as if it were p².</li><li>Forgetting that the three genotype frequencies must sum to 1.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-homozygote\\\">Hardy–Weinberg from q²</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/allele-frequency\\\">Allele frequency</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is p if 49% of a population is AA?\",\"a\":\"p = 0.7, so q = 0.3 and 42% should be heterozygotes.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I get p from a dominant phenotype?\",\"a\":\"Not without extra information — that is why recessive traits are the classical tool for estimating allele frequencies.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns dominant allele frequency (p), recessive allele frequency (q), heterozygote frequency and recessive homozygote frequency. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"homo_dom\",\"label\":\"Homozygous dominant frequency (p²)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.49\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"p\",\"label\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"q\",\"label\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hetero\",\"label\":\"Heterozygote frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"affected\",\"label\":\"Recessive homozygote frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Alleles\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"p\",\"f\":\"sqrt(homo_dom)\"},{\"l\":\"q\",\"f\":\"1-sqrt(homo_dom)\"},{\"l\":\"hetero\",\"f\":\"2*sqrt(homo_dom)*(1-sqrt(homo_dom))\"},{\"l\":\"affected\",\"f\":\"(1-sqrt(homo_dom))*(1-sqrt(homo_dom))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-dominant-homozygote\",\"slug\":\"hardy-weinberg-dominant-homozygote\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency\",\"title\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Homozygote Frequency Calculat\",\"desc\":\"Homozygous dominant frequency is p². Free hardy–weinberg from the dominant homozygote frequency calculator: enter your values and get dominant allele frequenc\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-hardy-weinberg-dominant-homozygote\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"homo_dom\",\"l\":\"Homozygous dominant frequency (p²)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p\",\"l\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"q\",\"l\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hetero\",\"l\":\"Heterozygote frequency\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"affected\",\"l\":\"Recessive homozygote frequency\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"homo_dom\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.49\",\"c\":null}]},\"p\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"q\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hetero\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"affected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Alleles\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"p\",\"f\":\"sqrt(homo_dom)\",\"r\":[\"homo_dom\"]},{\"l\":\"q\",\"f\":\"1-sqrt(homo_dom)\",\"r\":[\"homo_dom\"]},{\"l\":\"hetero\",\"f\":\"2*sqrt(homo_dom)*(1-sqrt(homo_dom))\",\"r\":[\"homo_dom\"]},{\"l\":\"affected\",\"f\":\"(1-sqrt(homo_dom))*(1-sqrt(homo_dom))\",\"r\":[\"homo_dom\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(79, 'hardy-weinberg-dominant-allele', 'biology', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency', 'Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency Calculator', 'Given p, the dominant allele\'s frequency, every genotype frequency follows from p + q = 1. Free hardy–weinberg from the dominant allele frequency calculator:', 'Given p, the dominant allele\'s frequency, every genotype frequency follows from p + q = 1. It returns recessive allele frequency (q), AA individuals expected, aa individuals expected and aa individuals expected, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator is the fastest route from an allele count to the expected genotype table, and it also reports how many individuals of each class a sample of a given size should contain. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency\",\"html\":\"<p>q = 1 − p;  AA = p²n;  Aa = 2pqn;  aa = q²n</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/hardy-weinberg-dominant-allele.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency — Expected counts of the three genotypes in the sample.\\\" title=\\\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency\\\"><figcaption>Expected counts of the three genotypes in the sample.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>dominant allele frequency</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>number of individuals sampled</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>2pqn</code></td><td>expected heterozygote count</td><td>individuals</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: dominant allele frequency (p) of <strong>0.8</strong>, sample size of <strong>500</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives recessive allele frequency (q) of <strong>0.2</strong>, aa individuals expected of <strong>320</strong> individuals, aa individuals expected of <strong>160</strong> individuals, aa individuals expected of <strong>20</strong> individuals, carriers of <strong>32</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Expected counts are rarely whole numbers; do not round them before a chi-square test, because the rounding error accumulates.</p></li><li><p>With small samples, drift alone can move genotype frequencies a long way from p², 2pq, q², so the test has little power.</p></li><li><p>Allele frequencies estimated from a sample carry their own uncertainty, roughly √(p(1−p)/2n) per allele.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Multiplying by a percentage sample size instead of a count.</li><li>Treating the expected heterozygote count as a prediction for one family rather than for a population.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-recessive-allele\\\">Hardy–Weinberg from q</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/allele-frequency\\\">Allele frequency</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I count alleles in a sample of n individuals?\",\"a\":\"There are 2n alleles at an autosomal locus, so p is the number of dominant alleles divided by 2n.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is 2pq multiplied by n?\",\"a\":\"Because 2pq is a frequency; multiplying by the number of individuals turns it into an expected count.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns recessive allele frequency (q), AA individuals expected, aa individuals expected and aa individuals expected. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"p\",\"label\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Sample size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"q\",\"label\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"aa\",\"label\":\"AA individuals expected\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ab\",\"label\":\"Aa individuals expected\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"bb\",\"label\":\"aa individuals expected\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"carrier_pct\",\"label\":\"Carriers\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Genotypes\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"q\",\"f\":\"1-p\"},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"p*p*n\"},{\"l\":\"ab\",\"f\":\"2*p*(1-p)*n\"},{\"l\":\"bb\",\"f\":\"(1-p)*(1-p)*n\"},{\"l\":\"carrier_pct\",\"f\":\"2*p*(1-p)*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-dominant-allele\",\"slug\":\"hardy-weinberg-dominant-allele\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency\",\"title\":\"Hardy–Weinberg from the Dominant Allele Frequency Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Given p, the dominant allele\'s frequency, every genotype frequency follows from p + q = 1. Free hardy–weinberg from the dominant allele frequency calculator:\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-hardy-weinberg-dominant-allele\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p\",\"l\":\"Dominant allele frequency (p)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"Sample size\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"q\",\"l\":\"Recessive allele frequency (q)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"aa\",\"l\":\"AA individuals expected\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ab\",\"l\":\"Aa individuals expected\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bb\",\"l\":\"aa individuals expected\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"carrier_pct\",\"l\":\"Carriers\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"p\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.8\",\"c\":null}]},\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"500\",\"c\":null}]},\"q\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"aa\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ab\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"bb\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"carrier_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Genotypes\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"q\",\"f\":\"1-p\",\"r\":[\"p\"]},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"p*p*n\",\"r\":[\"p\",\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"ab\",\"f\":\"2*p*(1-p)*n\",\"r\":[\"p\",\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"bb\",\"f\":\"(1-p)*(1-p)*n\",\"r\":[\"p\",\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"carrier_pct\",\"f\":\"2*p*(1-p)*100\",\"r\":[\"p\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(80, 'dihybrid-cross', 'biology', 'Dihybrid Cross Calculator (9:3:3:1)', 'Dihybrid Cross Calculator (9:3:3:1)', 'Dihybrid Cross (9:3:3:1) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A dihybrid cross follows two genes at once. Free dihybrid cross (9:3:3:1) calculator: enter your values and get dominant / dominant, dominant / recessive, rec', 'A dihybrid cross follows two genes at once. It returns dominant / dominant, dominant / recessive, recessive / dominant and recessive / recessive, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nWith independent assortment the F2 of a double heterozygote splits 9:3:3:1, and this calculator gives those counts, the phenotype and genotype tables, and the ratio you would expect from a sample of any size. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Dihybrid Cross (9:3:3:1)\",\"html\":\"<p>AaBb × AaBb → 9 A_B_ : 3 A_bb : 3 aaB_ : 1 aabb</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/dihybrid-cross.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Dihybrid Cross Calculator (9:3:3:1) — The 4 × 4 Punnett square behind the 9:3:3:1 ratio.\\\" title=\\\"Dihybrid Cross Calculator (9:3:3:1)\\\"><figcaption>The 4 × 4 Punnett square behind the 9:3:3:1 ratio.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>number of F2 offspring scored</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>:16</code></td><td>sixteen equally likely gamete combinations</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>recombinant</code></td><td>the two mixed phenotypes, 6/16</td><td>share</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Dihybrid Cross (9:3:3:1)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: f2 offspring of <strong>320</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives dominant / dominant of <strong>180</strong> individuals, dominant / recessive of <strong>60</strong> individuals, recessive / dominant of <strong>60</strong> individuals, recessive / recessive of <strong>20</strong> individuals, phenotype classes of <strong>4</strong>, genotype classes of <strong>9</strong>, recombinant phenotypes of <strong>37.5</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The 9:3:3:1 ratio requires the two genes to be on different chromosomes, or far enough apart on the same one to assort independently.</p></li><li><p>Linked genes shift the ratio toward the parental classes; measuring the departure is how recombination frequency is estimated.</p></li><li><p>Genotype rather than phenotype data splits the 9:3:3:1 into nine classes: 1:2:1:2:4:2:1:2:1.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Scoring too few offspring — with 20 plants, the 1/16 class may be absent by chance.</li><li>Pooling data across crosses with different parents.</li><li>Assuming independent assortment for two genes on the same chromosome.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/phenotypic-ratio\\\">Phenotypic ratio</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/chi-square-genetics-test\\\">Chi-square test for genetics</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/recombination-frequency\\\">Recombination frequency</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why 16 combinations?\",\"a\":\"Each parent makes four gamete types, and 4 × 4 = 16 equally likely fertilisations.\"},{\"q\":\"What ratio do I get if the genes are linked?\",\"a\":\"More parental and fewer recombinant classes than 9:3:3:1 — the exact proportions depend on the recombination frequency.\"},{\"q\":\"How many genotypes come out of a dihybrid cross?\",\"a\":\"Nine, from AABB to aabb.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Dihybrid Cross (9:3:3:1) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns dominant / dominant, dominant / recessive, recessive / dominant and recessive / recessive. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"F2 offspring\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"320\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dom_dom\",\"label\":\"Dominant / dominant\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"dom_rec\",\"label\":\"Dominant / recessive\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rec_dom\",\"label\":\"Recessive / dominant\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rec_rec\",\"label\":\"Recessive / recessive\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pheno_classes\",\"label\":\"Phenotype classes\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"4\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"geno_classes\",\"label\":\"Genotype classes\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"9\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"recomb\",\"label\":\"Recombinant phenotypes\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"F2 ratio\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"dom_dom\",\"f\":\"n*9/16\"},{\"l\":\"dom_rec\",\"f\":\"n*3/16\"},{\"l\":\"rec_dom\",\"f\":\"n*3/16\"},{\"l\":\"rec_rec\",\"f\":\"n*1/16\"},{\"l\":\"pheno_classes\",\"f\":\"4\"},{\"l\":\"geno_classes\",\"f\":\"9\"},{\"l\":\"recomb\",\"f\":\"6/16*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/dihybrid-cross\",\"slug\":\"dihybrid-cross\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Dihybrid Cross Calculator (9:3:3:1)\",\"title\":\"Dihybrid Cross (9:3:3:1) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A dihybrid cross follows two genes at once. Free dihybrid cross (9:3:3:1) calculator: enter your values and get dominant / dominant, dominant / recessive, rec\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-dihybrid-cross\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"F2 offspring\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dom_dom\",\"l\":\"Dominant / dominant\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dom_rec\",\"l\":\"Dominant / recessive\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rec_dom\",\"l\":\"Recessive / dominant\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rec_rec\",\"l\":\"Recessive / recessive\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pheno_classes\",\"l\":\"Phenotype classes\",\"h\":\"4\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"geno_classes\",\"l\":\"Genotype classes\",\"h\":\"9\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"recomb\",\"l\":\"Recombinant phenotypes\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"320\",\"c\":null}]},\"dom_dom\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"dom_rec\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rec_dom\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rec_rec\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pheno_classes\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"geno_classes\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"recomb\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"F2 ratio\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"dom_dom\",\"f\":\"n*9/16\",\"r\":[\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"dom_rec\",\"f\":\"n*3/16\",\"r\":[\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"rec_dom\",\"f\":\"n*3/16\",\"r\":[\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"rec_rec\",\"f\":\"n*1/16\",\"r\":[\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"pheno_classes\",\"f\":\"4\",\"r\":[]},{\"l\":\"geno_classes\",\"f\":\"9\",\"r\":[]},{\"l\":\"recomb\",\"f\":\"6/16*100\",\"r\":[]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'grid', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(81, 'phenotypic-ratio', 'biology', 'Phenotypic Ratio Calculator', 'Phenotypic Ratio Calculator', 'Phenotypic Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Phenotypic ratios are what you count on a bench, but the numbers that matter are the proportions they imply. Free phenotypic ratio calculator: enter your valu', 'Phenotypic ratios are what you count on a bench, but the numbers that matter are the proportions they imply. It returns total scored, ratio to the smallest class, class 1 share and chi-square vs 9:3:3:1, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator turns raw counts into a ratio in lowest whole numbers, the percentage of each class and the chi-square test against an expected Mendelian ratio. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Phenotypic Ratio\",\"html\":\"<p>Ratio = each class ÷ the smallest class;  χ² = Σ (O − E)² ÷ E</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/phenotypic-ratio.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Phenotypic Ratio Calculator — Observed classes against the expected ratio.\\\" title=\\\"Phenotypic Ratio Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Observed classes against the expected ratio.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>O</code></td><td>observed count in a class</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>E</code></td><td>expected count from the ratio</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>χ²</code></td><td>test statistic, 3 degrees of freedom for four classes</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Phenotypic Ratio?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: class 1 count of <strong>180</strong>, class 2 count of <strong>62</strong>, class 3 count of <strong>58</strong>, class 4 count of <strong>20</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives total scored of <strong>320</strong>, ratio to the smallest class of <strong>16</strong>, class 1 share of <strong>56.25</strong>%, chi-square vs 9:3:3:1 of <strong>0.1333</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>χ² below 7.81</td><td><strong>Fits 9:3:3:1</strong></td><td>the 5% critical value at 3 df</td></tr><tr><td>7.81 – 11.34</td><td><strong>Marginal</strong></td><td>between the 5% and 1% critical values</td></tr><tr><td>above 11.34</td><td><strong>Does not fit</strong></td><td>linkage, lethality or scoring error</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Ratios are only comparable when every class has the same expected weight; a cross with lethal genotypes will not give whole-number Mendelian ratios.</p></li><li><p>Chi-square assumes independent observations. Counts from the same plant or the same family are not independent, which inflates the statistic.</p></li><li><p>The 5% critical values are 3.84 (1 df), 5.99 (2 df) and 7.81 (3 df).</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Dividing by the largest class instead of the smallest, which gives ratios below 1.</li><li>Comparing a chi-square to a critical value with the wrong number of degrees of freedom.</li><li>Reporting a ratio from fewer than about 100 individuals.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/dihybrid-cross\\\">Dihybrid cross</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/chi-square-genetics-test\\\">Chi-square test for genetics</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/recombination-frequency\\\">Recombination frequency</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I get a ratio like 9:3:3:1 from counts?\",\"a\":\"Divide every count by the smallest, then scale to whole numbers — 180:62:58:20 is about 9:3:3:1.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a large chi-square mean?\",\"a\":\"The observations are unlikely under the expected ratio: look for linkage, lethality or a scoring error.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Phenotypic Ratio actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns total scored, ratio to the smallest class, class 1 share and chi-square vs 9:3:3:1. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Class 1 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"180\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Class 2 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"62\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Class 3 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"58\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Class 4 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total scored\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Ratio to the smallest class\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pct1\",\"label\":\"Class 1 share\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"chi\",\"label\":\"Chi-square vs 9:3:3:1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Ratio\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"a+b+c+d\"},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"(a+b+c+d)/min(min(a,b),min(c,d))\"},{\"l\":\"pct1\",\"f\":\"a/(a+b+c+d)*100\"},{\"l\":\"chi\",\"f\":\"pow(a-(a+b+c+d)*9/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)*9/16)+pow(b-(a+b+c+d)*3/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)*3/16)+pow(c-(a+b+c+d)*3/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)*3/16)+pow(d-(a+b+c+d)/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)/16)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/phenotypic-ratio\",\"slug\":\"phenotypic-ratio\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Phenotypic Ratio Calculator\",\"title\":\"Phenotypic Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Phenotypic ratios are what you count on a bench, but the numbers that matter are the proportions they imply. Free phenotypic ratio calculator: enter your valu\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-phenotypic-ratio\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a\",\"l\":\"Class 1 count\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"b\",\"l\":\"Class 2 count\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"c\",\"l\":\"Class 3 count\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"d\",\"l\":\"Class 4 count\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total scored\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Ratio to the smallest class\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pct1\",\"l\":\"Class 1 share\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"chi\",\"l\":\"Chi-square vs 9:3:3:1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"a\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"180\",\"c\":null}]},\"b\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"62\",\"c\":null}]},\"c\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"58\",\"c\":null}]},\"d\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pct1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"chi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Ratio\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"a+b+c+d\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"]},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"(a+b+c+d)/min(min(a,b),min(c,d))\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"]},{\"l\":\"pct1\",\"f\":\"a/(a+b+c+d)*100\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"]},{\"l\":\"chi\",\"f\":\"pow(a-(a+b+c+d)*9/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)*9/16)+pow(b-(a+b+c+d)*3/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)*3/16)+pow(c-(a+b+c+d)*3/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)*3/16)+pow(d-(a+b+c+d)/16,2)/((a+b+c+d)/16)\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'grid', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(82, 'chi-square-genetics-test', 'biology', 'Chi-Square Test for Genetics', 'Chi-Square Test for Genetics', 'Chi-Square Test for Genetics Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A chi-square goodness-of-fit test answers one question: are these counts close enough to the ratio I expected? This calculator computes the statistic, the deg', 'A chi-square goodness-of-fit test answers one question: are these counts close enough to the ratio I expected? It returns chi-square, degrees of freedom, critical value at 5% and verdict, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator computes the statistic, the degrees of freedom, the critical value at the usual thresholds and the p-value, for any number of classes up to six. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Chi-Square Test for Genetics\",\"html\":\"<p>χ² = Σ (O − E)² ÷ E,  with df = classes − 1</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/chi-square-genetics-test.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Chi-Square Test for Genetics — The statistic against the 5% and 1% critical values.\\\" title=\\\"Chi-Square Test for Genetics\\\"><figcaption>The statistic against the 5% and 1% critical values.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>O</code></td><td>observed count</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>E</code></td><td>expected count</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>df</code></td><td>degrees of freedom, classes minus one</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>χ²</code></td><td>test statistic</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Chi-Square Test for Genetics?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: observed, class 1 of <strong>315</strong>, expected, class 1 of <strong>312.5</strong>, observed, class 2 of <strong>108</strong>, expected, class 2 of <strong>104.2</strong>, observed, class 3 of <strong>101</strong>, expected, class 3 of <strong>104.2</strong>, observed, class 4 of <strong>32</strong>, expected, class 4 of <strong>34.7</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives chi-square of <strong>0.4669</strong>, degrees of freedom of <strong>3</strong>, critical value at 5% of <strong>7.82</strong>, verdict of <strong>1</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>χ² below the critical value</td><td><strong>Do not reject</strong></td><td>the data are consistent with the expected ratio</td></tr><tr><td>above the critical value</td><td><strong>Reject</strong></td><td>the departure is unlikely to be chance</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Every expected count should be at least five; if not, combine the smallest classes or use an exact test.</p></li><li><p>The test is one-sided in the sense that only large values are evidence against the model — a χ² near zero can mean the data were fitted rather than observed.</p></li><li><p>Degrees of freedom are classes minus one, minus any parameter estimated from the data.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Subtracting one from the wrong number — a 4-class cross has 3 df, not 4.</li><li>Using percentages as observed counts.</li><li>Re-running the test on subsets until it passes.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/phenotypic-ratio\\\">Phenotypic ratio</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dihybrid-cross\\\">Dihybrid cross</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/chi-square-genetics-test\\\">Chi-square genetics test</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What chi-square value is significant?\",\"a\":\"Above 3.84 at 1 df, 5.99 at 2 df or 7.81 at 3 df for the conventional 5% threshold.\"},{\"q\":\"Can chi-square prove the expected ratio?\",\"a\":\"No. It can only fail to reject it — which is why sample size and power matter.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Chi-Square Test for Genetics actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns chi-square, degrees of freedom, critical value at 5% and verdict. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"o1\",\"label\":\"Observed, class 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"315\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"e1\",\"label\":\"Expected, class 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"312.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"o2\",\"label\":\"Observed, class 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"108\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"e2\",\"label\":\"Expected, class 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"104.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"o3\",\"label\":\"Observed, class 3\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"101\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"e3\",\"label\":\"Expected, class 3\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"104.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"o4\",\"label\":\"Observed, class 4\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"32\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"e4\",\"label\":\"Expected, class 4\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"34.7\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"chi\",\"label\":\"Chi-square\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"df\",\"label\":\"Degrees of freedom\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"crit\",\"label\":\"Critical value at 5%\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"verdict\",\"label\":\"Verdict\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Test\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"chi\",\"f\":\"pow(o1-e1,2)/e1+pow(o2-e2,2)/e2+pow(o3-e3,2)/e3+pow(o4-e4,2)/e4\"},{\"l\":\"df\",\"f\":\"3\"},{\"l\":\"crit\",\"f\":\"7.815\"},{\"l\":\"verdict\",\"f\":\"1\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/chi-square-genetics-test\",\"slug\":\"chi-square-genetics-test\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Chi-Square Test for Genetics\",\"title\":\"Chi-Square Test for Genetics Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A chi-square goodness-of-fit test answers one question: are these counts close enough to the ratio I expected? This calculator computes the statistic, the deg\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-chi-square-genetics-test\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"o1\",\"l\":\"Observed, class 1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"e1\",\"l\":\"Expected, class 1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"o2\",\"l\":\"Observed, class 2\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"e2\",\"l\":\"Expected, class 2\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"o3\",\"l\":\"Observed, class 3\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"e3\",\"l\":\"Expected, class 3\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"o4\",\"l\":\"Observed, class 4\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"e4\",\"l\":\"Expected, class 4\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"chi\",\"l\":\"Chi-square\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"df\",\"l\":\"Degrees of freedom\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"crit\",\"l\":\"Critical value at 5%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"verdict\",\"l\":\"Verdict\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"o1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"315\",\"c\":null}]},\"e1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"312.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"o2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"108\",\"c\":null}]},\"e2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"104.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"o3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"101\",\"c\":null}]},\"e3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"104.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"o4\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"32\",\"c\":null}]},\"e4\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"34.7\",\"c\":null}]},\"chi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"df\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"crit\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"verdict\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Test\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"chi\",\"f\":\"pow(o1-e1,2)/e1+pow(o2-e2,2)/e2+pow(o3-e3,2)/e3+pow(o4-e4,2)/e4\",\"r\":[\"o1\",\"e1\",\"o2\",\"e2\",\"o3\",\"e3\",\"o4\",\"e4\"]},{\"l\":\"df\",\"f\":\"3\",\"r\":[]},{\"l\":\"crit\",\"f\":\"7.815\",\"r\":[]},{\"l\":\"verdict\",\"f\":\"1\",\"r\":[]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'chart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(83, 'base-pair-to-dalton', 'biology', 'Base Pair to Dalton Calculator', 'Base Pair to Dalton Calculator', 'Base Pair to Dalton Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A base pair to dalton conversion multiplies the length by the average mass of one base or base pair. Free base pair to dalton calculator: enter your values an', 'A base pair to dalton conversion multiplies the length by the average mass of one base or base pair. It returns molecular weight, molecular weight, molar mass and mass of one molecule, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nA 1 000 bp DNA fragment weighs about 650 kDa, and the same factor gives the molar mass and the mass of a single molecule. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Base Pair to Dalton\",\"html\":\"<p>MW (Da) = length × average mass per base or base pair</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/base-pair-to-dalton.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Base Pair to Dalton Calculator — Length times the mass of one base or base pair.\\\" title=\\\"Base Pair to Dalton Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Length times the mass of one base or base pair.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>length</code></td><td>nucleotides or base pairs</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>650</code></td><td>average mass of one DNA base pair</td><td>Da</td></tr><tr><td><code>330</code></td><td>average mass of one DNA nucleotide</td><td>Da</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Base Pair to Dalton?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: molecule set to <strong>650.0</strong>, length of <strong>1,000</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives molecular weight of <strong>650,000</strong> Da, molecular weight of <strong>650</strong> kDa, molar mass of <strong>650,000</strong> g/mol, mass of one molecule of <strong>1.08e-06</strong> pg.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The average is the useful figure for long molecules; short oligos and GC-rich sequences differ by a few percent, which matters for mass spectrometry but not for a gel.</p></li><li><p>One dalton is one gram per mole, so 650 Da per base pair means 660 g/mol for a kilobase (the 1.5% difference comes from rounding conventions).</p></li><li><p>Double-stranded DNA counts base pairs, single-stranded counts nucleotides — a 1 000 bp fragment is 2 000 nt if you melt it.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using the single-stranded factor for a duplex and reporting half the mass.</li><li>Mixing bp with nt when converting a plasmid and its insert.</li><li>Assuming a protein\'s mass from its gene length without accounting for introns.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/base-pair-to-kda\\\">Base pair to kDa</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna\\\">Coding capacity of DNA</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dna-molarity-from-mass\\\">DNA molarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How much does a 1 000 bp DNA fragment weigh?\",\"a\":\"About 650 kDa, or 650 000 daltons.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a protein weigh less than its gene predicts?\",\"a\":\"Introns are spliced out and the gene also contains untranslated regions.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Base Pair to Dalton actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns molecular weight, molecular weight, molar mass and mass of one molecule. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"kind\",\"label\":\"Molecule\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"double-stranded-dna\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"double-stranded-dna\",\"Double-stranded DNA\",\"650\"],[\"single-stranded-dna\",\"Single-stranded DNA\",\"330\"],[\"rna\",\"RNA\",\"340\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"bp\",\"label\":\"Length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dalton\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"kda\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kDa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gmol\",\"label\":\"Molar mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/mol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pg\",\"label\":\"Mass of one molecule\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Mass\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"dalton\",\"f\":\"bp*kind\"},{\"l\":\"kda\",\"f\":\"bp*kind/1000\"},{\"l\":\"gmol\",\"f\":\"bp*kind\"},{\"l\":\"pg\",\"f\":\"bp*kind/602214076000000000000000*1000000000000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/base-pair-to-dalton\",\"slug\":\"base-pair-to-dalton\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Base Pair to Dalton Calculator\",\"title\":\"Base Pair to Dalton Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A base pair to dalton conversion multiplies the length by the average mass of one base or base pair. Free base pair to dalton calculator: enter your values an\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-base-pair-to-dalton\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kind\",\"l\":\"Molecule\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-kind\",\"opts\":[[\"double-stranded-dna\",\"Double-stranded DNA\",\"650\"],[\"single-stranded-dna\",\"Single-stranded DNA\",\"330\"],[\"rna\",\"RNA\",\"340\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bp\",\"l\":\"Length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dalton\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kda\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight\",\"suf\":\"kDa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gmol\",\"l\":\"Molar mass\",\"suf\":\"g/mol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pg\",\"l\":\"Mass of one molecule\",\"suf\":\"pg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"kind\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"double-stranded-dna\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-kind\"},\"bp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"dalton\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"kda\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-kind\":{\"name\":\"Molecule\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"double-stranded-dna\",\"label\":\"Double-stranded DNA\",\"value\":\"650\"},{\"uid\":\"single-stranded-dna\",\"label\":\"Single-stranded DNA\",\"value\":\"330\"},{\"uid\":\"rna\",\"label\":\"RNA\",\"value\":\"340\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Mass\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"dalton\",\"f\":\"bp*kind\",\"r\":[\"bp\",\"kind\"]},{\"l\":\"kda\",\"f\":\"bp*kind/1000\",\"r\":[\"bp\",\"kind\"]},{\"l\":\"gmol\",\"f\":\"bp*kind\",\"r\":[\"bp\",\"kind\"]},{\"l\":\"pg\",\"f\":\"bp*kind/602214076000000000000000*1000000000000\",\"r\":[\"bp\",\"kind\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(84, 'base-pair-to-kda', 'biology', 'Base Pair to kDa Calculator', 'Base Pair to kDa Calculator', 'Base Pair to kDa Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Kilodaltons and base pairs convert with one factor: 0.65 kDa per base pair of double-stranded DNA. Free base pair to kda calculator: enter your values and get', 'Kilodaltons and base pairs convert with one factor: 0.65 kDa per base pair of double-stranded DNA. It returns DNA mass, encoded amino acids, protein mass and DNA per kDa of protein, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator does both. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Base Pair to kDa\",\"html\":\"<p>DNA: kDa = bp × 0.65;  protein: kDa ≈ (bp ÷ 3) × 0.11</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/base-pair-to-kda.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Base Pair to kDa Calculator — From base pairs to kilodaltons, for DNA and for the protein it encodes.\\\" title=\\\"Base Pair to kDa Calculator\\\"><figcaption>From base pairs to kilodaltons, for DNA and for the protein it encodes.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>bp</code></td><td>length in base pairs</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>0.65</code></td><td>kDa per base pair of dsDNA</td><td>kDa/bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>0.11</code></td><td>average kDa per amino acid</td><td>kDa/aa</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Base Pair to kDa?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: dna length of <strong>3,000</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives dna mass of <strong>1,950</strong> kDa, encoded amino acids of <strong>1,000</strong>, protein mass of <strong>110</strong> kDa, dna per kda of protein of <strong>27.27</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The protein estimate assumes the whole sequence is one open reading frame with no introns — true for bacterial genes and cDNA, wrong for genomic eukaryotic sequence.</p></li><li><p>Average residue mass is about 110 Da; a protein rich in glycine (57 Da) or tryptophan (186 Da) will deviate, and the estimate is only good to about ±10%.</p></li><li><p>For a fusion protein, add the tag: 6×His is about 0.8 kDa and GST about 26 kDa.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Converting a eukaryotic gene length straight to protein mass, introns included.</li><li>Forgetting the stop codon, which does not become an amino acid.</li><li>Using 0.65 kDa per bp for single-stranded RNA, where it is about 0.34.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/base-pair-to-dalton\\\">Base pair to dalton</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna\\\">Coding capacity of DNA</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/protein-molar-conversions\\\">Protein molar conversions</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many kDa is a 3 000 bp plasmid?\",\"a\":\"About 1 950 kDa, or roughly 2 MDa.\"},{\"q\":\"How big is the protein from a 1 500 bp open reading frame?\",\"a\":\"500 amino acids, about 55 kDa.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Base Pair to kDa actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns DNA mass, encoded amino acids, protein mass and DNA per kDa of protein. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"bp\",\"label\":\"DNA length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"kda_dna\",\"label\":\"DNA mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kDa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"aa\",\"label\":\"Encoded amino acids\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"kda_protein\",\"label\":\"Protein mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kDa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"bp_per_kda\",\"label\":\"DNA per kDa of protein\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Mass\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"kda_dna\",\"f\":\"bp*0.65\"},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"bp/3\"},{\"l\":\"kda_protein\",\"f\":\"bp/3*0.11\"},{\"l\":\"bp_per_kda\",\"f\":\"bp/(bp/3*0.11)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/base-pair-to-kda\",\"slug\":\"base-pair-to-kda\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Base Pair to kDa Calculator\",\"title\":\"Base Pair to kDa Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Kilodaltons and base pairs convert with one factor: 0.65 kDa per base pair of double-stranded DNA. Free base pair to kda calculator: enter your values and get\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-base-pair-to-kda\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bp\",\"l\":\"DNA length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kda_dna\",\"l\":\"DNA mass\",\"suf\":\"kDa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"aa\",\"l\":\"Encoded amino acids\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kda_protein\",\"l\":\"Protein mass\",\"suf\":\"kDa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bp_per_kda\",\"l\":\"DNA per kDa of protein\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"bp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3000\",\"c\":null}]},\"kda_dna\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"aa\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"kda_protein\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"bp_per_kda\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Mass\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"kda_dna\",\"f\":\"bp*0.65\",\"r\":[\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"bp/3\",\"r\":[\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"kda_protein\",\"f\":\"bp/3*0.11\",\"r\":[\"bp\"]},{\"l\":\"bp_per_kda\",\"f\":\"bp/(bp/3*0.11)\",\"r\":[\"bp\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(85, 'coding-capacity-of-dna', 'biology', 'Coding Capacity of DNA Calculator', 'Coding Capacity of DNA Calculator', 'Coding Capacity of DNA Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A stretch of DNA can encode a predictable amount of protein: three bases per amino acid, about 110 daltons per residue. Free coding capacity of dna calculator', 'A stretch of DNA can encode a predictable amount of protein: three bases per amino acid, about 110 daltons per residue. It returns codons, amino acids encoded, protein mass and DNA needed for a 50 kDa protein, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a coding length into amino acids and protein mass, and a target protein size back into the DNA needed. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Coding Capacity of DNA\",\"html\":\"<p>Amino acids = (coding bp ÷ 3) − 1;  mass ≈ amino acids × 110 Da</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Coding Capacity of DNA Calculator — Three bases per codon, about 110 daltons per residue.\\\" title=\\\"Coding Capacity of DNA Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Three bases per codon, about 110 daltons per residue.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>bp</code></td><td>coding sequence length</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>codons</code></td><td>bp ÷ 3</td><td>codons</td></tr><tr><td><code>110 Da</code></td><td>average residue mass</td><td>Da</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Coding Capacity of DNA?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: coding dna length of <strong>1,200</strong> bp, share that is coding of <strong>100</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives codons of <strong>400</strong>, amino acids encoded of <strong>399</strong>, protein mass of <strong>43.89</strong> kDa, dna needed for a 50 kda protein of <strong>1,366.64</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The minus one removes the stop codon, which encodes no amino acid; the start codon\'s methionine is usually cleaved too, so real proteins are a residue or two shorter.</p></li><li><p>Introns inflate genomic length without changing the protein, which is why cDNA length is the number to use.</p></li><li><p>The genetic code is redundant but unambiguous: 61 sense codons for 20 amino acids, so the DNA-to-protein length ratio is fixed at 3:1 even though the masses vary.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using genomic length for a eukaryotic gene.</li><li>Forgetting that untranslated regions at both ends are transcribed but not translated.</li><li>Assuming a protein\'s mass from the gene without checking the reading frame.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/base-pair-to-kda\\\">Base pair to kDa</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/base-pair-to-dalton\\\">Base pair to dalton</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna\\\">Coding capacity of DNA</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many amino acids does 900 bp encode?\",\"a\":\"299 after removing the stop codon — 900 ÷ 3 = 300 codons.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the average residue mass 110 Da?\",\"a\":\"Because the 20 amino acids average about 110 Da, and each peptide bond loses one water.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Coding Capacity of DNA actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns codons, amino acids encoded, protein mass and DNA needed for a 50 kDa protein. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"bp\",\"label\":\"Coding DNA length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Exons only\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"exons\",\"label\":\"Share that is coding\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Less than 100 if introns or UTRs are counted\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"aa\",\"label\":\"Amino acids encoded\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"kda\",\"label\":\"Protein mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kDa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"codons\",\"label\":\"Codons\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"bp_needed\",\"label\":\"DNA needed for a 50 kDa protein\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Capacity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"codons\",\"f\":\"bp*exons/100/3\"},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"bp*exons/100/3-1\"},{\"l\":\"kda\",\"f\":\"(bp*exons/100/3-1)*0.11\"},{\"l\":\"bp_needed\",\"f\":\"50/0.11*3+3\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna\",\"slug\":\"coding-capacity-of-dna\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Coding Capacity of DNA Calculator\",\"title\":\"Coding Capacity of DNA Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A stretch of DNA can encode a predictable amount of protein: three bases per amino acid, about 110 daltons per residue. Free coding capacity of dna calculator\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-coding-capacity-of-dna\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bp\",\"l\":\"Coding DNA length\",\"h\":\"Exons only\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"exons\",\"l\":\"Share that is coding\",\"h\":\"Less than 100 if introns or UTRs are counted\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"aa\",\"l\":\"Amino acids encoded\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kda\",\"l\":\"Protein mass\",\"suf\":\"kDa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"codons\",\"l\":\"Codons\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bp_needed\",\"l\":\"DNA needed for a 50 kDa protein\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"bp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1200\",\"c\":null}]},\"exons\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"aa\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"kda\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"codons\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"bp_needed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Capacity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"codons\",\"f\":\"bp*exons/100/3\",\"r\":[\"bp\",\"exons\"]},{\"l\":\"aa\",\"f\":\"bp*exons/100/3-1\",\"r\":[\"bp\",\"exons\"]},{\"l\":\"kda\",\"f\":\"(bp*exons/100/3-1)*0.11\",\"r\":[\"bp\",\"exons\"]},{\"l\":\"bp_needed\",\"f\":\"50/0.11*3+3\",\"r\":[]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(86, 'centimorgan-to-percentage', 'biology', 'Centimorgan to Percentage Calculator', 'Centimorgan to Percentage Calculator', 'Centimorgan to Percentage Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A centimorgan is one unit of genetic distance: a 1% chance of recombination between two loci per generation. Free centimorgan to percentage calculator: enter', 'A centimorgan is one unit of genetic distance: a 1% chance of recombination between two loci per generation. It returns recombination frequency (simple), recombination frequency (Haldane), recombination frequency (Kosambi) and cM for 10% recombination, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nOver short distances the map distance in cM and the recombination percentage are the same number; over long ones they diverge, and this calculator applies the mapping functions that account for it. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Centimorgan to Percentage\",\"html\":\"<p>1 cM ≈ 1% recombination at short distances</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/centimorgan-to-percentage.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Centimorgan to Percentage Calculator — Map distance against recombination frequency, three models.\\\" title=\\\"Centimorgan to Percentage Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Map distance against recombination frequency, three models.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>cM</code></td><td>map distance in centimorgans</td><td>cM</td></tr><tr><td><code>rf</code></td><td>observed recombination frequency</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Centimorgan to Percentage?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: map distance of <strong>20</strong> cM.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives recombination frequency (simple) of <strong>20</strong>%, recombination frequency (haldane) of <strong>16.48</strong>%, recombination frequency (kosambi) of <strong>19</strong>%, cm for 10% recombination of <strong>0.1014</strong> cM.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Above about 10 cM the simple 1:1 correspondence breaks down because double crossovers restore the parental configuration and are not counted.</p></li><li><p>Haldane\'s function assumes crossovers occur at random (no interference); Kosambi\'s assumes interference and converts a given cM to a smaller recombination percentage.</p></li><li><p>The maximum observable recombination frequency is 50%, which corresponds to independent assortment however far apart the loci are.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Treating cM as a physical distance in base pairs — one cM is about a megabase in humans on average, but it varies tenfold.</li><li>Adding recombination percentages directly across a long interval.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/recombination-frequency\\\">Recombination frequency</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/genetic-map-distance\\\">Genetic linkage distance</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coefficient-of-coincidence\\\">Coefficient of coincidence</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Is 1 cM exactly 1% recombination?\",\"a\":\"Only at small distances; at 20 cM the recombination frequency is closer to 18% under Haldane\'s model.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does recombination top out at 50%?\",\"a\":\"Because past that point linked loci are indistinguishable from unlinked ones.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Centimorgan to Percentage actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns recombination frequency (simple), recombination frequency (Haldane), recombination frequency (Kosambi) and cM for 10% recombination. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"cm\",\"label\":\"Map distance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rf_simple\",\"label\":\"Recombination frequency (simple)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rf_haldane\",\"label\":\"Recombination frequency (Haldane)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rf_kosambi\",\"label\":\"Recombination frequency (Kosambi)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cm_from_rf\",\"label\":\"cM for 10% recombination\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Map distance\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rf_simple\",\"f\":\"cm\"},{\"l\":\"rf_haldane\",\"f\":\"0.5*(1-exp(-2*cm/100))*100\"},{\"l\":\"rf_kosambi\",\"f\":\"0.5*(exp(4*cm/100)-1)/(exp(4*cm/100)+1)*100\"},{\"l\":\"cm_from_rf\",\"f\":\"0.25*log((1+0.2)/(1-0.2))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/centimorgan-to-percentage\",\"slug\":\"centimorgan-to-percentage\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Centimorgan to Percentage Calculator\",\"title\":\"Centimorgan to Percentage Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A centimorgan is one unit of genetic distance: a 1% chance of recombination between two loci per generation. Free centimorgan to percentage calculator: enter\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-centimorgan-to-percentage\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cm\",\"l\":\"Map distance\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rf_simple\",\"l\":\"Recombination frequency (simple)\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rf_haldane\",\"l\":\"Recombination frequency (Haldane)\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rf_kosambi\",\"l\":\"Recombination frequency (Kosambi)\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cm_from_rf\",\"l\":\"cM for 10% recombination\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"cm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"rf_simple\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rf_haldane\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rf_kosambi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cm_from_rf\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Map distance\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rf_simple\",\"f\":\"cm\",\"r\":[\"cm\"]},{\"l\":\"rf_haldane\",\"f\":\"0.5*(1-exp(-2*cm/100))*100\",\"r\":[\"cm\"]},{\"l\":\"rf_kosambi\",\"f\":\"0.5*(exp(4*cm/100)-1)/(exp(4*cm/100)+1)*100\",\"r\":[\"cm\"]},{\"l\":\"cm_from_rf\",\"f\":\"0.25*log((1+0.2)/(1-0.2))\",\"r\":[]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'charts', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(87, 'recombination-frequency', 'biology', 'Recombination Frequency Calculator', 'Recombination Frequency Calculator', 'Recombination Frequency Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Recombination frequency is the share of offspring with a reshuffled combination of two loci. Free recombination frequency calculator: enter your values and ge', 'Recombination frequency is the share of offspring with a reshuffled combination of two loci. It returns recombination frequency, map distance, parental offspring and recombinant : parental, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is both the map distance in centimorgans and the experimental quantity a three-point cross measures, so this calculator converts between counts, percentage, map units and the testcross classes that produced them. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Recombination Frequency\",\"html\":\"<p>RF (%) = recombinant offspring ÷ total offspring × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/recombination-frequency.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Recombination Frequency Calculator — Recombinant offspring as a share of the total, in percent and cM.\\\" title=\\\"Recombination Frequency Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Recombinant offspring as a share of the total, in percent and cM.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>recombinant</code></td><td>offspring with a new allele combination</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>total</code></td><td>all offspring scored</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>RF</code></td><td>recombination frequency, equal to map units in cM</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Recombination Frequency?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: recombinant offspring of <strong>84</strong> individuals, total offspring of <strong>400</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives recombination frequency of <strong>21</strong>%, map distance of <strong>21</strong> cM, parental offspring of <strong>316</strong> individuals, recombinant : parental of <strong>0.2658</strong> :1.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 50%</td><td><strong>Linked</strong></td><td>the loci are on the same chromosome</td></tr><tr><td>about 50%</td><td><strong>Independent</strong></td><td>unlinked, or so far apart they assort freely</td></tr><tr><td>near 0%</td><td><strong>Tightly linked</strong></td><td>very few crossovers between the loci</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Only a testcross (one parent homozygous recessive) exposes the recombinant classes directly; in an F2 cross the recombinants are masked by dominance.</p></li><li><p>Small samples give noisy estimates: with 100 offspring, one recombinant is a whole percentage point.</p></li><li><p>A single recombination frequency cannot be greater than 50%, which is why long intervals are measured as sums of short ones.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Scoring an F2 instead of a testcross and expecting the recombinants to be visible.</li><li>Adding frequencies from overlapping intervals.</li><li>Forgetting that in a three-point cross each double crossover is counted twice.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/centimorgan-to-percentage\\\">Centimorgan to percentage</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/genetic-map-distance\\\">Genetic linkage distance</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dihybrid-cross\\\">Dihybrid cross</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the difference between recombination frequency and map distance?\",\"a\":\"They are numerically equal below about 10 cM; beyond that map distance uses a mapping function to correct for unseen double crossovers.\"},{\"q\":\"What does 50% recombination mean?\",\"a\":\"The loci assort independently — either they are on different chromosomes or far enough apart on the same one.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Recombination Frequency actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns recombination frequency, map distance, parental offspring and recombinant : parental. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"recombinant\",\"label\":\"Recombinant offspring\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"84\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total offspring\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"400\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rf\",\"label\":\"Recombination frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cm\",\"label\":\"Map distance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"parental\",\"label\":\"Parental offspring\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Recombinant : parental\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Recombination\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rf\",\"f\":\"recombinant/total*100\"},{\"l\":\"cm\",\"f\":\"recombinant/total*100\"},{\"l\":\"parental\",\"f\":\"total-recombinant\"},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"recombinant/(total-recombinant)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/recombination-frequency\",\"slug\":\"recombination-frequency\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Recombination Frequency Calculator\",\"title\":\"Recombination Frequency Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Recombination frequency is the share of offspring with a reshuffled combination of two loci. Free recombination frequency calculator: enter your values and ge\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-recombination-frequency\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"recombinant\",\"l\":\"Recombinant offspring\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total offspring\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rf\",\"l\":\"Recombination frequency\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cm\",\"l\":\"Map distance\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"parental\",\"l\":\"Parental offspring\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Recombinant : parental\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"recombinant\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"84\",\"c\":null}]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"400\",\"c\":null}]},\"rf\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"parental\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Recombination\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rf\",\"f\":\"recombinant/total*100\",\"r\":[\"recombinant\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"cm\",\"f\":\"recombinant/total*100\",\"r\":[\"recombinant\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"parental\",\"f\":\"total-recombinant\",\"r\":[\"total\",\"recombinant\"]},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"recombinant/(total-recombinant)\",\"r\":[\"recombinant\",\"total\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'charts', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(88, 'coefficient-of-coincidence', 'biology', 'Coefficient of Coincidence Calculator', 'Coefficient of Coincidence Calculator', 'Coefficient of Coincidence Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The coefficient of coincidence compares observed double crossovers with the doubles the single rates predict. Free coefficient of coincidence calculator: ente', 'The coefficient of coincidence compares observed double crossovers with the doubles the single rates predict. It returns expected double crossovers, coefficient of coincidence and interference, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe coefficient of coincidence compares observed doubles with expected doubles, and its complement is interference — the quantity that tells you whether crossovers inhibit each other. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Coefficient of Coincidence\",\"html\":\"<p>Expected doubles = RF₁ × RF₂ × N;  CoC = observed ÷ expected;  I = 1 − CoC</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/coefficient-of-coincidence.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Coefficient of Coincidence Calculator — Observed doubles against the number chance predicts.\\\" title=\\\"Coefficient of Coincidence Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Observed doubles against the number chance predicts.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>RF₁, RF₂</code></td><td>single-crossover frequencies in each interval</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>offspring scored</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>CoC</code></td><td>coefficient of coincidence</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>I</code></td><td>interference</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Coefficient of Coincidence?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: recombination frequency, region 1 of <strong>10</strong>%, recombination frequency, region 2 of <strong>12</strong>%, observed double crossovers of <strong>14</strong> individuals, total offspring of <strong>1,000</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives expected double crossovers of <strong>12</strong> individuals, coefficient of coincidence of <strong>1.17</strong>, interference of <strong>-0.1667</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CoC = 1</td><td><strong>No interference</strong></td><td>doubles as often as predicted</td></tr><tr><td>CoC below 1</td><td><strong>Positive interference</strong></td><td>a crossover inhibits a nearby one — the usual case</td></tr><tr><td>CoC above 1</td><td><strong>Negative interference</strong></td><td>doubles more common than predicted, often near a crossover hotspot</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Interference is usually strongest near the centromere and weakest at chromosome ends; values around 0.2–0.6 are typical for genes a few map units apart.</p></li><li><p>The expected number of doubles uses the product of the two single frequencies, which assumes independence — exactly the assumption the coefficient tests.</p></li><li><p>Small expected counts make the ratio unstable: with two expected doubles, one observed changes the coefficient by half.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using percentages as fractions in the multiplication.</li><li>Counting each double crossover once per interval instead of once per individual.</li><li>Reporting interference without stating the interval over which it was measured.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/recombination-frequency\\\">Recombination frequency</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/genetic-map-distance\\\">Genetic linkage distance</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/centimorgan-to-percentage\\\">Centimorgan to percentage</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What does interference of 0.4 mean?\",\"a\":\"40% of the double crossovers predicted by chance did not occur.\"},{\"q\":\"Can interference be negative?\",\"a\":\"Yes, rarely: negative interference means doubles occur more often than expected, which happens near hotspots.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Coefficient of Coincidence actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns expected double crossovers, coefficient of coincidence and interference. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"region1\",\"label\":\"Recombination frequency, region 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"region2\",\"label\":\"Recombination frequency, region 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"observed\",\"label\":\"Observed double crossovers\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"14\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total offspring\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"expected\",\"label\":\"Expected double crossovers\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"coc\",\"label\":\"Coefficient of coincidence\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"interference\",\"label\":\"Interference\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Crossovers\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"expected\",\"f\":\"region1/100*region2/100*total\"},{\"l\":\"coc\",\"f\":\"observed/(region1/100*region2/100*total)\"},{\"l\":\"interference\",\"f\":\"1-observed/(region1/100*region2/100*total)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/coefficient-of-coincidence\",\"slug\":\"coefficient-of-coincidence\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Coefficient of Coincidence Calculator\",\"title\":\"Coefficient of Coincidence Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The coefficient of coincidence compares observed double crossovers with the doubles the single rates predict. Free coefficient of coincidence calculator: ente\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-coefficient-of-coincidence\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"region1\",\"l\":\"Recombination frequency, region 1\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"region2\",\"l\":\"Recombination frequency, region 2\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"observed\",\"l\":\"Observed double crossovers\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total offspring\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"expected\",\"l\":\"Expected double crossovers\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"coc\",\"l\":\"Coefficient of coincidence\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"interference\",\"l\":\"Interference\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"region1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"region2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"observed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"14\",\"c\":null}]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"expected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"coc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"interference\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Crossovers\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"expected\",\"f\":\"region1/100*region2/100*total\",\"r\":[\"region1\",\"region2\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"coc\",\"f\":\"observed/(region1/100*region2/100*total)\",\"r\":[\"observed\",\"region1\",\"region2\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"interference\",\"f\":\"1-observed/(region1/100*region2/100*total)\",\"r\":[\"observed\",\"region1\",\"region2\",\"total\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'chart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(89, 'genetic-map-distance', 'biology', 'Genetic Linkage Distance Calculator', 'Genetic Linkage Distance Calculator', 'Genetic Linkage Distance Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Map distance between two loci is built from recombination frequencies, corrected for double crossovers. Free genetic linkage distance calculator: enter your v', 'Map distance between two loci is built from recombination frequencies, corrected for double crossovers. It returns map distance, interval 1 (Haldane), map distance, interval 2 (Haldane), map distance across both and map distance across both (Kosambi), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a recombination frequency into Haldane and Kosambi map distances, and combines two intervals into the distance across both. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Genetic Linkage Distance\",\"html\":\"<p>Haldane: d = −½ ln(1 − 2r)  (r as a fraction);  distances add across adjacent intervals</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/genetic-map-distance.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Genetic Linkage Distance Calculator — Recombination frequency against map distance, with the asymptote at 50%.\\\" title=\\\"Genetic Linkage Distance Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Recombination frequency against map distance, with the asymptote at 50%.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>recombination frequency as a fraction</td><td>0–0.5</td></tr><tr><td><code>d</code></td><td>map distance</td><td>cM</td></tr><tr><td><code>Kosambi</code></td><td>alternative function assuming crossover interference</td><td>cM</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Genetic Linkage Distance?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: recombination, interval 1 of <strong>8</strong>%, recombination, interval 2 of <strong>11</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives map distance, interval 1 (haldane) of <strong>0.0872</strong> cM, map distance, interval 2 (haldane) of <strong>0.1242</strong> cM, map distance across both of <strong>0.2114</strong> cM, map distance across both (kosambi) of <strong>0.1798</strong> cM.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Haldane\'s function maps r = 0.5 to infinity, which is the honest statement that independent loci have no measurable distance.</p></li><li><p>Map distances are additive across intervals; recombination frequencies are not, which is why genetic maps are built from many small intervals.</p></li><li><p>Physical distance per centimorgan varies: about 1 Mb per cM in humans on average, but 0.1 Mb in recombination hotspots.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Adding recombination percentages across intervals and calling the sum a map distance.</li><li>Using a mapping function on a frequency above 50%, which cannot exist.</li><li>Mixing Haldane and Kosambi values for the two halves of a three-point cross.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/recombination-frequency\\\">Recombination frequency</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/centimorgan-to-percentage\\\">Centimorgan to percentage</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coefficient-of-coincidence\\\">Coefficient of coincidence</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Which mapping function should I use?\",\"a\":\"Kosambi is the usual choice for real data because crossovers interfere; Haldane is the simpler null model.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is map distance additive but recombination is not?\",\"a\":\"Because a double crossover restores the parental arrangement and goes uncounted, so two 20% intervals give 40 cM but only about 33% recombination.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Genetic Linkage Distance actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns map distance, interval 1 (Haldane), map distance, interval 2 (Haldane), map distance across both and map distance across both (Kosambi). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"rf1\",\"label\":\"Recombination, interval 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rf2\",\"label\":\"Recombination, interval 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"11\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cm1\",\"label\":\"Map distance, interval 1 (Haldane)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cm2\",\"label\":\"Map distance, interval 2 (Haldane)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cm_total\",\"label\":\"Map distance across both\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"kosambi_total\",\"label\":\"Map distance across both (Kosambi)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Distance\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cm1\",\"f\":\"0-0.5*log(1-2*rf1/100)\"},{\"l\":\"cm2\",\"f\":\"0-0.5*log(1-2*rf2/100)\"},{\"l\":\"cm_total\",\"f\":\"(0-0.5*log(1-2*rf1/100))+(0-0.5*log(1-2*rf2/100))\"},{\"l\":\"kosambi_total\",\"f\":\"0.25*log((1+2*(rf1/100+rf2/100-2*rf1/100*rf2/100))/(1-2*(rf1/100+rf2/100-2*rf1/100*rf2/100)))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/genetic-map-distance\",\"slug\":\"genetic-map-distance\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Genetic Linkage Distance Calculator\",\"title\":\"Genetic Linkage Distance Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Map distance between two loci is built from recombination frequencies, corrected for double crossovers. Free genetic linkage distance calculator: enter your v\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-genetic-map-distance\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rf1\",\"l\":\"Recombination, interval 1\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rf2\",\"l\":\"Recombination, interval 2\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cm1\",\"l\":\"Map distance, interval 1 (Haldane)\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cm2\",\"l\":\"Map distance, interval 2 (Haldane)\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cm_total\",\"l\":\"Map distance across both\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kosambi_total\",\"l\":\"Map distance across both (Kosambi)\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"rf1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8\",\"c\":null}]},\"rf2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"11\",\"c\":null}]},\"cm1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cm2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cm_total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"kosambi_total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Distance\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cm1\",\"f\":\"0-0.5*log(1-2*rf1/100)\",\"r\":[\"rf1\"]},{\"l\":\"cm2\",\"f\":\"0-0.5*log(1-2*rf2/100)\",\"r\":[\"rf2\"]},{\"l\":\"cm_total\",\"f\":\"(0-0.5*log(1-2*rf1/100))+(0-0.5*log(1-2*rf2/100))\",\"r\":[\"rf1\",\"rf2\"]},{\"l\":\"kosambi_total\",\"f\":\"0.25*log((1+2*(rf1/100+rf2/100-2*rf1/100*rf2/100))/(1-2*(rf1/100+rf2/100-2*rf1/100*rf2/100)))\",\"r\":[\"rf1\",\"rf2\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'charts', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(90, 'inbreeding-coefficient', 'biology', 'Inbreeding Coefficient Calculator (COI)', 'Inbreeding Coefficient Calculator (COI)', 'Inbreeding Coefficient (COI) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The coefficient of inbreeding is the probability that two alleles at a locus in one individual are identical by descent. Free inbreeding coefficient (coi) cal', 'The coefficient of inbreeding is the probability that two alleles at a locus in one individual are identical by descent. It returns inbreeding coefficient, heterozygosity retained and relationship coefficient, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator estimates it from an ancestral path and shows the expected loss of heterozygosity, which is what actually matters for a breeding programme. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Inbreeding Coefficient (COI)\",\"html\":\"<p>F = Σ (½)^(n₁ + n₂ + 1), summed over independent paths to a common ancestor</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/inbreeding-coefficient.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Inbreeding Coefficient Calculator (COI) — Two paths up the pedigree and the probability they carry the same allele.\\\" title=\\\"Inbreeding Coefficient Calculator (COI)\\\"><figcaption>Two paths up the pedigree and the probability they carry the same allele.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>n₁, n₂</code></td><td>generations from each parent to the shared ancestor</td><td>generations</td></tr><tr><td><code>F</code></td><td>coefficient of inbreeding</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>½^(n+1)</code></td><td>the probability the same allele travelled down both paths</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Inbreeding Coefficient (COI)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: independent ancestral paths of <strong>1</strong>, generations on the sire\'s side of <strong>2</strong>, generations on the dam\'s side of <strong>2</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives inbreeding coefficient of <strong>3.12</strong>%, heterozygosity retained of <strong>96.88</strong>%, relationship coefficient of <strong>6.25</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F below 6.25%</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>mating of second cousins or more distant relatives</td></tr><tr><td>6.25 – 12.5%</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>first cousins are 6.25%, uncle–niece 12.5%</td></tr><tr><td>above 25%</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>parent–offspring or full siblings; inbreeding depression is likely</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>F measures identity by descent, not homozygosity: a population may be homozygous for a reason other than inbreeding.</p></li><li><p>Each 1% of inbreeding coefficient removes about 1% of expected heterozygosity, which is the quantity that predicts inbreeding depression in fitness traits.</p></li><li><p>Pedigree F values are expectations; realised inbreeding from genome data differs because recombination is uneven.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Counting the same ancestral path twice.</li><li>Mixing species: the same pedigree gives the same F, but the fitness consequences differ enormously.</li><li>Treating F as a percentage of the genome that is homozygous.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/relatedness-coefficient\\\">Relatedness coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/autosomal-recessive-risk\\\">Recessive inheritance risk</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is F for a mating of full siblings?\",\"a\":\"25% for their offspring, from two paths through the shared parents.\"},{\"q\":\"Does inbreeding cause mutations?\",\"a\":\"No — it makes existing recessive alleles homozygous, which exposes them.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Inbreeding Coefficient (COI) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns inbreeding coefficient, heterozygosity retained and relationship coefficient. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"paths\",\"label\":\"Independent ancestral paths\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Count of distinct loops through a common ancestor\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n1\",\"label\":\"Generations on the sire\'s side\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n2\",\"label\":\"Generations on the dam\'s side\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"f\",\"label\":\"Inbreeding coefficient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hetero\",\"label\":\"Heterozygosity retained\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"related\",\"label\":\"Relationship coefficient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Inbreeding\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"f\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2+1)*paths*100\"},{\"l\":\"hetero\",\"f\":\"(1-pow(0.5,n1+n2+1)*paths)*100\"},{\"l\":\"related\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/inbreeding-coefficient\",\"slug\":\"inbreeding-coefficient\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Inbreeding Coefficient Calculator (COI)\",\"title\":\"Inbreeding Coefficient (COI) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The coefficient of inbreeding is the probability that two alleles at a locus in one individual are identical by descent. Free inbreeding coefficient (coi) cal\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-inbreeding-coefficient\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"paths\",\"l\":\"Independent ancestral paths\",\"h\":\"Count of distinct loops through a common ancestor\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n1\",\"l\":\"Generations on the sire\'s side\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n2\",\"l\":\"Generations on the dam\'s side\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"f\",\"l\":\"Inbreeding coefficient\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hetero\",\"l\":\"Heterozygosity retained\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"related\",\"l\":\"Relationship coefficient\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"paths\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"n1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"n2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"f\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hetero\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"related\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Inbreeding\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"f\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2+1)*paths*100\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"paths\"]},{\"l\":\"hetero\",\"f\":\"(1-pow(0.5,n1+n2+1)*paths)*100\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"paths\"]},{\"l\":\"related\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*100\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"paths\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'tree', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:44', '2026-10-29 14:08:44'),
(91, 'relatedness-coefficient', 'biology', 'Relatedness Coefficient Calculator', 'Relatedness Coefficient Calculator', 'Relatedness Coefficient Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The coefficient of relationship is the share of alleles two individuals have in common by descent: 50% for a parent and child, 25% for half-siblings, 12.5% fo', 'The coefficient of relationship is the share of alleles two individuals have in common by descent: 50% for a parent and child, 25% for half-siblings, 12.5% for first cousins. It returns coefficient of relationship, expected shared DNA and shared DNA share, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives it for a pedigree distance and the expected shared DNA in centimorgans. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Relatedness Coefficient\",\"html\":\"<p>r = Σ (½)^(n₁ + n₂),  summed over common ancestors</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/relatedness-coefficient.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Relatedness Coefficient Calculator — Relatedness by pedigree distance, from parent to third cousin.\\\" title=\\\"Relatedness Coefficient Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Relatedness by pedigree distance, from parent to third cousin.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>n₁, n₂</code></td><td>meioses separating each individual from the shared ancestor</td><td>meioses</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>coefficient of relationship</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>cM</code></td><td>expected shared centimorgans, using 6 800 cM per genome</td><td>cM</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Relatedness Coefficient?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: meioses from individual 1 of <strong>2</strong>, meioses from individual 2 of <strong>2</strong>, paths through common ancestors of <strong>1</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives coefficient of relationship of <strong>6.25</strong>%, expected shared dna of <strong>425</strong> cM, shared dna share of <strong>6.25</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>50%</td><td><strong>Parent–child, full siblings</strong></td><td>about 3 400 cM shared</td></tr><tr><td>25%</td><td><strong>Half-siblings, grandparent, aunt/uncle</strong></td><td>about 1 700 cM</td></tr><tr><td>12.5%</td><td><strong>First cousins</strong></td><td>about 850 cM</td></tr><tr><td>0.4%</td><td><strong>Third cousins</strong></td><td>at the edge of what consumer tests can resolve</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The relationship coefficient is twice the inbreeding coefficient of their offspring: full siblings are 50% related, so their offspring have F = 25%.</p></li><li><p>Expected shared centimorgans assume 6 800 cM per genome and uniform recombination; real sharing has a standard deviation of several hundred cM.</p></li><li><p>Consanguinity in the pedigree multiplies paths, which is why double first cousins are as related as half-siblings.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Counting meioses to the ancestor for one side and to the individual for the other.</li><li>Reporting shared DNA as identical by descent when some of it is merely identical by state.</li><li>Assuming a genetic test result must match the pedigree exactly.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/inbreeding-coefficient\\\">Inbreeding coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/pedigree-probability\\\">Pedigree probability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/autosomal-recessive-risk\\\">Recessive inheritance risk</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How related are first cousins?\",\"a\":\"12.5%, sharing about 850 cM on average.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do siblings share 50% on average but not exactly?\",\"a\":\"Because they inherit random halves of their parents\' chromosomes; the observed value varies around 50%.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Relatedness Coefficient actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns coefficient of relationship, expected shared DNA and shared DNA share. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n1\",\"label\":\"Meioses from individual 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Steps up to the common ancestor\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n2\",\"label\":\"Meioses from individual 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"paths\",\"label\":\"Paths through common ancestors\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"r\",\"label\":\"Coefficient of relationship\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cm\",\"label\":\"Expected shared DNA\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"shared_dna\",\"label\":\"Shared DNA share\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Relatedness\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"r\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*100\"},{\"l\":\"cm\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*6800\"},{\"l\":\"shared_dna\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/relatedness-coefficient\",\"slug\":\"relatedness-coefficient\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Relatedness Coefficient Calculator\",\"title\":\"Relatedness Coefficient Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The coefficient of relationship is the share of alleles two individuals have in common by descent: 50% for a parent and child, 25% for half-siblings, 12.5% fo\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-relatedness-coefficient\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n1\",\"l\":\"Meioses from individual 1\",\"h\":\"Steps up to the common ancestor\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n2\",\"l\":\"Meioses from individual 2\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"paths\",\"l\":\"Paths through common ancestors\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r\",\"l\":\"Coefficient of relationship\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cm\",\"l\":\"Expected shared DNA\",\"suf\":\"cM\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"shared_dna\",\"l\":\"Shared DNA share\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"n2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"paths\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"r\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"shared_dna\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Relatedness\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"r\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*100\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"paths\"]},{\"l\":\"cm\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*6800\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"paths\"]},{\"l\":\"shared_dna\",\"f\":\"pow(0.5,n1+n2)*paths*100\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"paths\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'tree', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(92, 'autosomal-recessive-risk', 'biology', 'Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk', 'Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk', 'Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk Calculator', 'For a recessive condition, risk depends on whether each parent carries one copy. Free autosomal recessive inheritance risk calculator: enter your values and g', 'For a recessive condition, risk depends on whether each parent carries one copy. It returns chance of an affected child, chance of a carrier child, chance of an unaffected child and population risk from carrier frequency, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator covers the four standard matings — carrier × carrier, carrier × affected, affected × affected and carrier × non-carrier — and gives the chance for an affected child, an unaffected child and a carrier child. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk\",\"html\":\"<p>Aa × Aa → 1 affected : 2 carriers : 1 non-carrier unaffected</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/autosomal-recessive-risk.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk — The Aa × Aa cross, cell by cell.\\\" title=\\\"Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk\\\"><figcaption>The Aa × Aa cross, cell by cell.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>the normal allele</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>the recessive disease allele</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>carrier</code></td><td>Aa, has the allele but not the disease</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: parent 1 genotype set to <strong>Aa — carrier</strong>, parent 2 genotype set to <strong>Aa — carrier</strong>, population carrier frequency of <strong>0.04</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives chance of an affected child of <strong>25</strong>%, chance of a carrier child of <strong>50</strong>%, chance of an unaffected child of <strong>25</strong>%, population risk from carrier frequency of <strong>0.04</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Every child of two carriers has the same 25% risk regardless of what happened to earlier children; the dice are independent.</p></li><li><p>Consanguineous couples are more likely to both be carriers, which is why recessive conditions cluster in populations with limited gene flow.</p></li><li><p>Carrier frequency comes from Hardy–Weinberg: 2pq, which for a rare allele is close to 2q.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Multiplying the parents\' risks together instead of working from their genotypes.</li><li>Reading 25% as \'one in four of our children\' rather than a per-child probability.</li><li>Confusing a carrier with an affected individual.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/inbreeding-coefficient\\\">Inbreeding coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/autosomal-dominant-pedigree\\\">Dominant pedigree</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"If both parents are carriers, why can a child be unaffected?\",\"a\":\"Because there is a 75% chance the child inherits at least one normal allele.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the risk if one parent is affected and one is a carrier?\",\"a\":\"50% affected, 50% carrier, none completely free of the allele.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns chance of an affected child, chance of a carrier child, chance of an unaffected child and population risk from carrier frequency. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"parent1\",\"label\":\"Parent 1 genotype\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"aa-carrier\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"aa-non-carrier\",\"AA — non-carrier\",\"0\"],[\"aa-carrier\",\"Aa — carrier\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-affected\",\"aa — affected\",\"1\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"parent2\",\"label\":\"Parent 2 genotype\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"aa-carrier\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"aa-non-carrier\",\"AA — non-carrier\",\"0\"],[\"aa-carrier\",\"Aa — carrier\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-affected\",\"aa — affected\",\"1\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"carrier_freq\",\"label\":\"Population carrier frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.04\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional — 2pq from Hardy–Weinberg\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"affected\",\"label\":\"Chance of an affected child\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"carrier_child\",\"label\":\"Chance of a carrier child\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"unaffected\",\"label\":\"Chance of an unaffected child\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"population_risk\",\"label\":\"Population risk from carrier frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Risk\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"affected\",\"f\":\"parent1*parent2*100\"},{\"l\":\"carrier_child\",\"f\":\"(parent1*(1-parent2)+(1-parent1)*parent2)*100\"},{\"l\":\"unaffected\",\"f\":\"(1-parent1)*(1-parent2)*100\"},{\"l\":\"population_risk\",\"f\":\"pow(carrier_freq,2)*0.25*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/autosomal-recessive-risk\",\"slug\":\"autosomal-recessive-risk\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk\",\"title\":\"Autosomal Recessive Inheritance Risk Calculator\",\"desc\":\"For a recessive condition, risk depends on whether each parent carries one copy. Free autosomal recessive inheritance risk calculator: enter your values and g\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-autosomal-recessive-risk\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"parent1\",\"l\":\"Parent 1 genotype\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-parent1\",\"opts\":[[\"aa-non-carrier\",\"AA — non-carrier\",\"0\"],[\"aa-carrier\",\"Aa — carrier\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-affected\",\"aa — affected\",\"1\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"parent2\",\"l\":\"Parent 2 genotype\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-parent2\",\"opts\":[[\"aa-non-carrier\",\"AA — non-carrier\",\"0\"],[\"aa-carrier\",\"Aa — carrier\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-affected\",\"aa — affected\",\"1\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"carrier_freq\",\"l\":\"Population carrier frequency\",\"h\":\"Optional — 2pq from Hardy–Weinberg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"affected\",\"l\":\"Chance of an affected child\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"carrier_child\",\"l\":\"Chance of a carrier child\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"unaffected\",\"l\":\"Chance of an unaffected child\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"population_risk\",\"l\":\"Population risk from carrier frequency\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"parent1\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"aa-carrier\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-parent1\"},\"parent2\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"aa-carrier\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-parent2\"},\"carrier_freq\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.04\",\"c\":null}]},\"affected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"carrier_child\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"unaffected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"population_risk\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-parent1\":{\"name\":\"Parent 1 genotype\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"aa-non-carrier\",\"label\":\"AA — non-carrier\",\"value\":\"0\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-carrier\",\"label\":\"Aa — carrier\",\"value\":\"0.5\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-affected\",\"label\":\"aa — affected\",\"value\":\"1\"}]},\"ds-parent2\":{\"name\":\"Parent 2 genotype\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"aa-non-carrier\",\"label\":\"AA — non-carrier\",\"value\":\"0\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-carrier\",\"label\":\"Aa — carrier\",\"value\":\"0.5\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-affected\",\"label\":\"aa — affected\",\"value\":\"1\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Risk\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"affected\",\"f\":\"parent1*parent2*100\",\"r\":[\"parent1\",\"parent2\"]},{\"l\":\"carrier_child\",\"f\":\"(parent1*(1-parent2)+(1-parent1)*parent2)*100\",\"r\":[\"parent1\",\"parent2\"]},{\"l\":\"unaffected\",\"f\":\"(1-parent1)*(1-parent2)*100\",\"r\":[\"parent1\",\"parent2\"]},{\"l\":\"population_risk\",\"f\":\"pow(carrier_freq,2)*0.25*100\",\"r\":[\"carrier_freq\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'tree', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(93, 'autosomal-dominant-pedigree', 'biology', 'Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer', 'Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer', 'Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer Calculator', 'A dominant allele shows in every generation and has an affected parent for every affected child. Free autosomal dominant pedigree analyzer calculator: enter y', 'A dominant allele shows in every generation and has an affected parent for every affected child. It returns chance of an affected child, chance of an unaffected child, chance the child carries the allele and expected affected fraction at generation 5, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator works out the genotype of each parent, the risk for their children and the chance a healthy child of an affected carrier-free parent still carries the allele. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer\",\"html\":\"<p>Aa × aa → 1 affected : 1 unaffected;  each generation halves the allele\'s share</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/autosomal-dominant-pedigree.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer — An affected heterozygote mated with an unaffected partner.\\\" title=\\\"Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer\\\"><figcaption>An affected heterozygote mated with an unaffected partner.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>the dominant disease allele</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>the normal allele</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>penetrance</code></td><td>share of carriers who show the trait</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: parent 1 genotype set to <strong>Aa — affected heterozygote</strong>, parent 2 genotype set to <strong>0.0</strong>, penetrance of <strong>100</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives chance of an affected child of <strong>50</strong>%, chance of an unaffected child of <strong>50</strong>%, chance the child carries the allele of <strong>50</strong>%, expected affected fraction at generation 5 of <strong>3.12</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Dominant alleles are lost from a pedigree as carriers die without children: the expected share of affected offspring after five generations of mating with unaffected partners is about 3%.</p></li><li><p>Incomplete penetrance makes a dominant allele appear to skip a generation, which is the classic mimic of a recessive pattern.</p></li><li><p>Huntington\'s disease, Marfan syndrome and familial hypercholesterolaemia are the usual textbook autosomal dominant examples.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Assuming a dominant trait must appear in every generation when penetrance is incomplete.</li><li>Ignoring new mutations, which can produce an affected child in an unaffected family.</li><li>Confusing an affected homozygote with a heterozygote — the first passes the allele to every child.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/autosomal-recessive-risk\\\">Recessive inheritance risk</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/pedigree-probability\\\">Pedigree probability</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/relatedness-coefficient\\\">Relatedness coefficient</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Can two affected parents have an unaffected child?\",\"a\":\"Yes, if both are heterozygotes: the child may inherit the normal allele from each.\"},{\"q\":\"What does an affected parent pass on?\",\"a\":\"A heterozygote passes the allele to 50% of children; a homozygote to all of them.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns chance of an affected child, chance of an unaffected child, chance the child carries the allele and expected affected fraction at generation 5. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"parent1\",\"label\":\"Parent 1 genotype\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"aa-affected-homozygote\",\"AA — affected homozygote\",\"1\"],[\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"Aa — affected heterozygote\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-unaffected\",\"aa — unaffected\",\"0\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"parent2\",\"label\":\"Parent 2 genotype\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"aa-unaffected\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"aa-affected-homozygote\",\"AA — affected homozygote\",\"1\"],[\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"Aa — affected heterozygote\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-unaffected\",\"aa — unaffected\",\"0\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"penetrance\",\"label\":\"Penetrance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Below 100% if carriers can appear unaffected\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"affected\",\"label\":\"Chance of an affected child\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"unaffected\",\"label\":\"Chance of an unaffected child\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"carriers\",\"label\":\"Chance the child carries the allele\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"generations\",\"label\":\"Expected affected fraction at generation 5\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Pedigree\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"affected\",\"f\":\"(1-(1-parent1)*(1-parent2))*penetrance/100*100\"},{\"l\":\"unaffected\",\"f\":\"(1-(1-(1-parent1)*(1-parent2))*penetrance/100)*100\"},{\"l\":\"carriers\",\"f\":\"(parent1*(1-parent2)+(1-parent1)*parent2)*penetrance/100*100\"},{\"l\":\"generations\",\"f\":\"pow(1-(1-parent1)*(1-parent2),5)*penetrance/100*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/autosomal-dominant-pedigree\",\"slug\":\"autosomal-dominant-pedigree\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer\",\"title\":\"Autosomal Dominant Pedigree Analyzer Calculator\",\"desc\":\"A dominant allele shows in every generation and has an affected parent for every affected child. Free autosomal dominant pedigree analyzer calculator: enter y\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-autosomal-dominant-pedigree\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"parent1\",\"l\":\"Parent 1 genotype\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-parent1\",\"opts\":[[\"aa-affected-homozygote\",\"AA — affected homozygote\",\"1\"],[\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"Aa — affected heterozygote\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-unaffected\",\"aa — unaffected\",\"0\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"parent2\",\"l\":\"Parent 2 genotype\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-parent2\",\"opts\":[[\"aa-affected-homozygote\",\"AA — affected homozygote\",\"1\"],[\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"Aa — affected heterozygote\",\"0.5\"],[\"aa-unaffected\",\"aa — unaffected\",\"0\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"penetrance\",\"l\":\"Penetrance\",\"h\":\"Below 100% if carriers can appear unaffected\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"affected\",\"l\":\"Chance of an affected child\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"unaffected\",\"l\":\"Chance of an unaffected child\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"carriers\",\"l\":\"Chance the child carries the allele\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"generations\",\"l\":\"Expected affected fraction at generation 5\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"parent1\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-parent1\"},\"parent2\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"aa-unaffected\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-parent2\"},\"penetrance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"affected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"unaffected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"carriers\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"generations\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-parent1\":{\"name\":\"Parent 1 genotype\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"aa-affected-homozygote\",\"label\":\"AA — affected homozygote\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"label\":\"Aa — affected heterozygote\",\"value\":\"0.5\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-unaffected\",\"label\":\"aa — unaffected\",\"value\":\"0\"}]},\"ds-parent2\":{\"name\":\"Parent 2 genotype\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"aa-affected-homozygote\",\"label\":\"AA — affected homozygote\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-affected-heterozygote\",\"label\":\"Aa — affected heterozygote\",\"value\":\"0.5\"},{\"uid\":\"aa-unaffected\",\"label\":\"aa — unaffected\",\"value\":\"0\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Pedigree\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"affected\",\"f\":\"(1-(1-parent1)*(1-parent2))*penetrance/100*100\",\"r\":[\"parent1\",\"parent2\",\"penetrance\"]},{\"l\":\"unaffected\",\"f\":\"(1-(1-(1-parent1)*(1-parent2))*penetrance/100)*100\",\"r\":[\"parent1\",\"parent2\",\"penetrance\"]},{\"l\":\"carriers\",\"f\":\"(parent1*(1-parent2)+(1-parent1)*parent2)*penetrance/100*100\",\"r\":[\"parent1\",\"parent2\",\"penetrance\"]},{\"l\":\"generations\",\"f\":\"pow(1-(1-parent1)*(1-parent2),5)*penetrance/100*100\",\"r\":[\"parent1\",\"parent2\",\"penetrance\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'tree', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(94, 'pedigree-probability', 'biology', 'Pedigree Probability Calculator', 'Pedigree Probability Calculator', 'Pedigree Probability Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Pedigree problems ask for the chance a future child is affected, given what the family tree shows. Free pedigree probability calculator: enter your values and', 'Pedigree problems ask for the chance a future child is affected, given what the family tree shows. It returns chance of an affected child, odds against and expressed as 1 in, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator combines the probability a parent is a carrier with the transmission risk, and reports the joint probability in the form a genetics problem expects. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Pedigree Probability\",\"html\":\"<p>P(affected) = P(parent carrier) × P(partner carrier) × P(transmission)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/pedigree-probability.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Pedigree Probability Calculator — Carrier chance times partner chance times transmission.\\\" title=\\\"Pedigree Probability Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Carrier chance times partner chance times transmission.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>carrier</code></td><td>probability the parent carries the allele</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>transmission</code></td><td>probability two carriers have an affected child</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>joint</code></td><td>product of independent probabilities</td><td>0–1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Pedigree Probability?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: chance the parent is a carrier of <strong>0.667</strong>, chance the partner is a carrier of <strong>0.04</strong>, transmission if both are carriers of <strong>0.25</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives chance of an affected child of <strong>0.667</strong>%, odds against of <strong>148.93</strong> :1, expressed as 1 in of <strong>149.93</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Unaffected siblings of an affected child have a 2/3 chance of being carriers, not 3/4: the affected genotype is excluded from the sample space.</p></li><li><p>Multiply only independent events; a shared ancestor makes the parents\' genotypes dependent, which is what the inbreeding coefficient corrects for.</p></li><li><p>Bayesian updating with test results replaces the prior carrier probability before you multiply.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using 0.25 for the parent\'s carrier probability instead of the pedigree-derived value.</li><li>Forgetting to exclude an affected sibling from the denominator.</li><li>Adding probabilities of independent events instead of multiplying them.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/autosomal-recessive-risk\\\">Recessive inheritance risk</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/inbreeding-coefficient\\\">Inbreeding coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/statistics/bayes-theorem\\\">Bayes\' theorem</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is an unaffected sibling\'s carrier risk 2/3?\",\"a\":\"Of the three genotypes that produce an unaffected child (AA, Aa, aA), two are carriers.\"},{\"q\":\"How does a genetic test change the answer?\",\"a\":\"It replaces the carrier probability with the test\'s posterior probability, which is then multiplied by the partner\'s risk and the transmission rate.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Pedigree Probability actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns chance of an affected child, odds against and expressed as 1 in. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"carrier_prob\",\"label\":\"Chance the parent is a carrier\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.667\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From the pedigree\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"partner_carrier\",\"label\":\"Chance the partner is a carrier\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.04\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Population frequency\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"transmission\",\"label\":\"Transmission if both are carriers\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.25 for a recessive trait\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"joint\",\"label\":\"Chance of an affected child\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"odds\",\"label\":\"Odds against\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"one_in\",\"label\":\"Expressed as 1 in\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Probability\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"joint\",\"f\":\"carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission*100\"},{\"l\":\"odds\",\"f\":\"(1-carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission)/(carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission)\"},{\"l\":\"one_in\",\"f\":\"1/(carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/pedigree-probability\",\"slug\":\"pedigree-probability\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Pedigree Probability Calculator\",\"title\":\"Pedigree Probability Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Pedigree problems ask for the chance a future child is affected, given what the family tree shows. Free pedigree probability calculator: enter your values and\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-pedigree-probability\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"carrier_prob\",\"l\":\"Chance the parent is a carrier\",\"h\":\"From the pedigree\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"partner_carrier\",\"l\":\"Chance the partner is a carrier\",\"h\":\"Population frequency\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"transmission\",\"l\":\"Transmission if both are carriers\",\"h\":\"0.25 for a recessive trait\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"joint\",\"l\":\"Chance of an affected child\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"odds\",\"l\":\"Odds against\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"one_in\",\"l\":\"Expressed as 1 in\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"carrier_prob\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.667\",\"c\":null}]},\"partner_carrier\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.04\",\"c\":null}]},\"transmission\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.25\",\"c\":null}]},\"joint\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"odds\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"one_in\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Probability\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"joint\",\"f\":\"carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission*100\",\"r\":[\"carrier_prob\",\"partner_carrier\",\"transmission\"]},{\"l\":\"odds\",\"f\":\"(1-carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission)/(carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission)\",\"r\":[\"carrier_prob\",\"partner_carrier\",\"transmission\"]},{\"l\":\"one_in\",\"f\":\"1/(carrier_prob*partner_carrier*transmission)\",\"r\":[\"carrier_prob\",\"partner_carrier\",\"transmission\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'tree', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(95, 'shannon-wiener-index', 'biology', 'Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index Calculator', 'Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index Calculator', 'Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The Shannon–Wiener index measures how uncertain you would be, picking one individual at random, about which species it belongs to. Free shannon–wiener diversi', 'The Shannon–Wiener index measures how uncertain you would be, picking one individual at random, about which species it belongs to. It returns species richness, shannon index H′, pielou evenness J′ and maximum H′ for this richness, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt rises with both the number of species and the evenness of their abundances, which is why it is the default diversity measure in ecology. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index\",\"html\":\"<p>H′ = −Σ pᵢ ln pᵢ,  where pᵢ = nᵢ ÷ N</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/shannon-wiener-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index Calculator — Species abundances, and the diversity they add up to.\\\" title=\\\"Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Species abundances, and the diversity they add up to.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>nᵢ</code></td><td>individuals of species i</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>pᵢ</code></td><td>that species\' share of all individuals</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>H′</code></td><td>Shannon–Wiener index</td><td>nats</td></tr><tr><td><code>J′</code></td><td>Pielou evenness, H′ ÷ ln S</td><td>0–1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: species 1 count of <strong>40</strong> individuals, species 2 count of <strong>25</strong> individuals, species 3 count of <strong>15</strong> individuals, species 4 count of <strong>10</strong> individuals, species 5 count of <strong>6</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives species richness of <strong>5</strong>, shannon index h′ of <strong>1.41</strong>, pielou evenness j′ of <strong>0.8786</strong>, maximum h′ for this richness of <strong>1.61</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>H′ below 1.0</td><td><strong>Low diversity</strong></td><td>dominated by one or two species</td></tr><tr><td>1.0 – 2.5</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>typical of many temperate communities</td></tr><tr><td>above 2.5</td><td><strong>High diversity</strong></td><td>even, species-rich assemblages such as reefs or rainforest</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>H′ is in nats when the logarithm is natural; versions using log₂ or log₁₀ give different numbers, so always state the base.</p></li><li><p>The index is sample-size dependent — a bigger sample almost always raises it. Compare rarefied values, or fix the effort between sites.</p></li><li><p>Pielou evenness J′ = H′ / ln S divides out richness, so a community can be poor but very even and score a high J′.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Mixing log bases between the index and the evenness calculation.</li><li>Comparing H′ from quadrats of different size or counted by different people.</li><li>Entering zeros for unrecorded species, which does not change H′ but does change richness.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/biodiversity-index\\\">Biodiversity index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/species-richness\\\">Species richness</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/sorensen-index\\\">Sørensen similarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good Shannon index value?\",\"a\":\"There is no universal threshold: values between 1.5 and 3.5 are common in nature, and the number is only meaningful next to another sample taken the same way.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between H′ and the Simpson index?\",\"a\":\"Shannon weights rare species more heavily; Simpson is dominated by the commonest ones.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns species richness, shannon index H′, pielou evenness J′ and maximum H′ for this richness. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n1\",\"label\":\"Species 1 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n2\",\"label\":\"Species 2 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n3\",\"label\":\"Species 3 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"15\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n4\",\"label\":\"Species 4 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n5\",\"label\":\"Species 5 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"h\",\"label\":\"Shannon index H′\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"evenness\",\"label\":\"Pielou evenness J′\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"richness\",\"label\":\"Species richness\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"max_h\",\"label\":\"Maximum H′ for this richness\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Diversity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"richness\",\"f\":\"min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))\"},{\"l\":\"h\",\"f\":\"(0-((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))))\"},{\"l\":\"evenness\",\"f\":\"((0-((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))))/log(max(1,min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5)))))\"},{\"l\":\"max_h\",\"f\":\"log(max(1,min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/shannon-wiener-index\",\"slug\":\"shannon-wiener-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index Calculator\",\"title\":\"Shannon–Wiener Diversity Index Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The Shannon–Wiener index measures how uncertain you would be, picking one individual at random, about which species it belongs to. Free shannon–wiener diversi\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-shannon-wiener-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n1\",\"l\":\"Species 1 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n2\",\"l\":\"Species 2 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n3\",\"l\":\"Species 3 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n4\",\"l\":\"Species 4 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n5\",\"l\":\"Species 5 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"h\",\"l\":\"Shannon index H′\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"evenness\",\"l\":\"Pielou evenness J′\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"richness\",\"l\":\"Species richness\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"max_h\",\"l\":\"Maximum H′ for this richness\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"n2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"n3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"15\",\"c\":null}]},\"n4\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"n5\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"h\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"evenness\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"richness\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"max_h\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Diversity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"richness\",\"f\":\"min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"h\",\"f\":\"(0-((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"evenness\",\"f\":\"((0-((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n1,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n2,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n3,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n4,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))+(max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5))*log((max(n5,0.000001)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)))))/log(max(1,min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5)))))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"max_h\",\"f\":\"log(max(1,min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(96, 'biodiversity-index', 'biology', 'Biodiversity Index Calculator (Simpson)', 'Biodiversity Index Calculator (Simpson)', 'Biodiversity Index (Simpson) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The Simpson index is the probability that two individuals drawn at random belong to the same species. Free biodiversity index (simpson) calculator: enter your', 'The Simpson index is the probability that two individuals drawn at random belong to the same species. It returns total individuals, simpson dominance D, simpson diversity 1 − D and inverse Simpson 1 ÷ D, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nSubtract it from one and you have Simpson\'s diversity, the probability that they belong to different species — the most intuitive diversity number there is. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Biodiversity Index (Simpson)\",\"html\":\"<p>D = Σ nᵢ(nᵢ − 1) ÷ [N(N − 1)];  diversity = 1 − D;  inverse = 1 ÷ D</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/biodiversity-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Biodiversity Index Calculator (Simpson) — Dominance against diversity for the same counts.\\\" title=\\\"Biodiversity Index Calculator (Simpson)\\\"><figcaption>Dominance against diversity for the same counts.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>nᵢ</code></td><td>individuals of species i</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>all individuals</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>D</code></td><td>probability two draws are the same species</td><td>0–1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Biodiversity Index (Simpson)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: species 1 count of <strong>40</strong> individuals, species 2 count of <strong>25</strong> individuals, species 3 count of <strong>15</strong> individuals, species 4 count of <strong>10</strong> individuals, species 5 count of <strong>6</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives total individuals of <strong>96</strong>, simpson dominance d of <strong>0.273</strong>, simpson diversity 1 − d of <strong>0.727</strong>, inverse simpson 1 ÷ d of <strong>3.66</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 − D below 0.5</td><td><strong>Low diversity</strong></td><td>one or two species dominate</td></tr><tr><td>0.5 – 0.8</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>mixed community with clear dominants</td></tr><tr><td>above 0.8</td><td><strong>High diversity</strong></td><td>even, species-rich community</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The n(n − 1) form is the unbiased estimator for sampling without replacement; the simpler Σ p² is the population version and is slightly smaller.</p></li><li><p>Inverse Simpson, 1/D, is read as \'the effective number of equally common species\' and is usually easier to explain than the index itself.</p></li><li><p>Simpson is less sensitive to rare species than Shannon, so it is the index to use when sampling is incomplete.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using the biased Σ p² formula and calling it the unbiased Simpson index.</li><li>Averaging index values from replicate quadrats instead of computing from pooled counts.</li><li>Reporting D (dominance) when you mean 1 − D (diversity).</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/shannon-wiener-index\\\">Shannon–Wiener index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/species-richness\\\">Species richness</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/berger-parker-index\\\">Berger–Parker index</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Is a higher Simpson index more diverse?\",\"a\":\"It depends which one: D (dominance) rises with uniformity of a single species being drawn twice, so higher D means lower diversity. 1 − D and 1/D rise with diversity.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does Simpson need the n − 1 terms?\",\"a\":\"Because the second draw cannot be the same individual as the first.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Biodiversity Index (Simpson) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns total individuals, simpson dominance D, simpson diversity 1 − D and inverse Simpson 1 ÷ D. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n1\",\"label\":\"Species 1 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n2\",\"label\":\"Species 2 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n3\",\"label\":\"Species 3 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"15\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n4\",\"label\":\"Species 4 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n5\",\"label\":\"Species 5 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dom\",\"label\":\"Simpson dominance D\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"div\",\"label\":\"Simpson diversity 1 − D\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"inv\",\"label\":\"Inverse Simpson 1 ÷ D\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Total individuals\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Diversity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n1+n2+n3+n4+n5\"},{\"l\":\"dom\",\"f\":\"(max(n1,0)*(max(n1,0)-1)+max(n2,0)*(max(n2,0)-1)+max(n3,0)*(max(n3,0)-1)+max(n4,0)*(max(n4,0)-1)+max(n5,0)*(max(n5,0)-1))/((n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5-1))\"},{\"l\":\"div\",\"f\":\"1-(max(n1,0)*(max(n1,0)-1)+max(n2,0)*(max(n2,0)-1)+max(n3,0)*(max(n3,0)-1)+max(n4,0)*(max(n4,0)-1)+max(n5,0)*(max(n5,0)-1))/((n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5-1))\"},{\"l\":\"inv\",\"f\":\"1/((max(n1,0)*(max(n1,0)-1)+max(n2,0)*(max(n2,0)-1)+max(n3,0)*(max(n3,0)-1)+max(n4,0)*(max(n4,0)-1)+max(n5,0)*(max(n5,0)-1))/((n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5-1)))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/biodiversity-index\",\"slug\":\"biodiversity-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Biodiversity Index Calculator (Simpson)\",\"title\":\"Biodiversity Index (Simpson) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The Simpson index is the probability that two individuals drawn at random belong to the same species. Free biodiversity index (simpson) calculator: enter your\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-biodiversity-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n1\",\"l\":\"Species 1 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n2\",\"l\":\"Species 2 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n3\",\"l\":\"Species 3 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n4\",\"l\":\"Species 4 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n5\",\"l\":\"Species 5 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dom\",\"l\":\"Simpson dominance D\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"div\",\"l\":\"Simpson diversity 1 − D\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"inv\",\"l\":\"Inverse Simpson 1 ÷ D\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"Total individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"n2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"n3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"15\",\"c\":null}]},\"n4\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"n5\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"dom\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"div\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"inv\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Diversity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n1+n2+n3+n4+n5\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"dom\",\"f\":\"(max(n1,0)*(max(n1,0)-1)+max(n2,0)*(max(n2,0)-1)+max(n3,0)*(max(n3,0)-1)+max(n4,0)*(max(n4,0)-1)+max(n5,0)*(max(n5,0)-1))/((n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5-1))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"div\",\"f\":\"1-(max(n1,0)*(max(n1,0)-1)+max(n2,0)*(max(n2,0)-1)+max(n3,0)*(max(n3,0)-1)+max(n4,0)*(max(n4,0)-1)+max(n5,0)*(max(n5,0)-1))/((n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5-1))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"inv\",\"f\":\"1/((max(n1,0)*(max(n1,0)-1)+max(n2,0)*(max(n2,0)-1)+max(n3,0)*(max(n3,0)-1)+max(n4,0)*(max(n4,0)-1)+max(n5,0)*(max(n5,0)-1))/((n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5-1)))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(97, 'berger-parker-index', 'biology', 'Berger–Parker Index Calculator', 'Berger–Parker Index Calculator', 'Berger–Parker Index Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The Berger–Parker index is the simplest dominance measure in ecology: the share of all individuals belonging to the commonest species. Free berger–parker inde', 'The Berger–Parker index is the simplest dominance measure in ecology: the share of all individuals belonging to the commonest species. It returns berger–Parker index d, reciprocal 1/d and dominance, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt needs no logarithm, no assumption about sampling, and it is easy to explain to a non-specialist. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Berger–Parker Index\",\"html\":\"<p>d = N_max ÷ N</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/berger-parker-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Berger–Parker Index Calculator — The dominant species against everything else.\\\" title=\\\"Berger–Parker Index Calculator\\\"><figcaption>The dominant species against everything else.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>N_max</code></td><td>individuals of the commonest species</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>all individuals in the sample</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>d</code></td><td>dominance</td><td>0–1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Berger–Parker Index?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: count of the commonest species of <strong>120</strong> individuals, total individuals of <strong>400</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives berger–parker index d of <strong>0.3</strong>, reciprocal 1/d of <strong>3.33</strong>, dominance of <strong>30</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>d below 0.2</td><td><strong>Even community</strong></td><td>no species holds more than a fifth</td></tr><tr><td>0.2 – 0.5</td><td><strong>Moderate dominance</strong></td><td>typical of many temperate samples</td></tr><tr><td>above 0.5</td><td><strong>Strong dominance</strong></td><td>one species holds most individuals</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The reciprocal 1/d is the effective number of species and is easier to read: d = 0.25 means about four equally common species.</p></li><li><p>Berger–Parker ignores everything about the other species except their total, so two very different communities can share an index.</p></li><li><p>It is widely used in pollution and disturbance studies because it responds quickly and needs no specialist taxonomy beyond the dominant species.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the total of a subsample in the numerator and the whole sample in the denominator.</li><li>Treating the index as a diversity measure — it is a dominance measure, so higher is less diverse.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/biodiversity-index\\\">Biodiversity index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/relative-abundance\\\">Relative abundance</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/shannon-wiener-index\\\">Shannon–Wiener index</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What does a Berger–Parker index of 0.1 mean?\",\"a\":\"The commonest species is 10% of all individuals.\"},{\"q\":\"How does it relate to Simpson\'s index?\",\"a\":\"Both are dominance measures; Berger–Parker uses only the top species, Simpson uses all of them.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Berger–Parker Index actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns berger–Parker index d, reciprocal 1/d and dominance. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"dominant\",\"label\":\"Count of the commonest species\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"120\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total individuals\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"400\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"index\",\"label\":\"Berger–Parker index d\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"reciprocal\",\"label\":\"Reciprocal 1/d\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"dominance\",\"label\":\"Dominance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Dominance\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"index\",\"f\":\"dominant/total\"},{\"l\":\"reciprocal\",\"f\":\"total/dominant\"},{\"l\":\"dominance\",\"f\":\"dominant/total*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/berger-parker-index\",\"slug\":\"berger-parker-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Berger–Parker Index Calculator\",\"title\":\"Berger–Parker Index Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The Berger–Parker index is the simplest dominance measure in ecology: the share of all individuals belonging to the commonest species. Free berger–parker inde\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-berger-parker-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dominant\",\"l\":\"Count of the commonest species\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total individuals\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"index\",\"l\":\"Berger–Parker index d\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"reciprocal\",\"l\":\"Reciprocal 1/d\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dominance\",\"l\":\"Dominance\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"dominant\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"120\",\"c\":null}]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"400\",\"c\":null}]},\"index\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"reciprocal\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"dominance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Dominance\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"index\",\"f\":\"dominant/total\",\"r\":[\"dominant\",\"total\"]},{\"l\":\"reciprocal\",\"f\":\"total/dominant\",\"r\":[\"total\",\"dominant\"]},{\"l\":\"dominance\",\"f\":\"dominant/total*100\",\"r\":[\"dominant\",\"total\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(98, 'species-richness', 'biology', 'Species Richness Calculator', 'Species Richness Calculator', 'Species Richness Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Species richness is simply the number of species present — the most basic biodiversity measure and the one every other index is compared with. Free species ri', 'Species richness is simply the number of species present — the most basic biodiversity measure and the one every other index is compared with. It returns total individuals, species richness S, margalef index and menhinick index, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator counts them from a list of abundances, adds the Margalef richness index and estimates how many species a larger sample would find. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Species Richness\",\"html\":\"<p>S = number of species with at least one individual;  Margalef = (S − 1) ÷ ln N</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/species-richness.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Species Richness Calculator — Five species and the individuals counted in each.\\\" title=\\\"Species Richness Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Five species and the individuals counted in each.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>S</code></td><td>species richness</td><td>species</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>total individuals counted</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>Margalef</code></td><td>richness corrected for effort</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Species Richness?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: species 1 count of <strong>40</strong> individuals, species 2 count of <strong>25</strong> individuals, species 3 count of <strong>15</strong> individuals, species 4 count of <strong>10</strong> individuals, species 5 count of <strong>6</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives total individuals of <strong>96</strong>, species richness s of <strong>5</strong>, margalef index of <strong>0.8764</strong>, menhinick index of <strong>0.5103</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Richness depends on effort: doubling the area or the time almost always adds species, which is why raw S is a poor comparison between studies.</p></li><li><p>Margalef and Menhinick divide by a function of N, so they compare samples of different size — imperfectly, but far better than raw counts.</p></li><li><p>Rarefaction curves plot expected richness against sample size and show when the inventory has plateaued.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Recording zero for a species you simply did not see, then reporting S as complete.</li><li>Comparing richness between a 1 m² quadrat and a 100 m² plot.</li><li>Counting morphospecies as species without saying so.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/shannon-wiener-index\\\">Shannon–Wiener index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/quadrat-sampling-density\\\">Quadrat sampling</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/sorensen-index\\\">Sørensen similarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the difference between richness and diversity?\",\"a\":\"Richness counts species; diversity also weighs how evenly individuals are spread among them.\"},{\"q\":\"How many samples do I need before richness stops rising?\",\"a\":\"Until the rarefaction curve flattens — on average 20–50 standardised samples for invertebrates, more for plants.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Species Richness actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns total individuals, species richness S, margalef index and menhinick index. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n1\",\"label\":\"Species 1 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n2\",\"label\":\"Species 2 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n3\",\"label\":\"Species 3 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"15\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n4\",\"label\":\"Species 4 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n5\",\"label\":\"Species 5 count\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"richness\",\"label\":\"Species richness S\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Total individuals\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"margalef\",\"label\":\"Margalef index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"menhinick\",\"label\":\"Menhinick index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Richness\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n1+n2+n3+n4+n5\"},{\"l\":\"richness\",\"f\":\"min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))\"},{\"l\":\"margalef\",\"f\":\"(min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))-1)/log(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)\"},{\"l\":\"menhinick\",\"f\":\"(min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5)))/sqrt(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/species-richness\",\"slug\":\"species-richness\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Species Richness Calculator\",\"title\":\"Species Richness Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Species richness is simply the number of species present — the most basic biodiversity measure and the one every other index is compared with. Free species ri\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-species-richness\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n1\",\"l\":\"Species 1 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n2\",\"l\":\"Species 2 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n3\",\"l\":\"Species 3 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n4\",\"l\":\"Species 4 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n5\",\"l\":\"Species 5 count\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"richness\",\"l\":\"Species richness S\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"Total individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"margalef\",\"l\":\"Margalef index\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"menhinick\",\"l\":\"Menhinick index\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"n2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"n3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"15\",\"c\":null}]},\"n4\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"n5\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"richness\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"margalef\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"menhinick\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Richness\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n1+n2+n3+n4+n5\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"richness\",\"f\":\"min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"margalef\",\"f\":\"(min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5))-1)/log(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"menhinick\",\"f\":\"(min(1,max(0,n1))+min(1,max(0,n2))+min(1,max(0,n3))+min(1,max(0,n4))+min(1,max(0,n5)))/sqrt(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(99, 'sorensen-index', 'biology', 'Sørensen Similarity Index Calculator', 'Sørensen Similarity Index Calculator', 'Sørensen Similarity Index Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The Sørensen index compares the species lists of two sites: it is twice the number of shared species divided by the total number of species records in both. F', 'The Sørensen index compares the species lists of two sites: it is twice the number of shared species divided by the total number of species records in both. It returns sørensen similarity, jaccard similarity, sørensen dissimilarity and beta diversity (Sørensen), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt runs from 0 (nothing in common) to 1 (identical communities) and is the standard beta-diversity measure. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Sørensen Similarity Index\",\"html\":\"<p>Sørensen = 2C ÷ (A + B),  where A + C and B + C are the two species totals</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/sorensen-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Sørensen Similarity Index Calculator — Shared and unique species between two sites.\\\" title=\\\"Sørensen Similarity Index Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Shared and unique species between two sites.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>C</code></td><td>species present at both sites</td><td>species</td></tr><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>species only at the first site</td><td>species</td></tr><tr><td><code>B</code></td><td>species only at the second site</td><td>species</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Sørensen Similarity Index?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: species only at site a of <strong>12</strong> species, species only at site b of <strong>8</strong> species, species at both sites of <strong>20</strong> species.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives sørensen similarity of <strong>0.6667</strong>, jaccard similarity of <strong>0.5</strong>, sørensen dissimilarity of <strong>33.33</strong>%, beta diversity (sørensen) of <strong>0.3333</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 0.25</td><td><strong>Very different</strong></td><td>the sites share few species</td></tr><tr><td>0.25 – 0.6</td><td><strong>Moderate overlap</strong></td><td>some shared core, many local species</td></tr><tr><td>above 0.6</td><td><strong>Similar</strong></td><td>the two communities largely coincide</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Sørensen is the same as the Dice coefficient on presence–absence data; Jaccard is always lower because it divides by the union rather than the average.</p></li><li><p>The index ignores abundance: one shared rare species counts as much as a shared dominant.</p></li><li><p>Sørensen dissimilarity, 1 − similarity, plus the species turnover between sites is beta diversity, which measures how much communities change across a landscape.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using total species per site instead of the shared and unique counts.</li><li>Comparing sites sampled with different effort, which inflates the unique lists.</li><li>Reporting Jaccard and calling it Sørensen, which overstates dissimilarity.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/species-richness\\\">Species richness</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/shannon-wiener-index\\\">Shannon–Wiener index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/relative-abundance\\\">Relative abundance</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Are Sørensen and Dice the same?\",\"a\":\"Yes on presence–absence data — the arithmetic is identical.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is Sørensen preferred to Jaccard in ecology?\",\"a\":\"It is less sensitive to the total number of species, so it compares samples of different richness more fairly.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Sørensen Similarity Index actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns sørensen similarity, jaccard similarity, sørensen dissimilarity and beta diversity (Sørensen). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Species only at site A\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"species\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Species only at site B\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"species\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Species at both sites\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"species\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"sorensen\",\"label\":\"Sørensen similarity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"jaccard\",\"label\":\"Jaccard similarity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"dissimilarity\",\"label\":\"Sørensen dissimilarity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"beta\",\"label\":\"Beta diversity (Sørensen)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Similarity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"sorensen\",\"f\":\"2*c/(2*c+a+b)\"},{\"l\":\"jaccard\",\"f\":\"c/(a+b+c)\"},{\"l\":\"dissimilarity\",\"f\":\"(1-2*c/(2*c+a+b))*100\"},{\"l\":\"beta\",\"f\":\"(a+b)/(2*c+a+b)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/sorensen-index\",\"slug\":\"sorensen-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Sørensen Similarity Index Calculator\",\"title\":\"Sørensen Similarity Index Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The Sørensen index compares the species lists of two sites: it is twice the number of shared species divided by the total number of species records in both. F\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-sorensen-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a\",\"l\":\"Species only at site A\",\"suf\":\"species\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"b\",\"l\":\"Species only at site B\",\"suf\":\"species\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"c\",\"l\":\"Species at both sites\",\"suf\":\"species\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sorensen\",\"l\":\"Sørensen similarity\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"jaccard\",\"l\":\"Jaccard similarity\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dissimilarity\",\"l\":\"Sørensen dissimilarity\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"beta\",\"l\":\"Beta diversity (Sørensen)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"a\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"b\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8\",\"c\":null}]},\"c\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"sorensen\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"jaccard\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"dissimilarity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"beta\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Similarity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"sorensen\",\"f\":\"2*c/(2*c+a+b)\",\"r\":[\"c\",\"a\",\"b\"]},{\"l\":\"jaccard\",\"f\":\"c/(a+b+c)\",\"r\":[\"c\",\"a\",\"b\"]},{\"l\":\"dissimilarity\",\"f\":\"(1-2*c/(2*c+a+b))*100\",\"r\":[\"c\",\"a\",\"b\"]},{\"l\":\"beta\",\"f\":\"(a+b)/(2*c+a+b)\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(100, 'relative-abundance', 'biology', 'Relative Abundance Calculator', 'Relative Abundance Calculator', 'Relative Abundance Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Relative abundance turns raw counts into the share of the community each species represents — the input every diversity index, ordination and community table', 'Relative abundance turns raw counts into the share of the community each species represents — the input every diversity index, ordination and community table starts from. It returns community size, species 1 relative abundance, species 2 relative abundance and top three species combined, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives percentages, proportions and the cumulative total. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Relative Abundance\",\"html\":\"<p>pᵢ = nᵢ ÷ N × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/relative-abundance.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Relative Abundance Calculator — Counts and the share of the community each species holds.\\\" title=\\\"Relative Abundance Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Counts and the share of the community each species holds.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>nᵢ</code></td><td>individuals of species i</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>total individuals</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>pᵢ</code></td><td>relative abundance</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Relative Abundance?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: species 1 of <strong>40</strong> individuals, species 2 of <strong>25</strong> individuals, species 3 of <strong>15</strong> individuals, species 4 of <strong>10</strong> individuals, species 5 of <strong>6</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives community size of <strong>96</strong> individuals, species 1 relative abundance of <strong>41.67</strong>%, species 2 relative abundance of <strong>26.04</strong>%, top three species combined of <strong>83.33</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Relative abundance makes samples of different size comparable, which absolute counts do not.</p></li><li><p>Percentages hide effort: 100% of a sample of three individuals is not the same statement as 100% of a sample of 3 000.</p></li><li><p>In sequencing data relative abundance is read out of the number of reads per taxon and is usually reported after rarefying to a common depth.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using relative abundance as if it were an absolute density.</li><li>Summing percentages from different samples.</li><li>Comparing relative abundances between studies that rarefied to different depths.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/shannon-wiener-index\\\">Shannon–Wiener index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/berger-parker-index\\\">Berger–Parker index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/quadrat-sampling-density\\\">Quadrat sampling</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I calculate relative abundance as a proportion?\",\"a\":\"Divide the count by the total without multiplying by 100 — 0.4 rather than 40%.\"},{\"q\":\"Should the percentages add to 100?\",\"a\":\"They do, up to rounding; the calculator works from full precision so the small residual is only display.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Relative Abundance actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns community size, species 1 relative abundance, species 2 relative abundance and top three species combined. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n1\",\"label\":\"Species 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n2\",\"label\":\"Species 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n3\",\"label\":\"Species 3\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"15\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n4\",\"label\":\"Species 4\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n5\",\"label\":\"Species 5\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"p1\",\"label\":\"Species 1 relative abundance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"p2\",\"label\":\"Species 2 relative abundance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Community size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"top3\",\"label\":\"Top three species combined\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Abundance\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n1+n2+n3+n4+n5\"},{\"l\":\"p1\",\"f\":\"n1/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*100\"},{\"l\":\"p2\",\"f\":\"n2/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*100\"},{\"l\":\"top3\",\"f\":\"(n1+n2+n3)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/relative-abundance\",\"slug\":\"relative-abundance\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Relative Abundance Calculator\",\"title\":\"Relative Abundance Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Relative abundance turns raw counts into the share of the community each species represents — the input every diversity index, ordination and community table\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-relative-abundance\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n1\",\"l\":\"Species 1\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n2\",\"l\":\"Species 2\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n3\",\"l\":\"Species 3\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n4\",\"l\":\"Species 4\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n5\",\"l\":\"Species 5\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p1\",\"l\":\"Species 1 relative abundance\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p2\",\"l\":\"Species 2 relative abundance\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"Community size\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"top3\",\"l\":\"Top three species combined\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"n2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"n3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"15\",\"c\":null}]},\"n4\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"n5\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"p1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"p2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"top3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Abundance\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n1+n2+n3+n4+n5\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"p1\",\"f\":\"n1/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*100\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"p2\",\"f\":\"n2/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*100\",\"r\":[\"n2\",\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]},{\"l\":\"top3\",\"f\":\"(n1+n2+n3)/(n1+n2+n3+n4+n5)*100\",\"r\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(101, 'mark-recapture-population', 'biology', 'Mark–Recapture Population Calculator (Lincoln–Petersen)', 'Mark–Recapture Population Calculator (Lincoln–Petersen)', 'Mark–Recapture Population (Lincoln–Petersen) Calculator', 'Mark a sample, release it, catch a second sample and count how many are marked: the share of marked animals in the second sample estimates the share of the wh', 'Mark a sample, release it, catch a second sample and count how many are marked: the share of marked animals in the second sample estimates the share of the whole population. It returns population estimate (Lincoln–Petersen), chapman-corrected estimate, standard error and 95% lower bound, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives the Lincoln–Petersen estimate, the Chapman small-sample correction and a confidence interval. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Mark–Recapture Population (Lincoln–Petersen)\",\"html\":\"<p>N ≈ (M × C) ÷ R,  with Chapman\'s correction N = [(M+1)(C+1) ÷ (R+1)] − 1</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/mark-recapture-population.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Mark–Recapture Population Calculator (Lincoln–Petersen) — Marked animals, second sample and recaptures give the population size.\\\" title=\\\"Mark–Recapture Population Calculator (Lincoln–Petersen)\\\"><figcaption>Marked animals, second sample and recaptures give the population size.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>M</code></td><td>marked and released in the first sample</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>C</code></td><td>total caught in the second sample</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>R</code></td><td>marked individuals in the second sample</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>estimated population size</td><td>individuals</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Mark–Recapture Population (Lincoln–Petersen)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: animals marked and released (m) of <strong>120</strong> individuals, animals in the second sample (c) of <strong>100</strong> individuals, marked animals recaptured (r) of <strong>22</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives population estimate (lincoln–petersen) of <strong>545.45</strong> individuals, chapman-corrected estimate of <strong>530.35</strong> individuals, standard error of <strong>527.58</strong> individuals, 95% lower bound of <strong>-3,389.75</strong> individuals, 95% upper bound of <strong>4,450.44</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>R above 20</td><td><strong>Reliable</strong></td><td>the estimate is stable and the interval is narrow</td></tr><tr><td>R 5 – 20</td><td><strong>Use the correction</strong></td><td>Chapman\'s version removes most of the bias</td></tr><tr><td>R below 5</td><td><strong>Poor</strong></td><td>collect another sample; the interval is wider than the estimate</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The method assumes a closed population (no births, deaths, immigration or emigration between samples), equal catchability and that marks are not lost.</p></li><li><p>Trapshy animals violate equal catchability and inflate the estimate; baited traps and trap-happy animals deflate it.</p></li><li><p>The Chapman estimator is less biased than the plain ratio at the sample sizes field biologists actually achieve.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Sampling too soon after marking, before the animals have mixed back into the population.</li><li>Marking only the largest individuals, which makes them easier to recapture.</li><li>Reporting a point estimate with no interval, which overstates what a single sample can say.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/quadrat-sampling-density\\\">Quadrat sampling density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/catch-per-unit-effort\\\">Catch per unit effort</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/logistic-population-growth\\\">Logistic growth</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is my recapture rate so low?\",\"a\":\"Either the population is large or the second sample was small; the estimator needs at least five to twenty recaptures to be meaningful.\"},{\"q\":\"What if marked animals die from handling?\",\"a\":\"That violates the closure assumption and biases the estimate; use a model that allows for losses on capture (Cormack–Jolly–Seber).\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Mark–Recapture Population (Lincoln–Petersen) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns population estimate (Lincoln–Petersen), chapman-corrected estimate, standard error and 95% lower bound. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"marked\",\"label\":\"Animals marked and released (M)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"120\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"second\",\"label\":\"Animals in the second sample (C)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"recaptured\",\"label\":\"Marked animals recaptured (R)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"22\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"estimate\",\"label\":\"Population estimate (Lincoln–Petersen)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"chapman\",\"label\":\"Chapman-corrected estimate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"se\",\"label\":\"Standard error\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"low\",\"label\":\"95% lower bound\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"high\",\"label\":\"95% upper bound\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Estimate\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"estimate\",\"f\":\"marked*second/recaptured\"},{\"l\":\"chapman\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1\"},{\"l\":\"se\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1*sqrt((marked-recaptured+1)*(second-recaptured+1)/((recaptured+1)*(recaptured+2)))\"},{\"l\":\"low\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1-1.96*(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)*sqrt((marked-recaptured+1)*(second-recaptured+1)/((recaptured+1)*(recaptured+2)))\"},{\"l\":\"high\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1+1.96*(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)*sqrt((marked-recaptured+1)*(second-recaptured+1)/((recaptured+1)*(recaptured+2)))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/mark-recapture-population\",\"slug\":\"mark-recapture-population\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Mark–Recapture Population Calculator (Lincoln–Petersen)\",\"title\":\"Mark–Recapture Population (Lincoln–Petersen) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Mark a sample, release it, catch a second sample and count how many are marked: the share of marked animals in the second sample estimates the share of the wh\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-mark-recapture-population\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"marked\",\"l\":\"Animals marked and released (M)\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"second\",\"l\":\"Animals in the second sample (C)\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"recaptured\",\"l\":\"Marked animals recaptured (R)\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"estimate\",\"l\":\"Population estimate (Lincoln–Petersen)\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"chapman\",\"l\":\"Chapman-corrected estimate\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"se\",\"l\":\"Standard error\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"low\",\"l\":\"95% lower bound\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"high\",\"l\":\"95% upper bound\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"marked\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"120\",\"c\":null}]},\"second\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"recaptured\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"22\",\"c\":null}]},\"estimate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"chapman\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"se\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"low\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"high\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Estimate\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"estimate\",\"f\":\"marked*second/recaptured\",\"r\":[\"marked\",\"second\",\"recaptured\"]},{\"l\":\"chapman\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1\",\"r\":[\"marked\",\"second\",\"recaptured\"]},{\"l\":\"se\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1*sqrt((marked-recaptured+1)*(second-recaptured+1)/((recaptured+1)*(recaptured+2)))\",\"r\":[\"marked\",\"second\",\"recaptured\"]},{\"l\":\"low\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1-1.96*(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)*sqrt((marked-recaptured+1)*(second-recaptured+1)/((recaptured+1)*(recaptured+2)))\",\"r\":[\"marked\",\"second\",\"recaptured\"]},{\"l\":\"high\",\"f\":\"(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)-1+1.96*(marked+1)*(second+1)/(recaptured+1)*sqrt((marked-recaptured+1)*(second-recaptured+1)/((recaptured+1)*(recaptured+2)))\",\"r\":[\"marked\",\"second\",\"recaptured\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'paw', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(102, 'quadrat-sampling-density', 'biology', 'Quadrat Sampling Density Calculator', 'Quadrat Sampling Density Calculator', 'Quadrat Sampling Density Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Quadrat sampling estimates density from a small measured area and scales it up. Free quadrat sampling density calculator: enter your values and get mean per q', 'Quadrat sampling estimates density from a small measured area and scales it up. It returns mean per quadrat, density per m², density per hectare and estimated population on the site, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives individuals per square metre and per hectare, the mean and standard error per quadrat, and the total population for a site of known area. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Quadrat Sampling Density\",\"html\":\"<p>Density = individuals ÷ (quadrats × quadrat area);  N = density × site area</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/quadrat-sampling-density.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Quadrat Sampling Density Calculator — Quadrats counted, scaled to a square metre and to the whole site.\\\" title=\\\"Quadrat Sampling Density Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Quadrats counted, scaled to a square metre and to the whole site.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>individuals counted in all quadrats</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>number of quadrats</td><td>quadrats</td></tr><tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>area of one quadrat</td><td>m²</td></tr><tr><td><code>D</code></td><td>density</td><td>individuals/m²</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Quadrat Sampling Density?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: individuals counted of <strong>180</strong> individuals, number of quadrats of <strong>12</strong>, quadrat area of <strong>0.25</strong> m², site area of <strong>2</strong> ha.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives mean per quadrat of <strong>15</strong> individuals, density per m² of <strong>60</strong> /m², density per hectare of <strong>600,000</strong> /ha, estimated population on the site of <strong>1,200,000</strong> individuals.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Quadrat size should be scaled to the organism: 0.25 m² for grassland plants, 1 m² for shrubs, much smaller for soil invertebrates.</p></li><li><p>Placement matters more than number: random or systematic placement avoids the roadside and path-edge bias that inflates counts.</p></li><li><p>Counting zero in a quadrat is data — it must be included, or the density comes out too high.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Reporting the total count rather than the density, which cannot be compared between sites.</li><li>Averaging densities from quadrats of different size.</li><li>Sampling only where the species is easy to see.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/species-richness\\\">Species richness</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/mark-recapture-population\\\">Mark–recapture</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/logistic-population-growth\\\">Logistic growth</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many quadrats do I need?\",\"a\":\"Enough for the standard error to settle — often 10–30, checked with a running mean.\"},{\"q\":\"Should I sample randomly or systematically?\",\"a\":\"Random placement supports the statistics; a systematic grid is easier in the field and usually acceptable.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Quadrat Sampling Density actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns mean per quadrat, density per m², density per hectare and estimated population on the site. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"individuals\",\"label\":\"Individuals counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"180\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Across all quadrats\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"quadrats\",\"label\":\"Number of quadrats\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"size\",\"label\":\"Quadrat area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.25 m² = 50 × 50 cm\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"site\",\"label\":\"Site area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"density\",\"label\":\"Density per m²\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_ha\",\"label\":\"Density per hectare\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mean_quadrat\",\"label\":\"Mean per quadrat\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Estimated population on the site\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Density\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"mean_quadrat\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats\"},{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats/size\"},{\"l\":\"per_ha\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats/size*10000\"},{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats/size*site*10000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/quadrat-sampling-density\",\"slug\":\"quadrat-sampling-density\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Quadrat Sampling Density Calculator\",\"title\":\"Quadrat Sampling Density Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Quadrat sampling estimates density from a small measured area and scales it up. Free quadrat sampling density calculator: enter your values and get mean per q\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-quadrat-sampling-density\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"individuals\",\"l\":\"Individuals counted\",\"h\":\"Across all quadrats\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"quadrats\",\"l\":\"Number of quadrats\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"size\",\"l\":\"Quadrat area\",\"h\":\"0.25 m² = 50 × 50 cm\",\"suf\":\"m²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"site\",\"l\":\"Site area\",\"suf\":\"ha\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"density\",\"l\":\"Density per m²\",\"suf\":\"/m²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_ha\",\"l\":\"Density per hectare\",\"suf\":\"/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mean_quadrat\",\"l\":\"Mean per quadrat\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Estimated population on the site\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"individuals\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"180\",\"c\":null}]},\"quadrats\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"size\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.25\",\"c\":null}]},\"site\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"density\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_ha\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mean_quadrat\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Density\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"mean_quadrat\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats\",\"r\":[\"individuals\",\"quadrats\"]},{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats/size\",\"r\":[\"individuals\",\"quadrats\",\"size\"]},{\"l\":\"per_ha\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats/size*10000\",\"r\":[\"individuals\",\"quadrats\",\"size\"]},{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"individuals/quadrats/size*site*10000\",\"r\":[\"individuals\",\"quadrats\",\"size\",\"site\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(103, 'catch-per-unit-effort', 'biology', 'Catch Per Unit Effort Calculator', 'Catch Per Unit Effort Calculator', 'Catch Per Unit Effort Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Catch per unit effort standardises catch by how hard you fished: fish per hour, per hundred hooks, per trawl. Free catch per unit effort calculator: enter you', 'Catch per unit effort standardises catch by how hard you fished: fish per hour, per hundred hooks, per trawl. It returns catch per unit effort, change in CPUE and implied annual change, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is the classic index of abundance, and comparing it over seasons or years is how a decline is detected before a stock collapses. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Catch Per Unit Effort\",\"html\":\"<p>CPUE = catch ÷ effort;  change (%) = (CPUE_now − CPUE_before) ÷ CPUE_before × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/catch-per-unit-effort.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Catch Per Unit Effort Calculator — CPUE over surveys and the trend it implies.\\\" title=\\\"Catch Per Unit Effort Calculator\\\"><figcaption>CPUE over surveys and the trend it implies.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>catch</code></td><td>individuals or biomass landed</td><td>individuals or kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>effort</code></td><td>hours, hooks, tows or traps</td><td>effort units</td></tr><tr><td><code>CPUE</code></td><td>catch per unit effort</td><td>individuals per unit</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Catch Per Unit Effort?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: catch of <strong>340</strong> individuals, effort of <strong>40</strong> hours, cpue in an earlier survey of <strong>12</strong> /hour.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives catch per unit effort of <strong>8.5</strong> /hour, change in cpue of <strong>-29.17</strong>%, implied annual change of <strong>-5.83</strong> %/year.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>CPUE is assumed proportional to abundance; that assumption fails when fishers concentrate on the last remaining shoals, so catchability rises as the stock falls.</p></li><li><p>Always state the effort unit. A vessel that doubles its gear changes CPUE without any change in the fish.</p></li><li><p>The percentage change divided by the number of years gives a rough annual rate; a proper series needs a regression of log CPUE against time.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Comparing CPUE across different gear, seasons or vessels.</li><li>Reporting catch totals and calling them CPUE.</li><li>Fitting a trend to two points.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/maximum-sustainable-yield\\\">Maximum sustainable yield</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/encounter-rate\\\">Encounter rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/mark-recapture-population\\\">Mark–recapture</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is CPUE used instead of total catch?\",\"a\":\"Because total catch mixes abundance with effort — more boats catch more fish even when the stock is unchanged.\"},{\"q\":\"What is hyperstability?\",\"a\":\"CPUE staying high while abundance falls, because fishers learn where the remaining fish are.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Catch Per Unit Effort actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns catch per unit effort, change in CPUE and implied annual change. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"catch_count\",\"label\":\"Catch\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"340\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"effort\",\"label\":\"Effort\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Hours, hooks, tows or traps\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"hours\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cpue\",\"label\":\"Catch per unit effort\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/hour\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"previous\",\"label\":\"CPUE in an earlier survey\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/hour\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"change\",\"label\":\"Change in CPUE\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"decline_rate\",\"label\":\"Implied annual change\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%/year\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"CPUE\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cpue\",\"f\":\"catch_count/effort\"},{\"l\":\"change\",\"f\":\"(catch_count/effort-previous)/previous*100\"},{\"l\":\"decline_rate\",\"f\":\"(catch_count/effort-previous)/previous*100/5\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/catch-per-unit-effort\",\"slug\":\"catch-per-unit-effort\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Catch Per Unit Effort Calculator\",\"title\":\"Catch Per Unit Effort Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Catch per unit effort standardises catch by how hard you fished: fish per hour, per hundred hooks, per trawl. Free catch per unit effort calculator: enter you\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-catch-per-unit-effort\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"catch_count\",\"l\":\"Catch\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"effort\",\"l\":\"Effort\",\"h\":\"Hours, hooks, tows or traps\",\"suf\":\"hours\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cpue\",\"l\":\"Catch per unit effort\",\"suf\":\"/hour\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"previous\",\"l\":\"CPUE in an earlier survey\",\"suf\":\"/hour\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"change\",\"l\":\"Change in CPUE\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"decline_rate\",\"l\":\"Implied annual change\",\"suf\":\"%/year\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"catch_count\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"340\",\"c\":null}]},\"effort\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"cpue\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"previous\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"change\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"decline_rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"CPUE\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cpue\",\"f\":\"catch_count/effort\",\"r\":[\"catch_count\",\"effort\"]},{\"l\":\"change\",\"f\":\"(catch_count/effort-previous)/previous*100\",\"r\":[\"catch_count\",\"effort\",\"previous\"]},{\"l\":\"decline_rate\",\"f\":\"(catch_count/effort-previous)/previous*100/5\",\"r\":[\"catch_count\",\"effort\",\"previous\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'fish', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(104, 'encounter-rate', 'biology', 'Encounter Rate Calculator', 'Encounter Rate Calculator', 'Encounter Rate Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Encounter rate is how often a forager meets a food item per unit of search — per metre, per minute or per kilometre. Free encounter rate calculator: enter you', 'Encounter rate is how often a forager meets a food item per unit of search — per metre, per minute or per kilometre. It returns encounters per hour, encounters per km, implied prey density and encounter rate (model), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt links search time, prey density and detection distance, and it is the quantity foraging models and camera-trap studies are built on. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Encounter Rate\",\"html\":\"<p>Encounter rate = encounters ÷ effort;  density ≈ encounters ÷ (distance × 2 × detection radius)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/encounter-rate.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Encounter Rate Calculator — Encounters, search effort and detection distance.\\\" title=\\\"Encounter Rate Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Encounters, search effort and detection distance.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>encounters</code></td><td>number of prey or species detected</td><td>events</td></tr><tr><td><code>effort</code></td><td>time or distance covered</td><td>h or km</td></tr><tr><td><code>detection radius</code></td><td>distance at which an item is noticed</td><td>m</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Encounter Rate?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: encounters recorded of <strong>48</strong> events, search time of <strong>6</strong> hours, distance travelled of <strong>12</strong> km, detection radius of <strong>25</strong> m.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives encounters per hour of <strong>8</strong> /h, encounters per km of <strong>4</strong> /km, implied prey density of <strong>160</strong> /km², encounter rate (model) of <strong>48</strong> /h.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The density estimate is a strip transect: the area searched is distance × twice the detection radius, so a larger detection radius lowers the implied density.</p></li><li><p>Encounter rate rises linearly with prey density only when detection is perfect; in practice it saturates as visibility drops.</p></li><li><p>In camera-trap work the equivalent is the trapping rate, expressed per trap-night rather than per hour of search.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the time spent in the field rather than the time spent searching.</li><li>Ignoring missed detections, which biases density downwards.</li><li>Comparing encounter rates between habitats of different visibility without correcting.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/catch-per-unit-effort\\\">Catch per unit effort</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/quadrat-sampling-density\\\">Quadrat sampling</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/mark-recapture-population\\\">Mark–recapture</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I convert an encounter rate to a density?\",\"a\":\"Divide by the searched area — distance travelled times twice the detection radius.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does visibility matter so much?\",\"a\":\"Because detection distance defines the searched area; halve it and the implied density doubles.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Encounter Rate actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns encounters per hour, encounters per km, implied prey density and encounter rate (model). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"encounters\",\"label\":\"Encounters recorded\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"48\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"events\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"time\",\"label\":\"Search time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"hours\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"distance\",\"label\":\"Distance travelled\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"km\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"detect\",\"label\":\"Detection radius\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rate_time\",\"label\":\"Encounters per hour\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rate_distance\",\"label\":\"Encounters per km\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/km\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"density\",\"label\":\"Implied prey density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/km²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"encounter_all\",\"label\":\"Encounter rate (model)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"/h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Encounter\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rate_time\",\"f\":\"encounters/time\"},{\"l\":\"rate_distance\",\"f\":\"encounters/distance\"},{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"encounters/distance/detect*1000\"},{\"l\":\"encounter_all\",\"f\":\"encounters/distance/detect*1000*2*detect/1000*time\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/encounter-rate\",\"slug\":\"encounter-rate\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Encounter Rate Calculator\",\"title\":\"Encounter Rate Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Encounter rate is how often a forager meets a food item per unit of search — per metre, per minute or per kilometre. Free encounter rate calculator: enter you\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-encounter-rate\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"encounters\",\"l\":\"Encounters recorded\",\"suf\":\"events\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"time\",\"l\":\"Search time\",\"suf\":\"hours\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"distance\",\"l\":\"Distance travelled\",\"suf\":\"km\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"detect\",\"l\":\"Detection radius\",\"suf\":\"m\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate_time\",\"l\":\"Encounters per hour\",\"suf\":\"/h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate_distance\",\"l\":\"Encounters per km\",\"suf\":\"/km\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"density\",\"l\":\"Implied prey density\",\"suf\":\"/km²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"encounter_all\",\"l\":\"Encounter rate (model)\",\"suf\":\"/h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"encounters\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"48\",\"c\":null}]},\"time\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"distance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"detect\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"rate_time\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rate_distance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"density\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"encounter_all\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Encounter\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rate_time\",\"f\":\"encounters/time\",\"r\":[\"encounters\",\"time\"]},{\"l\":\"rate_distance\",\"f\":\"encounters/distance\",\"r\":[\"encounters\",\"distance\"]},{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"encounters/distance/detect*1000\",\"r\":[\"encounters\",\"distance\",\"detect\"]},{\"l\":\"encounter_all\",\"f\":\"encounters/distance/detect*1000*2*detect/1000*time\",\"r\":[\"encounters\",\"distance\",\"detect\",\"time\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'paw', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(105, 'maximum-sustainable-yield', 'biology', 'Maximum Sustainable Yield Calculator', 'Maximum Sustainable Yield Calculator', 'Maximum Sustainable Yield Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Maximum sustainable yield is half the carrying capacity, taken at half the maximum growth rate. Free maximum sustainable yield calculator: enter your values a', 'Maximum sustainable yield is half the carrying capacity, taken at half the maximum growth rate. It returns maximum sustainable yield, stock size at MSY, sustainable harvest rate and yield at half of K, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives the yield, the stock size to leave behind and the effort that produces it. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Maximum Sustainable Yield\",\"html\":\"<p>MSY = r × K ÷ 4,  taken at N = K ÷ 2</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/maximum-sustainable-yield.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Maximum Sustainable Yield Calculator — Yield against stock size, peaking at half the carrying capacity.\\\" title=\\\"Maximum Sustainable Yield Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Yield against stock size, peaking at half the carrying capacity.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>intrinsic rate of increase</td><td>per year</td></tr><tr><td><code>K</code></td><td>carrying capacity</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>MSY</code></td><td>maximum sustainable yield</td><td>individuals/year</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Maximum Sustainable Yield?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: carrying capacity (k) of <strong>10,000</strong> individuals, intrinsic growth rate (r) of <strong>0.6</strong> per year.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives maximum sustainable yield of <strong>1,500</strong> individuals/year, stock size at msy of <strong>5,000</strong> individuals, sustainable harvest rate of <strong>30</strong> %/year, yield at half of k of <strong>1,500</strong> individuals/year.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>harvest below 50% of MSY</td><td><strong>Conservative</strong></td><td>the stock stays near K</td></tr><tr><td>harvest at MSY</td><td><strong>Maximum</strong></td><td>productive but leaves no margin for bad years</td></tr><tr><td>harvest above MSY</td><td><strong>Overfishing</strong></td><td>the stock declines and the yield falls with it</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>MSY is a theoretical maximum, not a management target: real stocks fluctuate, and fishing at MSY in a bad year pushes the population below the level that produces it.</p></li><li><p>The model assumes logistic growth, no age structure and no environmental variability. Modern management uses reference points such as B_MSY and F_MSY with buffers.</p></li><li><p>Because yield peaks at K/2, a depleted stock can produce more surplus than a full one — which is why recovery plans sometimes allow fishing before the stock is fully rebuilt.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using r as a percentage in one place and a fraction in another.</li><li>Treating MSY as a fixed tonnage when it depends on the current stock size.</li><li>Applying a single-species formula to a multi-species fishery without accounting for bycatch.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/logistic-population-growth\\\">Logistic growth</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/exponential-population-growth\\\">Exponential growth</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/catch-per-unit-effort\\\">Catch per unit effort</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is MSY at half the carrying capacity?\",\"a\":\"Because logistic growth is fastest at K/2, so the population replaces harvested individuals most quickly there.\"},{\"q\":\"Is MSY always sustainable?\",\"a\":\"No — it is sustainable only in the deterministic model. In practice it is a ceiling, and management sets targets well below it.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Maximum Sustainable Yield actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns maximum sustainable yield, stock size at MSY, sustainable harvest rate and yield at half of K. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"k\",\"label\":\"Carrying capacity (K)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"r\",\"label\":\"Intrinsic growth rate (r)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per year\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"msy\",\"label\":\"Maximum sustainable yield\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals/year\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"stock_at_msy\",\"label\":\"Stock size at MSY\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"harvest_rate\",\"label\":\"Sustainable harvest rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%/year\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"surplus_50\",\"label\":\"Yield at half of K\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals/year\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Yield\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"msy\",\"f\":\"r*k/4\"},{\"l\":\"stock_at_msy\",\"f\":\"k/2\"},{\"l\":\"harvest_rate\",\"f\":\"r/2*100\"},{\"l\":\"surplus_50\",\"f\":\"r*(k/2)*(1-0.5)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/maximum-sustainable-yield\",\"slug\":\"maximum-sustainable-yield\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Maximum Sustainable Yield Calculator\",\"title\":\"Maximum Sustainable Yield Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Maximum sustainable yield is half the carrying capacity, taken at half the maximum growth rate. Free maximum sustainable yield calculator: enter your values a\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-maximum-sustainable-yield\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"k\",\"l\":\"Carrying capacity (K)\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r\",\"l\":\"Intrinsic growth rate (r)\",\"suf\":\"per year\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"msy\",\"l\":\"Maximum sustainable yield\",\"suf\":\"individuals/year\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stock_at_msy\",\"l\":\"Stock size at MSY\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"harvest_rate\",\"l\":\"Sustainable harvest rate\",\"suf\":\"%/year\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"surplus_50\",\"l\":\"Yield at half of K\",\"suf\":\"individuals/year\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"k\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10000\",\"c\":null}]},\"r\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.6\",\"c\":null}]},\"msy\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"stock_at_msy\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"harvest_rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"surplus_50\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Yield\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"msy\",\"f\":\"r*k/4\",\"r\":[\"r\",\"k\"]},{\"l\":\"stock_at_msy\",\"f\":\"k/2\",\"r\":[\"k\"]},{\"l\":\"harvest_rate\",\"f\":\"r/2*100\",\"r\":[\"r\"]},{\"l\":\"surplus_50\",\"f\":\"r*(k/2)*(1-0.5)\",\"r\":[\"r\",\"k\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'fish', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(106, 'logistic-population-growth', 'biology', 'Logistic Population Growth Calculator', 'Logistic Population Growth Calculator', 'Logistic Population Growth Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Logistic growth starts exponential and slows as a population approaches its carrying capacity. Free logistic population growth calculator: enter your values a', 'Logistic growth starts exponential and slows as a population approaches its carrying capacity. It returns population after that time, growth rate at the start, population at half of K and time to reach K/2, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator projects the population size forward, gives the growth rate at the current size, and shows when the population reaches half of K — the point of fastest growth. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Logistic Population Growth\",\"html\":\"<p>N(t) = K ÷ [1 + ((K − N₀) ÷ N₀) e^(−rt)]</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/logistic-population-growth.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Logistic Population Growth Calculator — The S-shaped curve, from exponential to a plateau at K.\\\" title=\\\"Logistic Population Growth Calculator\\\"><figcaption>The S-shaped curve, from exponential to a plateau at K.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>N₀</code></td><td>starting population</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>K</code></td><td>carrying capacity</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>intrinsic rate of increase</td><td>per time</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>time elapsed</td><td>time units</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Logistic Population Growth?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: starting population of <strong>50</strong> individuals, carrying capacity of <strong>1,000</strong> individuals, intrinsic growth rate of <strong>0.4</strong> per unit time, time of <strong>10</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives population after that time of <strong>741.84</strong> individuals, growth rate at the start of <strong>19</strong> individuals/time, population at half of k of <strong>500</strong> individuals, time to reach k/2 of <strong>0</strong> time units.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The inflection point is at N = K/2, where growth is fastest; after it the curve flattens toward K without ever crossing it.</p></li><li><p>r sets the speed and K sets the ceiling: a large r with a small K produces a sharp rise and an early plateau.</p></li><li><p>The model assumes a closed population with no age structure, and it ignores the lag that produces overshoot and collapse in real populations.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using a per-generation r with a time step in years.</li><li>Starting above K, where the formula predicts decline — which is correct, but often unexpected.</li><li>Mixing a percentage growth rate with the exponential r: for small rates they coincide, for large ones they do not.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/exponential-population-growth\\\">Exponential growth</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/logistic-population-growth\\\">Logistic growth</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/maximum-sustainable-yield\\\">Maximum sustainable yield</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the difference between exponential and logistic growth?\",\"a\":\"Exponential growth has no ceiling; logistic growth slows as the population approaches K.\"},{\"q\":\"When is growth fastest?\",\"a\":\"At half the carrying capacity, which is the inflection point of the sigmoid curve.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Logistic Population Growth actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns population after that time, growth rate at the start, population at half of K and time to reach K/2. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n0\",\"label\":\"Starting population\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"k\",\"label\":\"Carrying capacity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"r\",\"label\":\"Intrinsic growth rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per unit time\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"t\",\"label\":\"Time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Population after that time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"growth\",\"label\":\"Growth rate at the start\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals/time\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"n_half\",\"label\":\"Population at half of K\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"t_half\",\"label\":\"Time to reach K/2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"time units\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Growth\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"k/(1+((k-n0)/n0)*exp(-1*r*t))\"},{\"l\":\"growth\",\"f\":\"r*n0*(1-n0/k)\"},{\"l\":\"n_half\",\"f\":\"k/2\"},{\"l\":\"t_half\",\"f\":\"log((k/(k/2))-1)/((k-n0)/n0)/r\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/logistic-population-growth\",\"slug\":\"logistic-population-growth\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Logistic Population Growth Calculator\",\"title\":\"Logistic Population Growth Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Logistic growth starts exponential and slows as a population approaches its carrying capacity. Free logistic population growth calculator: enter your values a\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-logistic-population-growth\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n0\",\"l\":\"Starting population\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"k\",\"l\":\"Carrying capacity\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r\",\"l\":\"Intrinsic growth rate\",\"suf\":\"per unit time\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t\",\"l\":\"Time\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"Population after that time\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"growth\",\"l\":\"Growth rate at the start\",\"suf\":\"individuals/time\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n_half\",\"l\":\"Population at half of K\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t_half\",\"l\":\"Time to reach K/2\",\"suf\":\"time units\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n0\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"k\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"r\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"t\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"growth\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"n_half\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"t_half\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Growth\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"k/(1+((k-n0)/n0)*exp(-1*r*t))\",\"r\":[\"k\",\"n0\",\"r\",\"t\"]},{\"l\":\"growth\",\"f\":\"r*n0*(1-n0/k)\",\"r\":[\"r\",\"n0\",\"k\"]},{\"l\":\"n_half\",\"f\":\"k/2\",\"r\":[\"k\"]},{\"l\":\"t_half\",\"f\":\"log((k/(k/2))-1)/((k-n0)/n0)/r\",\"r\":[\"k\",\"n0\",\"r\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'chart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(107, 'exponential-population-growth', 'biology', 'Exponential Population Growth Calculator', 'Exponential Population Growth Calculator', 'Exponential Population Growth Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Unlimited growth follows N = N₀e^(rt): a constant per-capita rate applied to an ever larger population. Free exponential population growth calculator: enter y', 'Unlimited growth follows N = N₀e^(rt): a constant per-capita rate applied to an ever larger population. It returns population after that time, increase, doubling time and time to reach 10× the start, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator projects the size, the time to a target, and the doubling time that goes with the rate. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Exponential Population Growth\",\"html\":\"<p>N(t) = N₀ × e^(rt);  doubling time = ln2 ÷ r</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/exponential-population-growth.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Exponential Population Growth Calculator — Exponential growth plotted over time.\\\" title=\\\"Exponential Population Growth Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Exponential growth plotted over time.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>N₀</code></td><td>starting population</td><td>individuals</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>per-capita growth rate</td><td>per time</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>elapsed time</td><td>time units</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Exponential Population Growth?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: starting population of <strong>100</strong> individuals, growth rate of <strong>0.05</strong> per year, time of <strong>20</strong> years.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives population after that time of <strong>271.83</strong> individuals, increase of <strong>2.72</strong> ×, doubling time of <strong>13.86</strong> years, time to reach 10× the start of <strong>46.05</strong> years.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>A constant percentage growth rate means a constantly shrinking doubling time in absolute terms: at 5% a year the population doubles in 14 years, every time.</p></li><li><p>Compounding is why exponential curves look flat and then explode — the first doubling is invisible, the fifth is not.</p></li><li><p>Nothing grows exponentially forever; the value of the calculation is to show how quickly a limit approaches, not to predict the distant future.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Entering 5 for 5% instead of 0.05.</li><li>Mixing an annual rate with a monthly time step.</li><li>Averaging growth rates arithmetically across periods instead of exponentiating.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/logistic-population-growth\\\">Logistic growth</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/maximum-sustainable-yield\\\">Maximum sustainable yield</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\\\">Bacterial growth curve</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I find the doubling time?\",\"a\":\"Divide 70 by the percentage growth rate for a quick estimate, or use ln2 ÷ r for the exact value.\"},{\"q\":\"What if r is negative?\",\"a\":\"The population decays exponentially — the same formula, with a shrinking half-life instead of a doubling time.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Exponential Population Growth actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns population after that time, increase, doubling time and time to reach 10× the start. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n0\",\"label\":\"Starting population\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"r\",\"label\":\"Growth rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.05\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.05 = 5% a year\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per year\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"t\",\"label\":\"Time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"years\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Population after that time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"individuals\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fold\",\"label\":\"Increase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"doubling\",\"label\":\"Doubling time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"years\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"target_time\",\"label\":\"Time to reach 10× the start\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"years\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Growth\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n0*exp(r*t)\"},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"exp(r*t)\"},{\"l\":\"doubling\",\"f\":\"0.6931471805599453/r\"},{\"l\":\"target_time\",\"f\":\"log(10)/r\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/exponential-population-growth\",\"slug\":\"exponential-population-growth\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Exponential Population Growth Calculator\",\"title\":\"Exponential Population Growth Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Unlimited growth follows N = N₀e^(rt): a constant per-capita rate applied to an ever larger population. Free exponential population growth calculator: enter y\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-exponential-population-growth\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n0\",\"l\":\"Starting population\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r\",\"l\":\"Growth rate\",\"h\":\"0.05 = 5% a year\",\"suf\":\"per year\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t\",\"l\":\"Time\",\"suf\":\"years\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"Population after that time\",\"suf\":\"individuals\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fold\",\"l\":\"Increase\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"doubling\",\"l\":\"Doubling time\",\"suf\":\"years\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target_time\",\"l\":\"Time to reach 10× the start\",\"suf\":\"years\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n0\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"r\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.05\",\"c\":null}]},\"t\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fold\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"doubling\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"target_time\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Growth\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"n\",\"f\":\"n0*exp(r*t)\",\"r\":[\"n0\",\"r\",\"t\"]},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"exp(r*t)\",\"r\":[\"r\",\"t\"]},{\"l\":\"doubling\",\"f\":\"0.6931471805599453/r\",\"r\":[\"r\"]},{\"l\":\"target_time\",\"f\":\"log(10)/r\",\"r\":[\"r\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'chart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:45', '2026-10-29 14:08:45'),
(108, 'q10-temperature-coefficient', 'biology', 'Q10 Temperature Coefficient Calculator', 'Q10 Temperature Coefficient Calculator', 'Q10 Temperature Coefficient Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Q10 is how many times faster a biological rate runs for every 10 °C of warming. Free q10 temperature coefficient calculator: enter your values and get Q10, ra', 'Q10 is how many times faster a biological rate runs for every 10 °C of warming. It returns Q10, rate at t1 + 10 °C and apparent activation energy, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nA Q10 of 2 — the rule of thumb for most enzyme and respiration rates — means doubling per 10 °C, and it is the standard way to compare thermal sensitivity across processes. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Q10 Temperature Coefficient\",\"html\":\"<p>Q10 = (R₂ ÷ R₁)^[10 ÷ (T₂ − T₁)]</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/q10-temperature-coefficient.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Q10 Temperature Coefficient Calculator — Rate against temperature, and the fold change per 10 °C.\\\" title=\\\"Q10 Temperature Coefficient Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Rate against temperature, and the fold change per 10 °C.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>R₁, R₂</code></td><td>rates at two temperatures</td><td>any unit</td></tr><tr><td><code>T₁, T₂</code></td><td>the two temperatures</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>Q10</code></td><td>fold change per 10 °C</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Q10 Temperature Coefficient?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: rate at temperature 1 of <strong>10</strong> units, temperature 1 of <strong>20</strong>°C, rate at temperature 2 of <strong>22</strong> units, temperature 2 of <strong>30</strong>°C.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives q10 of <strong>2.2</strong>, rate at t1 + 10 °c of <strong>22</strong> units, apparent activation energy of <strong>58.26</strong> kJ/mol.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1.0 – 1.5</td><td><strong>Weakly temperature dependent</strong></td><td>physical processes, diffusion</td></tr><tr><td>1.5 – 2.5</td><td><strong>Typical biological range</strong></td><td>most enzyme reactions and respiration</td></tr><tr><td>above 3</td><td><strong>Strongly temperature dependent</strong></td><td>or a rate still below its optimum</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Q10 is only meaningful over a range where the rate is monotonic: above an enzyme\'s optimum the rate falls with temperature and Q10 becomes less than one.</p></li><li><p>The apparent activation energy is the Arrhenius equivalent of the same measurement; 50–70 kJ/mol is typical of biological rates.</p></li><li><p>Ecosystem respiration commonly shows Q10 near 2, which is why warming of 2 °C is expected to raise soil respiration by about 15% — before acclimation.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using a temperature difference that is not exactly 10 °C without the exponent.</li><li>Mixing °C and K in the formula — differences are fine, but the activation-energy calculation needs kelvin.</li><li>Fitting Q10 across a range that includes a thermal optimum.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/chemistry/arrhenius-equation\\\">Arrhenius equation</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/activation-energy\\\">Activation energy</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/respiratory-quotient\\\">Respiratory quotient</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What does Q10 = 2 mean?\",\"a\":\"The rate doubles for every 10 °C increase in temperature.\"},{\"q\":\"Is Q10 constant?\",\"a\":\"No — it varies with temperature and species, which is why ecologists now fit Arrhenius-type curves instead.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Q10 Temperature Coefficient actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns Q10, rate at t1 + 10 °C and apparent activation energy. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"r1\",\"label\":\"Rate at temperature 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"units\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"t1\",\"label\":\"Temperature 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"r2\",\"label\":\"Rate at temperature 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"22\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"units\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"t2\",\"label\":\"Temperature 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"30\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"q10\",\"label\":\"Q10\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rate_10\",\"label\":\"Rate at t1 + 10 °C\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"units\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"activation\",\"label\":\"Apparent activation energy\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kJ/mol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Temperature response\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"q10\",\"f\":\"pow(r2/r1,10/(t2-t1))\"},{\"l\":\"rate_10\",\"f\":\"r1*pow(r2/r1,10/(t2-t1))\"},{\"l\":\"activation\",\"f\":\"8.314*(t1+273.15)*(t2+273.15)/((t2+273.15)-(t1+273.15))*log(r2/r1)/1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/q10-temperature-coefficient\",\"slug\":\"q10-temperature-coefficient\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Q10 Temperature Coefficient Calculator\",\"title\":\"Q10 Temperature Coefficient Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Q10 is how many times faster a biological rate runs for every 10 °C of warming. Free q10 temperature coefficient calculator: enter your values and get Q10, ra\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-q10-temperature-coefficient\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r1\",\"l\":\"Rate at temperature 1\",\"suf\":\"units\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t1\",\"l\":\"Temperature 1\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r2\",\"l\":\"Rate at temperature 2\",\"suf\":\"units\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"t2\",\"l\":\"Temperature 2\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"q10\",\"l\":\"Q10\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate_10\",\"l\":\"Rate at t1 + 10 °C\",\"suf\":\"units\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"activation\",\"l\":\"Apparent activation energy\",\"suf\":\"kJ/mol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"r1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"t1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"r2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"22\",\"c\":null}]},\"t2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"30\",\"c\":null}]},\"q10\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rate_10\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"activation\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Temperature response\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"q10\",\"f\":\"pow(r2/r1,10/(t2-t1))\",\"r\":[\"r2\",\"r1\",\"t2\",\"t1\"]},{\"l\":\"rate_10\",\"f\":\"r1*pow(r2/r1,10/(t2-t1))\",\"r\":[\"r1\",\"r2\",\"t2\",\"t1\"]},{\"l\":\"activation\",\"f\":\"8.314*(t1+273.15)*(t2+273.15)/((t2+273.15)-(t1+273.15))*log(r2/r1)/1000\",\"r\":[\"t1\",\"t2\",\"r2\",\"r1\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'thermometer', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(109, 'net-primary-productivity', 'biology', 'Net Primary Productivity Calculator', 'Net Primary Productivity Calculator', 'Net Primary Productivity Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Net primary productivity is what plants accumulate after their own respiration: gross photosynthesis minus the carbon they burn to stay alive. Free net primar', 'Net primary productivity is what plants accumulate after their own respiration: gross photosynthesis minus the carbon they burn to stay alive. It returns net primary productivity, per hectare, dry biomass equivalent and energy equivalent, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts between carbon, biomass and energy, and scales a measured rate to a hectare and a year. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Net Primary Productivity\",\"html\":\"<p>NPP = GPP − R_a;  carbon × 2.2 ≈ dry biomass</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/net-primary-productivity.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Net Primary Productivity Calculator — Gross production, respiration and what is left over.\\\" title=\\\"Net Primary Productivity Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Gross production, respiration and what is left over.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GPP</code></td><td>gross primary production</td><td>g C/m²/yr</td></tr><tr><td><code>R_a</code></td><td>autotrophic respiration</td><td>g C/m²/yr</td></tr><tr><td><code>NPP</code></td><td>net primary productivity</td><td>g C/m²/yr</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Net Primary Productivity?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: gross primary production of <strong>2,200</strong> g C/m²/yr, plant respiration of <strong>900</strong> g C/m²/yr.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives net primary productivity of <strong>1,300</strong> g C/m²/yr, per hectare of <strong>13,000</strong> kg C/ha/yr, dry biomass equivalent of <strong>2,860</strong> g/m²/yr, energy equivalent of <strong>111.54</strong> MJ/m²/yr, net ecosystem production (less 10% heterotroph) of <strong>1,170</strong> g C/m²/yr.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 200 g C/m²/yr</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>deserts, tundra, open ocean</td></tr><tr><td>400 – 1 200</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>temperate grassland and forest</td></tr><tr><td>above 1 500</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>tropical rainforest, wetlands, estuaries</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>NPP is the carbon available to herbivores and decomposers; NEP subtracts their respiration and is the number that matters for carbon storage.</p></li><li><p>The 2.2 factor converts carbon to dry biomass; energy conversion uses about 17–20 kJ per g carbon, or roughly 39 kJ per g of biomass.</p></li><li><p>Global terrestrial NPP is around 56 Pg carbon a year, roughly half of it in the tropics.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Reporting GPP when you mean NPP.</li><li>Converting carbon to biomass with the 0.5 factor used for soil organic matter.</li><li>Scaling a single-day measurement to a year without accounting for seasonality.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/photosynthesis-rate\\\">Photosynthesis rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/respiratory-quotient\\\">Respiratory quotient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/q10-temperature-coefficient\\\">Q10 coefficient</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the difference between GPP and NPP?\",\"a\":\"GPP is everything photosynthesised; NPP is what remains after the plant respires.\"},{\"q\":\"Can NPP be negative?\",\"a\":\"Not over a season for a living plant community — but a net carbon loss can occur in disturbed or dark systems.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Net Primary Productivity actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns net primary productivity, per hectare, dry biomass equivalent and energy equivalent. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"gpp\",\"label\":\"Gross primary production\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"respiration\",\"label\":\"Plant respiration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"900\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"npp\",\"label\":\"Net primary productivity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_ha\",\"label\":\"Per hectare\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg C/ha/yr\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"biomass\",\"label\":\"Dry biomass equivalent\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"About 2.2 g biomass per g C\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/m²/yr\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"energy\",\"label\":\"Energy equivalent\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"About 39 kJ per g dry biomass\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"MJ/m²/yr\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"nep\",\"label\":\"Net ecosystem production (less 10% heterotroph)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Productivity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"npp\",\"f\":\"gpp-respiration\"},{\"l\":\"per_ha\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*10\"},{\"l\":\"biomass\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*2.2\"},{\"l\":\"energy\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*2.2*39/1000\"},{\"l\":\"nep\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*0.9\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/net-primary-productivity\",\"slug\":\"net-primary-productivity\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Net Primary Productivity Calculator\",\"title\":\"Net Primary Productivity Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Net primary productivity is what plants accumulate after their own respiration: gross photosynthesis minus the carbon they burn to stay alive. Free net primar\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-net-primary-productivity\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gpp\",\"l\":\"Gross primary production\",\"suf\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"respiration\",\"l\":\"Plant respiration\",\"suf\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"npp\",\"l\":\"Net primary productivity\",\"suf\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_ha\",\"l\":\"Per hectare\",\"suf\":\"kg C/ha/yr\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"biomass\",\"l\":\"Dry biomass equivalent\",\"h\":\"About 2.2 g biomass per g C\",\"suf\":\"g/m²/yr\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"energy\",\"l\":\"Energy equivalent\",\"h\":\"About 39 kJ per g dry biomass\",\"suf\":\"MJ/m²/yr\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nep\",\"l\":\"Net ecosystem production (less 10% heterotroph)\",\"suf\":\"g C/m²/yr\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"gpp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2200\",\"c\":null}]},\"respiration\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"900\",\"c\":null}]},\"npp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_ha\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"biomass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"energy\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"nep\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Productivity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"npp\",\"f\":\"gpp-respiration\",\"r\":[\"gpp\",\"respiration\"]},{\"l\":\"per_ha\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*10\",\"r\":[\"gpp\",\"respiration\"]},{\"l\":\"biomass\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*2.2\",\"r\":[\"gpp\",\"respiration\"]},{\"l\":\"energy\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*2.2*39/1000\",\"r\":[\"gpp\",\"respiration\"]},{\"l\":\"nep\",\"f\":\"(gpp-respiration)*0.9\",\"r\":[\"gpp\",\"respiration\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(110, 'allometric-equation', 'biology', 'Allometric Equation Calculator', 'Allometric Equation Calculator', 'Allometric Equation Calculator — Formula & Results', 'An allometric equation scales a trait as a power of body mass: Y = aM^b. Free allometric equation calculator: enter your values and get predicted value, log₁₀', 'An allometric equation scales a trait as a power of body mass: Y = aM^b. It returns predicted value, log₁₀ of the prediction, change for a doubling of mass and predicted surface exponent, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe exponent is the interesting part — 0.75 for metabolic rate, 1 for volume, 0.67 for surface area — and this calculator applies any allometric equation, reports the exponent\'s meaning and converts between log and linear forms. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Allometric Equation\",\"html\":\"<p>Y = a × M^b</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/allometric-equation.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Allometric Equation Calculator — A power law on linear axes, and the same line on log axes.\\\" title=\\\"Allometric Equation Calculator\\\"><figcaption>A power law on linear axes, and the same line on log axes.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>coefficient, in units of Y per M^b</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>b</code></td><td>scaling exponent</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>M</code></td><td>body mass</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>Y</code></td><td>the trait being predicted</td><td>trait units</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Allometric Equation?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: coefficient (a) of <strong>3.4</strong>, exponent (b) of <strong>0.75</strong>, body mass of <strong>70</strong> kg.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives predicted value of <strong>82.28</strong>, log₁₀ of the prediction of <strong>1.92</strong>, change for a doubling of mass of <strong>1.68</strong> ×, predicted surface exponent of <strong>0.67</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>b ≈ 0.67</td><td><strong>Surface scaling</strong></td><td>areas scale slower than volume</td></tr><tr><td>b ≈ 0.75</td><td><strong>Metabolic scaling</strong></td><td>Kleiber\'s law for metabolic rate</td></tr><tr><td>b ≈ 1.0</td><td><strong>Isometric</strong></td><td>the trait scales in proportion to mass</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>An exponent below 1 means the trait grows slower than mass: bigger animals have relatively lower metabolic rates per kilogram.</p></li><li><p>Coefficients are unit-specific — a mouse-to-elephant equation in kg cannot be fed grams.</p></li><li><p>Log–log plots turn the equation into a straight line whose slope is b and intercept is log a, which is how the parameters are actually fitted.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Fitting a straight line to untransformed data and calling the slope an exponent.</li><li>Using a coefficient published in different units.</li><li>Extrapolating far beyond the size range the equation was fitted over.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/basal-metabolic-rate\\\">Basal metabolic rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/q10-temperature-coefficient\\\">Q10 coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/net-primary-productivity\\\">Net primary productivity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is Kleiber\'s law?\",\"a\":\"Metabolic rate scales as mass^0.75, so a tenfold heavier animal uses about 5.6 times the energy.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do exponents cluster at 3/4?\",\"a\":\"Because transport networks — blood vessels, tracheae, xylem — constrain how fast resources can reach tissue as body size grows.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Allometric Equation actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns predicted value, log₁₀ of the prediction, change for a doubling of mass and predicted surface exponent. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Coefficient (a)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From the published equation\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Exponent (b)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.75\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mass\",\"label\":\"Body mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"70\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"y\",\"label\":\"Predicted value\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"log_y\",\"label\":\"log₁₀ of the prediction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"doubling_mass\",\"label\":\"Change for a doubling of mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"×\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"surface_exp\",\"label\":\"Predicted surface exponent\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.67\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Allometry\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"y\",\"f\":\"a*pow(mass,b)\"},{\"l\":\"log_y\",\"f\":\"log10(a*pow(mass,b))\"},{\"l\":\"doubling_mass\",\"f\":\"pow(2,b)\"},{\"l\":\"surface_exp\",\"f\":\"0.67\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/allometric-equation\",\"slug\":\"allometric-equation\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Allometric Equation Calculator\",\"title\":\"Allometric Equation Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"An allometric equation scales a trait as a power of body mass: Y = aM^b. Free allometric equation calculator: enter your values and get predicted value, log₁₀\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-allometric-equation\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a\",\"l\":\"Coefficient (a)\",\"h\":\"From the published equation\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"b\",\"l\":\"Exponent (b)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mass\",\"l\":\"Body mass\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"y\",\"l\":\"Predicted value\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"log_y\",\"l\":\"log₁₀ of the prediction\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"doubling_mass\",\"l\":\"Change for a doubling of mass\",\"suf\":\"×\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"surface_exp\",\"l\":\"Predicted surface exponent\",\"h\":\"0.67\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"a\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"b\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.75\",\"c\":null}]},\"mass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"70\",\"c\":null}]},\"y\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"log_y\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"doubling_mass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"surface_exp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Allometry\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"y\",\"f\":\"a*pow(mass,b)\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"mass\",\"b\"]},{\"l\":\"log_y\",\"f\":\"log10(a*pow(mass,b))\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"mass\",\"b\"]},{\"l\":\"doubling_mass\",\"f\":\"pow(2,b)\",\"r\":[\"b\"]},{\"l\":\"surface_exp\",\"f\":\"0.67\",\"r\":[]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'chart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(111, 'basal-metabolic-rate', 'biology', 'Basal Metabolic Rate Calculator (Mifflin–St Jeor)', 'Basal Metabolic Rate Calculator (Mifflin–St Jeor)', 'Basal Metabolic Rate (Mifflin–St Jeor) Calculator', 'Basal metabolic rate is the energy a body spends at complete rest. Free basal metabolic rate (mifflin–st jeor) calculator: enter your values and get basal met', 'Basal metabolic rate is the energy a body spends at complete rest. It returns basal metabolic rate, total daily energy expenditure, BMR per kg and BMR, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe Mifflin–St Jeor equation is the most accurate simple predictor for most adults, and this calculator adds the activity factor that turns BMR into total daily energy expenditure. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Basal Metabolic Rate (Mifflin–St Jeor)\",\"html\":\"<p>Male: BMR = 10W + 6.25H − 5A + 5;  female: BMR = 10W + 6.25H − 5A − 161</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/basal-metabolic-rate.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Basal Metabolic Rate Calculator (Mifflin–St Jeor) — Resting burn, activity, and the daily total.\\\" title=\\\"Basal Metabolic Rate Calculator (Mifflin–St Jeor)\\\"><figcaption>Resting burn, activity, and the daily total.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>W</code></td><td>weight in kilograms</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>H</code></td><td>height in centimetres</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>age in years</td><td>years</td></tr><tr><td><code>TDEE</code></td><td>BMR × activity factor</td><td>kcal/day</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Basal Metabolic Rate (Mifflin–St Jeor)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: sex set to <strong>5.0</strong>, weight of <strong>75</strong> kg, height of <strong>178</strong> cm, age of <strong>30</strong> years, activity level set to <strong>Light (×1.375)</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives basal metabolic rate of <strong>1,717.5</strong> kcal/day, total daily energy expenditure of <strong>2,361.56</strong> kcal/day, bmr per kg of <strong>22.9</strong> kcal/kg/day, bmr of <strong>7,186.02</strong> kJ/day.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>This page shows the male form on its default settings; the female equation subtracts 161 instead of adding 5, which is about 166 kcal lower for the same body.</p></li><li><p>BMR assumes complete rest; TDEE multiplies it by an activity factor that is the largest source of error in the whole calculation.</p></li><li><p>Measured BMR by indirect calorimetry is typically within 10% of the prediction for healthy adults, and further out in obesity, illness and old age.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using pounds or inches without converting.</li><li>Adding exercise calories twice — the activity factor already includes them.</li><li>Treating TDEE as a prescription rather than an estimate, then eating back every estimated calorie burned.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/tidal-volume-lung-capacity\\\">Tidal volume and lung capacity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/basal-metabolic-rate\\\">Basal metabolic rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/respiratory-quotient\\\">Respiratory quotient</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How accurate is Mifflin–St Jeor?\",\"a\":\"It predicts BMR within about 10% for most adults and is more accurate than the older Harris–Benedict equation.\"},{\"q\":\"Should I eat my BMR or my TDEE?\",\"a\":\"TDEE is what you burn; BMR is the floor. Eating below BMR is rarely advisable without medical supervision.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Basal Metabolic Rate (Mifflin–St Jeor) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns basal metabolic rate, total daily energy expenditure, BMR per kg and BMR. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sex\",\"label\":\"Sex\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"male-5-kcal\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"male-5-kcal\",\"Male (+5 kcal)\",\"5\"],[\"female-161-kcal\",\"Female (−161 kcal)\",\"-161\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"weight\",\"label\":\"Weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"75\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"height\",\"label\":\"Height\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"178\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"age\",\"label\":\"Age\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"30\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"years\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"activity\",\"label\":\"Activity level\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"light-1-375\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"sedentary-1-2\",\"Sedentary (×1.2)\",\"1.2\"],[\"light-1-375\",\"Light (×1.375)\",\"1.375\"],[\"moderate-1-55\",\"Moderate (×1.55)\",\"1.55\"],[\"active-1-725\",\"Active (×1.725)\",\"1.725\"],[\"very-active-1-9\",\"Very active (×1.9)\",\"1.9\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"bmr\",\"label\":\"Basal metabolic rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kcal/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tdee\",\"label\":\"Total daily energy expenditure\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kcal/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_kg\",\"label\":\"BMR per kg\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kcal/kg/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"kj\",\"label\":\"BMR\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kJ/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Energy\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"bmr\",\"f\":\"10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex\"},{\"l\":\"tdee\",\"f\":\"(10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex)*activity\"},{\"l\":\"per_kg\",\"f\":\"(10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex)/weight\"},{\"l\":\"kj\",\"f\":\"(10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex)*4.184\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/basal-metabolic-rate\",\"slug\":\"basal-metabolic-rate\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Basal Metabolic Rate Calculator (Mifflin–St Jeor)\",\"title\":\"Basal Metabolic Rate (Mifflin–St Jeor) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Basal metabolic rate is the energy a body spends at complete rest. Free basal metabolic rate (mifflin–st jeor) calculator: enter your values and get basal met\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-basal-metabolic-rate\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sex\",\"l\":\"Sex\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-sex\",\"opts\":[[\"male-5-kcal\",\"Male (+5 kcal)\",\"5\"],[\"female-161-kcal\",\"Female (−161 kcal)\",\"-161\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"weight\",\"l\":\"Weight\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"height\",\"l\":\"Height\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"age\",\"l\":\"Age\",\"suf\":\"years\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"activity\",\"l\":\"Activity level\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-activity\",\"opts\":[[\"sedentary-1-2\",\"Sedentary (×1.2)\",\"1.2\"],[\"light-1-375\",\"Light (×1.375)\",\"1.375\"],[\"moderate-1-55\",\"Moderate (×1.55)\",\"1.55\"],[\"active-1-725\",\"Active (×1.725)\",\"1.725\"],[\"very-active-1-9\",\"Very active (×1.9)\",\"1.9\"]]}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bmr\",\"l\":\"Basal metabolic rate\",\"suf\":\"kcal/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tdee\",\"l\":\"Total daily energy expenditure\",\"suf\":\"kcal/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_kg\",\"l\":\"BMR per kg\",\"suf\":\"kcal/kg/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kj\",\"l\":\"BMR\",\"suf\":\"kJ/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sex\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"male-5-kcal\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-sex\"},\"weight\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"75\",\"c\":null}]},\"height\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"178\",\"c\":null}]},\"age\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"30\",\"c\":null}]},\"activity\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"light-1-375\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-activity\"},\"bmr\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tdee\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_kg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"kj\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-sex\":{\"name\":\"Sex\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"male-5-kcal\",\"label\":\"Male (+5 kcal)\",\"value\":\"5\"},{\"uid\":\"female-161-kcal\",\"label\":\"Female (−161 kcal)\",\"value\":\"-161\"}]},\"ds-activity\":{\"name\":\"Activity level\",\"uiType\":\"SELECT\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"sedentary-1-2\",\"label\":\"Sedentary (×1.2)\",\"value\":\"1.2\"},{\"uid\":\"light-1-375\",\"label\":\"Light (×1.375)\",\"value\":\"1.375\"},{\"uid\":\"moderate-1-55\",\"label\":\"Moderate (×1.55)\",\"value\":\"1.55\"},{\"uid\":\"active-1-725\",\"label\":\"Active (×1.725)\",\"value\":\"1.725\"},{\"uid\":\"very-active-1-9\",\"label\":\"Very active (×1.9)\",\"value\":\"1.9\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Energy\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"bmr\",\"f\":\"10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex\",\"r\":[\"weight\",\"height\",\"age\",\"sex\"]},{\"l\":\"tdee\",\"f\":\"(10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex)*activity\",\"r\":[\"weight\",\"height\",\"age\",\"sex\",\"activity\"]},{\"l\":\"per_kg\",\"f\":\"(10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex)/weight\",\"r\":[\"weight\",\"height\",\"age\",\"sex\"]},{\"l\":\"kj\",\"f\":\"(10*weight+6.25*height-5*age+sex)*4.184\",\"r\":[\"weight\",\"height\",\"age\",\"sex\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'heart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(112, 'tidal-volume-lung-capacity', 'biology', 'Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity Calculator', 'Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity Calculator', 'Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Tidal volume is the air moved in one quiet breath, about 6–8 mL per kilogram of ideal body weight. Free tidal volume and lung capacity calculator: enter your', 'Tidal volume is the air moved in one quiet breath, about 6–8 mL per kilogram of ideal body weight. It returns ideal body weight, tidal volume, minute ventilation and anatomical dead space, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator predicts it from height and sex, adds the standard lung volumes and converts to minute ventilation for a given respiratory rate. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity\",\"html\":\"<p>IBW (kg) = 50 + 0.91 × (height − 152.4);  V_T = 7 mL/kg IBW;  V_E = V_T × rate</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/tidal-volume-lung-capacity.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity Calculator — Tidal volume, respiratory rate and the minute volume they produce.\\\" title=\\\"Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Tidal volume, respiratory rate and the minute volume they produce.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>IBW</code></td><td>ideal body weight</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>V_T</code></td><td>tidal volume</td><td>mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>V_E</code></td><td>minute ventilation</td><td>L/min</td></tr><tr><td><code>V_D</code></td><td>anatomical dead space, about 2.2 mL/kg IBW</td><td>mL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: height of <strong>175</strong> cm, respiratory rate of <strong>14</strong> breaths/min, tidal volume per kg ideal body weight of <strong>7</strong> mL/kg.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives ideal body weight of <strong>70.57</strong> kg, tidal volume of <strong>493.96</strong> mL, minute ventilation of <strong>6.92</strong> L/min, anatomical dead space of <strong>155.25</strong> mL.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4 – 6 mL/kg IBW</td><td><strong>Lung-protective</strong></td><td>the target in ARDS ventilation</td></tr><tr><td>6 – 8 mL/kg IBW</td><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>normal adult ventilation</td></tr><tr><td>above 10 mL/kg IBW</td><td><strong>Large</strong></td><td>risk of volutrauma in injured lungs</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Tidal volume is set from ideal body weight, not actual weight, because lung size scales with height rather than with fat mass.</p></li><li><p>Minute ventilation is tidal volume times respiratory rate — a small fast breath ventilates the same as a large slow one, but not the same alveoli.</p></li><li><p>Vital capacity predictions are ±20%, and the gender difference in the reference equation is about 0.6 L.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using actual body weight in an obese patient, which gives dangerously large tidal volumes.</li><li>Confusing tidal volume with minute volume.</li><li>Entering height in inches in a centimetre field.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/respiratory-quotient\\\">Respiratory quotient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/acid-base-balance\\\">Acid-base balance</a>. <a href=\\\"/health/mean-arterial-pressure\\\">Mean arterial pressure</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why use ideal body weight?\",\"a\":\"Because lung volume follows height; using actual weight in obesity over-ventilates the patient.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a normal tidal volume?\",\"a\":\"About 500 mL for an adult man and 400 mL for an adult woman at rest.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns ideal body weight, tidal volume, minute ventilation and anatomical dead space. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"height\",\"label\":\"Height\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"175\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rate\",\"label\":\"Respiratory rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"14\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"breaths/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"per_kg\",\"label\":\"Tidal volume per kg ideal body weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"7\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"6–8 is the usual target\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL/kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ibw\",\"label\":\"Ideal body weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"vt\",\"label\":\"Tidal volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"minute\",\"label\":\"Minute ventilation\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"dead_space\",\"label\":\"Anatomical dead space\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Volumes\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"ibw\",\"f\":\"50+0.91*(height-152.4)\"},{\"l\":\"vt\",\"f\":\"(50+0.91*(height-152.4))*per_kg\"},{\"l\":\"minute\",\"f\":\"(50+0.91*(height-152.4))*per_kg*rate/1000\"},{\"l\":\"dead_space\",\"f\":\"(50+0.91*(height-152.4))*2.2\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/tidal-volume-lung-capacity\",\"slug\":\"tidal-volume-lung-capacity\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity Calculator\",\"title\":\"Tidal Volume and Lung Capacity Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Tidal volume is the air moved in one quiet breath, about 6–8 mL per kilogram of ideal body weight. Free tidal volume and lung capacity calculator: enter your\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-tidal-volume-lung-capacity\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"height\",\"l\":\"Height\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate\",\"l\":\"Respiratory rate\",\"suf\":\"breaths/min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_kg\",\"l\":\"Tidal volume per kg ideal body weight\",\"h\":\"6–8 is the usual target\",\"suf\":\"mL/kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ibw\",\"l\":\"Ideal body weight\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vt\",\"l\":\"Tidal volume\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"minute\",\"l\":\"Minute ventilation\",\"suf\":\"L/min\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dead_space\",\"l\":\"Anatomical dead space\",\"suf\":\"mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"height\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"175\",\"c\":null}]},\"rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"14\",\"c\":null}]},\"per_kg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"7\",\"c\":null}]},\"ibw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"vt\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"minute\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"dead_space\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Volumes\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"ibw\",\"f\":\"50+0.91*(height-152.4)\",\"r\":[\"height\"]},{\"l\":\"vt\",\"f\":\"(50+0.91*(height-152.4))*per_kg\",\"r\":[\"height\",\"per_kg\"]},{\"l\":\"minute\",\"f\":\"(50+0.91*(height-152.4))*per_kg*rate/1000\",\"r\":[\"height\",\"per_kg\",\"rate\"]},{\"l\":\"dead_space\",\"f\":\"(50+0.91*(height-152.4))*2.2\",\"r\":[\"height\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'heart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(113, 'respiratory-quotient', 'biology', 'Respiratory Quotient Calculator', 'Respiratory Quotient Calculator', 'Respiratory Quotient Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The respiratory quotient is carbon dioxide produced divided by oxygen consumed. Free respiratory quotient calculator: enter your values and get respiratory qu', 'The respiratory quotient is carbon dioxide produced divided by oxygen consumed. It returns respiratory quotient, non-protein RQ, energy equivalent and fat share of fuel, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt identifies which fuel a tissue is burning — 1.0 for carbohydrate, 0.7 for fat, 0.8 for protein — and it is the link between gas exchange and energy expenditure. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Respiratory Quotient\",\"html\":\"<p>RQ = VCO₂ ÷ VO₂</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/respiratory-quotient.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Respiratory Quotient Calculator — Where the ratio falls between fat and carbohydrate.\\\" title=\\\"Respiratory Quotient Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Where the ratio falls between fat and carbohydrate.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>VCO₂</code></td><td>carbon dioxide produced</td><td>mL/min</td></tr><tr><td><code>VO₂</code></td><td>oxygen consumed</td><td>mL/min</td></tr><tr><td><code>RQ</code></td><td>respiratory quotient</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Respiratory Quotient?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: co₂ produced of <strong>200</strong> mL/min, o₂ consumed of <strong>250</strong> mL/min.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives respiratory quotient of <strong>0.8</strong>, non-protein rq of <strong>0.8</strong>, energy equivalent of <strong>4.04</strong> kcal/L O₂, fat share of fuel of <strong>66.67</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.7</td><td><strong>Fat</strong></td><td>almost pure lipid oxidation</td></tr><tr><td>0.8</td><td><strong>Protein / mixed</strong></td><td>typical of a mixed diet at rest</td></tr><tr><td>1.0</td><td><strong>Carbohydrate</strong></td><td>pure glucose oxidation</td></tr><tr><td>above 1.0</td><td><strong>Not oxidation</strong></td><td>lipogenesis or hyperventilation — check the measurement</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>An RQ above 1.0 cannot come from burning a fuel: it appears during lipogenesis, when CO₂ is produced without corresponding O₂ use, or when the subject hyperventilates.</p></li><li><p>The energy equivalent rises with RQ, from 4.69 kcal per litre of O₂ for fat to 5.05 for carbohydrate, which is why indirect calorimetry needs the RQ.</p></li><li><p>For whole-body measurements the value is usually called the respiratory exchange ratio (RER) and includes protein oxidation.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using RQ from a spirometry session where the subject was breathing hard.</li><li>Assuming 0.82 is always right for a resting subject.</li><li>Mixing volumes in litres and millilitres between the two boxes.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/basal-metabolic-rate\\\">Basal metabolic rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/tidal-volume-lung-capacity\\\">Tidal volume</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/q10-temperature-coefficient\\\">Q10 coefficient</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal RQ?\",\"a\":\"0.7–1.0, with about 0.8 for a mixed diet at rest.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is RQ higher on a high-carbohydrate diet?\",\"a\":\"Because carbohydrate oxidation releases more CO₂ per O₂ consumed than fat does.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Respiratory Quotient actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns respiratory quotient, non-protein RQ, energy equivalent and fat share of fuel. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"co2\",\"label\":\"CO₂ produced\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"o2\",\"label\":\"O₂ consumed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"250\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL/min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rq\",\"label\":\"Respiratory quotient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fuel\",\"label\":\"Non-protein RQ\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"energy\",\"label\":\"Energy equivalent\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kcal/L O₂\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fat_pct\",\"label\":\"Fat share of fuel\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Gas exchange\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rq\",\"f\":\"co2/o2\"},{\"l\":\"fuel\",\"f\":\"co2/o2\"},{\"l\":\"energy\",\"f\":\"5.047*co2/o2\"},{\"l\":\"fat_pct\",\"f\":\"(1-(co2/o2-0.7)/0.3)*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/respiratory-quotient\",\"slug\":\"respiratory-quotient\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Respiratory Quotient Calculator\",\"title\":\"Respiratory Quotient Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The respiratory quotient is carbon dioxide produced divided by oxygen consumed. Free respiratory quotient calculator: enter your values and get respiratory qu\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-respiratory-quotient\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"co2\",\"l\":\"CO₂ produced\",\"suf\":\"mL/min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"o2\",\"l\":\"O₂ consumed\",\"suf\":\"mL/min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rq\",\"l\":\"Respiratory quotient\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fuel\",\"l\":\"Non-protein RQ\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"energy\",\"l\":\"Energy equivalent\",\"suf\":\"kcal/L O₂\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fat_pct\",\"l\":\"Fat share of fuel\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"co2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"200\",\"c\":null}]},\"o2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"250\",\"c\":null}]},\"rq\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fuel\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"energy\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fat_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Gas exchange\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rq\",\"f\":\"co2/o2\",\"r\":[\"co2\",\"o2\"]},{\"l\":\"fuel\",\"f\":\"co2/o2\",\"r\":[\"co2\",\"o2\"]},{\"l\":\"energy\",\"f\":\"5.047*co2/o2\",\"r\":[\"co2\",\"o2\"]},{\"l\":\"fat_pct\",\"f\":\"(1-(co2/o2-0.7)/0.3)*100\",\"r\":[\"co2\",\"o2\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'heart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(114, 'ghk-membrane-potential', 'biology', 'Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential Calculator', 'Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential Calculator', 'Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential Calculator', 'The Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equation predicts a cell\'s resting membrane potential from all three permeant ions at once — sodium, potassium and chloride — weighte', 'The Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equation predicts a cell\'s resting membrane potential from all three permeant ions at once — sodium, potassium and chloride — weighted by their permeabilities. It returns membrane potential, potassium equilibrium potential, sodium equilibrium potential and chloride equilibrium potential, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is why the resting potential sits near the potassium equilibrium potential rather than at it. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential\",\"html\":\"<p>V_m = 61.5 log₁₀[(P_K[K]ₒ + P_Na[Na]ₒ + P_Cl[Cl]ᵢ) ÷ (P_K[K]ᵢ + P_Na[Na]ᵢ + P_Cl[Cl]ₒ)]</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/ghk-membrane-potential.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential Calculator — Three equilibrium potentials and where the cell actually sits.\\\" title=\\\"Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Three equilibrium potentials and where the cell actually sits.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>[X]ₒ, [X]ᵢ</code></td><td>outside and inside concentrations</td><td>mM</td></tr><tr><td><code>P</code></td><td>relative permeability</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>61.5</code></td><td>RT/F × 2.303 at 37 °C</td><td>mV</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: potassium outside of <strong>4</strong> mM, potassium inside of <strong>140</strong> mM, sodium outside of <strong>145</strong> mM, sodium inside of <strong>12</strong> mM, chloride outside of <strong>110</strong> mM, chloride inside of <strong>10</strong> mM, potassium permeability of <strong>1</strong> relative, sodium permeability of <strong>0.04</strong> relative, chloride permeability of <strong>0.45</strong> relative.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives membrane potential of <strong>-69.09</strong> mV, potassium equilibrium potential of <strong>-94.96</strong> mV, sodium equilibrium potential of <strong>66.55</strong> mV, chloride equilibrium potential of <strong>-64.05</strong> mV.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>-90 to -70 mV</td><td><strong>Resting neuron</strong></td><td>typical mammalian neuron at rest</td></tr><tr><td>-60 to -40 mV</td><td><strong>Depolarised</strong></td><td>or a cell with higher sodium leak</td></tr><tr><td>-30 to 0 mV</td><td><strong>Excitable peak</strong></td><td>during an action potential</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Note that chloride is inverted: it is an anion, so inside and outside swap places in the numerator and denominator.</p></li><li><p>The potassium equilibrium potential is set by the ratio [K]ₒ/[K]ᵢ; raising extracellular potassium from 4 to 8 mM depolarises the cell by about 18 mV, which is why hyperkalaemia disturbs cardiac rhythm.</p></li><li><p>The 61.5 mV constant is RT/F in base-10 form at 37 °C; at 20 °C it is 58 mV.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Putting chloride the same way round as the cations.</li><li>Using conductances where permeabilities belong — they are not interchangeable.</li><li>Entering concentrations in mmol/L for one ion and mol/L for another.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/ghk-membrane-potential\\\">GHK membrane potential</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/acid-base-balance\\\">Acid–base balance</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\\\">Osmolarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is the resting potential near E_K?\",\"a\":\"Because at rest potassium permeability dominates, so the membrane sits close to the potassium equilibrium potential without quite reaching it.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if sodium permeability rises?\",\"a\":\"The potential depolarises toward the sodium equilibrium potential — the basis of the action potential.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns membrane potential, potassium equilibrium potential, sodium equilibrium potential and chloride equilibrium potential. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"ko\",\"label\":\"Potassium outside\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ki\",\"label\":\"Potassium inside\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"140\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"nao\",\"label\":\"Sodium outside\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"145\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"nai\",\"label\":\"Sodium inside\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"clo\",\"label\":\"Chloride outside\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"110\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cli\",\"label\":\"Chloride inside\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mM\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"pk\",\"label\":\"Potassium permeability\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"relative\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"pna\",\"label\":\"Sodium permeability\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.04\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"relative\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"pcl\",\"label\":\"Chloride permeability\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.45\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"relative\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"vm\",\"label\":\"Membrane potential\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mV\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ek\",\"label\":\"Potassium equilibrium potential\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mV\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ena\",\"label\":\"Sodium equilibrium potential\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mV\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ecl\",\"label\":\"Chloride equilibrium potential\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mV\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Membrane\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"vm\",\"f\":\"61.5*log10((pk*ko+pna*nao+pcl*cli)/(pk*ki+pna*nai+pcl*clo))\"},{\"l\":\"ek\",\"f\":\"61.5*log10(ko/ki)\"},{\"l\":\"ena\",\"f\":\"61.5*log10(nao/nai)\"},{\"l\":\"ecl\",\"f\":\"-61.5*log10(clo/cli)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/ghk-membrane-potential\",\"slug\":\"ghk-membrane-potential\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential Calculator\",\"title\":\"Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz Membrane Potential Calculator\",\"desc\":\"The Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equation predicts a cell\'s resting membrane potential from all three permeant ions at once — sodium, potassium and chloride — weighte\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-ghk-membrane-potential\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ko\",\"l\":\"Potassium outside\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ki\",\"l\":\"Potassium inside\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nao\",\"l\":\"Sodium outside\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nai\",\"l\":\"Sodium inside\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"clo\",\"l\":\"Chloride outside\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cli\",\"l\":\"Chloride inside\",\"suf\":\"mM\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pk\",\"l\":\"Potassium permeability\",\"suf\":\"relative\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pna\",\"l\":\"Sodium permeability\",\"suf\":\"relative\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pcl\",\"l\":\"Chloride permeability\",\"suf\":\"relative\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vm\",\"l\":\"Membrane potential\",\"suf\":\"mV\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ek\",\"l\":\"Potassium equilibrium potential\",\"suf\":\"mV\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ena\",\"l\":\"Sodium equilibrium potential\",\"suf\":\"mV\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ecl\",\"l\":\"Chloride equilibrium potential\",\"suf\":\"mV\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"ko\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"ki\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"140\",\"c\":null}]},\"nao\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"145\",\"c\":null}]},\"nai\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"clo\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"110\",\"c\":null}]},\"cli\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"pk\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"pna\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.04\",\"c\":null}]},\"pcl\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.45\",\"c\":null}]},\"vm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ek\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ena\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ecl\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Membrane\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"vm\",\"f\":\"61.5*log10((pk*ko+pna*nao+pcl*cli)/(pk*ki+pna*nai+pcl*clo))\",\"r\":[\"pk\",\"ko\",\"pna\",\"nao\",\"pcl\",\"cli\",\"ki\",\"nai\",\"clo\"]},{\"l\":\"ek\",\"f\":\"61.5*log10(ko/ki)\",\"r\":[\"ko\",\"ki\"]},{\"l\":\"ena\",\"f\":\"61.5*log10(nao/nai)\",\"r\":[\"nao\",\"nai\"]},{\"l\":\"ecl\",\"f\":\"-61.5*log10(clo/cli)\",\"r\":[\"clo\",\"cli\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'heart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(115, 'acid-base-balance', 'biology', 'Acid–Base Balance Calculator (ABG)', 'Acid–Base Balance Calculator (ABG)', 'Acid–Base Balance (ABG) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'An arterial blood gas reports pH, CO₂ and bicarbonate, and the acid–base balance comes from comparing them. Free acid–base balance (abg) calculator: enter you', 'An arterial blood gas reports pH, CO₂ and bicarbonate, and the acid–base balance comes from comparing them. It returns anion gap, albumin-corrected gap, expected PaCO₂ (Winter\'s formula) and base excess estimate, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives the anion gap, the respiratory compensation expected for a metabolic acidosis, and flags the classic mixed disorders. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Acid–Base Balance (ABG)\",\"html\":\"<p>Anion gap = Na⁺ − Cl⁻ − HCO₃⁻;  Winter\'s formula: expected PaCO₂ = 1.5 × HCO₃⁻ + 8</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/acid-base-balance.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Acid–Base Balance Calculator (ABG) — Measured CO₂ against what Winter\'s formula predicts.\\\" title=\\\"Acid–Base Balance Calculator (ABG)\\\"><figcaption>Measured CO₂ against what Winter\'s formula predicts.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>AG</code></td><td>anion gap</td><td>mmol/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>PaCO₂</code></td><td>arterial carbon dioxide tension</td><td>mmHg</td></tr><tr><td><code>HCO₃⁻</code></td><td>bicarbonate</td><td>mmol/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>BE</code></td><td>base excess</td><td>mmol/L</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Winter\'s formula and the albumin correction for the anion gap are standard bedside acid–base rules.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Acid–Base Balance (ABG)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: ph of <strong>7.32</strong>, paco₂ of <strong>28</strong> mmHg, bicarbonate of <strong>14</strong> mmol/L, sodium of <strong>140</strong> mmol/L, chloride of <strong>105</strong> mmol/L, albumin of <strong>40</strong> g/L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives anion gap of <strong>21</strong> mmol/L, albumin-corrected gap of <strong>111</strong> mmol/L, expected paco₂ (winter\'s formula) of <strong>29</strong> mmHg, base excess estimate of <strong>-10.38</strong> mmol/L, measured minus winter-predicted paco2 of <strong>-1</strong> mmHg.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AG 8 – 12</td><td><strong>Normal</strong></td><td>no unmeasured anion accumulation</td></tr><tr><td>AG 12 – 20</td><td><strong>Raised</strong></td><td>ketoacidosis, lactate, renal failure</td></tr><tr><td>AG above 20</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>a large unmeasured anion load</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Winter\'s formula predicts the CO₂ a metabolic acidosis should produce: a measured CO₂ above the predicted range means a coexisting respiratory acidosis, below it a respiratory alkalosis.</p></li><li><p>The anion gap falls by about 2.5 mmol/L for every 10 g/L of albumin below normal, so a \'normal\' gap in a hypoalbuminaemic patient may be a raised gap.</p></li><li><p>Base excess is the metabolically interesting number because it is independent of the respiratory component.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Interpreting pH without checking whether the CO₂ and bicarbonate changes are in the same direction.</li><li>Ignoring albumin when the anion gap is normal.</li><li>Using venous values for oxygenation calculations.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/acid-base-balance\\\">Acid-base balance</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\\\">Osmolarity</a>. <a href=\\\"/health/blood-pressure\\\">Blood pressure</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal anion gap?\",\"a\":\"About 8–12 mmol/L, higher if the laboratory measures chloride by a different method.\"},{\"q\":\"How do I recognise a mixed disorder?\",\"a\":\"When the measured CO₂ or bicarbonate does not match the compensation the primary disorder should produce.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Acid–Base Balance (ABG) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns anion gap, albumin-corrected gap, expected PaCO₂ (Winter\'s formula) and base excess estimate. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"ph\",\"label\":\"pH\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"7.32\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"paco2\",\"label\":\"PaCO₂\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"28\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmHg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"hco3\",\"label\":\"Bicarbonate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"14\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"na\",\"label\":\"Sodium\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"140\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cl\",\"label\":\"Chloride\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"105\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"albumin\",\"label\":\"Albumin\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gap\",\"label\":\"Anion gap\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gap_corrected\",\"label\":\"Albumin-corrected gap\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"expected_co2\",\"label\":\"Expected PaCO₂ (Winter\'s formula)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmHg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"base_excess\",\"label\":\"Base excess estimate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"co2_gap\",\"label\":\"Measured minus Winter-predicted PaCO2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmHg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Acid–base\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"gap\",\"f\":\"na-cl-hco3\"},{\"l\":\"gap_corrected\",\"f\":\"na-cl-hco3+2.5*(40-albumin/10)\"},{\"l\":\"expected_co2\",\"f\":\"1.5*hco3+8\"},{\"l\":\"base_excess\",\"f\":\"0.93*(hco3-24)+13.5*(ph-7.4)\"},{\"l\":\"co2_gap\",\"f\":\"paco2-(1.5*hco3+8)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/acid-base-balance\",\"slug\":\"acid-base-balance\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Acid–Base Balance Calculator (ABG)\",\"title\":\"Acid–Base Balance (ABG) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"An arterial blood gas reports pH, CO₂ and bicarbonate, and the acid–base balance comes from comparing them. Free acid–base balance (abg) calculator: enter you\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-acid-base-balance\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ph\",\"l\":\"pH\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"paco2\",\"l\":\"PaCO₂\",\"suf\":\"mmHg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hco3\",\"l\":\"Bicarbonate\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"na\",\"l\":\"Sodium\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cl\",\"l\":\"Chloride\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"albumin\",\"l\":\"Albumin\",\"suf\":\"g/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gap\",\"l\":\"Anion gap\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gap_corrected\",\"l\":\"Albumin-corrected gap\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"expected_co2\",\"l\":\"Expected PaCO₂ (Winter\'s formula)\",\"suf\":\"mmHg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"base_excess\",\"l\":\"Base excess estimate\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"co2_gap\",\"l\":\"Measured minus Winter-predicted PaCO2\",\"suf\":\"mmHg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"ph\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"7.32\",\"c\":null}]},\"paco2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"28\",\"c\":null}]},\"hco3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"14\",\"c\":null}]},\"na\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"140\",\"c\":null}]},\"cl\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"105\",\"c\":null}]},\"albumin\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"gap\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gap_corrected\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"expected_co2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"base_excess\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"co2_gap\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Acid–base\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"gap\",\"f\":\"na-cl-hco3\",\"r\":[\"na\",\"cl\",\"hco3\"]},{\"l\":\"gap_corrected\",\"f\":\"na-cl-hco3+2.5*(40-albumin/10)\",\"r\":[\"na\",\"cl\",\"hco3\",\"albumin\"]},{\"l\":\"expected_co2\",\"f\":\"1.5*hco3+8\",\"r\":[\"hco3\"]},{\"l\":\"base_excess\",\"f\":\"0.93*(hco3-24)+13.5*(ph-7.4)\",\"r\":[\"hco3\",\"ph\"]},{\"l\":\"co2_gap\",\"f\":\"paco2-(1.5*hco3+8)\",\"r\":[\"paco2\",\"hco3\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'heart', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(116, 'growing-degree-days', 'biology', 'Growing Degree Days Calculator', 'Growing Degree Days Calculator', 'Growing Degree Days Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Growing degree days count the heat a crop has accumulated above its base temperature. Free growing degree days calculator: enter your values and get growing d', 'Growing degree days count the heat a crop has accumulated above its base temperature. It returns growing degree days for the day, over seven days at this weather and accumulated over 60 days, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThey predict development far better than calendar days do, which is why sowing dates, variety maturity and pest emergence are all scheduled on them. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Growing Degree Days\",\"html\":\"<p>GDD = max(0, (T_max + T_min)/2 − T_base)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/growing-degree-days.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Growing Degree Days Calculator — Degree days accumulating across a season.\\\" title=\\\"Growing Degree Days Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Degree days accumulating across a season.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T_max, T_min</code></td><td>daily maximum and minimum air temperature</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>T_base</code></td><td>temperature below which development stops</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>GDD</code></td><td>degree days accumulated in one day</td><td>°C·day</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Growing Degree Days?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: method set to <strong>0.0</strong>, daily maximum of <strong>33</strong>°C, daily minimum of <strong>12</strong>°C, base temperature of <strong>10</strong>°C, upper cut-off of <strong>30</strong>°C.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives growing degree days for the day of <strong>12.5</strong> GDD, over seven days at this weather of <strong>87.5</strong> GDD, accumulated over 60 days of <strong>750</strong> GDD.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 GDD</td><td><strong>No development</strong></td><td>below the base temperature all day</td></tr><tr><td>5 – 15 GDD/day</td><td><strong>Normal growing weather</strong></td><td>typical of a temperate summer</td></tr><tr><td>above 25 GDD/day</td><td><strong>Very rapid</strong></td><td>heat that may also stress the crop</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Base temperatures are crop-specific: 0 °C for cool-season grasses, 5 °C for wheat, 10 °C for maize and soybean, 15.5 °C for cotton.</p></li><li><p>The upper cut-off matters in hot climates: without it, a 40 °C day adds heat the plant cannot use and maturity predictions come out early.</p></li><li><p>Accumulate from sowing, not from 1 January, if you are timing a crop rather than a season.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using the mean of hourly readings instead of the daily maximum and minimum.</li><li>Mixing Fahrenheit maxima with Celsius minima.</li><li>Assuming a single GDD target fits every variety — early and late maturities differ by hundreds of degree days.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/days-to-maturity\\\">Days to maturity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/q10-temperature-coefficient\\\">Q10 coefficient</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many GDD does maize need to mature?\",\"a\":\"About 2 400–2 800 GDD (50 °F base) depending on the hybrid; in Celsius-base-10 terms, roughly 1 300–1 500.\"},{\"q\":\"What if the minimum is below the base temperature?\",\"a\":\"The standard method still uses the daily mean; more refined models floor each half-day at the base temperature.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Growing Degree Days actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns growing degree days for the day, over seven days at this weather and accumulated over 60 days. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"method\",\"label\":\"Method\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"mean-temperature-no-cap\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"mean-temperature-no-cap\",\"Mean temperature, no cap\",\"0\"],[\"cap-the-maximum-at-the-upper-cut-off\",\"Cap the maximum at the upper cut-off\",\"1\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tmax\",\"label\":\"Daily maximum\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"33\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tmin\",\"label\":\"Daily minimum\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"base\",\"label\":\"Base temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"10 °C for maize, 5 °C for wheat\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"upper\",\"label\":\"Upper cut-off\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"30\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Growth stops above this in many models\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gdd\",\"label\":\"Growing degree days for the day\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"GDD\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gdd_week\",\"label\":\"Over seven days at this weather\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"GDD\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"since_sowing\",\"label\":\"Accumulated over 60 days\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"GDD\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Degree days\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"gdd\",\"f\":\"if_else(method>0,max(0,(min(tmax,upper)+tmin)/2-base),max(0,(tmax+tmin)/2-base))\"},{\"l\":\"gdd_week\",\"f\":\"if_else(method>0,max(0,(min(tmax,upper)+tmin)/2-base),max(0,(tmax+tmin)/2-base))*7\"},{\"l\":\"since_sowing\",\"f\":\"if_else(method>0,max(0,(min(tmax,upper)+tmin)/2-base),max(0,(tmax+tmin)/2-base))*60\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/growing-degree-days\",\"slug\":\"growing-degree-days\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Growing Degree Days Calculator\",\"title\":\"Growing Degree Days Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Growing degree days count the heat a crop has accumulated above its base temperature. Free growing degree days calculator: enter your values and get growing d\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-growing-degree-days\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"method\",\"l\":\"Method\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-method\",\"opts\":[[\"mean-temperature-no-cap\",\"Mean temperature, no cap\",\"0\"],[\"cap-the-maximum-at-the-upper-cut-off\",\"Cap the maximum at the upper cut-off\",\"1\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tmax\",\"l\":\"Daily maximum\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tmin\",\"l\":\"Daily minimum\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"base\",\"l\":\"Base temperature\",\"h\":\"10 °C for maize, 5 °C for wheat\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"upper\",\"l\":\"Upper cut-off\",\"h\":\"Growth stops above this in many models\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gdd\",\"l\":\"Growing degree days for the day\",\"suf\":\"GDD\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gdd_week\",\"l\":\"Over seven days at this weather\",\"suf\":\"GDD\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"since_sowing\",\"l\":\"Accumulated over 60 days\",\"suf\":\"GDD\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"method\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"mean-temperature-no-cap\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-method\"},\"tmax\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"33\",\"c\":null}]},\"tmin\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"base\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"upper\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"30\",\"c\":null}]},\"gdd\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gdd_week\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"since_sowing\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-method\":{\"name\":\"Method\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"mean-temperature-no-cap\",\"label\":\"Mean temperature, no cap\",\"value\":\"0\"},{\"uid\":\"cap-the-maximum-at-the-upper-cut-off\",\"label\":\"Cap the maximum at the upper cut-off\",\"value\":\"1\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Degree days\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"gdd\",\"f\":\"if_else(method>0,max(0,(min(tmax,upper)+tmin)/2-base),max(0,(tmax+tmin)/2-base))\",\"r\":[\"method\",\"tmax\",\"upper\",\"tmin\",\"base\"]},{\"l\":\"gdd_week\",\"f\":\"if_else(method>0,max(0,(min(tmax,upper)+tmin)/2-base),max(0,(tmax+tmin)/2-base))*7\",\"r\":[\"method\",\"tmax\",\"upper\",\"tmin\",\"base\"]},{\"l\":\"since_sowing\",\"f\":\"if_else(method>0,max(0,(min(tmax,upper)+tmin)/2-base),max(0,(tmax+tmin)/2-base))*60\",\"r\":[\"method\",\"tmax\",\"upper\",\"tmin\",\"base\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(117, 'npk-fertilizer-blend', 'biology', 'NPK Fertilizer Blend Calculator', 'NPK Fertilizer Blend Calculator', 'NPK Fertilizer Blend Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A fertilizer grade such as 20-10-10 tells you the percentage of nitrogen, phosphate and potash by weight — not the mass to spread. Free npk fertilizer blend c', 'A fertilizer grade such as 20-10-10 tells you the percentage of nitrogen, phosphate and potash by weight — not the mass to spread. It returns product to apply (limited by N), product limited by P, product limited by K and bags of 50 kg per hectare, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a nutrient requirement into kilograms of product per hectare and the fill weight for a spreader. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the NPK Fertilizer Blend\",\"html\":\"<p>Product (kg/ha) = nutrient required ÷ percentage in the product × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/npk-fertilizer-blend.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: NPK Fertilizer Blend Calculator — Nutrient requirement against the product rate for each nutrient.\\\" title=\\\"NPK Fertilizer Blend Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Nutrient requirement against the product rate for each nutrient.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>N, P₂O₅, K₂O</code></td><td>nutrient requirement</td><td>kg/ha</td></tr><tr><td><code>grade</code></td><td>percentage of each nutrient in the product</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>rate</code></td><td>product needed</td><td>kg/ha</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the NPK Fertilizer Blend?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: nitrogen required of <strong>120</strong> kg/ha, phosphate required (p₂o₅) of <strong>60</strong> kg/ha, potash required (k₂o) of <strong>60</strong> kg/ha, n in the product of <strong>20</strong>%, p₂o₅ in the product of <strong>10</strong>%, k₂o in the product of <strong>10</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives product to apply (limited by n) of <strong>600</strong> kg/ha, product limited by p of <strong>600</strong> kg/ha, product limited by k of <strong>600</strong> kg/ha, bags of 50 kg per hectare of <strong>12</strong> bags.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>The grade is by weight of N, P₂O₅ and K₂O — not elemental phosphorus and potassium. To convert, P₂O₅ × 0.44 = P and K₂O × 0.83 = K.</p></li><li><p>A single blend rarely matches all three requirements, so the product rate is set by the nutrient that runs out first and the rest is topped up with straight fertilizers.</p></li><li><p>Fertilizer grade percentages must sum to at most 100; a 20-10-10 blend is 40% nutrient and 60% carrier.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Treating P₂O₅ as elemental P, which overstates phosphate by a factor of 2.3.</li><li>Dividing by the grade instead of multiplying by 100 ÷ grade.</li><li>Ignoring the nutrient already in the soil test.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/fertilizer-injector-ratio\\\">Fertilizer injector ratio</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/lime-requirement\\\">Lime requirement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How much 20-10-10 gives 120 kg of nitrogen?\",\"a\":\"600 kg per hectare — 120 ÷ 0.20.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the bag count differ from the rate?\",\"a\":\"Because bags are sold in fixed weights; divide the rate by the bag weight.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the NPK Fertilizer Blend actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns product to apply (limited by N), product limited by P, product limited by K and bags of 50 kg per hectare. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"n_need\",\"label\":\"Nitrogen required\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"120\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"p_need\",\"label\":\"Phosphate required (P₂O₅)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"60\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"k_need\",\"label\":\"Potash required (K₂O)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"60\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n_pct\",\"label\":\"N in the product\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"p_pct\",\"label\":\"P₂O₅ in the product\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"k_pct\",\"label\":\"K₂O in the product\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rate\",\"label\":\"Product to apply (limited by N)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rate_p\",\"label\":\"Product limited by P\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rate_k\",\"label\":\"Product limited by K\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"bag\",\"label\":\"Bags of 50 kg per hectare\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bags\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Blend\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"n_need/n_pct*100\"},{\"l\":\"rate_p\",\"f\":\"p_need/p_pct*100\"},{\"l\":\"rate_k\",\"f\":\"k_need/k_pct*100\"},{\"l\":\"bag\",\"f\":\"n_need/n_pct*100/50\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/npk-fertilizer-blend\",\"slug\":\"npk-fertilizer-blend\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"NPK Fertilizer Blend Calculator\",\"title\":\"NPK Fertilizer Blend Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A fertilizer grade such as 20-10-10 tells you the percentage of nitrogen, phosphate and potash by weight — not the mass to spread. Free npk fertilizer blend c\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-npk-fertilizer-blend\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n_need\",\"l\":\"Nitrogen required\",\"suf\":\"kg/ha\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p_need\",\"l\":\"Phosphate required (P₂O₅)\",\"suf\":\"kg/ha\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"k_need\",\"l\":\"Potash required (K₂O)\",\"suf\":\"kg/ha\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n_pct\",\"l\":\"N in the product\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p_pct\",\"l\":\"P₂O₅ in the product\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"k_pct\",\"l\":\"K₂O in the product\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate\",\"l\":\"Product to apply (limited by N)\",\"suf\":\"kg/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate_p\",\"l\":\"Product limited by P\",\"suf\":\"kg/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate_k\",\"l\":\"Product limited by K\",\"suf\":\"kg/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bag\",\"l\":\"Bags of 50 kg per hectare\",\"suf\":\"bags\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"n_need\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"120\",\"c\":null}]},\"p_need\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"60\",\"c\":null}]},\"k_need\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"60\",\"c\":null}]},\"n_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"p_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"k_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rate_p\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rate_k\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"bag\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Blend\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"n_need/n_pct*100\",\"r\":[\"n_need\",\"n_pct\"]},{\"l\":\"rate_p\",\"f\":\"p_need/p_pct*100\",\"r\":[\"p_need\",\"p_pct\"]},{\"l\":\"rate_k\",\"f\":\"k_need/k_pct*100\",\"r\":[\"k_need\",\"k_pct\"]},{\"l\":\"bag\",\"f\":\"n_need/n_pct*100/50\",\"r\":[\"n_need\",\"n_pct\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(118, 'fertilizer-injector-ratio', 'biology', 'Fertilizer Injector Ratio Calculator', 'Fertilizer Injector Ratio Calculator', 'Fertilizer Injector Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Injector ratios tell you how much stock solution to mix so that the water reaching the plants carries the intended concentration. Free fertilizer injector rat', 'Injector ratios tell you how much stock solution to mix so that the water reaching the plants carries the intended concentration. It returns stock concentration, fertilizer per litre of stock and fertilizer per tank, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts between a target ppm in the irrigation line, the injector ratio and the weight of fertilizer for a stock tank of any size. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Fertilizer Injector Ratio\",\"html\":\"<p>Stock ppm = line ppm × injector ratio;  grams per litre = stock ppm ÷ (%N × 10)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/fertilizer-injector-ratio.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Fertilizer Injector Ratio Calculator — Line concentration, injector ratio and stock weight.\\\" title=\\\"Fertilizer Injector Ratio Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Line concentration, injector ratio and stock weight.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>target</code></td><td>desired concentration in the irrigation line</td><td>ppm</td></tr><tr><td><code>ratio</code></td><td>dilution factor of the injector</td><td>:1</td></tr><tr><td><code>%N</code></td><td>nitrogen content of the fertilizer</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Fertilizer Injector Ratio?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: target nitrogen in the line of <strong>150</strong> ppm, nitrogen in the fertilizer of <strong>20</strong>%, injector ratio of <strong>100</strong> :1, stock tank size of <strong>200</strong> L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives stock concentration of <strong>15,000</strong> ppm, fertilizer per litre of stock of <strong>0.0075</strong> g/L, fertilizer per tank of <strong>1.5</strong> kg.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>A 1:100 injector makes the stock 100 times stronger than the line, so a 150 ppm target needs 15 000 ppm in the tank.</p></li><li><p>Weigh fertilizers in grams per litre of stock, then check the electrical conductivity — it rises with concentration and is the quickest in-field check that the mix is right.</p></li><li><p>Nitrogen sources differ: calcium nitrate and ammonium nitrate carry different amounts of calcium and ammonium, which matters for pH drift in the root zone.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Confusing 1:100 with 1:99 — the difference is small for the stock strength but large for water accounting.</li><li>Mixing the stock in the irrigation line instead of the tank.</li><li>Using a fertilizer percentage as a decimal in one place and a percentage in another.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/npk-fertilizer-blend\\\">NPK fertilizer blend</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/ppm-to-molarity\\\">ppm to molarity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I mix 100 ppm nitrogen with 20% fertilizer at 1:100?\",\"a\":\"50 g per litre of stock — 100 × 100 ÷ (20 × 10).\"},{\"q\":\"Does the injector ratio affect the final EC?\",\"a\":\"Yes: the line EC is set by the diluted concentration, so a stronger stock at a higher ratio can deliver the same EC.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Fertilizer Injector Ratio actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns stock concentration, fertilizer per litre of stock and fertilizer per tank. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Target nitrogen in the line\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"150\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ppm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n_pct\",\"label\":\"Nitrogen in the fertilizer\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Injector ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"1:100 means 1 part stock to 99 parts water\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tank\",\"label\":\"Stock tank size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"stock_ppm\",\"label\":\"Stock concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ppm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_litre\",\"label\":\"Fertilizer per litre of stock\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_tank\",\"label\":\"Fertilizer per tank\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Injection\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"stock_ppm\",\"f\":\"target*ratio\"},{\"l\":\"per_litre\",\"f\":\"target*ratio/n_pct/100/1000\"},{\"l\":\"per_tank\",\"f\":\"target*ratio/n_pct/100/1000*tank\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/fertilizer-injector-ratio\",\"slug\":\"fertilizer-injector-ratio\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Fertilizer Injector Ratio Calculator\",\"title\":\"Fertilizer Injector Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Injector ratios tell you how much stock solution to mix so that the water reaching the plants carries the intended concentration. Free fertilizer injector rat\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-fertilizer-injector-ratio\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Target nitrogen in the line\",\"suf\":\"ppm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n_pct\",\"l\":\"Nitrogen in the fertilizer\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Injector ratio\",\"h\":\"1:100 means 1 part stock to 99 parts water\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tank\",\"l\":\"Stock tank size\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stock_ppm\",\"l\":\"Stock concentration\",\"suf\":\"ppm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_litre\",\"l\":\"Fertilizer per litre of stock\",\"suf\":\"g/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_tank\",\"l\":\"Fertilizer per tank\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"150\",\"c\":null}]},\"n_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"tank\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"200\",\"c\":null}]},\"stock_ppm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_litre\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_tank\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Injection\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"stock_ppm\",\"f\":\"target*ratio\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"ratio\"]},{\"l\":\"per_litre\",\"f\":\"target*ratio/n_pct/100/1000\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"ratio\",\"n_pct\"]},{\"l\":\"per_tank\",\"f\":\"target*ratio/n_pct/100/1000*tank\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"ratio\",\"n_pct\",\"tank\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(119, 'crop-water-requirement', 'biology', 'Crop Water Requirement Calculator (ETc)', 'Crop Water Requirement Calculator (ETc)', 'Crop Water Requirement (ETc) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A crop\'s water use is the reference evapotranspiration multiplied by a crop coefficient: ETc = Kc × ETo. Free crop water requirement (etc) calculator: enter y', 'A crop\'s water use is the reference evapotranspiration multiplied by a crop coefficient: ETc = Kc × ETo. It returns crop water use, seasonal use (120 days), volume to apply per hectare and hours a 10 m³/h system must run, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives daily and seasonal water use per hectare, the irrigation volume and how long a system must run to deliver it. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Crop Water Requirement (ETc)\",\"html\":\"<p>ETc = Kc × ETo;  volume (m³/ha) = ETc (mm) × 10 ÷ efficiency</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/crop-water-requirement.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Crop Water Requirement Calculator (ETc) — Reference ET, the crop coefficient, and the water it implies.\\\" title=\\\"Crop Water Requirement Calculator (ETc)\\\"><figcaption>Reference ET, the crop coefficient, and the water it implies.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ETo</code></td><td>reference evapotranspiration</td><td>mm/day</td></tr><tr><td><code>Kc</code></td><td>crop coefficient for the growth stage</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>ETc</code></td><td>crop evapotranspiration</td><td>mm/day</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Crop Water Requirement (ETc)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: reference evapotranspiration (eto) of <strong>5.4</strong> mm/day, crop coefficient (kc) of <strong>1.05</strong>, area of <strong>1</strong> ha, irrigation efficiency of <strong>80</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives crop water use of <strong>5.67</strong> mm/day, seasonal use (120 days) of <strong>680.4</strong> mm, volume to apply per hectare of <strong>70.88</strong> m³/ha/day, hours a 10 m³/h system must run of <strong>7.09</strong> h.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>One millimetre over one hectare is 10 m³ of water — the conversion that makes irrigation arithmetic easy.</p></li><li><p>Kc changes through the season: about 0.3 at emergence, 1.0–1.2 at mid-season, and falling as the crop senesces.</p></li><li><p>Irrigation efficiency divides the requirement: 80% efficiency means a fifth of the water never reaches the root zone.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using a single Kc for the whole season.</li><li>Treating mm as litres per square metre — they are numerically equal, which is a help, not a licence to mix the units.</li><li>Ignoring rainfall, which reduces the irrigation requirement.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/growing-degree-days\\\">Growing degree days</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/field-capacity\\\">Field capacity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/plant-available-water\\\">Plant available water</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a typical Kc?\",\"a\":\"0.3 for bare soil, 0.7 for early growth, 1.0–1.2 at full canopy, 0.5 at senescence.\"},{\"q\":\"How much water is 5 mm/day per hectare?\",\"a\":\"50 m³ per hectare per day.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Crop Water Requirement (ETc) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns crop water use, seasonal use (120 days), volume to apply per hectare and hours a 10 m³/h system must run. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"eto\",\"label\":\"Reference evapotranspiration (ETo)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From a weather station or FAO-56\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"kc\",\"label\":\"Crop coefficient (Kc)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.05\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"1.05 is a typical mid-season value\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"efficiency\",\"label\":\"Irrigation efficiency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"80\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Drip 90%, sprinkler 75%, furrow 60%\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"etc\",\"label\":\"Crop water use\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"season\",\"label\":\"Seasonal use (120 days)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Volume to apply per hectare\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³/ha/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hours\",\"label\":\"Hours a 10 m³/h system must run\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Water use\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"etc\",\"f\":\"eto*kc\"},{\"l\":\"season\",\"f\":\"eto*kc*120\"},{\"l\":\"volume\",\"f\":\"eto*kc*10/(efficiency/100)\"},{\"l\":\"hours\",\"f\":\"eto*kc*10/(efficiency/100)/area/10\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\",\"slug\":\"crop-water-requirement\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Crop Water Requirement Calculator (ETc)\",\"title\":\"Crop Water Requirement (ETc) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A crop\'s water use is the reference evapotranspiration multiplied by a crop coefficient: ETc = Kc × ETo. Free crop water requirement (etc) calculator: enter y\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-crop-water-requirement\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"eto\",\"l\":\"Reference evapotranspiration (ETo)\",\"h\":\"From a weather station or FAO-56\",\"suf\":\"mm/day\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kc\",\"l\":\"Crop coefficient (Kc)\",\"h\":\"1.05 is a typical mid-season value\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Area\",\"suf\":\"ha\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"efficiency\",\"l\":\"Irrigation efficiency\",\"h\":\"Drip 90%, sprinkler 75%, furrow 60%\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"etc\",\"l\":\"Crop water use\",\"suf\":\"mm/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"season\",\"l\":\"Seasonal use (120 days)\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Volume to apply per hectare\",\"suf\":\"m³/ha/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hours\",\"l\":\"Hours a 10 m³/h system must run\",\"suf\":\"h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"eto\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"kc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.05\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"efficiency\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"80\",\"c\":null}]},\"etc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"season\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hours\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Water use\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"etc\",\"f\":\"eto*kc\",\"r\":[\"eto\",\"kc\"]},{\"l\":\"season\",\"f\":\"eto*kc*120\",\"r\":[\"eto\",\"kc\"]},{\"l\":\"volume\",\"f\":\"eto*kc*10/(efficiency/100)\",\"r\":[\"eto\",\"kc\",\"efficiency\"]},{\"l\":\"hours\",\"f\":\"eto*kc*10/(efficiency/100)/area/10\",\"r\":[\"eto\",\"kc\",\"efficiency\",\"area\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(120, 'crop-water-stress-index', 'biology', 'Crop Water Stress Index Calculator', 'Crop Water Stress Index Calculator', 'Crop Water Stress Index Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The crop water stress index compares a canopy temperature with the wet and dry reference surfaces around it: 0 means a well-watered crop transpiring freely, 1', 'The crop water stress index compares a canopy temperature with the wet and dry reference surfaces around it: 0 means a well-watered crop transpiring freely, 1 means a fully closed stomata. It returns crop water stress index, estimated root-zone depletion and stress class, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is the standard way to turn a thermal image into an irrigation decision. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Crop Water Stress Index\",\"html\":\"<p>CWSI = (T_c − T_wet) ÷ (T_dry − T_wet)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/crop-water-stress-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Crop Water Stress Index Calculator — Canopy temperature between the wet and dry references.\\\" title=\\\"Crop Water Stress Index Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Canopy temperature between the wet and dry references.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T_c</code></td><td>canopy temperature</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>T_wet</code></td><td>temperature of a well-watered reference surface</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>T_dry</code></td><td>temperature of a dry, non-transpiring surface</td><td>°C</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Crop Water Stress Index?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: canopy temperature of <strong>28.5</strong>°C, wet reference temperature of <strong>22</strong>°C, dry reference temperature of <strong>36</strong>°C.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives crop water stress index of <strong>0.4643</strong>, estimated root-zone depletion of <strong>46.43</strong>%, stress class of <strong>1.39</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 – 0.2</td><td><strong>No stress</strong></td><td>transpiring freely; irrigate on schedule</td></tr><tr><td>0.2 – 0.4</td><td><strong>Mild stress</strong></td><td>the usual trigger for scheduling</td></tr><tr><td>0.4 – 0.7</td><td><strong>Moderate stress</strong></td><td>yield is beginning to be affected</td></tr><tr><td>above 0.7</td><td><strong>Severe stress</strong></td><td>stomata closed; growth has stopped</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The wet and dry references can be measured (wet and dry paper discs beside the canopy) or computed from air temperature and humidity.</p></li><li><p>Thermal readings need to be taken near solar noon, from a nadir angle, with the canopy filling the field of view.</p></li><li><p>CWSI rises before visible wilting, which is the point: it gives a day or two of warning.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Measuring the canopy from an oblique angle, which mixes in soil temperature.</li><li>Using air temperature as the wet reference — a transpiring surface is usually cooler.</li><li>Comparing CWSI between days with very different vapour pressure deficits.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/stomatal-density\\\">Stomatal density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/transpiration-rate\\\">Transpiration rate</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What CWSI should trigger irrigation?\",\"a\":\"Most scheduling programmes use 0.3–0.4 as the threshold; sensitive crops prefer the lower end.\"},{\"q\":\"Can CWSI be negative?\",\"a\":\"Yes — a canopy cooler than the wet reference means unusually high transpiration, often after irrigation.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Crop Water Stress Index actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns crop water stress index, estimated root-zone depletion and stress class. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"tc\",\"label\":\"Canopy temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"28.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"twet\",\"label\":\"Wet reference temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"22\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"A fully transpiring surface\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tdry\",\"label\":\"Dry reference temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"36\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"A non-transpiring surface\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cswi\",\"label\":\"Crop water stress index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"depletion\",\"label\":\"Estimated root-zone depletion\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"stress\",\"label\":\"Stress class\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Stress\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cswi\",\"f\":\"(tc-twet)/(tdry-twet)\"},{\"l\":\"depletion\",\"f\":\"(tc-twet)/(tdry-twet)*100\"},{\"l\":\"stress\",\"f\":\"(tc-twet)/(tdry-twet)*3\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/crop-water-stress-index\",\"slug\":\"crop-water-stress-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Crop Water Stress Index Calculator\",\"title\":\"Crop Water Stress Index Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The crop water stress index compares a canopy temperature with the wet and dry reference surfaces around it: 0 means a well-watered crop transpiring freely, 1\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-crop-water-stress-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tc\",\"l\":\"Canopy temperature\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"twet\",\"l\":\"Wet reference temperature\",\"h\":\"A fully transpiring surface\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tdry\",\"l\":\"Dry reference temperature\",\"h\":\"A non-transpiring surface\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cswi\",\"l\":\"Crop water stress index\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"depletion\",\"l\":\"Estimated root-zone depletion\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stress\",\"l\":\"Stress class\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"tc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"28.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"twet\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"22\",\"c\":null}]},\"tdry\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"36\",\"c\":null}]},\"cswi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"depletion\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"stress\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Stress\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cswi\",\"f\":\"(tc-twet)/(tdry-twet)\",\"r\":[\"tc\",\"twet\",\"tdry\"]},{\"l\":\"depletion\",\"f\":\"(tc-twet)/(tdry-twet)*100\",\"r\":[\"tc\",\"twet\",\"tdry\"]},{\"l\":\"stress\",\"f\":\"(tc-twet)/(tdry-twet)*3\",\"r\":[\"tc\",\"twet\",\"tdry\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(121, 'plant-available-water', 'biology', 'Field Capacity and Plant Available Water Calculator', 'Field Capacity and Plant Available Water Calculator', 'Field Capacity and Plant Available Water Calculator', 'Plant available water is what a soil holds between field capacity and the permanent wilting point. Free field capacity and plant available water calculator: e', 'Plant available water is what a soil holds between field capacity and the permanent wilting point. It returns plant available water, available water in the root zone, deficit to refill and days until wilting at 5 mm/day, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt decides how long a crop can go between irrigations, and it is one of the few soil properties worth measuring once because it barely changes. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Field Capacity and Plant Available Water\",\"html\":\"<p>PAW (%) = FC − PWP;  PAW (mm) = PAW% ÷ 100 × rooting depth</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/plant-available-water.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Field Capacity and Plant Available Water Calculator — Saturation, field capacity, wilting point, and what roots can reach.\\\" title=\\\"Field Capacity and Plant Available Water Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Saturation, field capacity, wilting point, and what roots can reach.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>FC</code></td><td>field capacity, volumetric</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>PWP</code></td><td>permanent wilting point, volumetric</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>PAW</code></td><td>plant available water</td><td>% or mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Field Capacity and Plant Available Water?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: field capacity of <strong>32</strong>%, permanent wilting point of <strong>15</strong>%, rooting depth of <strong>600</strong> mm, current water content of <strong>24</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives plant available water of <strong>17</strong>%, available water in the root zone of <strong>102</strong> mm, deficit to refill of <strong>48</strong> mm, days until wilting at 5 mm/day of <strong>10.8</strong> days.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 10%</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>sand and gravel; irrigate little and often</td></tr><tr><td>10 – 20%</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>sandy loam; the common middle ground</td></tr><tr><td>above 20%</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>clay loam and peat; long intervals between irrigations</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Sands hold little plant available water even though they drain freely; clays hold a lot but release it slowly.</p></li><li><p>The mm figure is what an irrigation schedule works in: 51 mm of available water at 5 mm/day is about ten days of buffer.</p></li><li><p>Field capacity and wilting point are measured on undisturbed cores — a texture class conversion is only a rough guide.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using gravimetric water content as if it were volumetric.</li><li>Applying the same rooting depth to seedlings and mature plants.</li><li>Ignoring compaction, which cuts rooting depth far below the value in the table.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-stress-index\\\">Crop water stress index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/field-capacity\\\">Field capacity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How long can a crop go between irrigations?\",\"a\":\"Divide the available water in the root zone by the daily crop water use.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does clay hold more water than sand but wilt sooner?\",\"a\":\"Because much of the water in clay is held too tightly for roots to extract.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Field Capacity and Plant Available Water actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns plant available water, available water in the root zone, deficit to refill and days until wilting at 5 mm/day. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"fc\",\"label\":\"Field capacity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"32\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Volumetric water content\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"pwp\",\"label\":\"Permanent wilting point\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"15\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"depth\",\"label\":\"Rooting depth\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"600\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"current\",\"label\":\"Current water content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"24\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"paw\",\"label\":\"Plant available water\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"paw_mm\",\"label\":\"Available water in the root zone\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"deficit\",\"label\":\"Deficit to refill\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"days\",\"label\":\"Days until wilting at 5 mm/day\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Water\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"paw\",\"f\":\"fc-pwp\"},{\"l\":\"paw_mm\",\"f\":\"(fc-pwp)/100*depth\"},{\"l\":\"deficit\",\"f\":\"(fc-current)/100*depth\"},{\"l\":\"days\",\"f\":\"(current-pwp)/100*depth/5\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/plant-available-water\",\"slug\":\"plant-available-water\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Field Capacity and Plant Available Water Calculator\",\"title\":\"Field Capacity and Plant Available Water Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Plant available water is what a soil holds between field capacity and the permanent wilting point. Free field capacity and plant available water calculator: e\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-plant-available-water\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fc\",\"l\":\"Field capacity\",\"h\":\"Volumetric water content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pwp\",\"l\":\"Permanent wilting point\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"depth\",\"l\":\"Rooting depth\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"current\",\"l\":\"Current water content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"paw\",\"l\":\"Plant available water\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"paw_mm\",\"l\":\"Available water in the root zone\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"deficit\",\"l\":\"Deficit to refill\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"days\",\"l\":\"Days until wilting at 5 mm/day\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"fc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"32\",\"c\":null}]},\"pwp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"15\",\"c\":null}]},\"depth\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"600\",\"c\":null}]},\"current\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"24\",\"c\":null}]},\"paw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"paw_mm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"deficit\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"days\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Water\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"paw\",\"f\":\"fc-pwp\",\"r\":[\"fc\",\"pwp\"]},{\"l\":\"paw_mm\",\"f\":\"(fc-pwp)/100*depth\",\"r\":[\"fc\",\"pwp\",\"depth\"]},{\"l\":\"deficit\",\"f\":\"(fc-current)/100*depth\",\"r\":[\"fc\",\"current\",\"depth\"]},{\"l\":\"days\",\"f\":\"(current-pwp)/100*depth/5\",\"r\":[\"current\",\"pwp\",\"depth\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(122, 'field-capacity', 'biology', 'Field Capacity Calculator', 'Field Capacity Calculator', 'Field Capacity Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Field capacity is the water a soil retains after excess has drained away — the upper limit of what a root zone can store. Free field capacity calculator: ente', 'Field capacity is the water a soil retains after excess has drained away — the upper limit of what a root zone can store. It returns field capacity (gravimetric), field capacity (volumetric), bulk density and water stored in the root zone, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator derives it from a wet and a dry sample weight, and reports the porosity and bulk density that go with it. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Field Capacity\",\"html\":\"<p>Gravimetric FC = (wet − dry) ÷ dry × 100;  volumetric = (wet − dry) ÷ volume × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/field-capacity.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Field Capacity Calculator — Wet mass, dry mass and the water held between them.\\\" title=\\\"Field Capacity Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Wet mass, dry mass and the water held between them.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>wet</code></td><td>mass at field capacity</td><td>g</td></tr><tr><td><code>dry</code></td><td>oven-dry mass at 105 °C</td><td>g</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume</code></td><td>volume of the undisturbed core</td><td>cm³</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Field Capacity?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: wet soil mass of <strong>620</strong> g, oven-dry soil mass of <strong>500</strong> g, core volume of <strong>300</strong> cm³.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives field capacity (gravimetric) of <strong>24</strong>%, field capacity (volumetric) of <strong>40</strong>%, bulk density of <strong>1.67</strong> g/cm³, water stored in the root zone of <strong>240</strong> mm.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Oven-dry means 105 °C to constant mass; a domestic oven at 100 °C leaves a percent or two of water behind.</p></li><li><p>Volumetric water content is the version irrigation models use, because roots explore volume rather than mass.</p></li><li><p>Bulk density above about 1.6 g/cm³ in a loam signals compaction, which lowers both infiltration and rooting depth.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Drying at too low a temperature and treating the result as oven-dry.</li><li>Using a disturbed sample, which loses the pore structure that holds the water.</li><li>Reporting gravimetric values where a volumetric figure is expected.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/plant-available-water\\\">Plant available water</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/solute-potential\\\">Solute potential</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is field capacity in mm?\",\"a\":\"Volumetric water content × rooting depth in mm — 30% over 600 mm is 180 mm.\"},{\"q\":\"How long does drainage take?\",\"a\":\"Two to three days in most soils after saturation.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Field Capacity actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns field capacity (gravimetric), field capacity (volumetric), bulk density and water stored in the root zone. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"wet\",\"label\":\"Wet soil mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"620\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dry\",\"label\":\"Oven-dry soil mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Core volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"300\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm³\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"fc\",\"label\":\"Field capacity (gravimetric)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"vol\",\"label\":\"Field capacity (volumetric)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"bulk\",\"label\":\"Bulk density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/cm³\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"water\",\"label\":\"Water stored in the root zone\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Soil water\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"fc\",\"f\":\"(wet-dry)/dry*100\"},{\"l\":\"vol\",\"f\":\"(wet-dry)/volume*100\"},{\"l\":\"bulk\",\"f\":\"dry/volume\"},{\"l\":\"water\",\"f\":\"(wet-dry)/volume*100*600/100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/field-capacity\",\"slug\":\"field-capacity\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Field Capacity Calculator\",\"title\":\"Field Capacity Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Field capacity is the water a soil retains after excess has drained away — the upper limit of what a root zone can store. Free field capacity calculator: ente\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-field-capacity\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"wet\",\"l\":\"Wet soil mass\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dry\",\"l\":\"Oven-dry soil mass\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Core volume\",\"suf\":\"cm³\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fc\",\"l\":\"Field capacity (gravimetric)\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vol\",\"l\":\"Field capacity (volumetric)\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bulk\",\"l\":\"Bulk density\",\"suf\":\"g/cm³\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"water\",\"l\":\"Water stored in the root zone\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"wet\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"620\",\"c\":null}]},\"dry\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"500\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"300\",\"c\":null}]},\"fc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"vol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"bulk\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"water\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Soil water\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"fc\",\"f\":\"(wet-dry)/dry*100\",\"r\":[\"wet\",\"dry\"]},{\"l\":\"vol\",\"f\":\"(wet-dry)/volume*100\",\"r\":[\"wet\",\"dry\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"bulk\",\"f\":\"dry/volume\",\"r\":[\"dry\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"water\",\"f\":\"(wet-dry)/volume*100*600/100\",\"r\":[\"wet\",\"dry\",\"volume\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(123, 'seeding-rate', 'biology', 'Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare Calculator', 'Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare Calculator', 'Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare Calculator', 'Seed rate depends on the target plant population, thousand-seed weight and germination: you must sow more seed than the plants you want, because some will not', 'Seed rate depends on the target plant population, thousand-seed weight and germination: you must sow more seed than the plants you want, because some will not establish. It returns seed rate, seeds per square metre, seeds per metre of row and plant spacing in the row, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives the seed weight per hectare and per square metre. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare\",\"html\":\"<p>Seed rate (kg/ha) = target plants × TSW ÷ 10⁶ ÷ germination ÷ establishment</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/seeding-rate.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare Calculator — Target plants, seed weight and germination to a seed rate.\\\" title=\\\"Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Target plants, seed weight and germination to a seed rate.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>target</code></td><td>plants wanted at harvest</td><td>plants/ha</td></tr><tr><td><code>TSW</code></td><td>thousand seed weight</td><td>g</td></tr><tr><td><code>germination</code></td><td>laboratory germination</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>establishment</code></td><td>field survival of germinated seed</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: target plants of <strong>300,000</strong> plants/ha, thousand seed weight of <strong>40</strong> g, germination of <strong>92</strong>%, field establishment of <strong>90</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives seed rate of <strong>14.49</strong> kg/ha, seeds per square metre of <strong>36.23</strong> seeds/m², seeds per metre of row of <strong>9.06</strong> seeds/m, plant spacing in the row of <strong>4.03e-08</strong> cm.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Thousand seed weight is the single most useful seed-lot measurement: it converts a plant target into kilograms without any guessing.</p></li><li><p>Germination and field establishment are separate: a lot can germinate at 95% in the laboratory and still establish at 80% in a rough seedbed.</p></li><li><p>Every doubling of target population roughly halves the plants per metre of row, which is where competition, lodging and disease pressure are decided.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using laboratory germination as if it were field establishment.</li><li>Confusing thousand seed weight with seed weight per plant.</li><li>Calculating for the target population rather than the sown population.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/thousand-seed-weight\\\">Thousand seed weight</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-germination\\\">Percent germination</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/growing-degree-days\\\">Growing degree days</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I convert a seed rate to seeds per square metre?\",\"a\":\"Divide the seed weight per hectare by the thousand seed weight and multiply by 1 000 ÷ 10 000.\"},{\"q\":\"Why sow more than the target?\",\"a\":\"Because a proportion of seeds never germinate or never establish.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns seed rate, seeds per square metre, seeds per metre of row and plant spacing in the row. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Target plants\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"300000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"plants/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tsw\",\"label\":\"Thousand seed weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"40\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"germination\",\"label\":\"Germination\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"92\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"establishment\",\"label\":\"Field establishment\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"90\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Survival of germinated seedlings\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rate\",\"label\":\"Seed rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_m2\",\"label\":\"Seeds per square metre\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds/m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"row\",\"label\":\"Seeds per metre of row\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"At 25 cm row spacing\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds/m\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"spacing\",\"label\":\"Plant spacing in the row\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Seed rate\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"target*tsw/1000/1000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)\"},{\"l\":\"per_m2\",\"f\":\"target/10000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)\"},{\"l\":\"row\",\"f\":\"target/10000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)*0.25\"},{\"l\":\"spacing\",\"f\":\"100/target/10000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)*0.25/0.25\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/seeding-rate\",\"slug\":\"seeding-rate\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare Calculator\",\"title\":\"Seeding Rate and Plants per Hectare Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Seed rate depends on the target plant population, thousand-seed weight and germination: you must sow more seed than the plants you want, because some will not\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-seeding-rate\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Target plants\",\"suf\":\"plants/ha\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tsw\",\"l\":\"Thousand seed weight\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"germination\",\"l\":\"Germination\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"establishment\",\"l\":\"Field establishment\",\"h\":\"Survival of germinated seedlings\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate\",\"l\":\"Seed rate\",\"suf\":\"kg/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_m2\",\"l\":\"Seeds per square metre\",\"suf\":\"seeds/m²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"row\",\"l\":\"Seeds per metre of row\",\"h\":\"At 25 cm row spacing\",\"suf\":\"seeds/m\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"spacing\",\"l\":\"Plant spacing in the row\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"300000\",\"c\":null}]},\"tsw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"40\",\"c\":null}]},\"germination\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"92\",\"c\":null}]},\"establishment\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"90\",\"c\":null}]},\"rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_m2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"row\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"spacing\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Seed rate\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"target*tsw/1000/1000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"tsw\",\"germination\",\"establishment\"]},{\"l\":\"per_m2\",\"f\":\"target/10000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"germination\",\"establishment\"]},{\"l\":\"row\",\"f\":\"target/10000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)*0.25\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"germination\",\"establishment\"]},{\"l\":\"spacing\",\"f\":\"100/target/10000/(germination/100)/(establishment/100)*0.25/0.25\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"germination\",\"establishment\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(124, 'percent-germination', 'biology', 'Percent Germination Calculator', 'Percent Germination Calculator', 'Percent Germination Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Percent germination is germinated seeds divided by seeds sown — the number on a seed packet, and the number that decides how much seed to buy. Free percent ge', 'Percent germination is germinated seeds divided by seeds sown — the number on a seed packet, and the number that decides how much seed to buy. It returns germination, viability (germinated + dormant), seeds needed for 10 000 plants and sowing rate for 1 000 plants/m², and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator adds the viability and vigour figures that explain a low result. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Percent Germination\",\"html\":\"<p>Germination (%) = germinated ÷ sown × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/percent-germination.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Percent Germination Calculator — Germinated, dormant and dead seed in a test lot.\\\" title=\\\"Percent Germination Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Germinated, dormant and dead seed in a test lot.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>germinated</code></td><td>seeds that produced a normal seedling</td><td>seeds</td></tr><tr><td><code>sown</code></td><td>seeds placed on the test</td><td>seeds</td></tr><tr><td><code>germination</code></td><td>the percentage</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Percent Germination?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: seeds germinated of <strong>88</strong> seeds, seeds sown of <strong>100</strong> seeds, seeds rotted or dead of <strong>5</strong> seeds.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives germination of <strong>88</strong>%, viability (germinated + dormant) of <strong>95</strong>%, seeds needed for 10 000 plants of <strong>11,363.64</strong> seeds, sowing rate for 1 000 plants/m² of <strong>1,136.36</strong> seeds/m².</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>above 90%</td><td><strong>Excellent</strong></td><td>sow at the planned rate</td></tr><tr><td>70 – 90%</td><td><strong>Acceptable</strong></td><td>increase the rate by the shortfall</td></tr><tr><td>below 70%</td><td><strong>Poor</strong></td><td>check storage, seed lot age and test conditions</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Standard tests count normal seedlings under specified conditions — 20 °C for most vegetables, alternating temperatures for grasses.</p></li><li><p>Dormant seed is viable but not counted as germinated; a tetrazolium test separates dormancy from death.</p></li><li><p>Vigour (how fast and uniformly seedlings emerge) predicts field establishment better than germination percentage alone.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Counting abnormal seedlings as germinated.</li><li>Testing too few seeds — 100 is the minimum for a useful percentage.</li><li>Testing freshly harvested seed that is still dormant.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/seeding-rate\\\">Seeding rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/thousand-seed-weight\\\">Thousand seed weight</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/growing-degree-days\\\">Growing degree days</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many seeds do I need for a 95% germination rate and 10 000 plants?\",\"a\":\"About 10 526 seeds.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is field emergence lower than the test result?\",\"a\":\"Soil temperature, crusting, pathogens and sowing depth all reduce it.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Percent Germination actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns germination, viability (germinated + dormant), seeds needed for 10 000 plants and sowing rate for 1 000 plants/m². Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"germinated\",\"label\":\"Seeds germinated\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"88\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"sown\",\"label\":\"Seeds sown\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dead\",\"label\":\"Seeds rotted or dead\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional — separates non-viable from dormant\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"germination\",\"label\":\"Germination\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"viability\",\"label\":\"Viability (germinated + dormant)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"seed_needed\",\"label\":\"Seeds needed for 10 000 plants\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"sow_rate\",\"label\":\"Sowing rate for 1 000 plants/m²\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds/m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Germination\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"germination\",\"f\":\"germinated/sown*100\"},{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"(sown-dead)/sown*100\"},{\"l\":\"seed_needed\",\"f\":\"10000/(germinated/sown)\"},{\"l\":\"sow_rate\",\"f\":\"1000/(germinated/sown)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/percent-germination\",\"slug\":\"percent-germination\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Percent Germination Calculator\",\"title\":\"Percent Germination Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Percent germination is germinated seeds divided by seeds sown — the number on a seed packet, and the number that decides how much seed to buy. Free percent ge\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-percent-germination\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"germinated\",\"l\":\"Seeds germinated\",\"suf\":\"seeds\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sown\",\"l\":\"Seeds sown\",\"suf\":\"seeds\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dead\",\"l\":\"Seeds rotted or dead\",\"h\":\"Optional — separates non-viable from dormant\",\"suf\":\"seeds\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"germination\",\"l\":\"Germination\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"viability\",\"l\":\"Viability (germinated + dormant)\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"seed_needed\",\"l\":\"Seeds needed for 10 000 plants\",\"suf\":\"seeds\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sow_rate\",\"l\":\"Sowing rate for 1 000 plants/m²\",\"suf\":\"seeds/m²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"germinated\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"88\",\"c\":null}]},\"sown\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"dead\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5\",\"c\":null}]},\"germination\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"viability\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"seed_needed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"sow_rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Germination\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"germination\",\"f\":\"germinated/sown*100\",\"r\":[\"germinated\",\"sown\"]},{\"l\":\"viability\",\"f\":\"(sown-dead)/sown*100\",\"r\":[\"sown\",\"dead\"]},{\"l\":\"seed_needed\",\"f\":\"10000/(germinated/sown)\",\"r\":[\"germinated\",\"sown\"]},{\"l\":\"sow_rate\",\"f\":\"1000/(germinated/sown)\",\"r\":[\"germinated\",\"sown\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:46', '2026-10-29 14:08:46'),
(125, 'thousand-seed-weight', 'biology', 'Thousand Seed Weight Calculator', 'Thousand Seed Weight Calculator', 'Thousand Seed Weight Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Thousand seed weight — the mass of a thousand seeds — converts plant populations into kilograms of seed and is the fastest way to compare seed lots. Free thou', 'Thousand seed weight — the mass of a thousand seeds — converts plant populations into kilograms of seed and is the fastest way to compare seed lots. It returns thousand seed weight, seeds per kilogram, TSW at 12% moisture and seeds per gram, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator standardises a sample of any size to the standard TSW figure and gives the seed count per kilogram. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Thousand Seed Weight\",\"html\":\"<p>TSW (g) = sample mass ÷ seeds in the sample × 1 000</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/thousand-seed-weight.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Thousand Seed Weight Calculator — A counted sample scaled to a thousand seeds.\\\" title=\\\"Thousand Seed Weight Calculator\\\"><figcaption>A counted sample scaled to a thousand seeds.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sample mass</code></td><td>mass of the counted seeds</td><td>g</td></tr><tr><td><code>sample count</code></td><td>number of seeds weighed</td><td>seeds</td></tr><tr><td><code>TSW</code></td><td>thousand seed weight</td><td>g</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Thousand Seed Weight?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: sample mass of <strong>4.2</strong> g, seeds in the sample of <strong>100</strong> seeds, moisture content of <strong>12</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives thousand seed weight of <strong>42</strong> g, seeds per kilogram of <strong>23,809.52</strong> seeds/kg, tsw at 12% moisture of <strong>42</strong> g, seeds per gram of <strong>23.81</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Count at least 100 seeds and weigh to 0.01 g; the error in TSW falls as the square root of the count, so a 100-seed sample is about ±5%.</p></li><li><p>Moisture matters: the same lot is heavier at 14% than at 10%, which is why standards quote TSW at a stated moisture.</p></li><li><p>TSW is a variety and seed-lot trait — it also predicts seedling vigour, because bigger seeds carry more reserves.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Weighing a scoop instead of counting the seeds in it.</li><li>Comparing TSW between lots at different moisture contents.</li><li>Using field-run seed (with chaff) in a weight-based rate calculation.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/seeding-rate\\\">Seeding rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-germination\\\">Percent germination</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/days-to-maturity\\\">Days to maturity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a typical thousand seed weight?\",\"a\":\"40 g for wheat, 300 g for maize, 150 g for soybean, 4 g for oilseed rape.\"},{\"q\":\"How does TSW affect seeding rate?\",\"a\":\"Directly and proportionally: doubling TSW doubles the kilograms needed for the same plant population.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Thousand Seed Weight actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns thousand seed weight, seeds per kilogram, TSW at 12% moisture and seeds per gram. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sample_mass\",\"label\":\"Sample mass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"sample_count\",\"label\":\"Seeds in the sample\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"moisture\",\"label\":\"Moisture content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional — standard is 12–14%\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tsw\",\"label\":\"Thousand seed weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"seeds_kg\",\"label\":\"Seeds per kilogram\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"seeds/kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tsw_dry\",\"label\":\"TSW at 12% moisture\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"seed_per_g\",\"label\":\"Seeds per gram\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Seed weight\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"tsw\",\"f\":\"sample_mass/sample_count*1000\"},{\"l\":\"seeds_kg\",\"f\":\"1000/(sample_mass/sample_count*1000)*1000\"},{\"l\":\"tsw_dry\",\"f\":\"sample_mass/sample_count*1000*(88/(100-moisture))\"},{\"l\":\"seed_per_g\",\"f\":\"sample_count/sample_mass\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/thousand-seed-weight\",\"slug\":\"thousand-seed-weight\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Thousand Seed Weight Calculator\",\"title\":\"Thousand Seed Weight Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Thousand seed weight — the mass of a thousand seeds — converts plant populations into kilograms of seed and is the fastest way to compare seed lots. Free thou\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-thousand-seed-weight\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sample_mass\",\"l\":\"Sample mass\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sample_count\",\"l\":\"Seeds in the sample\",\"suf\":\"seeds\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"moisture\",\"l\":\"Moisture content\",\"h\":\"Optional — standard is 12–14%\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tsw\",\"l\":\"Thousand seed weight\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"seeds_kg\",\"l\":\"Seeds per kilogram\",\"suf\":\"seeds/kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tsw_dry\",\"l\":\"TSW at 12% moisture\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"seed_per_g\",\"l\":\"Seeds per gram\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sample_mass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"sample_count\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"moisture\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"tsw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"seeds_kg\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tsw_dry\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"seed_per_g\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Seed weight\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"tsw\",\"f\":\"sample_mass/sample_count*1000\",\"r\":[\"sample_mass\",\"sample_count\"]},{\"l\":\"seeds_kg\",\"f\":\"1000/(sample_mass/sample_count*1000)*1000\",\"r\":[\"sample_mass\",\"sample_count\"]},{\"l\":\"tsw_dry\",\"f\":\"sample_mass/sample_count*1000*(88/(100-moisture))\",\"r\":[\"sample_mass\",\"sample_count\",\"moisture\"]},{\"l\":\"seed_per_g\",\"f\":\"sample_count/sample_mass\",\"r\":[\"sample_count\",\"sample_mass\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(126, 'days-to-maturity', 'biology', 'Days to Maturity Calculator', 'Days to Maturity Calculator', 'Days to Maturity Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Days to maturity tells you whether a crop will finish before the season does. Free days to maturity calculator: enter your values and get harvest day of year,', 'Days to maturity tells you whether a crop will finish before the season does. It returns harvest day of year, harvest month, days implied by the GDD target and days remaining after harvest (season 220 days), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a variety\'s maturity rating into a calendar date, adjusting for sowing date and the heat the season is actually delivering. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Days to Maturity\",\"html\":\"<p>Harvest day = sowing day + days to maturity</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/days-to-maturity.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Days to Maturity Calculator — Sowing date plus maturity rating gives the harvest date.\\\" title=\\\"Days to Maturity Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Sowing date plus maturity rating gives the harvest date.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sowing day</code></td><td>day of year the crop was sown</td><td>day</td></tr><tr><td><code>DTM</code></td><td>days to maturity for the variety</td><td>days</td></tr><tr><td><code>GDD</code></td><td>heat required, an alternative to DTM</td><td>°C·day</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Days to Maturity?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: sowing day of year of <strong>120</strong> day, days to maturity of <strong>95</strong> days, gdd to maturity of <strong>1,400</strong> GDD, average gdd per day of <strong>13</strong> GDD/day.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives harvest day of year of <strong>215</strong> day, harvest month of <strong>8</strong>, days implied by the gdd target of <strong>107.69</strong> days, days remaining after harvest (season 220 days) of <strong>5</strong> days.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Catalogue days-to-maturity ratings assume a reference temperature; a cool season stretches them and a hot one shortens them, which is why GDD is the better input.</p></li><li><p>Divide the GDD target by the average daily accumulation to get days — a crop needing 1 400 GDD at 13 GDD/day takes about 108 days.</p></li><li><p>Late sowing in a shortening season often needs a shorter-maturity variety rather than more inputs.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Counting from emergence in one place and from sowing in another.</li><li>Using last year\'s GDD accumulation for this year\'s forecast.</li><li>Assuming a fixed harvest date ignores the difference between physiological and harvest maturity.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/growing-degree-days\\\">Growing degree days</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/seeding-rate\\\">Seeding rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What does days to maturity mean?\",\"a\":\"The number of days from sowing (or transplanting) to a marketable or physiologically mature crop.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my crop mature later than the packet says?\",\"a\":\"Cooler than expected weather; the rating assumes a reference temperature.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Days to Maturity actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns harvest day of year, harvest month, days implied by the GDD target and days remaining after harvest (season 220 days). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sow_day\",\"label\":\"Sowing day of year\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"120\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"1 January = 1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dtm\",\"label\":\"Days to maturity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"95\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gdd_target\",\"label\":\"GDD to maturity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1400\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Optional — use instead of days\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"GDD\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gdd_per_day\",\"label\":\"Average GDD per day\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"13\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"GDD/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"harvest_day\",\"label\":\"Harvest day of year\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"harvest_month\",\"label\":\"Harvest month\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gdd_days\",\"label\":\"Days implied by the GDD target\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"slack\",\"label\":\"Days remaining after harvest (season 220 days)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Maturity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"harvest_day\",\"f\":\"sow_day+dtm\"},{\"l\":\"harvest_month\",\"f\":\"min(12,1+round((sow_day+dtm-15)/30))\"},{\"l\":\"gdd_days\",\"f\":\"gdd_target/gdd_per_day\"},{\"l\":\"slack\",\"f\":\"220-(sow_day+dtm)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/days-to-maturity\",\"slug\":\"days-to-maturity\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Days to Maturity Calculator\",\"title\":\"Days to Maturity Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Days to maturity tells you whether a crop will finish before the season does. Free days to maturity calculator: enter your values and get harvest day of year,\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-days-to-maturity\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sow_day\",\"l\":\"Sowing day of year\",\"h\":\"1 January = 1\",\"suf\":\"day\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dtm\",\"l\":\"Days to maturity\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gdd_target\",\"l\":\"GDD to maturity\",\"h\":\"Optional — use instead of days\",\"suf\":\"GDD\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gdd_per_day\",\"l\":\"Average GDD per day\",\"suf\":\"GDD/day\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"harvest_day\",\"l\":\"Harvest day of year\",\"suf\":\"day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"harvest_month\",\"l\":\"Harvest month\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gdd_days\",\"l\":\"Days implied by the GDD target\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"slack\",\"l\":\"Days remaining after harvest (season 220 days)\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sow_day\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"120\",\"c\":null}]},\"dtm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"95\",\"c\":null}]},\"gdd_target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1400\",\"c\":null}]},\"gdd_per_day\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"13\",\"c\":null}]},\"harvest_day\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"harvest_month\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gdd_days\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"slack\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Maturity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"harvest_day\",\"f\":\"sow_day+dtm\",\"r\":[\"sow_day\",\"dtm\"]},{\"l\":\"harvest_month\",\"f\":\"min(12,1+round((sow_day+dtm-15)/30))\",\"r\":[\"sow_day\",\"dtm\"]},{\"l\":\"gdd_days\",\"f\":\"gdd_target/gdd_per_day\",\"r\":[\"gdd_target\",\"gdd_per_day\"]},{\"l\":\"slack\",\"f\":\"220-(sow_day+dtm)\",\"r\":[\"sow_day\",\"dtm\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(127, 'stomatal-density', 'biology', 'Stomatal Density Calculator', 'Stomatal Density Calculator', 'Stomatal Density Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Stomatal density is the number of stomata per square millimetre of leaf surface — the structural limit on how fast a leaf can take up carbon dioxide. Free sto', 'Stomatal density is the number of stomata per square millimetre of leaf surface — the structural limit on how fast a leaf can take up carbon dioxide. It returns area sampled, stomatal density, stomatal density and pore index, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a count in a microscope field into density per mm² and per cm². The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Stomatal Density\",\"html\":\"<p>Density = stomata ÷ (fields × π × (diameter/2)²)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/stomatal-density.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Stomatal Density Calculator — A counted field, scaled to a square millimetre.\\\" title=\\\"Stomatal Density Calculator\\\"><figcaption>A counted field, scaled to a square millimetre.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>stomata</code></td><td>stomata counted across all fields</td><td>stomata</td></tr><tr><td><code>fields</code></td><td>number of microscope fields</td><td>fields</td></tr><tr><td><code>diameter</code></td><td>field-of-view diameter at that magnification</td><td>mm</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Stomatal Density?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: stomata counted of <strong>46</strong> stomata, fields counted of <strong>5</strong>, field of view diameter of <strong>2.2</strong> mm, stomatal pore index (optional) of <strong>0.0021</strong> mm².</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives area sampled of <strong>19.01</strong> mm², stomatal density of <strong>2.42</strong> per mm², stomatal density of <strong>242.02</strong> per cm², pore index of <strong>0.0051</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 100 per mm²</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>thick-leaved, shade or water-limited species</td></tr><tr><td>100 – 300</td><td><strong>Typical</strong></td><td>most mesophytic crop and wild species</td></tr><tr><td>above 400</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>sun-adapted grasses and fast-growing herbs</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Count on the abaxial (lower) surface of a dicot leaf and either surface of a grass; the two surfaces differ by a factor of two or more.</p></li><li><p>Stomatal density falls with rising atmospheric CO₂ and rises with growth temperature and light — which is why fossil leaves are used as palaeoclimate proxies.</p></li><li><p>Density is not conductance: guard-cell aperture and behaviour matter as much as count.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Forgetting to convert the field diameter from micrometres.</li><li>Counting only the top surface of a leaf whose stomata are mostly underneath.</li><li>Using a nail-varnish peel without noting which surface it came from.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/transpiration-rate\\\">Transpiration rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-stress-index\\\">Crop water stress index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/photosynthesis-rate\\\">Photosynthesis rate</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I calculate stomatal density?\",\"a\":\"Divide the number of stomata by the area of the field(s) they were counted in.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the stomatal ratio?\",\"a\":\"Density on the upper surface divided by density on the lower, about 0 for most trees and about 1 for grasses.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Stomatal Density actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns area sampled, stomatal density, stomatal density and pore index. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"stomata\",\"label\":\"Stomata counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"46\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"stomata\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"fields\",\"label\":\"Fields counted\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"diameter\",\"label\":\"Field of view diameter\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From the microscope magnification\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"spi\",\"label\":\"Stomatal pore index (optional)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.0021\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Mean pore area × density\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"density\",\"label\":\"Stomatal density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per mm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_cm2\",\"label\":\"Stomatal density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per cm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Area sampled\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"index\",\"label\":\"Pore index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Density\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2)\"},{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"stomata/(fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2))\"},{\"l\":\"per_cm2\",\"f\":\"stomata/(fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2))*100\"},{\"l\":\"index\",\"f\":\"stomata/(fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2))*spi\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/stomatal-density\",\"slug\":\"stomatal-density\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Stomatal Density Calculator\",\"title\":\"Stomatal Density Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Stomatal density is the number of stomata per square millimetre of leaf surface — the structural limit on how fast a leaf can take up carbon dioxide. Free sto\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-stomatal-density\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stomata\",\"l\":\"Stomata counted\",\"suf\":\"stomata\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fields\",\"l\":\"Fields counted\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"diameter\",\"l\":\"Field of view diameter\",\"h\":\"From the microscope magnification\",\"suf\":\"mm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"spi\",\"l\":\"Stomatal pore index (optional)\",\"h\":\"Mean pore area × density\",\"suf\":\"mm²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"density\",\"l\":\"Stomatal density\",\"suf\":\"per mm²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_cm2\",\"l\":\"Stomatal density\",\"suf\":\"per cm²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Area sampled\",\"suf\":\"mm²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"index\",\"l\":\"Pore index\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"stomata\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"46\",\"c\":null}]},\"fields\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5\",\"c\":null}]},\"diameter\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"spi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.0021\",\"c\":null}]},\"density\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_cm2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"index\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Density\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2)\",\"r\":[\"fields\",\"diameter\"]},{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"stomata/(fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2))\",\"r\":[\"stomata\",\"fields\",\"diameter\"]},{\"l\":\"per_cm2\",\"f\":\"stomata/(fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2))*100\",\"r\":[\"stomata\",\"fields\",\"diameter\"]},{\"l\":\"index\",\"f\":\"stomata/(fields*3.14159265358979*pow(diameter/2,2))*spi\",\"r\":[\"stomata\",\"fields\",\"diameter\",\"spi\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'microscope', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(128, 'vine-density', 'biology', 'Vine Density Calculator', 'Vine Density Calculator', 'Vine Density Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Vine density — vines per hectare — is set by row spacing and vine spacing, and it drives competition, fruit exposure and yield per vine. Free vine density cal', 'Vine density — vines per hectare — is set by row spacing and vine spacing, and it drives competition, fruit exposure and yield per vine. It returns vines per hectare, vines per 100 m of row, yield per vine and grams per bunch, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives density, vines per row, and the yield per vine that a target tonnes-per-hectare implies. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Vine Density\",\"html\":\"<p>Vines/ha = 10 000 ÷ (row spacing × vine spacing);  kg per vine = yield ÷ density</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/vine-density.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Vine Density Calculator — Row and vine spacing in a vineyard block.\\\" title=\\\"Vine Density Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Row and vine spacing in a vineyard block.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>row spacing</code></td><td>distance between rows</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td><code>vine spacing</code></td><td>distance between vines in the row</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td><code>density</code></td><td>vines per hectare</td><td>vines/ha</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Vine Density?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: row spacing of <strong>2.5</strong> m, vine spacing of <strong>1.2</strong> m, target yield of <strong>8</strong> t/ha, bunches per vine of <strong>24</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives vines per hectare of <strong>3,333.33</strong> vines/ha, vines per 100 m of row of <strong>83.33</strong> vines, yield per vine of <strong>2.4</strong> kg, grams per bunch of <strong>100</strong> g.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 3 000</td><td><strong>Low density</strong></td><td>bush vines, old-vine plots, dry-grown</td></tr><tr><td>3 000 – 6 000</td><td><strong>Typical</strong></td><td>most modern trellised vineyards</td></tr><tr><td>above 7 000</td><td><strong>High density</strong></td><td>cool-climate quality plantings</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Doubling density roughly halves the yield per vine for the same tonnes per hectare, which changes fruit composition as much as it changes tonnage.</p></li><li><p>Row spacing also has to fit the tractor: 2.0–2.5 m is common because machinery sets a floor.</p></li><li><p>Bunch weight is the least stable variable in the chain — it moves by 30% between seasons.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using feet for spacing in a metre-based formula.</li><li>Setting density from yield targets alone, ignoring vine vigour and water supply.</li><li>Confusing tonnes per hectare with tonnes per acre (1 t/ac ≈ 2.47 t/ha).</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/plant-population\\\">Plant population</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/growing-degree-days\\\">Growing degree days</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/huglin-index\\\">Huglin index</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many vines per hectare at 2.5 × 1.2 m?\",\"a\":\"About 3 333 vines.\"},{\"q\":\"Does higher density give better wine?\",\"a\":\"It can improve competition and fruit exposure, but only where water and nutrition allow it.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Vine Density actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns vines per hectare, vines per 100 m of row, yield per vine and grams per bunch. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"row_spacing\",\"label\":\"Row spacing\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"vine_spacing\",\"label\":\"Vine spacing\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"yield_tha\",\"label\":\"Target yield\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"t/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"bunches\",\"label\":\"Bunches per vine\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"24\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"density\",\"label\":\"Vines per hectare\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"vines/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_row\",\"label\":\"Vines per 100 m of row\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"vines\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"kg_vine\",\"label\":\"Yield per vine\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"g_bunch\",\"label\":\"Grams per bunch\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Vines\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"10000/(row_spacing*vine_spacing)\"},{\"l\":\"per_row\",\"f\":\"100/vine_spacing\"},{\"l\":\"kg_vine\",\"f\":\"yield_tha*1000/(10000/(row_spacing*vine_spacing))\"},{\"l\":\"g_bunch\",\"f\":\"yield_tha*1000/(10000/(row_spacing*vine_spacing))/bunches*1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/vine-density\",\"slug\":\"vine-density\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Vine Density Calculator\",\"title\":\"Vine Density Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Vine density — vines per hectare — is set by row spacing and vine spacing, and it drives competition, fruit exposure and yield per vine. Free vine density cal\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-vine-density\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"row_spacing\",\"l\":\"Row spacing\",\"suf\":\"m\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vine_spacing\",\"l\":\"Vine spacing\",\"suf\":\"m\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"yield_tha\",\"l\":\"Target yield\",\"suf\":\"t/ha\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bunches\",\"l\":\"Bunches per vine\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"density\",\"l\":\"Vines per hectare\",\"suf\":\"vines/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_row\",\"l\":\"Vines per 100 m of row\",\"suf\":\"vines\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"kg_vine\",\"l\":\"Yield per vine\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"g_bunch\",\"l\":\"Grams per bunch\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"row_spacing\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"vine_spacing\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"yield_tha\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8\",\"c\":null}]},\"bunches\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"24\",\"c\":null}]},\"density\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_row\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"kg_vine\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"g_bunch\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Vines\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"density\",\"f\":\"10000/(row_spacing*vine_spacing)\",\"r\":[\"row_spacing\",\"vine_spacing\"]},{\"l\":\"per_row\",\"f\":\"100/vine_spacing\",\"r\":[\"vine_spacing\"]},{\"l\":\"kg_vine\",\"f\":\"yield_tha*1000/(10000/(row_spacing*vine_spacing))\",\"r\":[\"yield_tha\",\"row_spacing\",\"vine_spacing\"]},{\"l\":\"g_bunch\",\"f\":\"yield_tha*1000/(10000/(row_spacing*vine_spacing))/bunches*1000\",\"r\":[\"yield_tha\",\"row_spacing\",\"vine_spacing\",\"bunches\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(129, 'huglin-index', 'biology', 'Huglin Index Calculator', 'Huglin Index Calculator', 'Huglin Index Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The Huglin index measures the heat available for grape ripening, weighting daytime temperatures more heavily than night ones. Free huglin index calculator: en', 'The Huglin index measures the heat available for grape ripening, weighting daytime temperatures more heavily than night ones. It returns contribution per day, huglin index and climate class (1 very cool … 6 too hot), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is the standard climate index for matching a grape variety to a site, and it decides which varieties can ripen there. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Huglin Index\",\"html\":\"<p>HI = K × [(T_max − 10) + (T_mean − 10)] ÷ 2, summed over the growing period</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/huglin-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Huglin Index Calculator — The heat accumulated across the growing period.\\\" title=\\\"Huglin Index Calculator\\\"><figcaption>The heat accumulated across the growing period.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T_max</code></td><td>mean daily maximum</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>T_mean</code></td><td>mean daily temperature</td><td>°C</td></tr><tr><td><code>K</code></td><td>daylength coefficient</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>HI</code></td><td>Huglin heliothermal index</td><td>°C·day</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Huglin Index?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: mean daily maximum of <strong>26</strong>°C, mean daily temperature of <strong>18</strong>°C, base temperature of <strong>10</strong>°C, days in the growing period of <strong>180</strong> days, daylength coefficient (k) of <strong>1.02</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives contribution per day of <strong>12.24</strong> °C·day, huglin index of <strong>2,203.2</strong>, climate class (1 very cool … 6 too hot) of <strong>4</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 1 500 (class 1)</td><td><strong>Very cool</strong></td><td>early varieties only; sparkling base wines</td></tr><tr><td>1 500 – 1 800 (class 2)</td><td><strong>Cool</strong></td><td>Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling</td></tr><tr><td>1 800 – 2 100 (class 3)</td><td><strong>Temperate</strong></td><td>Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Sauvignon Blanc</td></tr><tr><td>2 100 – 2 400 (class 4)</td><td><strong>Warm</strong></td><td>Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Grenache</td></tr><tr><td>2 400 – 3 000 (class 5)</td><td><strong>Very warm</strong></td><td>late-ripening Mediterranean varieties</td></tr><tr><td>above 3 000 (class 6)</td><td><strong>Too hot</strong></td><td>heat stress and irrigation become the limit</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The formula weights the maximum temperature fully, which is what distinguishes it from growing degree days.</p></li><li><p>The daylength coefficient K is 1.02 below 40° latitude and 1.00 above it, correcting for longer summer days in the south.</p></li><li><p>Huglin is usually calculated over the frost-free period from April to September in the northern hemisphere, October to March in the south.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Summing over a full year rather than the growing period.</li><li>Forgetting the K coefficient and comparing sites at different latitudes.</li><li>Using the Huglin index to compare varieties without checking their own heat requirements.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/growing-degree-days\\\">Growing degree days</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/vine-density\\\">Vine density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What Huglin index suits Cabernet Sauvignon?\",\"a\":\"About 2 100–2 400, which is why it struggles to ripen in cool maritime climates.\"},{\"q\":\"Is Huglin better than growing degree days?\",\"a\":\"For viticulture it usually is, because the extra weight on daytime heat matches ripening better than a plain mean.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Huglin Index actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns contribution per day, huglin index and climate class (1 very cool … 6 too hot). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"tmax\",\"label\":\"Mean daily maximum\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"26\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"April–September in the north\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tmean\",\"label\":\"Mean daily temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"18\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"base\",\"label\":\"Base temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"days\",\"label\":\"Days in the growing period\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"180\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"coef\",\"label\":\"Daylength coefficient (K)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.02\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"1.02 below 40° latitude, 1.00 above\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"huglin\",\"label\":\"Huglin index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"class_no\",\"label\":\"Climate class (1 very cool … 6 too hot)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_day\",\"label\":\"Contribution per day\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C·day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Huglin\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"per_day\",\"f\":\"coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)\"},{\"l\":\"huglin\",\"f\":\"coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days\"},{\"l\":\"class_no\",\"f\":\"if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=1500,1,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=1800,2,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=2100,3,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=2400,4,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=3000,5,6)))))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/huglin-index\",\"slug\":\"huglin-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Huglin Index Calculator\",\"title\":\"Huglin Index Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The Huglin index measures the heat available for grape ripening, weighting daytime temperatures more heavily than night ones. Free huglin index calculator: en\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-huglin-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tmax\",\"l\":\"Mean daily maximum\",\"h\":\"April–September in the north\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tmean\",\"l\":\"Mean daily temperature\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"base\",\"l\":\"Base temperature\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"days\",\"l\":\"Days in the growing period\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"coef\",\"l\":\"Daylength coefficient (K)\",\"h\":\"1.02 below 40° latitude, 1.00 above\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"huglin\",\"l\":\"Huglin index\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"class_no\",\"l\":\"Climate class (1 very cool … 6 too hot)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_day\",\"l\":\"Contribution per day\",\"suf\":\"°C·day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"tmax\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"26\",\"c\":null}]},\"tmean\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"18\",\"c\":null}]},\"base\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"days\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"180\",\"c\":null}]},\"coef\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.02\",\"c\":null}]},\"huglin\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"class_no\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_day\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Huglin\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"per_day\",\"f\":\"coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)\",\"r\":[\"coef\",\"tmax\",\"base\",\"tmean\"]},{\"l\":\"huglin\",\"f\":\"coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days\",\"r\":[\"coef\",\"tmax\",\"base\",\"tmean\",\"days\"]},{\"l\":\"class_no\",\"f\":\"if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=1500,1,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=1800,2,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=2100,3,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=2400,4,if_else(coef*((max(0,tmax-base)+max(0,tmean-base))/2)*days<=3000,5,6)))))\",\"r\":[\"coef\",\"tmax\",\"base\",\"tmean\",\"days\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(130, 'ndvi-calculator', 'biology', 'NDVI Calculator (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index)', 'NDVI Calculator (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index)', 'NDVI (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index) Calculator', 'NDVI compares near-infrared with red reflectance: healthy vegetation reflects strongly in the near-infrared and absorbs red. Free ndvi (normalised difference', 'NDVI compares near-infrared with red reflectance: healthy vegetation reflects strongly in the near-infrared and absorbs red. It returns NDVI, EVI approximation, estimated fractional cover and surface class (0 water … 6 very dense), and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThe result runs from −1 to +1 and is the most widely used satellite index of vegetation vigour. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the NDVI (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index)\",\"html\":\"<p>NDVI = (NIR − Red) ÷ (NIR + Red)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/ndvi-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: NDVI Calculator (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index) — Where the index falls between soil and dense canopy.\\\" title=\\\"NDVI Calculator (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index)\\\"><figcaption>Where the index falls between soil and dense canopy.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NIR</code></td><td>near-infrared reflectance</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>Red</code></td><td>red reflectance</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>NDVI</code></td><td>normalised difference vegetation index</td><td>−1 to 1</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the NDVI (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: near-infrared reflectance of <strong>0.42</strong>, red reflectance of <strong>0.08</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives ndvi of <strong>0.68</strong>, evi approximation of <strong>0.4474</strong>, estimated fractional cover of <strong>79.34</strong>%, surface class (0 water … 6 very dense) of <strong>5</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>−1 to 0 (class 0)</td><td><strong>Water, snow, cloud</strong></td><td>no vegetation signal</td></tr><tr><td>0 – 0.1 (class 1)</td><td><strong>Barren</strong></td><td>rock, sand, built-up</td></tr><tr><td>0.1 – 0.2 (class 2)</td><td><strong>Sparse</strong></td><td>bare soil with scattered plants</td></tr><tr><td>0.2 – 0.4 (class 3)</td><td><strong>Shrub and early crop</strong></td><td>dry grassland</td></tr><tr><td>0.4 – 0.6 (class 4)</td><td><strong>Moderate canopy</strong></td><td>growing crop</td></tr><tr><td>0.6 – 0.8 (class 5)</td><td><strong>Dense</strong></td><td>closed canopy at peak season</td></tr><tr><td>0.8 – 1 (class 6)</td><td><strong>Very dense</strong></td><td>rainforest or irrigated crop at full cover</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>NDVI saturates in dense canopies: above a leaf area index of about 3 it stops rising, which is why EVI was developed.</p></li><li><p>Atmospheric haze and thin cloud lower NDVI without changing the ground; compositing several dates reduces that.</p></li><li><p>Values are only comparable when computed from the same sensor and the same processing baseline — an NDVI from Landsat and one from Sentinel-2 need harmonising.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Mixing reflectance scaled 0–255 with reflectance scaled 0–1.</li><li>Comparing a growing-season maximum with an off-season image.</li><li>Reading NDVI as biomass in a saturated canopy.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/net-primary-productivity\\\">Net primary productivity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-stress-index\\\">Crop water stress index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/photosynthesis-rate\\\">Photosynthesis rate</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good NDVI?\",\"a\":\"0.6–0.8 for healthy dense crops; below 0.3 suggests bare soil or severe stress.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is NDVI negative over water?\",\"a\":\"Water absorbs near-infrared much more strongly than red, so NIR − Red goes negative.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the NDVI (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns NDVI, EVI approximation, estimated fractional cover and surface class (0 water … 6 very dense). Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"nir\",\"label\":\"Near-infrared reflectance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.42\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0 to 1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"red\",\"label\":\"Red reflectance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.08\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0 to 1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ndvi\",\"label\":\"NDVI\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"evi\",\"label\":\"EVI approximation\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cover\",\"label\":\"Estimated fractional cover\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"class_no\",\"label\":\"Surface class (0 water … 6 very dense)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Vegetation\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"ndvi\",\"f\":\"(nir-red)/(nir+red)\"},{\"l\":\"evi\",\"f\":\"2.5*(nir-red)/(nir+6*red+1)\"},{\"l\":\"cover\",\"f\":\"pow(max(0,(nir-red)/(nir+red)),0.6)*100\"},{\"l\":\"class_no\",\"f\":\"if_else((nir+red)==0,-1,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0,0,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.1,1,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.2,2,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.4,3,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.6,4,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.8,5,6)))))))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/ndvi-calculator\",\"slug\":\"ndvi-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"NDVI Calculator (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index)\",\"title\":\"NDVI (Normalised Difference Vegetation Index) Calculator\",\"desc\":\"NDVI compares near-infrared with red reflectance: healthy vegetation reflects strongly in the near-infrared and absorbs red. Free ndvi (normalised difference\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-ndvi-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"nir\",\"l\":\"Near-infrared reflectance\",\"h\":\"0 to 1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"red\",\"l\":\"Red reflectance\",\"h\":\"0 to 1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ndvi\",\"l\":\"NDVI\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"evi\",\"l\":\"EVI approximation\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cover\",\"l\":\"Estimated fractional cover\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"class_no\",\"l\":\"Surface class (0 water … 6 very dense)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"nir\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.42\",\"c\":null}]},\"red\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.08\",\"c\":null}]},\"ndvi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"evi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cover\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"class_no\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Vegetation\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"ndvi\",\"f\":\"(nir-red)/(nir+red)\",\"r\":[\"nir\",\"red\"]},{\"l\":\"evi\",\"f\":\"2.5*(nir-red)/(nir+6*red+1)\",\"r\":[\"nir\",\"red\"]},{\"l\":\"cover\",\"f\":\"pow(max(0,(nir-red)/(nir+red)),0.6)*100\",\"r\":[\"nir\",\"red\"]},{\"l\":\"class_no\",\"f\":\"if_else((nir+red)==0,-1,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0,0,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.1,1,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.2,2,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.4,3,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.6,4,if_else((nir-red)/(nir+red)<0.8,5,6)))))))\",\"r\":[\"nir\",\"red\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'satellite', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(131, 'lime-requirement', 'biology', 'Lime Requirement Calculator', 'Lime Requirement Calculator', 'Lime Requirement Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Lime raises soil pH, and the amount needed depends on how far the pH must rise and how heavy the soil is. Free lime requirement calculator: enter your values', 'Lime raises soil pH, and the amount needed depends on how far the pH must rise and how heavy the soil is. It returns lime required, as pure CaCO₃ equivalent, application splits recommended and pH increase per tonne, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator estimates the rate from a target pH and a buffer index, and warns when the change is large enough to split over seasons. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Lime Requirement\",\"html\":\"<p>Lime (t/ha) ≈ (target pH − current pH) × buffer factor × texture factor</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/lime-requirement.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Lime Requirement Calculator — Current pH, target pH and the lime between them.\\\" title=\\\"Lime Requirement Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Current pH, target pH and the lime between them.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ΔpH</code></td><td>change in pH required</td><td>pH units</td></tr><tr><td><code>buffer</code></td><td>buffer index from a soil test</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>texture</code></td><td>texture factor, 1.0 for sand down to 0.18 for clay</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Lime Requirement?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: current ph of <strong>5.4</strong>, target ph of <strong>6.5</strong>, soil texture set to <strong>Loam</strong>, buffer index of <strong>6.4</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives lime required of <strong>2.82</strong> t/ha, as pure caco₃ equivalent of <strong>2.53</strong> t/ha, application splits recommended of <strong>2</strong>, ph increase per tonne of <strong>0.3906</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Clay soils need more lime per unit of pH because they have far more exchange sites to neutralise.</p></li><li><p>Lime moves slowly — about 2.5 cm a year in the profile — so a large requirement is split over two or three seasons rather than applied at once.</p></li><li><p>Use a buffer index test where available; the texture factor alone is a rough estimate.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Applying the full rate when the requirement exceeds 4–5 t/ha.</li><li>Lime and ammonium fertilizer applied in the same operation, which loses nitrogen as ammonia.</li><li>Chasing a high pH where the crop prefers acid soil — blueberries at pH 7 will be chlorotic.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/npk-fertilizer-blend\\\">NPK fertilizer blend</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/field-capacity\\\">Field capacity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How much does pH rise per tonne of lime?\",\"a\":\"About 0.1–0.3 pH units on a loam; much more on sand, much less on clay.\"},{\"q\":\"Does lime replace magnesium?\",\"a\":\"Dolomitic lime does; calcitic lime does not, so check the soil test for magnesium.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Lime Requirement actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns lime required, as pure CaCO₃ equivalent, application splits recommended and pH increase per tonne. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"ph_now\",\"label\":\"Current pH\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ph_target\",\"label\":\"Target pH\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"texture\",\"label\":\"Soil texture\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"loam\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"sand\",\"Sand\",\"1\"],[\"sandy-loam\",\"Sandy loam\",\"0.6\"],[\"loam\",\"Loam\",\"0.4\"],[\"clay-loam\",\"Clay loam\",\"0.25\"],[\"clay\",\"Clay\",\"0.18\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"buffer\",\"label\":\"Buffer index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"From a lime requirement test; lower means more lime\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rate\",\"label\":\"Lime required\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"t/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pure_caco3\",\"label\":\"As pure CaCO₃ equivalent\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"t/ha\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"splits\",\"label\":\"Application splits recommended\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ph_gain\",\"label\":\"pH increase per tonne\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Lime\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"(ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture\"},{\"l\":\"pure_caco3\",\"f\":\"(ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture*0.9\"},{\"l\":\"splits\",\"f\":\"max(1,ceil((ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture/2))\"},{\"l\":\"ph_gain\",\"f\":\"(ph_target-ph_now)/((ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/lime-requirement\",\"slug\":\"lime-requirement\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Lime Requirement Calculator\",\"title\":\"Lime Requirement Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Lime raises soil pH, and the amount needed depends on how far the pH must rise and how heavy the soil is. Free lime requirement calculator: enter your values\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-lime-requirement\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ph_now\",\"l\":\"Current pH\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ph_target\",\"l\":\"Target pH\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"texture\",\"l\":\"Soil texture\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-texture\",\"opts\":[[\"sand\",\"Sand\",\"1\"],[\"sandy-loam\",\"Sandy loam\",\"0.6\"],[\"loam\",\"Loam\",\"0.4\"],[\"clay-loam\",\"Clay loam\",\"0.25\"],[\"clay\",\"Clay\",\"0.18\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"buffer\",\"l\":\"Buffer index\",\"h\":\"From a lime requirement test; lower means more lime\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate\",\"l\":\"Lime required\",\"suf\":\"t/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pure_caco3\",\"l\":\"As pure CaCO₃ equivalent\",\"suf\":\"t/ha\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"splits\",\"l\":\"Application splits recommended\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ph_gain\",\"l\":\"pH increase per tonne\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"ph_now\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"ph_target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"texture\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"loam\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-texture\"},\"buffer\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pure_caco3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"splits\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ph_gain\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-texture\":{\"name\":\"Soil texture\",\"uiType\":\"SELECT\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"sand\",\"label\":\"Sand\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"sandy-loam\",\"label\":\"Sandy loam\",\"value\":\"0.6\"},{\"uid\":\"loam\",\"label\":\"Loam\",\"value\":\"0.4\"},{\"uid\":\"clay-loam\",\"label\":\"Clay loam\",\"value\":\"0.25\"},{\"uid\":\"clay\",\"label\":\"Clay\",\"value\":\"0.18\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Lime\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"(ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture\",\"r\":[\"ph_target\",\"ph_now\",\"buffer\",\"texture\"]},{\"l\":\"pure_caco3\",\"f\":\"(ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture*0.9\",\"r\":[\"ph_target\",\"ph_now\",\"buffer\",\"texture\"]},{\"l\":\"splits\",\"f\":\"max(1,ceil((ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture/2))\",\"r\":[\"ph_target\",\"ph_now\",\"buffer\",\"texture\"]},{\"l\":\"ph_gain\",\"f\":\"(ph_target-ph_now)/((ph_target-ph_now)*buffer*texture)\",\"r\":[\"ph_target\",\"ph_now\",\"buffer\",\"texture\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'sprout', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(132, 'photosynthesis-rate', 'biology', 'Photosynthesis Rate Calculator', 'Photosynthesis Rate Calculator', 'Photosynthesis Rate Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Photosynthesis rate is the carbon fixed per unit leaf area per unit time. Free photosynthesis rate calculator: enter your values and get carbon fixed, rate, d', 'Photosynthesis rate is the carbon fixed per unit leaf area per unit time. It returns carbon fixed, rate, daily carbon gain at this rate and dry matter equivalent per day, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts between the ways it is reported — µmol CO₂ per m² per second, mg CO₂ per dm² per hour and grams of dry matter — so measurements from a gas analyser can be compared with growth data. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Photosynthesis Rate\",\"html\":\"<p>1 µmol CO₂/m²/s ≈ 1.584 mg CO₂/dm²/h</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/photosynthesis-rate.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Photosynthesis Rate Calculator — Leaf-level assimilation scaled to carbon and dry matter.\\\" title=\\\"Photosynthesis Rate Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Leaf-level assimilation scaled to carbon and dry matter.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>net assimilation rate</td><td>µmol CO₂/m²/s</td></tr><tr><td><code>44</code></td><td>molar mass of CO₂, g/mol</td><td>g/mol</td></tr><tr><td><code>12</code></td><td>molar mass of carbon</td><td>g/mol</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Photosynthesis Rate?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: net assimilation rate of <strong>12</strong> µmol CO₂/m²/s, leaf area of <strong>0.02</strong> m², measurement period of <strong>10</strong> min.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives carbon fixed of <strong>144</strong> µmol CO₂, rate of <strong>19.01</strong> mg CO₂/dm²/h, daily carbon gain at this rate of <strong>0.1493</strong> g C/m²/day, dry matter equivalent per day of <strong>0.3285</strong> g/m²/day.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<ul><li><p>Net assimilation is photosynthesis minus leaf respiration; gross photosynthesis is higher, and the difference is largest in shade leaves.</p></li><li><p>The 1.584 factor converts µmol CO₂ m⁻² s⁻¹ to mg CO₂ dm⁻² h⁻¹ and is worth remembering — it is 44 ÷ 1 000 × 36.</p></li><li><p>Extrapolating a leaf-level rate to a canopy overstates it, because lower leaves are shaded and older.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Comparing a net rate with a gross rate.</li><li>Extrapolating a mid-morning measurement across a whole day.</li><li>Using leaf area measured flat when the leaf was held at an angle.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/net-primary-productivity\\\">Net primary productivity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/transpiration-rate\\\">Transpiration rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/stomatal-density\\\">Stomatal density</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a typical photosynthesis rate?\",\"a\":\"10–25 µmol CO₂/m²/s for healthy C3 crop leaves in full sun; C4 grasses can exceed 40.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the rate lower at midday?\",\"a\":\"Stomatal closure under high vapour pressure deficit and photorespiration both reduce it.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Photosynthesis Rate actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns carbon fixed, rate, daily carbon gain at this rate and dry matter equivalent per day. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Net assimilation rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol CO₂/m²/s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Leaf area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.02\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"minutes\",\"label\":\"Measurement period\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"umol\",\"label\":\"Carbon fixed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol CO₂\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mg_dm2_h\",\"label\":\"Rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg CO₂/dm²/h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"g_m2_day\",\"label\":\"Daily carbon gain at this rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g C/m²/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"biomass\",\"label\":\"Dry matter equivalent per day\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/m²/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Rate\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"umol\",\"f\":\"a*area*minutes*60\"},{\"l\":\"mg_dm2_h\",\"f\":\"a*1.584\"},{\"l\":\"g_m2_day\",\"f\":\"a*0.012*86400/1000000*12\"},{\"l\":\"biomass\",\"f\":\"a*0.012*86400/1000000*12*2.2\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/photosynthesis-rate\",\"slug\":\"photosynthesis-rate\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Photosynthesis Rate Calculator\",\"title\":\"Photosynthesis Rate Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Photosynthesis rate is the carbon fixed per unit leaf area per unit time. Free photosynthesis rate calculator: enter your values and get carbon fixed, rate, d\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-photosynthesis-rate\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a\",\"l\":\"Net assimilation rate\",\"suf\":\"µmol CO₂/m²/s\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Leaf area\",\"suf\":\"m²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"minutes\",\"l\":\"Measurement period\",\"suf\":\"min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"umol\",\"l\":\"Carbon fixed\",\"suf\":\"µmol CO₂\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mg_dm2_h\",\"l\":\"Rate\",\"suf\":\"mg CO₂/dm²/h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"g_m2_day\",\"l\":\"Daily carbon gain at this rate\",\"suf\":\"g C/m²/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"biomass\",\"l\":\"Dry matter equivalent per day\",\"suf\":\"g/m²/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"a\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.02\",\"c\":null}]},\"minutes\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"umol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mg_dm2_h\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"g_m2_day\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"biomass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Rate\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"umol\",\"f\":\"a*area*minutes*60\",\"r\":[\"a\",\"area\",\"minutes\"]},{\"l\":\"mg_dm2_h\",\"f\":\"a*1.584\",\"r\":[\"a\"]},{\"l\":\"g_m2_day\",\"f\":\"a*0.012*86400/1000000*12\",\"r\":[\"a\"]},{\"l\":\"biomass\",\"f\":\"a*0.012*86400/1000000*12*2.2\",\"r\":[\"a\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(133, 'transpiration-rate', 'biology', 'Transpiration Rate Calculator', 'Transpiration Rate Calculator', 'Transpiration Rate Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Transpiration is water lost through stomata, driven by the vapour pressure difference between leaf and air. Free transpiration rate calculator: enter your val', 'Transpiration is water lost through stomata, driven by the vapour pressure difference between leaf and air. It returns transpiration rate, daily water use per m² of leaf, molar rate and approximate stomatal conductance, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts a measured loss into a rate per unit leaf area and a whole-plant water use, and links it to the water the roots must supply. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Transpiration Rate\",\"html\":\"<p>Transpiration rate (g/m²/h) = mass lost ÷ leaf area ÷ time in hours</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/transpiration-rate.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Transpiration Rate Calculator — Mass lost, leaf area and time to a transpiration rate.\\\" title=\\\"Transpiration Rate Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Mass lost, leaf area and time to a transpiration rate.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>mass</code></td><td>water lost by the shoot</td><td>g</td></tr><tr><td><code>area</code></td><td>leaf area of the shoot</td><td>m²</td></tr><tr><td><code>rate</code></td><td>transpiration rate</td><td>g/m²/h</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Transpiration Rate?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: mass lost of <strong>2.4</strong> g, leaf area of <strong>0.015</strong> m², duration of <strong>30</strong> min.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives transpiration rate of <strong>320</strong> g/m²/h, daily water use per m² of leaf of <strong>3.84</strong> L/m²/day, molar rate of <strong>4.94</strong> mmol/m²/s, approximate stomatal conductance of <strong>0.1122</strong> mol/m²/s.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 20 g/m²/h</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>shade, high humidity or closed stomata</td></tr><tr><td>20 – 100</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>typical glasshouse conditions</td></tr><tr><td>above 100</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>bright, dry, windy conditions</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Mass loss measured by weighing is total evapotranspiration from the shoot; a gas analyser measures the transpiration flux directly.</p></li><li><p>Water lost is water the roots must replace: at 1 L/m²/day a square metre of leaf needs about a litre a day, which scales quickly to whole-canopy irrigation.</p></li><li><p>Transpiration cools the leaf; a water-stressed leaf runs several degrees warmer, which is the principle behind thermal stress indices.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Weighing the pot rather than the shoot, so evaporation from the substrate is included.</li><li>Using leaf area measured on one side of the leaf when both are transpiring.</li><li>Reporting g/h without normalising to leaf area.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/photosynthesis-rate\\\">Photosynthesis rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/stomatal-density\\\">Stomatal density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/crop-water-requirement\\\">Crop water requirement</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I convert g/m²/h to mmol/m²/s?\",\"a\":\"Divide by 18 (grams per mole of water) and by 3.6.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does transpiration matter for nutrient uptake?\",\"a\":\"Because mass flow in the xylem is the main route by which nutrients reach the roots.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Transpiration Rate actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns transpiration rate, daily water use per m² of leaf, molar rate and approximate stomatal conductance. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"mass\",\"label\":\"Mass lost\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Leaf area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.015\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"minutes\",\"label\":\"Duration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"30\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rate\",\"label\":\"Transpiration rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/m²/h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_day\",\"label\":\"Daily water use per m² of leaf\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L/m²/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mmol\",\"label\":\"Molar rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/m²/s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"conductance\",\"label\":\"Approximate stomatal conductance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mol/m²/s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Water loss\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)\"},{\"l\":\"per_day\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)*12/1000\"},{\"l\":\"mmol\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)/18/3.6\"},{\"l\":\"conductance\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)/18/3.6/44\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/transpiration-rate\",\"slug\":\"transpiration-rate\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Transpiration Rate Calculator\",\"title\":\"Transpiration Rate Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Transpiration is water lost through stomata, driven by the vapour pressure difference between leaf and air. Free transpiration rate calculator: enter your val\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-transpiration-rate\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mass\",\"l\":\"Mass lost\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Leaf area\",\"suf\":\"m²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"minutes\",\"l\":\"Duration\",\"suf\":\"min\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rate\",\"l\":\"Transpiration rate\",\"suf\":\"g/m²/h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_day\",\"l\":\"Daily water use per m² of leaf\",\"suf\":\"L/m²/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mmol\",\"l\":\"Molar rate\",\"suf\":\"mmol/m²/s\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conductance\",\"l\":\"Approximate stomatal conductance\",\"suf\":\"mol/m²/s\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"mass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.015\",\"c\":null}]},\"minutes\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"30\",\"c\":null}]},\"rate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_day\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"conductance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Water loss\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rate\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)\",\"r\":[\"mass\",\"area\",\"minutes\"]},{\"l\":\"per_day\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)*12/1000\",\"r\":[\"mass\",\"area\",\"minutes\"]},{\"l\":\"mmol\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)/18/3.6\",\"r\":[\"mass\",\"area\",\"minutes\"]},{\"l\":\"conductance\",\"f\":\"mass/area/(minutes/60)/18/3.6/44\",\"r\":[\"mass\",\"area\",\"minutes\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(134, 'solute-potential', 'biology', 'Solute Potential Calculator', 'Solute Potential Calculator', 'Solute Potential Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Solute potential is the osmotic component of water potential: dissolved solutes lower it, pulling water in. Free solute potential calculator: enter your value', 'Solute potential is the osmotic component of water potential: dissolved solutes lower it, pulling water in. It returns solute potential, solute potential, concentration and equivalent NaCl concentration, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator applies the van \'t Hoff relation to give solute potential from concentration and temperature, and compares it with pure water at zero. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Solute Potential\",\"html\":\"<p>Ψ_s = −i × C × R × T</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/solute-potential.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Solute Potential Calculator — Concentration lowering the water potential below zero.\\\" title=\\\"Solute Potential Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Concentration lowering the water potential below zero.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>i</code></td><td>number of particles the solute dissociates into</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>C</code></td><td>concentration</td><td>mol/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>R</code></td><td>gas constant, 8.314</td><td>J/mol/K</td></tr><tr><td><code>T</code></td><td>absolute temperature</td><td>K</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Solute Potential?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: solute concentration of <strong>0.2</strong> mol/L, temperature of <strong>25</strong>°C, ionisation factor (i) of <strong>1</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives solute potential of <strong>-4.96e-04</strong> MPa, solute potential of <strong>-0.005</strong> bar, concentration of <strong>200</strong> mmol/L, equivalent nacl concentration of <strong>200</strong> mmol/L.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 MPa</td><td><strong>Pure water</strong></td><td>the maximum water potential</td></tr><tr><td>−0.1 to −0.5 MPa</td><td><strong>Typical plant cell</strong></td><td>most mesophytes at full turgor</td></tr><tr><td>−1 to −2 MPa</td><td><strong>Stressed or saline</strong></td><td>growth slows and stomata close</td></tr><tr><td>below −2 MPa</td><td><strong>Severe</strong></td><td>halophytes only; most crops die</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Solute potential is always negative or zero: solutes can only lower water potential, never raise it.</p></li><li><p>The ionisation factor doubles the effect of sodium chloride, which is why seawater at about 1 mol/L NaCl gives roughly −4.5 MPa.</p></li><li><p>Water potential also has a pressure component, so a turgid cell\'s total potential can be near zero even though its solute potential is strongly negative.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Forgetting i for a salt, which halves the apparent osmotic effect.</li><li>Using degrees Celsius in the gas constant term.</li><li>Reporting solute potential as positive because only the magnitude was calculated.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\\\">Osmolarity and osmotic pressure</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/water-potential\\\">Water potential</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/plant-available-water\\\">Plant available water</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is solute potential negative?\",\"a\":\"Because pure water at atmospheric pressure is defined as zero, and solutes always reduce the free energy of water.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the solute potential of 0.1 M sucrose at 20 °C?\",\"a\":\"About −0.24 MPa.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Solute Potential actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns solute potential, solute potential, concentration and equivalent NaCl concentration. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"conc\",\"label\":\"Solute concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"temp\",\"label\":\"Temperature\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"i\",\"label\":\"Ionisation factor (i)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"2 for NaCl, 1 for sucrose\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"psi\",\"label\":\"Solute potential\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"MPa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"bar\",\"label\":\"Solute potential\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bar\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"molarity_mmol\",\"label\":\"Concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"equiv_nacl\",\"label\":\"Equivalent NaCl concentration\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mmol/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Potential\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"psi\",\"f\":\"0-i*conc*8.314*(temp+273.15)/1000000\"},{\"l\":\"bar\",\"f\":\"0-i*conc*8.314*(temp+273.15)/100000\"},{\"l\":\"molarity_mmol\",\"f\":\"conc*1000\"},{\"l\":\"equiv_nacl\",\"f\":\"conc/i*1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/solute-potential\",\"slug\":\"solute-potential\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Solute Potential Calculator\",\"title\":\"Solute Potential Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Solute potential is the osmotic component of water potential: dissolved solutes lower it, pulling water in. Free solute potential calculator: enter your value\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-solute-potential\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"conc\",\"l\":\"Solute concentration\",\"suf\":\"mol/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"temp\",\"l\":\"Temperature\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"i\",\"l\":\"Ionisation factor (i)\",\"h\":\"2 for NaCl, 1 for sucrose\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"psi\",\"l\":\"Solute potential\",\"suf\":\"MPa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bar\",\"l\":\"Solute potential\",\"suf\":\"bar\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"molarity_mmol\",\"l\":\"Concentration\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"equiv_nacl\",\"l\":\"Equivalent NaCl concentration\",\"suf\":\"mmol/L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"conc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"temp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"i\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"psi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"bar\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"molarity_mmol\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"equiv_nacl\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Potential\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"psi\",\"f\":\"0-i*conc*8.314*(temp+273.15)/1000000\",\"r\":[\"i\",\"conc\",\"temp\"]},{\"l\":\"bar\",\"f\":\"0-i*conc*8.314*(temp+273.15)/100000\",\"r\":[\"i\",\"conc\",\"temp\"]},{\"l\":\"molarity_mmol\",\"f\":\"conc*1000\",\"r\":[\"conc\"]},{\"l\":\"equiv_nacl\",\"f\":\"conc/i*1000\",\"r\":[\"conc\",\"i\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(135, 'feed-conversion-ratio', 'biology', 'Feed Conversion Ratio Calculator', 'Feed Conversion Ratio Calculator', 'Feed Conversion Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Feed conversion ratio is the kilograms of feed it takes to add one kilogram of gain. Free feed conversion ratio calculator: enter your values and get total li', 'Feed conversion ratio is the kilograms of feed it takes to add one kilogram of gain. It returns total liveweight gain, feed conversion ratio, feed efficiency (gain per kg feed) and feed cost per kg of gain, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator returns FCR, its inverse (feed efficiency), the feed cost per kilogram of gain and the improvement from a target FCR. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Feed Conversion Ratio\",\"html\":\"<p>FCR = feed consumed ÷ liveweight gain;  feed efficiency = gain ÷ feed = 1 ÷ FCR</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/feed-conversion-ratio.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Feed Conversion Ratio Calculator — Feed in, gain out: the ratio is the whole story of feed cost.\\\" title=\\\"Feed Conversion Ratio Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Feed in, gain out: the ratio is the whole story of feed cost.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>feed</code></td><td>dry or as-fed feed consumed</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>gain</code></td><td>weight gained over the same period</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>FCR</code></td><td>feed conversion ratio</td><td>kg/kg</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> Feed conversion ratio is the standard performance measure in livestock recording schemes; values quoted here follow typical commercial breed benchmarks.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Feed Conversion Ratio?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: feed offered of <strong>1,200</strong> kg, starting weight of <strong>30</strong> kg, finishing weight of <strong>105</strong> kg, animals lost of <strong>0</strong> head, feed price of <strong>0.42</strong> per kg, target fcr of <strong>2.6</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives total liveweight gain of <strong>75</strong> kg, feed conversion ratio of <strong>16</strong> kg/kg, feed efficiency (gain per kg feed) of <strong>0.0625</strong> kg/kg, feed cost per kg of gain of <strong>6.72</strong> per kg, feed per surviving animal of <strong>1,200</strong> kg, feed the target fcr would need of <strong>195</strong> kg.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 1.8</td><td><strong>Excellent</strong></td><td>modern broilers and well-run pig units</td></tr><tr><td>1.8 – 2.5</td><td><strong>Good</strong></td><td>typical efficient pig finishing</td></tr><tr><td>2.5 – 4.0</td><td><strong>Average</strong></td><td>beef feedlot, older genetics</td></tr><tr><td>above 4.0</td><td><strong>Poor</strong></td><td>check feed wastage, health and weighing accuracy</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Always compare FCR over the same weight range. A pig from 30 to 105 kg converts far better than the same pig from 8 to 30 kg, because maintenance is spread over more gain.</p></li><li><p>Feed efficiency is the same information upside down: an FCR of 2.5 is an efficiency of 0.40 kg of gain per kg of feed.</p></li><li><p>Deaths do not change FCR as calculated on surviving animals unless you divide by the weight gain of the animals actually placed, which is what makes the figure honest.</p></li><li><p>Report whether the feed weight is as-fed or dry matter. Silage at 35% dry matter looks like a wonderful FCR until you realise most of the weight was water.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Recording feed delivered to the bunk instead of feed consumed — spillage and refusals inflate the number.</li><li>Weighing animals once, at the end, after a different interval for each pen.</li><li>Comparing FCR across species: poultry, pigs and cattle live on completely different scales.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/dry-matter-intake\\\">Dry matter intake</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/pig-weight\\\">Pig weight from measurements</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/chicken-feed-amount\\\">Chicken feed amount</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good feed conversion ratio for broilers?\",\"a\":\"Around 1.5–1.7 kg of feed per kg of liveweight for modern broiler strains over a 42-day cycle.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my FCR get worse as animals get heavier?\",\"a\":\"Maintenance requirements scale with body weight while gain does not, so the marginal cost of each extra kilogram rises.\"},{\"q\":\"Does adding water to feed change FCR?\",\"a\":\"It changes as-fed FCR but not dry-matter FCR, which is why dry-matter figures are the only ones that compare across farms.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Feed Conversion Ratio actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns total liveweight gain, feed conversion ratio, feed efficiency (gain per kg feed) and feed cost per kg of gain. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"feed\",\"label\":\"Feed offered\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Total over the period\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"start\",\"label\":\"Starting weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"30\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"end\",\"label\":\"Finishing weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"105\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"deaths\",\"label\":\"Animals lost\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Their feed was still consumed\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"head\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"price\",\"label\":\"Feed price\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.42\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Target FCR\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2.6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"What a good herd achieves\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"fcr\",\"label\":\"Feed conversion ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"efficiency\",\"label\":\"Feed efficiency (gain per kg feed)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_gain\",\"label\":\"Total liveweight gain\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cost\",\"label\":\"Feed cost per kg of gain\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"feed_per_head\",\"label\":\"Feed per surviving animal\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"target_feed\",\"label\":\"Feed the target FCR would need\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"total_gain\",\"f\":\"max(0,end-start)\"},{\"l\":\"fcr\",\"f\":\"feed/max(0.0001,end-start)\"},{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"max(0,end-start)/max(0.0001,feed)\"},{\"l\":\"cost\",\"f\":\"price*feed/max(0.0001,end-start)\"},{\"l\":\"feed_per_head\",\"f\":\"feed/max(1,100-deaths)*100\"},{\"l\":\"target_feed\",\"f\":\"max(0,end-start)*target\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/feed-conversion-ratio\",\"slug\":\"feed-conversion-ratio\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Feed Conversion Ratio Calculator\",\"title\":\"Feed Conversion Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Feed conversion ratio is the kilograms of feed it takes to add one kilogram of gain. Free feed conversion ratio calculator: enter your values and get total li\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-feed-conversion-ratio\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"feed\",\"l\":\"Feed offered\",\"h\":\"Total over the period\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"start\",\"l\":\"Starting weight\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"end\",\"l\":\"Finishing weight\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"deaths\",\"l\":\"Animals lost\",\"h\":\"Their feed was still consumed\",\"suf\":\"head\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"price\",\"l\":\"Feed price\",\"suf\":\"per kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Target FCR\",\"h\":\"What a good herd achieves\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fcr\",\"l\":\"Feed conversion ratio\",\"suf\":\"kg/kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"efficiency\",\"l\":\"Feed efficiency (gain per kg feed)\",\"suf\":\"kg/kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_gain\",\"l\":\"Total liveweight gain\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cost\",\"l\":\"Feed cost per kg of gain\",\"suf\":\"per kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"feed_per_head\",\"l\":\"Feed per surviving animal\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target_feed\",\"l\":\"Feed the target FCR would need\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"feed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1200\",\"c\":null}]},\"start\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"30\",\"c\":null}]},\"end\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"105\",\"c\":null}]},\"deaths\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0\",\"c\":null}]},\"price\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.42\",\"c\":null}]},\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2.6\",\"c\":null}]},\"fcr\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"efficiency\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_gain\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cost\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"feed_per_head\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"target_feed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Conversion\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"total_gain\",\"f\":\"max(0,end-start)\",\"r\":[\"end\",\"start\"]},{\"l\":\"fcr\",\"f\":\"feed/max(0.0001,end-start)\",\"r\":[\"feed\",\"end\",\"start\"]},{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"max(0,end-start)/max(0.0001,feed)\",\"r\":[\"end\",\"start\",\"feed\"]},{\"l\":\"cost\",\"f\":\"price*feed/max(0.0001,end-start)\",\"r\":[\"price\",\"feed\",\"end\",\"start\"]},{\"l\":\"feed_per_head\",\"f\":\"feed/max(1,100-deaths)*100\",\"r\":[\"feed\",\"deaths\"]},{\"l\":\"target_feed\",\"f\":\"max(0,end-start)*target\",\"r\":[\"end\",\"start\",\"target\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'wheat', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(136, 'hunting-tooth-ratio', 'biology', 'Hunting Tooth Ratio Calculator', 'Hunting Tooth Ratio Calculator', 'Hunting Tooth Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The hunting tooth ratio compares the number of teeth on the driving gear with the number on the driven gear, and it predicts how often the same pair of teeth', 'The hunting tooth ratio compares the number of teeth on the driving gear with the number on the driven gear, and it predicts how often the same pair of teeth meets. It returns gear ratio, hunting tooth number and share of one wear cycle per revolution, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIn biology the same arithmetic explains carnassial wear and the molar wear gradients used to age mammals. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Hunting Tooth Ratio\",\"html\":\"<p>Hunting tooth = (T₁ × T₂) ÷ gcd(T₁, T₂);  the same tooth pair meets again after that many revolutions of the driver</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/hunting-tooth-ratio.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Hunting Tooth Ratio Calculator — Twenty-one teeth against thirty-four: every pair eventually meets.\\\" title=\\\"Hunting Tooth Ratio Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Twenty-one teeth against thirty-four: every pair eventually meets.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T₁</code></td><td>teeth on the driver</td><td>teeth</td></tr><tr><td><code>T₂</code></td><td>teeth on the driven</td><td>teeth</td></tr><tr><td><code>gcd</code></td><td>greatest common divisor of the two counts</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p><strong>Where the relationship comes from.</strong> The hunting tooth principle is classical gear design; its application to mammalian dentition comes from comparative anatomy of occlusal wear.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Hunting Tooth Ratio?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: teeth on the driver (or upper molar) of <strong>21</strong>, teeth on the driven (or lower molar) of <strong>34</strong>, driver revolutions observed of <strong>100</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives gear ratio of <strong>1.62</strong> :1, hunting tooth number of <strong>714</strong> revolutions, share of one wear cycle per revolution of <strong>14.01</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>hunting tooth = T₁ × T₂</td><td><strong>Coprime counts</strong></td><td>every tooth meets every other tooth</td></tr><tr><td>hunting tooth = T₁</td><td><strong>Driven is a multiple of driver</strong></td><td>the same few pairs do all the work</td></tr><tr><td>between</td><td><strong>Partially hunting</strong></td><td>wear is spread over part of the gear</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Gear designers choose tooth counts with a large common multiple so wear is distributed; cheap gearboxes fail early precisely because this was ignored.</p></li><li><p>In zoology the ratio describes how the upper and lower cheek teeth occlude: matching counts produce grooves that repeat in the same place and wear faster.</p></li><li><p>Ungulates with a large hunting tooth number keep a longer grinding surface, which is one explanation for the many teeth in horse and elephant dentitions.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using the ratio of radii instead of the tooth counts — teeth must mesh one to one.</li><li>Forgetting that a 1:1 gear has the minimum possible hunting tooth, so it wears fastest.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/medullary-index\\\">Medullary index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cephalic-index\\\">Cephalic index</a>. <a href=\\\"/math/gcd\\\">Greatest common divisor</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is a prime number of teeth useful?\",\"a\":\"A prime count cannot share a factor with the mating gear, so every tooth meets every other tooth and wear spreads over the whole surface.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the hunting tooth for 21 and 34 teeth?\",\"a\":\"gcd(21, 34) = 1, so the hunting tooth is 21 × 34 = 714 driver revolutions.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Hunting Tooth Ratio actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns gear ratio, hunting tooth number and share of one wear cycle per revolution. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"driver\",\"label\":\"Teeth on the driver (or upper molar)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"21\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"driven\",\"label\":\"Teeth on the driven (or lower molar)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"34\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"revolutions\",\"label\":\"Driver revolutions observed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Gear ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\":1\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hunting\",\"label\":\"Hunting tooth number\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"revolutions\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hunting_pct\",\"label\":\"Share of one wear cycle per revolution\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Ratio\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"driven/driver\"},{\"l\":\"hunting\",\"f\":\"driver*driven/gcd(driver,driven)\"},{\"l\":\"hunting_pct\",\"f\":\"revolutions/(driver*driven/gcd(driver,driven))*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/hunting-tooth-ratio\",\"slug\":\"hunting-tooth-ratio\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Hunting Tooth Ratio Calculator\",\"title\":\"Hunting Tooth Ratio Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The hunting tooth ratio compares the number of teeth on the driving gear with the number on the driven gear, and it predicts how often the same pair of teeth\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-hunting-tooth-ratio\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"driver\",\"l\":\"Teeth on the driver (or upper molar)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"driven\",\"l\":\"Teeth on the driven (or lower molar)\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"revolutions\",\"l\":\"Driver revolutions observed\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Gear ratio\",\"suf\":\":1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hunting\",\"l\":\"Hunting tooth number\",\"suf\":\"revolutions\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hunting_pct\",\"l\":\"Share of one wear cycle per revolution\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"driver\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"21\",\"c\":null}]},\"driven\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"34\",\"c\":null}]},\"revolutions\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hunting\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hunting_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Ratio\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"driven/driver\",\"r\":[\"driven\",\"driver\"]},{\"l\":\"hunting\",\"f\":\"driver*driven/gcd(driver,driven)\",\"r\":[\"driver\",\"driven\"]},{\"l\":\"hunting_pct\",\"f\":\"revolutions/(driver*driven/gcd(driver,driven))*100\",\"r\":[\"revolutions\",\"driver\",\"driven\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'gear', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(137, 'pcr-cycle-calculator', 'biology', 'PCR Cycle Calculator', 'PCR Cycle Calculator', 'PCR Cycle Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A PCR run is a few dozen repeats of three steps, and the total time on the machine is simply the sum of those steps plus the ramp between them. Free pcr cycle', 'A PCR run is a few dozen repeats of three steps, and the total time on the machine is simply the sum of those steps plus the ramp between them. It returns time per cycle, total run time, fold amplification and copies at the end, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator turns your protocol into the time per cycle, the time to the answer and the number of copies produced. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the PCR Cycle\",\"html\":\"<p>Time per cycle = t_denature + t_anneal + t_extend + 3 × ramp;  copies = N₀ × (1 + E)^cycles</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/pcr-cycle-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: PCR Cycle Calculator — Denature, anneal, extend — repeated until the product dominates.\\\" title=\\\"PCR Cycle Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Denature, anneal, extend — repeated until the product dominates.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>E</code></td><td>efficiency per cycle (1.0 = perfect doubling)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>N₀</code></td><td>template copies at the start</td><td>copies</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>time for each step</td><td>s</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the PCR Cycle?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: denaturation time of <strong>30</strong> s, annealing time of <strong>30</strong> s, extension time of <strong>45</strong> s, cycles of <strong>35</strong>, ramp time between steps of <strong>4</strong> s, starting copies of <strong>1,000</strong>, amplification efficiency of <strong>95</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives time per cycle of <strong>117</strong> s, total run time of <strong>68.25</strong> min, fold amplification of <strong>14,164,831,011</strong> x, copies at the end of <strong>14,164,831,011,411</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>90 – 100%</td><td><strong>Efficient</strong></td><td>clean primers, good template quality</td></tr><tr><td>80 – 90%</td><td><strong>Workable</strong></td><td>some loss per cycle; enough product in 35 cycles</td></tr><tr><td>below 80%</td><td><strong>Poor</strong></td><td>inhibitors, primer dimers or wrong annealing temperature</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Ramp time is not small: a three-step protocol ramps six times per cycle, and on a slow block that can add half a minute to every cycle.</p></li><li><p>Two-step protocols drop the separate extension step because the polymerase works at the annealing temperature, which is why fast protocols are so much shorter.</p></li><li><p>Efficiency above 100% in real data almost always means the baseline was set badly, not that the reaction invented copies.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Forgetting the initial hot start and the final extension when quoting a run time.</li><li>Cutting extension time below about 30 s per kilobase for a long product.</li><li>Adding cycles instead of fixing a low efficiency — beyond about 40 cycles the plateau gives artefacts, not more product.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/pcr-annealing-temperature\\\">PCR annealing temperature</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/pcr-master-mix-calculator\\\">PCR master mix</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\\\">Oligo Tm</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How long does a 35-cycle PCR take?\",\"a\":\"With 30 s denaturation, 30 s annealing, 45 s extension and 4 s ramps, about 70 minutes.\"},{\"q\":\"How many copies after 30 cycles?\",\"a\":\"At 95% efficiency the target is multiplied by roughly 1.95³⁰ ≈ 5 × 10⁸.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the PCR Cycle actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns time per cycle, total run time, fold amplification and copies at the end. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"denature_t\",\"label\":\"Denaturation time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"30\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"anneal_t\",\"label\":\"Annealing time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"30\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"extend_t\",\"label\":\"Extension time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"45\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cycles\",\"label\":\"Cycles\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"35\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"ramp\",\"label\":\"Ramp time between steps\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Per transition\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"copies\",\"label\":\"Starting copies\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"efficiency\",\"label\":\"Amplification efficiency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"95\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"100% doubles every cycle\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cycle_time\",\"label\":\"Time per cycle\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total_time\",\"label\":\"Total run time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"min\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"final_copies\",\"label\":\"Copies at the end\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fold\",\"label\":\"Fold amplification\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"x\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Run\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"cycle_time\",\"f\":\"denature_t+anneal_t+extend_t+3*ramp\"},{\"l\":\"total_time\",\"f\":\"(denature_t+anneal_t+extend_t+3*ramp)*cycles/60\"},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"pow(1+efficiency/100,cycles)\"},{\"l\":\"final_copies\",\"f\":\"copies*pow(1+efficiency/100,cycles)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/pcr-cycle-calculator\",\"slug\":\"pcr-cycle-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"PCR Cycle Calculator\",\"title\":\"PCR Cycle Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A PCR run is a few dozen repeats of three steps, and the total time on the machine is simply the sum of those steps plus the ramp between them. Free pcr cycle\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-pcr-cycle-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"denature_t\",\"l\":\"Denaturation time\",\"suf\":\"s\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"anneal_t\",\"l\":\"Annealing time\",\"suf\":\"s\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"extend_t\",\"l\":\"Extension time\",\"suf\":\"s\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cycles\",\"l\":\"Cycles\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ramp\",\"l\":\"Ramp time between steps\",\"h\":\"Per transition\",\"suf\":\"s\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"copies\",\"l\":\"Starting copies\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"efficiency\",\"l\":\"Amplification efficiency\",\"h\":\"100% doubles every cycle\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cycle_time\",\"l\":\"Time per cycle\",\"suf\":\"s\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total_time\",\"l\":\"Total run time\",\"suf\":\"min\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"final_copies\",\"l\":\"Copies at the end\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fold\",\"l\":\"Fold amplification\",\"suf\":\"x\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"denature_t\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"30\",\"c\":null}]},\"anneal_t\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"30\",\"c\":null}]},\"extend_t\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"45\",\"c\":null}]},\"cycles\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"35\",\"c\":null}]},\"ramp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4\",\"c\":null}]},\"copies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"efficiency\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"95\",\"c\":null}]},\"cycle_time\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total_time\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"final_copies\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fold\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Run\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"cycle_time\",\"f\":\"denature_t+anneal_t+extend_t+3*ramp\",\"r\":[\"denature_t\",\"anneal_t\",\"extend_t\",\"ramp\"]},{\"l\":\"total_time\",\"f\":\"(denature_t+anneal_t+extend_t+3*ramp)*cycles/60\",\"r\":[\"denature_t\",\"anneal_t\",\"extend_t\",\"ramp\",\"cycles\"]},{\"l\":\"fold\",\"f\":\"pow(1+efficiency/100,cycles)\",\"r\":[\"efficiency\",\"cycles\"]},{\"l\":\"final_copies\",\"f\":\"copies*pow(1+efficiency/100,cycles)\",\"r\":[\"copies\",\"efficiency\",\"cycles\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(138, 'specific-productivity-qp', 'biology', 'Specific Productivity Calculator (qP)', 'Specific Productivity Calculator (qP)', 'Specific Productivity (qP) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Specific productivity is the amount of product one cell makes per day, which is the only fair way to compare cell lines, media and processes of different size', 'Specific productivity is the amount of product one cell makes per day, which is the only fair way to compare cell lines, media and processes of different sizes. It returns specific productivity, total product per batch, volumetric productivity and days needed for the target titre, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator derives qP from titre, cell density and culture time, then projects the titre a longer run would give. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Specific Productivity (qP)\",\"html\":\"<p>qP = titre ÷ (VCD × days) × 1000  [pg/cell/day],  with VCD in 10⁶ cells/mL</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/specific-productivity-qp.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Specific Productivity Calculator (qP) — Titre accumulates while viable cells hold — the area is the IVCC.\\\" title=\\\"Specific Productivity Calculator (qP)\\\"><figcaption>Titre accumulates while viable cells hold — the area is the IVCC.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>titre</code></td><td>product concentration at harvest</td><td>g/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>VCD</code></td><td>time-weighted mean viable cell density</td><td>10⁶/mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>duration of the production phase</td><td>days</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Specific Productivity (qP)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: product titre of <strong>2.5</strong> g/L, viable cell density of <strong>12</strong> 10⁶/mL, production phase of <strong>10</strong> days, working volume of <strong>2,000</strong> L, target titre of <strong>4</strong> g/L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives specific productivity of <strong>20.83</strong> pg/cell/d, total product per batch of <strong>5,000</strong> g, volumetric productivity of <strong>0.25</strong> g/L/day, days needed for the target titre of <strong>16</strong> days.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 – 10</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>unoptimised CHO pools</td></tr><tr><td>10 – 30</td><td><strong>Typical</strong></td><td>stable CHO fed-batch processes</td></tr><tr><td>30 – 60</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>optimised hosts, strong promoters, good media</td></tr><tr><td>above 60</td><td><strong>Very high</strong></td><td>check the assay — this is unusual</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Use the integral of viable cell concentration over time (IVCC) rather than a single VCD reading; a run that peaks at 20 × 10⁶ cells/mL for two days produces far less than one that holds 10 × 10⁶ for ten days.</p></li><li><p>qP is the fair comparison between a 2 L bioreactor and a 2 000 L tank, because titre alone rewards scale and harvest timing.</p></li><li><p>Volumetric productivity (g/L/day) is what capacity planning uses; specific productivity is what cell-line development optimises.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Plugging in peak VCD instead of the time-weighted average, which understates qP badly.</li><li>Comparing qP across products with different molecular weights without converting to molar terms.</li><li>Ignoring the harvest/clarification losses between the bioreactor and the reported titre.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\\\">Growth curve</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/specific-growth-rate-from-od\\\">Specific growth rate from OD</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/transfection-setup\\\">Transfection setup</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a typical qP for CHO cells?\",\"a\":\"Roughly 10–30 pg/cell/day in fed-batch, with high producers reaching 40–60.\"},{\"q\":\"How do I calculate IVCC?\",\"a\":\"Trapezoidal integration of viable cell density against time over the production phase; qP = product ÷ IVCC.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Specific Productivity (qP) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns specific productivity, total product per batch, volumetric productivity and days needed for the target titre. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"titre\",\"label\":\"Product titre\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"vcd\",\"label\":\"Viable cell density\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Time-weighted average, not the peak\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"10⁶/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"days\",\"label\":\"Production phase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Working volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Target titre\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4.0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"qp\",\"label\":\"Specific productivity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pg/cell/d\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total product per batch\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_litre_day\",\"label\":\"Volumetric productivity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g/L/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"days_needed\",\"label\":\"Days needed for the target titre\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Productivity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"qp\",\"f\":\"titre/(vcd*days)*1000\"},{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"titre*volume\"},{\"l\":\"per_litre_day\",\"f\":\"titre/days\"},{\"l\":\"days_needed\",\"f\":\"target/(titre/days)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/specific-productivity-qp\",\"slug\":\"specific-productivity-qp\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Specific Productivity Calculator (qP)\",\"title\":\"Specific Productivity (qP) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Specific productivity is the amount of product one cell makes per day, which is the only fair way to compare cell lines, media and processes of different size\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-specific-productivity-qp\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"titre\",\"l\":\"Product titre\",\"suf\":\"g/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"vcd\",\"l\":\"Viable cell density\",\"h\":\"Time-weighted average, not the peak\",\"suf\":\"10⁶/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"days\",\"l\":\"Production phase\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Working volume\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Target titre\",\"suf\":\"g/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"qp\",\"l\":\"Specific productivity\",\"suf\":\"pg/cell/d\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total product per batch\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_litre_day\",\"l\":\"Volumetric productivity\",\"suf\":\"g/L/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"days_needed\",\"l\":\"Days needed for the target titre\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"titre\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"vcd\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"days\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2000\",\"c\":null}]},\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4.0\",\"c\":null}]},\"qp\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_litre_day\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"days_needed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Productivity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"qp\",\"f\":\"titre/(vcd*days)*1000\",\"r\":[\"titre\",\"vcd\",\"days\"]},{\"l\":\"total\",\"f\":\"titre*volume\",\"r\":[\"titre\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"per_litre_day\",\"f\":\"titre/days\",\"r\":[\"titre\",\"days\"]},{\"l\":\"days_needed\",\"f\":\"target/(titre/days)\",\"r\":[\"target\",\"titre\",\"days\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(139, 'tff-membrane-area', 'biology', 'TFF Membrane Area and Flux Calculator', 'TFF Membrane Area and Flux Calculator', 'TFF Membrane Area and Flux Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A tangential-flow filtration step is sized by the volume to process and the flux the membrane will hold, and the membrane area then fixes how long the step ta', 'A tangential-flow filtration step is sized by the volume to process and the flux the membrane will hold, and the membrane area then fixes how long the step takes. It returns membrane area required, cassettes to install, diafiltration buffer needed and estimated step time, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator returns the area, the number of cassettes, the diafiltration volume and the step time at your flux. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the TFF Membrane Area and Flux\",\"html\":\"<p>Area = max(batch ÷ load capacity, batch ÷ flux-based area);  DF buffer = (V ÷ CF) × DV;  time ≈ (V + V·DV/CF) ÷ (area × flux)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/tff-membrane-area.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: TFF Membrane Area and Flux Calculator — Feed, retentate and permeate: area sets the pace of the step.\\\" title=\\\"TFF Membrane Area and Flux Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Feed, retentate and permeate: area sets the pace of the step.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>V</code></td><td>starting batch volume</td><td>L</td></tr><tr><td><code>J</code></td><td>permeate flux</td><td>L/m²/h</td></tr><tr><td><code>CF</code></td><td>concentration factor (final ÷ initial volume)</td><td>×</td></tr><tr><td><code>DV</code></td><td>diafiltration volumes</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the TFF Membrane Area and Flux?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: batch volume of <strong>500</strong> L, permeate flux of <strong>60</strong> L/m²/h, concentration factor of <strong>8</strong> x, diafiltration volumes of <strong>5</strong> DV, area per cassette of <strong>2.5</strong> m², maximum load per m² of <strong>100</strong> L/m².</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives membrane area required of <strong>8.33</strong> m², cassettes to install of <strong>4</strong> cassettes, diafiltration buffer needed of <strong>312.5</strong> L, estimated step time of <strong>2.12</strong> h, area set by the load limit of <strong>5</strong> m², which limit sets the area (1 = flux, 2 = load) of <strong>1</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>30 – 60</td><td><strong>Conservative flux</strong></td><td>high-mass, fouling feeds</td></tr><tr><td>60 – 100</td><td><strong>Typical</strong></td><td>a well-behaved mAb ultrafiltration step</td></tr><tr><td>above 100</td><td><strong>Aggressive</strong></td><td>only with pre-filtered, low-titre streams</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Flux falls as the feed concentrates, so the time estimate is optimistic unless the quoted flux is the average over the step, not the clean-water value.</p></li><li><p>Diafiltration is done at constant volume: you add buffer at the rate permeate leaves, and the buffer volume needed is (retentate volume) × DV.</p></li><li><p>Install at least one cassette more than the calculation suggests; a single cassette leaves no margin for a fouled membrane.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using clean-water flux for a cell-culture feed, where fouling cuts it by half or more.</li><li>Forgetting the hold-up volume of the system and tubing when quoting yield.</li><li>Sizing exactly to the load limit and then having to dilute the batch to finish the step.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/enzyme-specific-activity-yield\\\">Specific activity and yield</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/specific-productivity-qp\\\">Specific productivity</a>. <a href=\\\"/chemistry/molarity\\\">Molarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I choose a cassette size?\",\"a\":\"Round the calculated area up to whole cassettes, then check the retentate channel height gives a sensible crossflow for your pump.\"},{\"q\":\"What is one diafiltration volume?\",\"a\":\"A volume of buffer equal to the current retentate volume; five volumes remove about 99.3% of a freely permeating impurity.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the TFF Membrane Area and Flux actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns membrane area required, cassettes to install, diafiltration buffer needed and estimated step time. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Batch volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"flux\",\"label\":\"Permeate flux\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"60\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"30–100 for a typical mAb step\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L/m²/h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"concentration\",\"label\":\"Concentration factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"x\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"diavolumes\",\"label\":\"Diafiltration volumes\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"DV\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"cassette\",\"label\":\"Area per cassette\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"load\",\"label\":\"Maximum load per m²\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"100\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Process-specific\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L/m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Membrane area required\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cassettes\",\"label\":\"Cassettes to install\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cassettes\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"df_volume\",\"label\":\"Diafiltration buffer needed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"time\",\"label\":\"Estimated step time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"load_area\",\"label\":\"Area set by the load limit\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"binding\",\"label\":\"Which limit sets the area (1 = flux, 2 = load)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Sizing\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"max(volume/load,volume/flux)\"},{\"l\":\"cassettes\",\"f\":\"ceil(max(volume/load,volume/flux)/cassette)\"},{\"l\":\"df_volume\",\"f\":\"volume/concentration*diavolumes\"},{\"l\":\"time\",\"f\":\"(volume/area+volume/concentration*diavolumes/area)/flux+0.5\"},{\"l\":\"load_area\",\"f\":\"volume/load\"},{\"l\":\"binding\",\"f\":\"if_else(volume/load>volume/flux,2,1)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/tff-membrane-area\",\"slug\":\"tff-membrane-area\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"TFF Membrane Area and Flux Calculator\",\"title\":\"TFF Membrane Area and Flux Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A tangential-flow filtration step is sized by the volume to process and the flux the membrane will hold, and the membrane area then fixes how long the step ta\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-tff-membrane-area\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Batch volume\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"flux\",\"l\":\"Permeate flux\",\"h\":\"30–100 for a typical mAb step\",\"suf\":\"L/m²/h\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"concentration\",\"l\":\"Concentration factor\",\"suf\":\"x\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"diavolumes\",\"l\":\"Diafiltration volumes\",\"suf\":\"DV\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cassette\",\"l\":\"Area per cassette\",\"suf\":\"m²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"load\",\"l\":\"Maximum load per m²\",\"h\":\"Process-specific\",\"suf\":\"L/m²\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Membrane area required\",\"suf\":\"m²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cassettes\",\"l\":\"Cassettes to install\",\"suf\":\"cassettes\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"df_volume\",\"l\":\"Diafiltration buffer needed\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"time\",\"l\":\"Estimated step time\",\"suf\":\"h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"load_area\",\"l\":\"Area set by the load limit\",\"suf\":\"m²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"binding\",\"l\":\"Which limit sets the area (1 = flux, 2 = load)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"500\",\"c\":null}]},\"flux\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"60\",\"c\":null}]},\"concentration\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8\",\"c\":null}]},\"diavolumes\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5\",\"c\":null}]},\"cassette\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"load\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"100\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cassettes\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"df_volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"time\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"load_area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"binding\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Sizing\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"max(volume/load,volume/flux)\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"load\",\"flux\"]},{\"l\":\"cassettes\",\"f\":\"ceil(max(volume/load,volume/flux)/cassette)\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"load\",\"flux\",\"cassette\"]},{\"l\":\"df_volume\",\"f\":\"volume/concentration*diavolumes\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"concentration\",\"diavolumes\"]},{\"l\":\"time\",\"f\":\"(volume/area+volume/concentration*diavolumes/area)/flux+0.5\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"area\",\"concentration\",\"diavolumes\",\"flux\"]},{\"l\":\"load_area\",\"f\":\"volume/load\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"load\"]},{\"l\":\"binding\",\"f\":\"if_else(volume/load>volume/flux,2,1)\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"load\",\"flux\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flow', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(140, 'transfection-setup', 'biology', 'Transfection Setup Calculator', 'Transfection Setup Calculator', 'Transfection Setup Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Transfection protocols are written per well or per flask, and scaling them by hand is where experiments go wrong. Free transfection setup calculator: enter yo', 'Transfection protocols are written per well or per flask, and scaling them by hand is where experiments go wrong. It returns DNA per batch to make, reagent for the batch, diluent for the batch and total complex volume, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator scales DNA, transfection reagent and diluent from your reference format to the plate you actually have, with the complex formation time. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Transfection Setup\",\"html\":\"<p>Scale factor = wells × 1.1 for pipetting loss;  every component is multiplied by that factor, and the complex is formed at the reference ratio</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/transfection-setup.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Transfection Setup Calculator — Two dilutions, one mix, then straight onto the cells.\\\" title=\\\"Transfection Setup Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Two dilutions, one mix, then straight onto the cells.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>DNA</code></td><td>plasmid per well</td><td>µg</td></tr><tr><td><code>reagent</code></td><td>lipid or polymer per well</td><td>µL</td></tr><tr><td><code>diluent</code></td><td>serum-free medium per well</td><td>µL</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Transfection Setup?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: dna in the reference well of <strong>0.5</strong> µg, reagent in the reference well of <strong>1.5</strong> µL, diluent per reference well of <strong>50</strong> µL, wells (or vessels) needed of <strong>24</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives dna per batch to make of <strong>13.2</strong> µg, reagent for the batch of <strong>39.6</strong> µL, diluent for the batch of <strong>1,320</strong> µL, total complex volume of <strong>1,359.6</strong> µL, volume of complex per well of <strong>51.5</strong> µL.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1:2 – 1:3</td><td><strong>DNA to reagent</strong></td><td>typical for Lipofectamine-class reagents</td></tr><tr><td>15 – 30 min</td><td><strong>Complex formation</strong></td><td>room temperature, no serum, no antibiotics</td></tr><tr><td>4 – 6 h</td><td><strong>Exposure</strong></td><td>then change to complete medium for sensitive cells</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Make the DNA and the reagent dilutions in separate tubes before mixing; adding lipid straight onto DNA creates local excess and uneven complexes.</p></li><li><p>The 10% excess is not laziness: 24 wells need 25 wells\' worth because no pipette delivers exactly what it draws.</p></li><li><p>Scale by surface area, not by well number, when moving between plate formats — a 6-well well holds ten times the area of a 96-well well.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Scaling a 24-well protocol to a 6-well plate by well count only.</li><li>Leaving complexes on the cells overnight, which is toxic for most primary cells.</li><li>Using medium with antibiotics during complex formation.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cell-culture-vessel-reference\\\">Cell culture vessel reference</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/cell-seeding-density\\\">Seeding density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/transformation-efficiency\\\">Transformation efficiency</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How much DNA for a 6-well transfection?\",\"a\":\"About 1–2 µg per well for a 9.6 cm² surface, roughly double a 12-well well.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I scale this to a flask?\",\"a\":\"Yes, but scale on growth area: a T-75 flask is about 8 times a 6-well well.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Transfection Setup actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns DNA per batch to make, reagent for the batch, diluent for the batch and total complex volume. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"dna_ref\",\"label\":\"DNA in the reference well\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"reagent_ref\",\"label\":\"Reagent in the reference well\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"diluent_ref\",\"label\":\"Diluent per reference well\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"wells\",\"label\":\"Wells (or vessels) needed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"24\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Add 10% for pipetting loss\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"dna_batch\",\"label\":\"DNA per batch to make\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"reagent_batch\",\"label\":\"Reagent for the batch\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"diluent_batch\",\"label\":\"Diluent for the batch\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tube_total\",\"label\":\"Total complex volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_well\",\"label\":\"Volume of complex per well\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Setup\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"dna_batch\",\"f\":\"dna_ref*wells*1.1\"},{\"l\":\"reagent_batch\",\"f\":\"reagent_ref*wells*1.1\"},{\"l\":\"diluent_batch\",\"f\":\"diluent_ref*wells*1.1\"},{\"l\":\"tube_total\",\"f\":\"(reagent_ref+diluent_ref)*wells*1.1\"},{\"l\":\"per_well\",\"f\":\"(reagent_ref+diluent_ref)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/transfection-setup\",\"slug\":\"transfection-setup\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Transfection Setup Calculator\",\"title\":\"Transfection Setup Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Transfection protocols are written per well or per flask, and scaling them by hand is where experiments go wrong. Free transfection setup calculator: enter yo\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-transfection-setup\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dna_ref\",\"l\":\"DNA in the reference well\",\"suf\":\"µg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"reagent_ref\",\"l\":\"Reagent in the reference well\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"diluent_ref\",\"l\":\"Diluent per reference well\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"wells\",\"l\":\"Wells (or vessels) needed\",\"h\":\"Add 10% for pipetting loss\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"dna_batch\",\"l\":\"DNA per batch to make\",\"suf\":\"µg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"reagent_batch\",\"l\":\"Reagent for the batch\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"diluent_batch\",\"l\":\"Diluent for the batch\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tube_total\",\"l\":\"Total complex volume\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_well\",\"l\":\"Volume of complex per well\",\"suf\":\"µL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"dna_ref\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"reagent_ref\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"diluent_ref\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"wells\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"24\",\"c\":null}]},\"dna_batch\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"reagent_batch\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"diluent_batch\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tube_total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_well\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Setup\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"dna_batch\",\"f\":\"dna_ref*wells*1.1\",\"r\":[\"dna_ref\",\"wells\"]},{\"l\":\"reagent_batch\",\"f\":\"reagent_ref*wells*1.1\",\"r\":[\"reagent_ref\",\"wells\"]},{\"l\":\"diluent_batch\",\"f\":\"diluent_ref*wells*1.1\",\"r\":[\"diluent_ref\",\"wells\"]},{\"l\":\"tube_total\",\"f\":\"(reagent_ref+diluent_ref)*wells*1.1\",\"r\":[\"reagent_ref\",\"diluent_ref\",\"wells\"]},{\"l\":\"per_well\",\"f\":\"(reagent_ref+diluent_ref)\",\"r\":[\"reagent_ref\",\"diluent_ref\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'flask', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(141, 'bod-load', 'biology', 'BOD Load Calculator', 'BOD Load Calculator', 'BOD Load Calculator — Formula & Results', 'BOD load is the mass of oxygen-demanding material a waste stream delivers to a treatment plant each day, and it sizes the aeration tank. Free bod load calcula', 'BOD load is the mass of oxygen-demanding material a waste stream delivers to a treatment plant each day, and it sizes the aeration tank. It returns BOD load, BOD removed, population equivalent and food to microorganism ratio, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts flow and BOD concentration into kilograms per day, population equivalents and the effluent surcharge band. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the BOD Load\",\"html\":\"<p>BOD load (kg/day) = flow (m³/day) × BOD (mg/L) ÷ 1 000;  F/M = BOD load ÷ (tank volume × MLSS)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/bod-load.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: BOD Load Calculator — Load in, load out: the gap is what the biology removed.\\\" title=\\\"BOD Load Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Load in, load out: the gap is what the biology removed.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Q</code></td><td>flow</td><td>m³/day</td></tr><tr><td><code>BOD</code></td><td>5-day biochemical oxygen demand</td><td>mg/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>MLSS</code></td><td>mixed liquor suspended solids</td><td>mg/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>F/M</code></td><td>food to microorganism ratio</td><td>kg/kg/day</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the BOD Load?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: flow of <strong>500</strong> m³/day, influent bod of <strong>320</strong> mg/L, effluent bod of <strong>20</strong> mg/L, aeration tank volume of <strong>750</strong> m³, mlss of <strong>3,000</strong> mg/L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives bod load of <strong>160</strong> kg/day, bod removed of <strong>150</strong> kg/day, population equivalent of <strong>2,666.67</strong> people, food to microorganism ratio of <strong>0.0711</strong> kg/kg/day, removal efficiency of <strong>93.75</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.05 – 0.15</td><td><strong>Extended aeration</strong></td><td>long sludge age, low surplus sludge</td></tr><tr><td>0.2 – 0.4</td><td><strong>Conventional</strong></td><td>the classic activated-sludge window</td></tr><tr><td>0.4 – 0.6</td><td><strong>High rate</strong></td><td>fast, but sludge quality suffers</td></tr><tr><td>above 1.0</td><td><strong>Overloaded</strong></td><td>poor settling, carry-over and odour</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Population equivalent assumes 60 g of BOD per person per day, which is a European convention; check the local figure before quoting it in a permit application.</p></li><li><p>BOD is a five-day test, so the number always lags the plant by a week. COD is the fast proxy, and the BOD/COD ratio tells you how biodegradable the stream is.</p></li><li><p>F/M ratio, not load alone, decides whether the sludge settles. Two plants with the same load behave completely differently if one has triple the tank volume.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Mixing units: mg/L with L/s needs a factor of 86.4 to reach kg/day.</li><li>Using the influent BOD where the permit limit is on the effluent.</li><li>Treating BOD load as constant when the plant sees a diurnal peak two to three times the daily mean.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/svi-sludge-volume-index\\\">Sludge volume index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/solids-retention-time\\\">Solids retention time</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\\\">Dilution calculator</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I convert mg/L and m³/day to kg/day?\",\"a\":\"Multiply flow by concentration and divide by 1 000.\"},{\"q\":\"What population does 1 000 kg BOD/day serve?\",\"a\":\"About 16 700 people at 60 g per person per day.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the BOD Load actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns BOD load, BOD removed, population equivalent and food to microorganism ratio. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"flow\",\"label\":\"Flow\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"bod_in\",\"label\":\"Influent BOD\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"320\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"bod_out\",\"label\":\"Effluent BOD\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Aeration tank volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"750\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mlss\",\"label\":\"MLSS\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"load\",\"label\":\"BOD load\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"removed\",\"label\":\"BOD removed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pe\",\"label\":\"Population equivalent\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"people\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"f_m\",\"label\":\"Food to microorganism ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg/kg/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"efficiency\",\"label\":\"Removal efficiency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Load\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"load\",\"f\":\"flow*bod_in/1000\"},{\"l\":\"removed\",\"f\":\"flow*(bod_in-bod_out)/1000\"},{\"l\":\"pe\",\"f\":\"flow*bod_in/1000/0.06\"},{\"l\":\"f_m\",\"f\":\"flow*bod_in/1000/(volume*mlss/1000)\"},{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"(bod_in-bod_out)/bod_in*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/bod-load\",\"slug\":\"bod-load\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"BOD Load Calculator\",\"title\":\"BOD Load Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"BOD load is the mass of oxygen-demanding material a waste stream delivers to a treatment plant each day, and it sizes the aeration tank. Free bod load calcula\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-bod-load\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"flow\",\"l\":\"Flow\",\"suf\":\"m³/day\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bod_in\",\"l\":\"Influent BOD\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"bod_out\",\"l\":\"Effluent BOD\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Aeration tank volume\",\"suf\":\"m³\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mlss\",\"l\":\"MLSS\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"load\",\"l\":\"BOD load\",\"suf\":\"kg/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"removed\",\"l\":\"BOD removed\",\"suf\":\"kg/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pe\",\"l\":\"Population equivalent\",\"suf\":\"people\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"f_m\",\"l\":\"Food to microorganism ratio\",\"suf\":\"kg/kg/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"efficiency\",\"l\":\"Removal efficiency\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"flow\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"500\",\"c\":null}]},\"bod_in\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"320\",\"c\":null}]},\"bod_out\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"750\",\"c\":null}]},\"mlss\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3000\",\"c\":null}]},\"load\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"removed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pe\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"f_m\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"efficiency\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Load\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"load\",\"f\":\"flow*bod_in/1000\",\"r\":[\"flow\",\"bod_in\"]},{\"l\":\"removed\",\"f\":\"flow*(bod_in-bod_out)/1000\",\"r\":[\"flow\",\"bod_in\",\"bod_out\"]},{\"l\":\"pe\",\"f\":\"flow*bod_in/1000/0.06\",\"r\":[\"flow\",\"bod_in\"]},{\"l\":\"f_m\",\"f\":\"flow*bod_in/1000/(volume*mlss/1000)\",\"r\":[\"flow\",\"bod_in\",\"volume\",\"mlss\"]},{\"l\":\"efficiency\",\"f\":\"(bod_in-bod_out)/bod_in*100\",\"r\":[\"bod_in\",\"bod_out\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'droplet', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:47', '2026-10-29 14:08:47'),
(142, 'svi-sludge-volume-index', 'biology', 'Sludge Volume Index Calculator (SVI)', 'Sludge Volume Index Calculator (SVI)', 'Sludge Volume Index (SVI) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Sludge volume index is the volume one gram of sludge occupies after thirty minutes of settling, and it is the fastest signal that an activated-sludge plant is', 'Sludge volume index is the volume one gram of sludge occupies after thirty minutes of settling, and it is the fastest signal that an activated-sludge plant is about to bulk. It returns sludge volume index, sludge density index, return sludge ratio and return sludge flow, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator returns SVI, the sludge density index and the return-sludge ratio the clarifier needs. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Sludge Volume Index (SVI)\",\"html\":\"<p>SVI = settled volume (mL/L) × 1 000 ÷ MLSS (mg/L);  RAS ratio = MLSS ÷ (RAS MLSS − MLSS)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/svi-sludge-volume-index.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Sludge Volume Index Calculator (SVI) — Settling quality on one dial from dense to bulking.\\\" title=\\\"Sludge Volume Index Calculator (SVI)\\\"><figcaption>Settling quality on one dial from dense to bulking.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>SV₃₀</code></td><td>settled sludge volume in 30 minutes</td><td>mL/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>MLSS</code></td><td>mixed liquor suspended solids</td><td>mg/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>SVI</code></td><td>sludge volume index</td><td>mL/g</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Sludge Volume Index (SVI)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: settled sludge volume after 30 min of <strong>280</strong> mL/L, mlss of <strong>3,000</strong> mg/L, influent flow of <strong>500</strong> m³/day, return sludge mlss of <strong>8,000</strong> mg/L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives sludge volume index of <strong>93.33</strong> mL/g, sludge density index of <strong>1.07</strong>, return sludge ratio of <strong>60</strong>%, return sludge flow of <strong>300</strong> m³/day.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 80</td><td><strong>Dense, well settling</strong></td><td>excellent sludge; check for pin floc</td></tr><tr><td>80 – 150</td><td><strong>Normal</strong></td><td>typical healthy activated sludge</td></tr><tr><td>150 – 250</td><td><strong>Poor settling</strong></td><td>investigate loading, aeration and nutrients</td></tr><tr><td>above 250</td><td><strong>Bulking</strong></td><td>filamentous outbreak; the clarifier will lose solids</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>SVI is a ratio, so it rises both when sludge settles badly and when the MLSS reading is too low — always check the MLSS measurement before blaming filaments.</p></li><li><p>Sludge density index is simply 100 ÷ SVI, a measure of how many grams one litre of settled sludge contains.</p></li><li><p>The return-sludge ratio follows from a mass balance on the clarifier: solids leaving in the return must equal solids arriving from the aeration tank.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Reading the settled volume from a stirred or disturbed cylinder.</li><li>Measuring MLSS from a sample taken at the wrong point in the tank.</li><li>Raising the return rate to fight bulking without checking whether the clarifier can take the hydraulic load.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/bod-load\\\">BOD load</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/solids-retention-time\\\">Solids retention time</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/field-capacity\\\">Field capacity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal SVI?\",\"a\":\"About 100–150 mL/g for conventional activated sludge; below 80 suits membrane and oxidation-ditch plants.\"},{\"q\":\"What causes a high SVI?\",\"a\":\"Filamentous bacteria, low dissolved oxygen, nutrient deficiency (often phosphorus) or a sudden organic load.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Sludge Volume Index (SVI) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns sludge volume index, sludge density index, return sludge ratio and return sludge flow. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"settled\",\"label\":\"Settled sludge volume after 30 min\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"280\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Read from the cone\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mlss\",\"label\":\"MLSS\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"flow\",\"label\":\"Influent flow\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"return_mlss\",\"label\":\"Return sludge MLSS\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"svi\",\"label\":\"Sludge volume index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mL/g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"sdi\",\"label\":\"Sludge density index\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"return_ratio\",\"label\":\"Return sludge ratio\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"return_flow\",\"label\":\"Return sludge flow\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Sludge\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"svi\",\"f\":\"settled/mlss*1000\"},{\"l\":\"sdi\",\"f\":\"100/(settled/mlss*1000)\"},{\"l\":\"return_ratio\",\"f\":\"mlss/(return_mlss-mlss)*100\"},{\"l\":\"return_flow\",\"f\":\"flow*mlss/(return_mlss-mlss)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/svi-sludge-volume-index\",\"slug\":\"svi-sludge-volume-index\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Sludge Volume Index Calculator (SVI)\",\"title\":\"Sludge Volume Index (SVI) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Sludge volume index is the volume one gram of sludge occupies after thirty minutes of settling, and it is the fastest signal that an activated-sludge plant is\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-svi-sludge-volume-index\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"settled\",\"l\":\"Settled sludge volume after 30 min\",\"h\":\"Read from the cone\",\"suf\":\"mL/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mlss\",\"l\":\"MLSS\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"flow\",\"l\":\"Influent flow\",\"suf\":\"m³/day\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"return_mlss\",\"l\":\"Return sludge MLSS\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"svi\",\"l\":\"Sludge volume index\",\"suf\":\"mL/g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sdi\",\"l\":\"Sludge density index\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"return_ratio\",\"l\":\"Return sludge ratio\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"return_flow\",\"l\":\"Return sludge flow\",\"suf\":\"m³/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"settled\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"280\",\"c\":null}]},\"mlss\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3000\",\"c\":null}]},\"flow\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"500\",\"c\":null}]},\"return_mlss\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8000\",\"c\":null}]},\"svi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"sdi\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"return_ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"return_flow\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Sludge\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"svi\",\"f\":\"settled/mlss*1000\",\"r\":[\"settled\",\"mlss\"]},{\"l\":\"sdi\",\"f\":\"100/(settled/mlss*1000)\",\"r\":[\"settled\",\"mlss\"]},{\"l\":\"return_ratio\",\"f\":\"mlss/(return_mlss-mlss)*100\",\"r\":[\"mlss\",\"return_mlss\"]},{\"l\":\"return_flow\",\"f\":\"flow*mlss/(return_mlss-mlss)\",\"r\":[\"flow\",\"mlss\",\"return_mlss\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'droplet', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(143, 'solids-retention-time', 'biology', 'Solids Retention Time Calculator (SRT)', 'Solids Retention Time Calculator (SRT)', 'Solids Retention Time (SRT) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Solids retention time, or sludge age, is how long a bacterium stays in the system, and it decides which organisms survive there. Free solids retention time (s', 'Solids retention time, or sludge age, is how long a bacterium stays in the system, and it decides which organisms survive there. It returns solids inventory, solids retention time and wasting rate for the target SRT, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator returns SRT from the tank inventory and the daily sludge wasting, plus the wasting rate needed to hit a target age. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Solids Retention Time (SRT)\",\"html\":\"<p>SRT = (V × MLSS) ÷ (Q_w × MLSS_w + Q_e × TSS_e),  all masses in the same units</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/solids-retention-time.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Solids Retention Time Calculator (SRT) — Solids inventory divided by daily loss is the sludge age.\\\" title=\\\"Solids Retention Time Calculator (SRT)\\\"><figcaption>Solids inventory divided by daily loss is the sludge age.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>V</code></td><td>aeration tank volume</td><td>m³</td></tr><tr><td><code>Q_w</code></td><td>waste sludge flow</td><td>m³/day</td></tr><tr><td><code>MLSS_w</code></td><td>wasted sludge concentration</td><td>mg/L</td></tr><tr><td><code>Q_e</code></td><td>effluent flow</td><td>m³/day</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Solids Retention Time (SRT)?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: aeration tank volume of <strong>750</strong> m³, mlss of <strong>3,000</strong> mg/L, waste sludge flow of <strong>25</strong> m³/day, waste sludge mlss of <strong>8,000</strong> mg/L, effluent flow of <strong>500</strong> m³/day, effluent suspended solids of <strong>15</strong> mg/L, target srt of <strong>15</strong> days.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives solids inventory of <strong>2,250</strong> kg, solids retention time of <strong>10.84</strong> days, wasting rate for the target srt of <strong>17.81</strong> m³/day.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 – 4 days</td><td><strong>High rate</strong></td><td>fast growth, poor nitrification</td></tr><tr><td>5 – 10 days</td><td><strong>Conventional</strong></td><td>BOD removal, partial nitrification</td></tr><tr><td>10 – 20 days</td><td><strong>Nitrifying</strong></td><td>stable ammonia removal</td></tr><tr><td>above 25 days</td><td><strong>Extended aeration</strong></td><td>sludge stabilisation, low yield</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>SRT, not hydraulic retention time, controls the biology: a plant can have a four-hour hydraulic pass and a fifteen-day sludge age at the same time.</p></li><li><p>Nitrifying bacteria grow slowly, so a plant designed for ammonia removal needs an SRT of at least 8–10 days at 15 °C and more when colder.</p></li><li><p>Solids lost over the weir count. On a plant with poor clarification, effluent TSS can be the dominant term in the denominator.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Quoting hydraulic retention time when asked about sludge age.</li><li>Ignoring effluent suspended solids, which quietly shorten the SRT.</li><li>Wasting from the return line without using the return concentration in the calculation.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/svi-sludge-volume-index\\\">Sludge volume index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/bod-load\\\">BOD load</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\\\">Growth curve</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How long should sludge be kept in an activated-sludge plant?\",\"a\":\"5–10 days for BOD removal, 10–20 for nitrification, 25 and above for stabilisation.\"},{\"q\":\"Does SRT set sludge production?\",\"a\":\"Yes — surplus sludge falls roughly as 1/SRT, which is why long ages produce less waste sludge.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Solids Retention Time (SRT) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns solids inventory, solids retention time and wasting rate for the target SRT. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Aeration tank volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"750\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"mlss\",\"label\":\"MLSS\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"waste\",\"label\":\"Waste sludge flow\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"25\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"waste_mlss\",\"label\":\"Waste sludge MLSS\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"effluent\",\"label\":\"Effluent flow\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"500\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"effluent_tss\",\"label\":\"Effluent suspended solids\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"15\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"target\",\"label\":\"Target SRT\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"15\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"srt\",\"label\":\"Solids retention time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"days\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"sludge_age_mass\",\"label\":\"Solids inventory\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"waste_needed\",\"label\":\"Wasting rate for the target SRT\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"m³/day\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Retention\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"sludge_age_mass\",\"f\":\"volume*mlss/1000\"},{\"l\":\"srt\",\"f\":\"(volume*mlss/1000)/(waste*waste_mlss/1000+effluent*effluent_tss/1000)\"},{\"l\":\"waste_needed\",\"f\":\"((volume*mlss/1000)/target-effluent*effluent_tss/1000)/(waste_mlss/1000)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/solids-retention-time\",\"slug\":\"solids-retention-time\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Solids Retention Time Calculator (SRT)\",\"title\":\"Solids Retention Time (SRT) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Solids retention time, or sludge age, is how long a bacterium stays in the system, and it decides which organisms survive there. Free solids retention time (s\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-solids-retention-time\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Aeration tank volume\",\"suf\":\"m³\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mlss\",\"l\":\"MLSS\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"waste\",\"l\":\"Waste sludge flow\",\"suf\":\"m³/day\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"waste_mlss\",\"l\":\"Waste sludge MLSS\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"effluent\",\"l\":\"Effluent flow\",\"suf\":\"m³/day\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"effluent_tss\",\"l\":\"Effluent suspended solids\",\"suf\":\"mg/L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"target\",\"l\":\"Target SRT\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"srt\",\"l\":\"Solids retention time\",\"suf\":\"days\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sludge_age_mass\",\"l\":\"Solids inventory\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"waste_needed\",\"l\":\"Wasting rate for the target SRT\",\"suf\":\"m³/day\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"750\",\"c\":null}]},\"mlss\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3000\",\"c\":null}]},\"waste\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"25\",\"c\":null}]},\"waste_mlss\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8000\",\"c\":null}]},\"effluent\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"500\",\"c\":null}]},\"effluent_tss\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"15\",\"c\":null}]},\"target\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"15\",\"c\":null}]},\"srt\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"sludge_age_mass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"waste_needed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Retention\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"sludge_age_mass\",\"f\":\"volume*mlss/1000\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"mlss\"]},{\"l\":\"srt\",\"f\":\"(volume*mlss/1000)/(waste*waste_mlss/1000+effluent*effluent_tss/1000)\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"mlss\",\"waste\",\"waste_mlss\",\"effluent\",\"effluent_tss\"]},{\"l\":\"waste_needed\",\"f\":\"((volume*mlss/1000)/target-effluent*effluent_tss/1000)/(waste_mlss/1000)\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"mlss\",\"target\",\"effluent\",\"effluent_tss\",\"waste_mlss\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'clock', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(144, 'net-reproductive-rate', 'biology', 'Net Reproductive Rate Calculator (R₀)', 'Net Reproductive Rate Calculator (R₀)', 'Net Reproductive Rate (R₀) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The net reproductive rate is the number of daughters a female leaves over her whole life, and it is the single number that says whether a population will grow', 'The net reproductive rate is the number of daughters a female leaves over her whole life, and it is the single number that says whether a population will grow. It returns net reproductive rate, generation time, intrinsic rate of increase and doubling time, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nEnter age-specific survival and fertility and it returns R₀, generation time and the per-generation change. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Net Reproductive Rate (R₀)\",\"html\":\"<p>R₀ = Σ l(x)·m(x);  r ≈ ln R₀ ÷ T;  doubling time = ln 2 ÷ r</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/net-reproductive-rate.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Net Reproductive Rate Calculator (R₀) — Survival times fertility, summed over the lifetime.\\\" title=\\\"Net Reproductive Rate Calculator (R₀)\\\"><figcaption>Survival times fertility, summed over the lifetime.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>l(x)</code></td><td>survival to age x</td><td>probability</td></tr><tr><td><code>m(x)</code></td><td>daughters produced at age x</td><td>daughters</td></tr><tr><td><code>R₀</code></td><td>net reproductive rate</td><td>daughters per female</td></tr><tr><td><code>T</code></td><td>generation time</td><td>years</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Net Reproductive Rate (R₀)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: probability a newborn reaches breeding age of <strong>0.6</strong>, daughters per female per season of <strong>3.2</strong> daughters, breeding seasons per lifetime of <strong>2</strong>, age at first reproduction of <strong>1</strong> years.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives net reproductive rate of <strong>3.84</strong> daughters, generation time of <strong>1.5</strong> years, intrinsic rate of increase of <strong>1.35</strong> per year, doubling time of <strong>0.5152</strong> years.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>R₀ &lt; 1</td><td><strong>Declining</strong></td><td>each female is replaced by less than one daughter</td></tr><tr><td>R₀ = 1</td><td><strong>Stable</strong></td><td>exact replacement</td></tr><tr><td>1 &lt; R₀ &lt; 2</td><td><strong>Slow growth</strong></td><td>typical of long-lived vertebrates</td></tr><tr><td>R₀ &gt; 2</td><td><strong>Fast growth</strong></td><td>insects, weeds, invasive species</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>R₀ is a lifetime figure, so it compares across lifespans in a way that r does not; r is the per-year rate and is the one that goes into exponential growth models.</p></li><li><p>The approximation r ≈ ln R₀ / T is exact only in a stable age distribution. When the age structure is disturbed — after a drought or a cull — the realised rate differs for years.</p></li><li><p>A species with R₀ of 1.1 but a generation time of 15 years is effectively static on any management timescale.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Counting offspring rather than daughters — sons do not add to R₀.</li><li>Using the mean age at reproduction when reproduction is spread over several seasons.</li><li>Treating R₀ as a projection: it is an average over the whole life, not next year\'s growth.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/logistic-population-growth\\\">Logistic population growth</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/exponential-population-growth\\\">Exponential growth</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/maximum-sustainable-yield\\\">Maximum sustainable yield</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What does R₀ = 1 mean?\",\"a\":\"The population replaces itself exactly; every female is replaced by one daughter on average.\"},{\"q\":\"How is R₀ different from the intrinsic rate r?\",\"a\":\"R₀ is per generation and ignores timing; r is per unit time and includes it. A fast-breeding species can have the same R₀ as a slow one but a much larger r.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Net Reproductive Rate (R₀) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns net reproductive rate, generation time, intrinsic rate of increase and doubling time. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"survival\",\"label\":\"Probability a newborn reaches breeding age\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0–1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"fecundity\",\"label\":\"Daughters per female per season\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"3.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"daughters\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"seasons\",\"label\":\"Breeding seasons per lifetime\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"interval\",\"label\":\"Age at first reproduction\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"years\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"r0\",\"label\":\"Net reproductive rate\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"daughters\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"generation\",\"label\":\"Generation time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"years\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"r\",\"label\":\"Intrinsic rate of increase\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per year\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"doubling\",\"label\":\"Doubling time\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"years\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Life table\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"r0\",\"f\":\"survival*fecundity*seasons\"},{\"l\":\"generation\",\"f\":\"interval+(seasons-1)*interval/2\"},{\"l\":\"r\",\"f\":\"ln(max(0.000001,survival*fecundity*seasons))/max(0.5,interval)\"},{\"l\":\"doubling\",\"f\":\"ln(2)/(ln(max(0.000001,survival*fecundity*seasons))/max(0.5,interval))\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/net-reproductive-rate\",\"slug\":\"net-reproductive-rate\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Net Reproductive Rate Calculator (R₀)\",\"title\":\"Net Reproductive Rate (R₀) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The net reproductive rate is the number of daughters a female leaves over her whole life, and it is the single number that says whether a population will grow\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-net-reproductive-rate\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"survival\",\"l\":\"Probability a newborn reaches breeding age\",\"h\":\"0–1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fecundity\",\"l\":\"Daughters per female per season\",\"suf\":\"daughters\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"seasons\",\"l\":\"Breeding seasons per lifetime\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"interval\",\"l\":\"Age at first reproduction\",\"suf\":\"years\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r0\",\"l\":\"Net reproductive rate\",\"suf\":\"daughters\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"generation\",\"l\":\"Generation time\",\"suf\":\"years\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r\",\"l\":\"Intrinsic rate of increase\",\"suf\":\"per year\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"doubling\",\"l\":\"Doubling time\",\"suf\":\"years\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"survival\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.6\",\"c\":null}]},\"fecundity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"3.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"seasons\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"interval\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"r0\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"generation\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"r\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"doubling\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Life table\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"r0\",\"f\":\"survival*fecundity*seasons\",\"r\":[\"survival\",\"fecundity\",\"seasons\"]},{\"l\":\"generation\",\"f\":\"interval+(seasons-1)*interval/2\",\"r\":[\"interval\",\"seasons\"]},{\"l\":\"r\",\"f\":\"ln(max(0.000001,survival*fecundity*seasons))/max(0.5,interval)\",\"r\":[\"survival\",\"fecundity\",\"seasons\",\"interval\"]},{\"l\":\"doubling\",\"f\":\"ln(2)/(ln(max(0.000001,survival*fecundity*seasons))/max(0.5,interval))\",\"r\":[\"survival\",\"fecundity\",\"seasons\",\"interval\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'people', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(145, 'fixation-index-fst', 'biology', 'Fixation Index Calculator (Fst)', 'Fixation Index Calculator (Fst)', 'Fixation Index (Fst) Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Fst measures how much of the genetic variation in a set of populations is explained by the differences between them, on a scale from zero (panmixia) to one (c', 'Fst measures how much of the genetic variation in a set of populations is explained by the differences between them, on a scale from zero (panmixia) to one (complete fixation). It returns mean allele frequency, expected heterozygosity within populations, expected heterozygosity overall and fst, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator takes allele frequencies from two or more populations and returns Fst, heterozygosity and the migration rate it implies. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Fixation Index (Fst)\",\"html\":\"<p>Fst = (H_T − H_S) ÷ H_T;  H_T = 2p̄(1 − p̄);  H_S = mean of 2pᵢ(1 − pᵢ);  Fst ≈ 1/(4Nm + 1)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/fixation-index-fst.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Fixation Index Calculator (Fst) — From freely mixing to completely fixed.\\\" title=\\\"Fixation Index Calculator (Fst)\\\"><figcaption>From freely mixing to completely fixed.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>p̄</code></td><td>mean allele frequency across populations</td><td>0–1</td></tr><tr><td><code>H_T</code></td><td>heterozygosity expected with random mating overall</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>H_S</code></td><td>mean heterozygosity within populations</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Fixation Index (Fst)?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: allele frequency in population 1 of <strong>0.2</strong>, allele frequency in population 2 of <strong>0.7</strong>, sample size, population 1 of <strong>50</strong>, sample size, population 2 of <strong>50</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives mean allele frequency of <strong>0.45</strong>, expected heterozygosity within populations of <strong>0.37</strong>, expected heterozygosity overall of <strong>0.495</strong>, fst of <strong>0.2525</strong>, migrants per generation implied by fst of <strong>0.74</strong> per gen.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 – 0.05</td><td><strong>Little differentiation</strong></td><td>one interbreeding population</td></tr><tr><td>0.05 – 0.15</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>substructure, some restricted gene flow</td></tr><tr><td>0.15 – 0.25</td><td><strong>Great</strong></td><td>distinct subpopulations</td></tr><tr><td>above 0.25</td><td><strong>Very great</strong></td><td>near-separate species-level units</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>These bands are Wright\'s rules of thumb, and they were calibrated on protein polymorphisms. Genome-wide Fst values from SNP data are usually an order of magnitude smaller for the same biology.</p></li><li><p>Fst depends on the marker: microsatellites show more differentiation than SNPs at the same level of gene flow.</p></li><li><p>The migration estimate assumes an island model at equilibrium. A recent split looks the same as low migration, and no allele-frequency calculation can tell them apart.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Comparing Fst computed from different marker types.</li><li>Ignoring sample size, which biases Fst upward in small samples.</li><li>Reading Fst as a proportion of individuals that are migrants rather than a proportion of variation.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/allele-frequency\\\">Allele frequency</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/inbreeding-coefficient\\\">Inbreeding coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What Fst value indicates separate populations?\",\"a\":\"Above 0.15 is conventionally \'great differentiation\', but the number only means something against the marker and the species\' baseline.\"},{\"q\":\"Can Fst be negative?\",\"a\":\"Slightly, from sampling noise in small samples; the true value is bounded at zero.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Fixation Index (Fst) actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns mean allele frequency, expected heterozygosity within populations, expected heterozygosity overall and fst. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"p1\",\"label\":\"Allele frequency in population 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0–1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"p2\",\"label\":\"Allele frequency in population 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.7\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0–1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n1\",\"label\":\"Sample size, population 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n2\",\"label\":\"Sample size, population 2\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"p_bar\",\"label\":\"Mean allele frequency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"hs\",\"label\":\"Expected heterozygosity within populations\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ht\",\"label\":\"Expected heterozygosity overall\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"fst\",\"label\":\"Fst\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"migrants\",\"label\":\"Migrants per generation implied by Fst\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per gen\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Divergence\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"p_bar\",\"f\":\"(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2)\"},{\"l\":\"hs\",\"f\":\"(2*p1*(1-p1)+2*p2*(1-p2))/2\"},{\"l\":\"ht\",\"f\":\"2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))\"},{\"l\":\"fst\",\"f\":\"(2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))-(2*p1*(1-p1)+2*p2*(1-p2))/2)/max(0.000001,2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2)))\"},{\"l\":\"migrants\",\"f\":\"(1/max(0.000001,((2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))-(2*p1*(1-p1)+2*p2*(1-p2))/2)/max(0.000001,2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2)))))-1)/4\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/fixation-index-fst\",\"slug\":\"fixation-index-fst\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Fixation Index Calculator (Fst)\",\"title\":\"Fixation Index (Fst) Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Fst measures how much of the genetic variation in a set of populations is explained by the differences between them, on a scale from zero (panmixia) to one (c\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-fixation-index-fst\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p1\",\"l\":\"Allele frequency in population 1\",\"h\":\"0–1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p2\",\"l\":\"Allele frequency in population 2\",\"h\":\"0–1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n1\",\"l\":\"Sample size, population 1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n2\",\"l\":\"Sample size, population 2\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"p_bar\",\"l\":\"Mean allele frequency\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"hs\",\"l\":\"Expected heterozygosity within populations\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ht\",\"l\":\"Expected heterozygosity overall\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fst\",\"l\":\"Fst\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"migrants\",\"l\":\"Migrants per generation implied by Fst\",\"suf\":\"per gen\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"p1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"p2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.7\",\"c\":null}]},\"n1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"n2\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"p_bar\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"hs\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ht\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"fst\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"migrants\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Divergence\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"p_bar\",\"f\":\"(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2)\",\"r\":[\"p1\",\"n1\",\"p2\",\"n2\"]},{\"l\":\"hs\",\"f\":\"(2*p1*(1-p1)+2*p2*(1-p2))/2\",\"r\":[\"p1\",\"p2\"]},{\"l\":\"ht\",\"f\":\"2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))\",\"r\":[\"p1\",\"n1\",\"p2\",\"n2\"]},{\"l\":\"fst\",\"f\":\"(2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))-(2*p1*(1-p1)+2*p2*(1-p2))/2)/max(0.000001,2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2)))\",\"r\":[\"p1\",\"n1\",\"p2\",\"n2\"]},{\"l\":\"migrants\",\"f\":\"(1/max(0.000001,((2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))-(2*p1*(1-p1)+2*p2*(1-p2))/2)/max(0.000001,2*((p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2))*(1-(p1*n1+p2*n2)/(n1+n2)))))-1)/4\",\"r\":[\"p1\",\"n1\",\"p2\",\"n2\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(146, 'heritability-coefficient', 'biology', 'Heritability Coefficient Calculator', 'Heritability Coefficient Calculator', 'Heritability Coefficient Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Heritability is the share of the variation in a trait, in one population in one environment, that is attributable to genetic differences. Free heritability co', 'Heritability is the share of the variation in a trait, in one population in one environment, that is attributable to genetic differences. It returns heritability from parent-offspring regression, heritability from half-sib correlation, heritability from variance components and response to selection, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator estimates narrow-sense heritability from parent-offspring or half-sib data and shows the expected response to selection. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Heritability Coefficient\",\"html\":\"<p>h² = V_A ÷ (V_A + V_E);  h² = 2r(parent, offspring);  h² = 4r(half sibs);  R = h² × S</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/heritability-coefficient.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Heritability Coefficient Calculator — Additive plus environmental variance sets the ceiling.\\\" title=\\\"Heritability Coefficient Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Additive plus environmental variance sets the ceiling.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>V_A</code></td><td>additive genetic variance</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>V_E</code></td><td>environmental variance</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>S</code></td><td>selection differential</td><td>SD</td></tr><tr><td><code>R</code></td><td>response to selection</td><td>SD</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Heritability Coefficient?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: correlation between parent and offspring of <strong>0.35</strong>, correlation among half sibs of <strong>0.09</strong>, additive genetic variance of <strong>0.4</strong>, environmental variance of <strong>0.6</strong>, selection differential of <strong>1.2</strong> SD.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives heritability from parent-offspring regression of <strong>0.7</strong>, heritability from half-sib correlation of <strong>0.36</strong>, heritability from variance components of <strong>0.4</strong>, response to selection of <strong>0.48</strong> SD.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 – 0.2</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>environment dominates; breeding progress is slow</td></tr><tr><td>0.2 – 0.4</td><td><strong>Moderate</strong></td><td>typical for growth and yield traits</td></tr><tr><td>0.4 – 0.7</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>typical for body size and milk yield</td></tr><tr><td>0.7 – 1.0</td><td><strong>Very high</strong></td><td>highly canalised traits such as coat pattern</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Heritability is a property of a population in an environment, not of a gene. The same trait can be 0.6 in a uniform greenhouse and 0.2 in a variable field.</p></li><li><p>Narrow-sense heritability counts only additive effects, because that is the part that responds to selection; broad-sense includes dominance and interaction.</p></li><li><p>The breeder\'s equation R = h²S is the most useful form: it turns heritability into a prediction of how much the next generation moves.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Quoting a heritability figure for a trait without saying which population and environment.</li><li>Using a parent-offspring correlation from different environments, which inflates h².</li><li>Assuming that a high heritability means a trait cannot be changed by the environment.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/fixation-index-fst\\\">Fst</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/relatedness-coefficient\\\">Relatedness coefficient</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/heritability-coefficient\\\">Heritability</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the difference between h² and H²?\",\"a\":\"h² (narrow sense) is additive variance over total; H² (broad sense) is all genetic variance over total. Only h² predicts selection response.\"},{\"q\":\"Can heritability be greater than 1?\",\"a\":\"Not in a properly estimated design; a value above 1 signals a data or model error.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Heritability Coefficient actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns heritability from parent-offspring regression, heritability from half-sib correlation, heritability from variance components and response to selection. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"r_parent\",\"label\":\"Correlation between parent and offspring\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.35\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0–1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"r_halfsib\",\"label\":\"Correlation among half sibs\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.09\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0–1\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"additive\",\"label\":\"Additive genetic variance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.4\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"V_A\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"environment\",\"label\":\"Environmental variance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"V_E\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"selection\",\"label\":\"Selection differential\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"How far the selected parents sit above the mean\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"SD\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"h2_regression\",\"label\":\"Heritability from parent-offspring regression\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"h2_sib\",\"label\":\"Heritability from half-sib correlation\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"h2_variance\",\"label\":\"Heritability from variance components\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"response\",\"label\":\"Response to selection\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"SD\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Heritability\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"h2_regression\",\"f\":\"2*r_parent\"},{\"l\":\"h2_sib\",\"f\":\"4*r_halfsib\"},{\"l\":\"h2_variance\",\"f\":\"additive/(additive+environment)\"},{\"l\":\"response\",\"f\":\"additive/(additive+environment)*selection\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/heritability-coefficient\",\"slug\":\"heritability-coefficient\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Heritability Coefficient Calculator\",\"title\":\"Heritability Coefficient Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Heritability is the share of the variation in a trait, in one population in one environment, that is attributable to genetic differences. Free heritability co\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-heritability-coefficient\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r_parent\",\"l\":\"Correlation between parent and offspring\",\"h\":\"0–1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"r_halfsib\",\"l\":\"Correlation among half sibs\",\"h\":\"0–1\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"additive\",\"l\":\"Additive genetic variance\",\"h\":\"V_A\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"environment\",\"l\":\"Environmental variance\",\"h\":\"V_E\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"selection\",\"l\":\"Selection differential\",\"h\":\"How far the selected parents sit above the mean\",\"suf\":\"SD\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"h2_regression\",\"l\":\"Heritability from parent-offspring regression\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"h2_sib\",\"l\":\"Heritability from half-sib correlation\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"h2_variance\",\"l\":\"Heritability from variance components\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"response\",\"l\":\"Response to selection\",\"suf\":\"SD\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"r_parent\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.35\",\"c\":null}]},\"r_halfsib\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.09\",\"c\":null}]},\"additive\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.4\",\"c\":null}]},\"environment\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.6\",\"c\":null}]},\"selection\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.2\",\"c\":null}]},\"h2_regression\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"h2_sib\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"h2_variance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"response\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Heritability\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"h2_regression\",\"f\":\"2*r_parent\",\"r\":[\"r_parent\"]},{\"l\":\"h2_sib\",\"f\":\"4*r_halfsib\",\"r\":[\"r_halfsib\"]},{\"l\":\"h2_variance\",\"f\":\"additive/(additive+environment)\",\"r\":[\"additive\",\"environment\"]},{\"l\":\"response\",\"f\":\"additive/(additive+environment)*selection\",\"r\":[\"additive\",\"environment\",\"selection\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(147, 'watterson-estimator', 'biology', 'Watterson Estimator Calculator', 'Watterson Estimator Calculator', 'Watterson Estimator Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The Watterson estimator infers the population mutation parameter θ from the number of segregating sites in a sample, with a correction for how many sequences', 'The Watterson estimator infers the population mutation parameter θ from the number of segregating sites in a sample, with a correction for how many sequences you sequenced. It returns harmonic number a₁ (n − 1), watterson θ (per locus), θ per site and watterson θ per kb, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is the quickest way to compare diversity between samples of different sizes and to spot a population that is not at equilibrium. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Watterson Estimator\",\"html\":\"<p>θ_W = S ÷ a₁,  where a₁ = Σ 1/i for i = 1 … n−1</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/watterson-estimator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Watterson Estimator Calculator — More sequences means more expected variable sites.\\\" title=\\\"Watterson Estimator Calculator\\\"><figcaption>More sequences means more expected variable sites.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>S</code></td><td>number of segregating sites</td><td>sites</td></tr><tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>number of sequences in the sample</td><td>sequences</td></tr><tr><td><code>a₁</code></td><td>harmonic number of n − 1</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Watterson Estimator?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: segregating sites of <strong>24</strong>, sequence length of <strong>1,000</strong> bp, number of sequences of <strong>20</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives harmonic number a₁ (n − 1) of <strong>3.55</strong>, watterson θ (per locus) of <strong>6.76</strong>, θ per site of <strong>0.0068</strong>, watterson θ per kb of <strong>6.76</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 – 0.001</td><td><strong>Low diversity</strong></td><td>bottleneck, inbreeding or a small population</td></tr><tr><td>0.001 – 0.01</td><td><strong>Typical</strong></td><td>many outcrossing eukaryotes</td></tr><tr><td>0.01 – 0.05</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>large populations, viruses, bacteria</td></tr><tr><td>above 0.05</td><td><strong>Very high</strong></td><td>often a sign of recent admixture</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>θ_W uses only the count of variable sites, so it is cheap to compute and stable, but it is sensitive to sample size through a₁.</p></li><li><p>Comparing θ_W with π (average pairwise difference) is the basis of Tajima\'s D: they agree under a standard neutral model and diverge under selection or demographic change.</p></li><li><p>The estimator assumes an infinite-sites model with no recombination within the locus. Recombining data must be split into blocks.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Forgetting the a₁ correction and dividing S by n.</li><li>Comparing θ from samples of very different sizes without noting a₁.</li><li>Applying it to a locus with recombination, which biases θ downward.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/tajimas-d\\\">Tajima\'s D</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/fixation-index-fst\\\">Fixation index</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/hardy-weinberg-equilibrium\\\">Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why is a₁ in the denominator?\",\"a\":\"Because the expected number of segregating sites grows with the harmonic number of the sample size under the neutral coalescent.\"},{\"q\":\"Can θ_W estimate effective population size?\",\"a\":\"Yes, for a neutral locus: θ = 4Nₑμ for a diploid autosome, so Nₑ = θ ÷ (4μ).\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Watterson Estimator actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns harmonic number a₁ (n − 1), watterson θ (per locus), θ per site and watterson θ per kb. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sites\",\"label\":\"Segregating sites\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"24\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Columns that vary\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Sequence length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"n\",\"label\":\"Number of sequences\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"theta_w\",\"label\":\"Watterson θ (per locus)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"theta_per_site\",\"label\":\"θ per site\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"theta_per_kb\",\"label\":\"Watterson θ per kb\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"a1\",\"label\":\"Harmonic number a₁ (n − 1)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Diversity\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"a1\",\"f\":\"log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1))\"},{\"l\":\"theta_w\",\"f\":\"sites/(log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1)))\"},{\"l\":\"theta_per_site\",\"f\":\"sites/(log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1)))/length\"},{\"l\":\"theta_per_kb\",\"f\":\"sites/(log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1)))/length*1000\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/watterson-estimator\",\"slug\":\"watterson-estimator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Watterson Estimator Calculator\",\"title\":\"Watterson Estimator Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The Watterson estimator infers the population mutation parameter θ from the number of segregating sites in a sample, with a correction for how many sequences\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-watterson-estimator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sites\",\"l\":\"Segregating sites\",\"h\":\"Columns that vary\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Sequence length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"n\",\"l\":\"Number of sequences\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"theta_w\",\"l\":\"Watterson θ (per locus)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"theta_per_site\",\"l\":\"θ per site\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"theta_per_kb\",\"l\":\"Watterson θ per kb\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"a1\",\"l\":\"Harmonic number a₁ (n − 1)\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sites\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"24\",\"c\":null}]},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1000\",\"c\":null}]},\"n\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"theta_w\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"theta_per_site\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"theta_per_kb\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"a1\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Diversity\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"a1\",\"f\":\"log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1))\",\"r\":[\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"theta_w\",\"f\":\"sites/(log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1)))\",\"r\":[\"sites\",\"n\"]},{\"l\":\"theta_per_site\",\"f\":\"sites/(log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1)))/length\",\"r\":[\"sites\",\"n\",\"length\"]},{\"l\":\"theta_per_kb\",\"f\":\"sites/(log(max(n,2)-1)+0.5772156649+1/(2*(max(n,2)-1))-1/(12*(max(n,2)-1)*(max(n,2)-1)))/length*1000\",\"r\":[\"sites\",\"n\",\"length\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(148, 'absorbance-transmittance', 'biology', 'Absorbance to Transmittance Converter', 'Absorbance to Transmittance Converter', 'Absorbance to Transmittance Converter Calculator', 'Absorbance and transmittance are two ways of writing the same measurement, related by a base-ten logarithm. Free absorbance to transmittance converter calcula', 'Absorbance and transmittance are two ways of writing the same measurement, related by a base-ten logarithm. It returns transmittance, transmittance, concentration from Beer–Lambert and fraction of light lost, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis converter goes both ways, adds percent transmission and the concentration the reading implies at a chosen extinction coefficient. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Absorbance to Transmittance Converter\",\"html\":\"<p>A = −log₁₀(T) = 2 − log₁₀(%T);  T = 10^(−A);  A = ε·c·l</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/absorbance-transmittance.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Absorbance to Transmittance Converter — A logarithmic scale from full transmission to darkness.\\\" title=\\\"Absorbance to Transmittance Converter\\\"><figcaption>A logarithmic scale from full transmission to darkness.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>absorbance (optical density)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>T</code></td><td>transmittance as a fraction</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>ε</code></td><td>extinction coefficient</td><td>per mg/mL</td></tr><tr><td><code>l</code></td><td>path length</td><td>cm</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Absorbance to Transmittance Converter?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: absorbance of <strong>0.5</strong>, extinction coefficient of <strong>1</strong> per mg/mL, path length of <strong>1</strong> cm.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives transmittance of <strong>0.3162</strong>, transmittance of <strong>31.62</strong>%, concentration from beer–lambert of <strong>0.5</strong> mg/mL, fraction of light lost of <strong>68.38</strong>%.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 – 0.1</td><td><strong>Very dilute</strong></td><td>poor signal-to-noise on most instruments</td></tr><tr><td>0.1 – 1.0</td><td><strong>Reliable</strong></td><td>the linear window of a spectrophotometer</td></tr><tr><td>1.0 – 1.5</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>stray light starts to bend the curve</td></tr><tr><td>above 2.0</td><td><strong>Unreliable</strong></td><td>dilute the sample and multiply the result</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>An absorbance of 1.0 means 10% of the light got through; 2.0 means 1%. This is why a doubling of absorbance corresponds to ten times less light, not half.</p></li><li><p>Instruments quote linear range up to about 1.0–1.5 A, above which stray light makes the reading saturate. Always dilute and multiply.</p></li><li><p>Transmittance is sometimes quoted as a percentage in older methods; 50% T is 0.301 A, not 0.5 A.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Treating absorbance as linear in transmittance: doubling A does not halve T.</li><li>Using a cuvette with the wrong path length and forgetting to scale c.</li><li>Forgetting the blank, which shifts every reading by its own absorbance.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/beer-lambert-a280-protein-concentration\\\">Beer–Lambert for proteins</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/od600-to-cell-density\\\">OD600 to cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dilution-calculator-c1v1\\\">Dilution calculator</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is an absorbance of 2 in transmittance?\",\"a\":\"1% transmittance, or 0.01 as a fraction.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my reading stop increasing when I add more sample?\",\"a\":\"The instrument has reached the top of its linear range and stray light now dominates.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Absorbance to Transmittance Converter actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns transmittance, transmittance, concentration from Beer–Lambert and fraction of light lost. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"absorbance\",\"label\":\"Absorbance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"extinction\",\"label\":\"Extinction coefficient\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Use 1 for a plain conversion\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"path\",\"label\":\"Path length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"transmittance\",\"label\":\"Transmittance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"percent\",\"label\":\"Transmittance\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"concentration\",\"label\":\"Concentration from Beer–Lambert\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"mg/mL\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"lost\",\"label\":\"Fraction of light lost\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Optics\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"transmittance\",\"f\":\"pow(10,-absorbance)\"},{\"l\":\"percent\",\"f\":\"pow(10,-absorbance)*100\"},{\"l\":\"concentration\",\"f\":\"absorbance/(extinction*path)\"},{\"l\":\"lost\",\"f\":\"(1-pow(10,-absorbance))*100\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/absorbance-transmittance\",\"slug\":\"absorbance-transmittance\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Absorbance to Transmittance Converter\",\"title\":\"Absorbance to Transmittance Converter Calculator\",\"desc\":\"Absorbance and transmittance are two ways of writing the same measurement, related by a base-ten logarithm. Free absorbance to transmittance converter calcula\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-absorbance-transmittance\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"absorbance\",\"l\":\"Absorbance\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"extinction\",\"l\":\"Extinction coefficient\",\"h\":\"Use 1 for a plain conversion\",\"suf\":\"per mg/mL\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"path\",\"l\":\"Path length\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"transmittance\",\"l\":\"Transmittance\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"percent\",\"l\":\"Transmittance\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"concentration\",\"l\":\"Concentration from Beer–Lambert\",\"suf\":\"mg/mL\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"lost\",\"l\":\"Fraction of light lost\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"absorbance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"extinction\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.0\",\"c\":null}]},\"path\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1\",\"c\":null}]},\"transmittance\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"percent\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"concentration\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"lost\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Optics\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"transmittance\",\"f\":\"pow(10,-absorbance)\",\"r\":[\"absorbance\"]},{\"l\":\"percent\",\"f\":\"pow(10,-absorbance)*100\",\"r\":[\"absorbance\"]},{\"l\":\"concentration\",\"f\":\"absorbance/(extinction*path)\",\"r\":[\"absorbance\",\"extinction\",\"path\"]},{\"l\":\"lost\",\"f\":\"(1-pow(10,-absorbance))*100\",\"r\":[\"absorbance\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'light', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(149, 'centrifuge-rcf-rpm', 'biology', 'Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter', 'Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter', 'Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter Calculator — Formula & Results', 'RCF and RPM are the same centrifuge force in different units: RPM depends on the rotor, RCF does not. Free centrifuge rcf to rpm converter calculator: enter y', 'RCF and RPM are the same centrifuge force in different units: RPM depends on the rotor, RCF does not. It returns relative centrifugal force, speed for 10 000 × g and distance travelled per minute, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis converter goes between the two using the rotor radius, so a protocol written for someone else\'s centrifuge can be run on yours. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter\",\"html\":\"<p>RCF = 1.118 × 10⁻⁵ × r × (rpm)²,  with r in centimetres and RCF in multiples of g</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/centrifuge-rcf-rpm.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter — Force rises with the square of the speed.\\\" title=\\\"Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter\\\"><figcaption>Force rises with the square of the speed.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>rotor radius (centre to tube bottom)</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>rpm</code></td><td>revolutions per minute</td><td>rpm</td></tr><tr><td><code>RCF</code></td><td>relative centrifugal force</td><td>× g</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: rotor radius of <strong>8.5</strong> cm, speed of <strong>4,000</strong> rpm.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives relative centrifugal force of <strong>1,520.48</strong> × g, speed for 10 000 × g of <strong>10,258.16</strong> rpm, distance travelled per minute of <strong>2.14</strong> km.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 500 × g</td><td><strong>Gentle pelleting</strong></td><td>cells, nuclei, large precipitates</td></tr><tr><td>1 000 – 5 000 × g</td><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>bacteria, mitochondria</td></tr><tr><td>10 000 – 20 000 × g</td><td><strong>High</strong></td><td>cell debris, protein precipitates</td></tr><tr><td>above 100 000 × g</td><td><strong>Ultracentrifuge</strong></td><td>ribosomes, viruses, density gradients</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>RCF scales with the square of the speed and linearly with the radius, so a rotor with a 17 cm radius needs far fewer rpm than a small benchtop one to reach the same force.</p></li><li><p>Radius is measured horizontally from the spindle axis to the bottom of the tube when the rotor is in its running position, not the top of the tube.</p></li><li><p>Because force varies along the tube, only the maximum radius (the pellet end) is used for pelleting steps; for gradients, use the average radius.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Measuring the radius from the rotor edge instead of the spindle axis.</li><li>Copying rpm from a protocol run on a different rotor.</li><li>Assuming the same rpm always means the same force across a swinging-bucket and a fixed-angle rotor.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/hemocytometer-cell-counter\\\">Hemocytometer counting</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\\\">Osmolarity</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/absorbance-transmittance\\\">Absorbance and transmittance</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I convert 10 000 × g to rpm?\",\"a\":\"rpm = √(RCF ÷ (1.118 × 10⁻⁵ × r)). For an 8.5 cm radius, about 10 260 rpm.\"},{\"q\":\"Is RCF the same as g?\",\"a\":\"RCF is the force expressed as multiples of Earth\'s gravity, so 10 000 × g means ten thousand times gravity.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns relative centrifugal force, speed for 10 000 × g and distance travelled per minute. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"radius\",\"label\":\"Rotor radius\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"8.5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Measure to the bottom of the tube, not the top\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rpm\",\"label\":\"Speed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"4000\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"rpm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"rcf\",\"label\":\"Relative centrifugal force\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"× g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rpm_needed\",\"label\":\"Speed for 10 000 × g\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"rpm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_minute\",\"label\":\"Distance travelled per minute\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"km\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Centrifuge\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"rcf\",\"f\":\"1.118*pow(10,-5)*radius*rpm*rpm\"},{\"l\":\"rpm_needed\",\"f\":\"sqrt(10000/(1.118*pow(10,-5)*radius))\"},{\"l\":\"per_minute\",\"f\":\"2*pi*radius/100000*rpm\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/centrifuge-rcf-rpm\",\"slug\":\"centrifuge-rcf-rpm\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter\",\"title\":\"Centrifuge RCF to RPM Converter Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"RCF and RPM are the same centrifuge force in different units: RPM depends on the rotor, RCF does not. Free centrifuge rcf to rpm converter calculator: enter y\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-centrifuge-rcf-rpm\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"radius\",\"l\":\"Rotor radius\",\"h\":\"Measure to the bottom of the tube, not the top\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rpm\",\"l\":\"Speed\",\"suf\":\"rpm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rcf\",\"l\":\"Relative centrifugal force\",\"suf\":\"× g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rpm_needed\",\"l\":\"Speed for 10 000 × g\",\"suf\":\"rpm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_minute\",\"l\":\"Distance travelled per minute\",\"suf\":\"km\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"radius\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"8.5\",\"c\":null}]},\"rpm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"4000\",\"c\":null}]},\"rcf\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rpm_needed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_minute\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Centrifuge\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"rcf\",\"f\":\"1.118*pow(10,-5)*radius*rpm*rpm\",\"r\":[\"radius\",\"rpm\"]},{\"l\":\"rpm_needed\",\"f\":\"sqrt(10000/(1.118*pow(10,-5)*radius))\",\"r\":[\"radius\"]},{\"l\":\"per_minute\",\"f\":\"2*pi*radius/100000*rpm\",\"r\":[\"radius\",\"rpm\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'orbit', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(150, 'aquarium-water-volume', 'biology', 'Aquarium Water Volume Calculator', 'Aquarium Water Volume Calculator', 'Aquarium Water Volume Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Tank capacity is quoted as a nominal volume, but the water you actually hold is less once glass, substrate and decoration are accounted for. Free aquarium wat', 'Tank capacity is quoted as a nominal volume, but the water you actually hold is less once glass, substrate and decoration are accounted for. It returns gross volume, true water volume, true volume and weight of the water, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator returns gross and true volume, the weight of the water and the heater and filter turnover the tank needs. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Aquarium Water Volume\",\"html\":\"<p>Volume (L) = length × width × height ÷ 1 000;  subtract length × width × substrate depth</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/aquarium-water-volume.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Aquarium Water Volume Calculator — Length, width, height — then subtract what the gravel takes.\\\" title=\\\"Aquarium Water Volume Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Length, width, height — then subtract what the gravel takes.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>L, W, H</code></td><td>internal dimensions</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>substrate</code></td><td>gravel or sand depth</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>1 L</code></td><td>1 000 cm³ of water, 1 kg</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Aquarium Water Volume?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: shape set to <strong>1.0</strong>, length of <strong>90</strong> cm, width or diameter of <strong>45</strong> cm, water height of <strong>45</strong> cm, substrate depth of <strong>5</strong> cm.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives gross volume of <strong>182.25</strong> L, true water volume of <strong>162</strong> L, true volume of <strong>42.8</strong> US gal, weight of the water of <strong>162</strong> kg, filter turnover needed (5x per hour) of <strong>810</strong> L/h.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 20 L</td><td><strong>Nano</strong></td><td>very little buffering; small changes matter</td></tr><tr><td>20 – 60 L</td><td><strong>Small</strong></td><td>easy to maintain, few fish</td></tr><tr><td>60 – 200 L</td><td><strong>Community</strong></td><td>stable, forgiving of mistakes</td></tr><tr><td>above 200 L</td><td><strong>Large</strong></td><td>stable but heavy: check the floor loading</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Tanks are sold by external dimensions. A \'90 cm\' tank usually holds about 90 cm internally, but the glass thickness and the fill line cost another few litres.</p></li><li><p>Water weighs one kilogram per litre, so a 200 L tank is a quarter of a tonne before glass, substrate and cabinet — worth checking against a floor joist span.</p></li><li><p>The old rule of five turnovers per hour suits most freshwater community tanks; marine and cichlid setups want ten or more.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Using external dimensions and filling to the brim in the calculation.</li><li>Forgetting that substrate and rock displace water, which is most of the error in a heavily decorated tank.</li><li>Treating US gallons and imperial gallons as the same — they differ by 20%.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/fish-tank-stocking\\\">Fish tank stocking</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/ideal-fish-tank-size\\\">Ideal tank size</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/fish-per-liter\\\">Fish per litre</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many litres is a 90 × 45 × 45 cm tank?\",\"a\":\"182 L gross, and about 162 L with 5 cm of substrate.\"},{\"q\":\"How much does a 200 L tank weigh?\",\"a\":\"About 200 kg of water, plus 40–60 kg of glass and stand, plus substrate.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Aquarium Water Volume actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns gross volume, true water volume, true volume and weight of the water. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"shape\",\"label\":\"Shape\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"rectangular\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"rectangular\",\"Rectangular\",\"1\"],[\"bow-front\",\"Bow-front\",\"0.85\"],[\"cylinder\",\"Cylinder\",\"0.785\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"90\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"width\",\"label\":\"Width or diameter\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"45\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"height\",\"label\":\"Water height\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"45\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"substrate\",\"label\":\"Substrate depth\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"5\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Displaces water and must be subtracted\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"litres\",\"label\":\"Gross volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"net\",\"label\":\"True water volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gallons\",\"label\":\"True volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"US gal\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"weight\",\"label\":\"Weight of the water\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"turnover\",\"label\":\"Filter turnover needed (5x per hour)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L/h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Tank\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"litres\",\"f\":\"length*width*height*shape/1000\"},{\"l\":\"net\",\"f\":\"length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000\"},{\"l\":\"gallons\",\"f\":\"(length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000)/3.785\"},{\"l\":\"weight\",\"f\":\"length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000\"},{\"l\":\"turnover\",\"f\":\"(length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000)*5\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/aquarium-water-volume\",\"slug\":\"aquarium-water-volume\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Aquarium Water Volume Calculator\",\"title\":\"Aquarium Water Volume Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Tank capacity is quoted as a nominal volume, but the water you actually hold is less once glass, substrate and decoration are accounted for. Free aquarium wat\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-aquarium-water-volume\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"shape\",\"l\":\"Shape\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-shape\",\"opts\":[[\"rectangular\",\"Rectangular\",\"1\"],[\"bow-front\",\"Bow-front\",\"0.85\"],[\"cylinder\",\"Cylinder\",\"0.785\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Length\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"width\",\"l\":\"Width or diameter\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"height\",\"l\":\"Water height\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"substrate\",\"l\":\"Substrate depth\",\"h\":\"Displaces water and must be subtracted\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"litres\",\"l\":\"Gross volume\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"net\",\"l\":\"True water volume\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gallons\",\"l\":\"True volume\",\"suf\":\"US gal\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"weight\",\"l\":\"Weight of the water\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"turnover\",\"l\":\"Filter turnover needed (5x per hour)\",\"suf\":\"L/h\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"shape\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"rectangular\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-shape\"},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"90\",\"c\":null}]},\"width\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"45\",\"c\":null}]},\"height\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"45\",\"c\":null}]},\"substrate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"5\",\"c\":null}]},\"litres\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"net\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gallons\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"weight\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"turnover\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-shape\":{\"name\":\"Shape\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"rectangular\",\"label\":\"Rectangular\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"bow-front\",\"label\":\"Bow-front\",\"value\":\"0.85\"},{\"uid\":\"cylinder\",\"label\":\"Cylinder\",\"value\":\"0.785\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Tank\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"litres\",\"f\":\"length*width*height*shape/1000\",\"r\":[\"length\",\"width\",\"height\",\"shape\"]},{\"l\":\"net\",\"f\":\"length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000\",\"r\":[\"length\",\"width\",\"height\",\"shape\",\"substrate\"]},{\"l\":\"gallons\",\"f\":\"(length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000)/3.785\",\"r\":[\"length\",\"width\",\"height\",\"shape\",\"substrate\"]},{\"l\":\"weight\",\"f\":\"length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000\",\"r\":[\"length\",\"width\",\"height\",\"shape\",\"substrate\"]},{\"l\":\"turnover\",\"f\":\"(length*width*height*shape/1000-length*width*substrate/1000)*5\",\"r\":[\"length\",\"width\",\"height\",\"shape\",\"substrate\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'fish', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(151, 'fish-tank-stocking', 'biology', 'Fish Tank Stocking Calculator', 'Fish Tank Stocking Calculator', 'Fish Tank Stocking Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Stocking is limited by filter capacity and by the space adult fish need, and both are usually miscalculated because people stock for the size the fish are now', 'Stocking is limited by filter capacity and by the space adult fish need, and both are usually miscalculated because people stock for the size the fish are now. It returns stocking capacity, stocking level, adult biomass and turnovers per hour, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator gives the inch-per-gallon and surface-area limits, the biomass the filter must process and how many fish of each adult size fit. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Fish Tank Stocking\",\"html\":\"<p>Capacity (cm of adult fish) = volume (L) × turnover factor;  biomass ≈ count × (length/10)³ × 10 g</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/fish-tank-stocking.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Fish Tank Stocking Calculator — Length is not mass: the biomass curve grows as a cube.\\\" title=\\\"Fish Tank Stocking Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Length is not mass: the biomass curve grows as a cube.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>V</code></td><td>true water volume</td><td>L</td></tr><tr><td><code>L</code></td><td>adult body length of the species</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>factor</code></td><td>cm of fish per litre</td><td>cm/L</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Fish Tank Stocking?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: true water volume of <strong>160</strong> L, adult length of the species of <strong>6</strong> cm, fish planned of <strong>20</strong>, filter turnover of <strong>800</strong> L/h, stocking factor of <strong>1</strong> cm per L.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives stocking capacity of <strong>160</strong> cm, stocking level of <strong>75</strong>%, adult biomass of <strong>43.2</strong> g, turnovers per hour of <strong>5</strong> per hour, fish of this size that fit of <strong>26.67</strong> fish.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 60%</td><td><strong>Understocked</strong></td><td>safe, and the filter has headroom</td></tr><tr><td>60 – 90%</td><td><strong>Well stocked</strong></td><td>the target for a display tank</td></tr><tr><td>90 – 110%</td><td><strong>At the limit</strong></td><td>only with strong filtration and weekly changes</td></tr><tr><td>above 110%</td><td><strong>Overstocked</strong></td><td>ammonia spikes, disease and stunted growth</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The old inch-per-gallon rule is a volume proxy for oxygen demand and waste, which scale with mass, not length. It fails badly for deep-bodied fish, so the biomass line here is the more honest limit.</p></li><li><p>Freshwater community tanks: about 1 cm of adult fish per litre of net water; cichlids and goldfish need double the volume because they are taller and messier.</p></li><li><p>Filter turnover of five times the tank volume per hour is the usual starting point; the fish load is what actually decides whether it is enough.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Stocking by the size of the juveniles you bought.</li><li>Ignoring that fish grow: 20 small tetras and 20 silver dollars are not the same tank.</li><li>Adding all the fish at once, which overwhelms the bacteria before they multiply.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/aquarium-water-volume\\\">Aquarium water volume</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/ideal-fish-tank-size\\\">Ideal tank size</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/fish-per-liter\\\">Fish per litre</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How many fish can I keep in 100 L?\",\"a\":\"Roughly 100 cm of adult fish at 1 cm per litre — so about 16 fish of 6 cm, or 8 of 12 cm.\"},{\"q\":\"Does more filtration allow more fish?\",\"a\":\"It buys some headroom, but oxygen and swimming space set a hard ceiling.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Fish Tank Stocking actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns stocking capacity, stocking level, adult biomass and turnovers per hour. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"True water volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"160\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Adult length of the species\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Not the juvenile size\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"fish_count\",\"label\":\"Fish planned\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"20\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"filter\",\"label\":\"Filter turnover\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"800\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L/h\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"factor\",\"label\":\"Stocking factor\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.6 for goldfish, 0.8 for cichlids\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm per L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"capacity\",\"label\":\"Stocking capacity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"used\",\"label\":\"Stocking level\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"biomass\",\"label\":\"Adult biomass\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"g\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"turnover\",\"label\":\"Turnovers per hour\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per hour\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"max_fish\",\"label\":\"Fish of this size that fit\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"fish\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Stocking\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"capacity\",\"f\":\"volume*factor\"},{\"l\":\"used\",\"f\":\"fish_count*length/max(0.001,volume*factor)*100\"},{\"l\":\"biomass\",\"f\":\"fish_count*pow(length,3)/1000*10\"},{\"l\":\"turnover\",\"f\":\"filter/volume\"},{\"l\":\"max_fish\",\"f\":\"volume*factor/length\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/fish-tank-stocking\",\"slug\":\"fish-tank-stocking\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Fish Tank Stocking Calculator\",\"title\":\"Fish Tank Stocking Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Stocking is limited by filter capacity and by the space adult fish need, and both are usually miscalculated because people stock for the size the fish are now\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-fish-tank-stocking\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"True water volume\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Adult length of the species\",\"h\":\"Not the juvenile size\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fish_count\",\"l\":\"Fish planned\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"filter\",\"l\":\"Filter turnover\",\"suf\":\"L/h\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"factor\",\"l\":\"Stocking factor\",\"h\":\"0.6 for goldfish, 0.8 for cichlids\",\"suf\":\"cm per L\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"capacity\",\"l\":\"Stocking capacity\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"used\",\"l\":\"Stocking level\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"biomass\",\"l\":\"Adult biomass\",\"suf\":\"g\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"turnover\",\"l\":\"Turnovers per hour\",\"suf\":\"per hour\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"max_fish\",\"l\":\"Fish of this size that fit\",\"suf\":\"fish\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"160\",\"c\":null}]},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"fish_count\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"20\",\"c\":null}]},\"filter\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"800\",\"c\":null}]},\"factor\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.0\",\"c\":null}]},\"capacity\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"used\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"biomass\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"turnover\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"max_fish\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Stocking\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"capacity\",\"f\":\"volume*factor\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"factor\"]},{\"l\":\"used\",\"f\":\"fish_count*length/max(0.001,volume*factor)*100\",\"r\":[\"fish_count\",\"length\",\"volume\",\"factor\"]},{\"l\":\"biomass\",\"f\":\"fish_count*pow(length,3)/1000*10\",\"r\":[\"fish_count\",\"length\"]},{\"l\":\"turnover\",\"f\":\"filter/volume\",\"r\":[\"filter\",\"volume\"]},{\"l\":\"max_fish\",\"f\":\"volume*factor/length\",\"r\":[\"volume\",\"factor\",\"length\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'fish', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(152, 'ideal-fish-tank-size', 'biology', 'Ideal Fish Tank Size Calculator', 'Ideal Fish Tank Size Calculator', 'Ideal Fish Tank Size Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The right tank is set by the largest adult fish, because a fish needs a length to swim and a volume for its waste. Free ideal fish tank size calculator: enter', 'The right tank is set by the largest adult fish, because a fish needs a length to swim and a volume for its waste. It returns recommended volume, minimum tank length, minimum tank depth and recommended volume, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator turns your species list into a recommended volume, footprint and minimum dimension, and flags when the plan is too small. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Ideal Fish Tank Size\",\"html\":\"<p>Minimum length = 8 × adult body length;  minimum depth = 3 × body length;  volume grows with the square of length times the school size</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/ideal-fish-tank-size.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Ideal Fish Tank Size Calculator — Eight lengths long, three deep — the swimming rule.\\\" title=\\\"Ideal Fish Tank Size Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Eight lengths long, three deep — the swimming rule.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>L</code></td><td>adult body length of the largest fish</td><td>cm</td></tr><tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>number of fish in the school</td><td>fish</td></tr><tr><td><code>temperament</code></td><td>multiplier for aggression</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Ideal Fish Tank Size?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: adult length of the largest fish of <strong>12</strong> cm, number in the school of <strong>6</strong>, temperament set to <strong>1.0</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives recommended volume of <strong>123.84</strong> L, minimum tank length of <strong>96</strong> cm, minimum tank depth of <strong>36</strong> cm, recommended volume of <strong>32.72</strong> US gal.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8 × body length</td><td><strong>Minimum tank length</strong></td><td>three body lengths to accelerate, five to cruise, eight to feel safe</td></tr><tr><td>3 × body length</td><td><strong>Minimum depth</strong></td><td>room to turn without touching the glass</td></tr><tr><td>6 × body length</td><td><strong>Comfortable</strong></td><td>what a permanent home should really be</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Swimming length matters more than volume for active species: a 30 cm fish needs a tank at least 240 cm long, whatever the litres say.</p></li><li><p>Territorial species need footprint, not depth. A tall narrow tank gives litres without giving the territory a cichlid actually defends.</p></li><li><p>Volume scales with the cube of linear size, so doubling a fish\'s length multiplies its waste output by about eight.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Buying by litres alone and ending up with a tall, short tank.</li><li>Sizing for juveniles and then discovering the fish triples in length.</li><li>Ignoring the school size: many \'peaceful\' species are only peaceful in groups.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/aquarium-water-volume\\\">Aquarium water volume</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/fish-tank-stocking\\\">Fish tank stocking</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/fish-per-liter\\\">Fish per litre</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What size tank does a 12 cm fish need?\",\"a\":\"At least 96 cm long and 36 cm deep, which in practice means a 120 cm tank.\"},{\"q\":\"Do goldfish need a big tank?\",\"a\":\"Yes — they reach 25–30 cm, so 120 cm and 200 L or more, not the bowl they are sold with.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Ideal Fish Tank Size actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns recommended volume, minimum tank length, minimum tank depth and recommended volume. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"fish_length\",\"label\":\"Adult length of the largest fish\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"12\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"school\",\"label\":\"Number in the school\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"temperament\",\"label\":\"Temperament\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"peaceful-community\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"peaceful-community\",\"Peaceful community\",\"1\"],[\"semi-aggressive\",\"Semi-aggressive\",\"1.4\"],[\"territorial\",\"Territorial\",\"1.8\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"min_volume\",\"label\":\"Recommended volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"L\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"min_length\",\"label\":\"Minimum tank length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"min_depth\",\"label\":\"Minimum tank depth\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"cm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gallons\",\"label\":\"Recommended volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"US gal\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Sizing\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"min_volume\",\"f\":\"max(20,fish_length*fish_length*school*0.6*temperament/10+fish_length*school)\"},{\"l\":\"min_length\",\"f\":\"max(45,fish_length*8)\"},{\"l\":\"min_depth\",\"f\":\"max(30,fish_length*3)\"},{\"l\":\"gallons\",\"f\":\"max(20,fish_length*fish_length*school*0.6*temperament/10+fish_length*school)/3.785\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/ideal-fish-tank-size\",\"slug\":\"ideal-fish-tank-size\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Ideal Fish Tank Size Calculator\",\"title\":\"Ideal Fish Tank Size Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The right tank is set by the largest adult fish, because a fish needs a length to swim and a volume for its waste. Free ideal fish tank size calculator: enter\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-ideal-fish-tank-size\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fish_length\",\"l\":\"Adult length of the largest fish\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"school\",\"l\":\"Number in the school\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"temperament\",\"l\":\"Temperament\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-temperament\",\"opts\":[[\"peaceful-community\",\"Peaceful community\",\"1\"],[\"semi-aggressive\",\"Semi-aggressive\",\"1.4\"],[\"territorial\",\"Territorial\",\"1.8\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"min_volume\",\"l\":\"Recommended volume\",\"suf\":\"L\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"min_length\",\"l\":\"Minimum tank length\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"min_depth\",\"l\":\"Minimum tank depth\",\"suf\":\"cm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gallons\",\"l\":\"Recommended volume\",\"suf\":\"US gal\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"fish_length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"12\",\"c\":null}]},\"school\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"6\",\"c\":null}]},\"temperament\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"peaceful-community\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-temperament\"},\"min_volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"min_length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"min_depth\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gallons\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-temperament\":{\"name\":\"Temperament\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"peaceful-community\",\"label\":\"Peaceful community\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"semi-aggressive\",\"label\":\"Semi-aggressive\",\"value\":\"1.4\"},{\"uid\":\"territorial\",\"label\":\"Territorial\",\"value\":\"1.8\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Sizing\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"min_volume\",\"f\":\"max(20,fish_length*fish_length*school*0.6*temperament/10+fish_length*school)\",\"r\":[\"fish_length\",\"school\",\"temperament\"]},{\"l\":\"min_length\",\"f\":\"max(45,fish_length*8)\",\"r\":[\"fish_length\"]},{\"l\":\"min_depth\",\"f\":\"max(30,fish_length*3)\",\"r\":[\"fish_length\"]},{\"l\":\"gallons\",\"f\":\"max(20,fish_length*fish_length*school*0.6*temperament/10+fish_length*school)/3.785\",\"r\":[\"fish_length\",\"school\",\"temperament\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'fish', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(153, 'mushroom-yield', 'biology', 'Mushroom Yield Calculator', 'Mushroom Yield Calculator', 'Mushroom Yield Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Mushroom yield is reported as biological efficiency: kilograms of fresh mushrooms per 100 kg of dry substrate. Free mushroom yield calculator: enter your valu', 'Mushroom yield is reported as biological efficiency: kilograms of fresh mushrooms per 100 kg of dry substrate. It returns biological efficiency, wet substrate weight, yield per block and blocks for a 100 kg harvest, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator converts your harvest and substrate weights into BE, the yield per block and the number of blocks a target harvest needs. Press Calculate and the results appear in the grey boxes; after the first press they keep up with every edit, so there is no page to reload. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Mushroom Yield\",\"html\":\"<p>BE (%) = fresh mushroom weight ÷ dry substrate weight × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/mushroom-yield.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Mushroom Yield Calculator — Fresh harvest against dry substrate: the ratio is the score.\\\" title=\\\"Mushroom Yield Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Fresh harvest against dry substrate: the ratio is the score.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>fresh</code></td><td>fresh mushrooms harvested</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>dry substrate</code></td><td>substrate dry matter at spawning</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><code>BE</code></td><td>biological efficiency</td><td>%</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Mushroom Yield?\",\"html\":\"<p>Every input sits in the same order the formula is written, so the page reads like the equation.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: fresh mushrooms harvested of <strong>18</strong> kg, substrate dry weight of <strong>10</strong> kg, substrate moisture of <strong>65</strong>%, number of blocks of <strong>10</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives biological efficiency of <strong>180</strong>%, wet substrate weight of <strong>28.57</strong> kg, yield per block of <strong>1.8</strong> kg, blocks for a 100 kg harvest of <strong>55.56</strong> blocks.</p>\\n<p>Those numbers come from the same solver the page runs in your browser, not from an illustration, so typing different inputs moves the answer exactly as the arithmetic says.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>The number on its own is not a judgement. These are the bands most often used when the result is quoted in a paper, a report or a label:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 40%</td><td><strong>Poor</strong></td><td>contamination, wrong moisture or bad spawn run</td></tr><tr><td>40 – 70%</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td>typical of a first flush only</td></tr><tr><td>70 – 100%</td><td><strong>Good</strong></td><td>normal for oyster mushrooms over three flushes</td></tr><tr><td>above 100%</td><td><strong>Excellent</strong></td><td>achievable because the mushroom is mostly water</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>BE above 100% is possible and normal: mushrooms are about 90% water, so the fresh weight can exceed the dry substrate they grew on.</p></li><li><p>Always state whether the substrate weight is wet or dry. A 65% moisture block weighs nearly three times its dry matter, so the two numbers are not comparable.</p></li><li><p>Yield is counted over all flushes. Quoting the first flush only flatters the strain.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>These are the errors that account for almost every wrong answer people bring to this calculator:</p><ul><li>Using wet substrate weight in the denominator, which halves the apparent BE.</li><li>Harvesting after the veil breaks — heavier, but a lower grade and a shorter shelf life.</li><li>Comparing species: shiitake and oyster mushrooms have very different ceilings.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>This tool answers one step of the question. The neighbouring steps have their own pages:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/seeding-rate\\\">Seeding rate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/percent-germination\\\">Percent germination</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dry-matter-intake\\\">Dry matter intake</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good biological efficiency for oyster mushrooms?\",\"a\":\"70–100% over three flushes on straw or sawdust blocks.\"},{\"q\":\"How much substrate do I need for 100 kg of mushrooms?\",\"a\":\"At 80% BE, about 125 kg of dry substrate — roughly 360 kg of wet blocks.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Mushroom Yield actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns biological efficiency, wet substrate weight, yield per block and blocks for a 100 kg harvest. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"fresh\",\"label\":\"Fresh mushrooms harvested\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"18\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"substrate\",\"label\":\"Substrate dry weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Dry weight, not wet\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"moisture\",\"label\":\"Substrate moisture\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"65\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Wet weight = dry ÷ (1 − moisture)\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"blocks\",\"label\":\"Number of blocks\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"10\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"be\",\"label\":\"Biological efficiency\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"wet_weight\",\"label\":\"Wet substrate weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"per_block\",\"label\":\"Yield per block\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kg\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"blocks_needed\",\"label\":\"Blocks for a 100 kg harvest\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"blocks\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Yield\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"be\",\"f\":\"fresh/substrate*100\"},{\"l\":\"wet_weight\",\"f\":\"substrate/(1-moisture/100)\"},{\"l\":\"per_block\",\"f\":\"fresh/blocks\"},{\"l\":\"blocks_needed\",\"f\":\"100/(fresh/blocks)\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/mushroom-yield\",\"slug\":\"mushroom-yield\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Mushroom Yield Calculator\",\"title\":\"Mushroom Yield Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Mushroom yield is reported as biological efficiency: kilograms of fresh mushrooms per 100 kg of dry substrate. Free mushroom yield calculator: enter your valu\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-mushroom-yield\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"fresh\",\"l\":\"Fresh mushrooms harvested\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"substrate\",\"l\":\"Substrate dry weight\",\"h\":\"Dry weight, not wet\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"moisture\",\"l\":\"Substrate moisture\",\"h\":\"Wet weight = dry ÷ (1 − moisture)\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"blocks\",\"l\":\"Number of blocks\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"be\",\"l\":\"Biological efficiency\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"wet_weight\",\"l\":\"Wet substrate weight\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"per_block\",\"l\":\"Yield per block\",\"suf\":\"kg\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"blocks_needed\",\"l\":\"Blocks for a 100 kg harvest\",\"suf\":\"blocks\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"fresh\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"18\",\"c\":null}]},\"substrate\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"moisture\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"65\",\"c\":null}]},\"blocks\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"10\",\"c\":null}]},\"be\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"wet_weight\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"per_block\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"blocks_needed\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Yield\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"be\",\"f\":\"fresh/substrate*100\",\"r\":[\"fresh\",\"substrate\"]},{\"l\":\"wet_weight\",\"f\":\"substrate/(1-moisture/100)\",\"r\":[\"substrate\",\"moisture\"]},{\"l\":\"per_block\",\"f\":\"fresh/blocks\",\"r\":[\"fresh\",\"blocks\"]},{\"l\":\"blocks_needed\",\"f\":\"100/(fresh/blocks)\",\"r\":[\"fresh\",\"blocks\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'leaf', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(154, 'cell-surface-area-volume-ratio', 'biology', 'Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio Calculator', 'Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio Calculator', 'Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio Calculator — Formula & Resul', 'A cell\'s surface area to volume ratio sets how fast it can exchange materials, and it falls as the cell grows — which is why cells divide instead of getting b', 'A cell\'s surface area to volume ratio sets how fast it can exchange materials, and it falls as the cell grows — which is why cells divide instead of getting bigger. It returns surface area, volume, surface area to volume and total uptake capacity, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator returns the ratio for spheres, rods and cubes, and the size at which diffusion becomes limiting. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio\",\"html\":\"<p>Sphere: A = 4πr², V = 4⁄3πr³, A/V = 3/r;  cube: A/V = 6/s</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cell-surface-area-volume-ratio.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio Calculator — The ratio falls as 1/size — the geometric reason cells divide.\\\" title=\\\"Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio Calculator\\\"><figcaption>The ratio falls as 1/size — the geometric reason cells divide.</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>radius</td><td>µm</td></tr><tr><td><code>s</code></td><td>side length</td><td>µm</td></tr><tr><td><code>A/V</code></td><td>surface area to volume ratio</td><td>per µm</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: shape set to <strong>1.0</strong>, size (diameter or length) of <strong>2</strong> µm, surface flux of <strong>1</strong> µmol/m²/s.</p>\\n<p>Substituting them into the formula gives surface area of <strong>12.57</strong> µm², volume of <strong>4.19</strong> µm³, surface area to volume of <strong>3</strong> per µm, total uptake capacity of <strong>12.57</strong> µmol/s.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6 per µm</td><td><strong>1 µm sphere</strong></td><td>enormous exchange capacity</td></tr><tr><td>3 per µm</td><td><strong>2 µm sphere</strong></td><td>typical bacterium</td></tr><tr><td>0.6 per µm</td><td><strong>10 µm sphere</strong></td><td>typical eukaryotic cell</td></tr><tr><td>below 0.2 per µm</td><td><strong>Very large</strong></td><td>diffusion alone cannot feed the cell</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>For a sphere the ratio is exactly 3/r, so a cell that doubles its diameter halves its exchange capacity per unit volume — the reason growth triggers division.</p></li><li><p>Rod shapes keep a higher ratio than spheres at the same volume, which is one explanation for the success of bacilli in nutrient-poor environments.</p></li><li><p>Above roughly 1 mm, diffusion cannot supply oxygen to the centre; that is when organisms need a circulatory system.</p></li><li><p>Real cells are not smooth spheres: microvilli and cristae raise the effective area several-fold, and this calculation is the lower bound.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Using the diameter where the formula needs the radius.</li><li>Comparing a rod\'s ratio with a sphere\'s at the same \'size\' — the two are different lengths.</li><li>Forgetting that units matter: a ratio in per micrometre is a thousand times one in per millimetre.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/cell-density\\\">Cell density</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/bacterial-growth-curve\\\">Growth curve</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/osmolarity-osmotic-pressure\\\">Osmolarity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Why do cells stay small?\",\"a\":\"Because the surface to volume ratio falls as size rises, and beyond a point the membrane cannot import nutrients or export waste fast enough.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the A/V ratio of a 2 µm coccus?\",\"a\":\"3/(1 µm) = 3 µm⁻¹.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns surface area, volume, surface area to volume and total uptake capacity. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"shape\",\"label\":\"Shape\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"sphere-coccus\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"sphere-coccus\",\"Sphere (coccus)\",\"1\"],[\"rod-2-1-bacillus\",\"Rod, 2:1 (bacillus)\",\"2\"],[\"cube\",\"Cube\",\"3\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"size\",\"label\":\"Size (diameter or length)\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"2\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"flux\",\"label\":\"Surface flux\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"1.0\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Substrate uptake per area\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol/m²/s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"area\",\"label\":\"Surface area\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µm²\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"volume\",\"label\":\"Volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µm³\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"ratio\",\"label\":\"Surface area to volume\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"per µm\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"uptake\",\"label\":\"Total uptake capacity\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"µmol/s\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[{\"formula\":\"Geometry\",\"directions\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4*pi*pow(size/2,2),if_else(shape==2,2*pi*pow(size/2,2)+2*pi*size/2*size*2,6*size*size))\"},{\"l\":\"volume\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4/3*pi*pow(size/2,3),if_else(shape==2,pi*pow(size/2,2)*size*2,pow(size,3)))\"},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4*pi*pow(size/2,2),if_else(shape==2,2*pi*pow(size/2,2)+2*pi*size/2*size*2,6*size*size))/if_else(shape==1,4/3*pi*pow(size/2,3),if_else(shape==2,pi*pow(size/2,2)*size*2,pow(size,3)))\"},{\"l\":\"uptake\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4*pi*pow(size/2,2),if_else(shape==2,2*pi*pow(size/2,2)+2*pi*size/2*size*2,6*size*size))*flux\"}]}]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cell-surface-area-volume-ratio\",\"slug\":\"cell-surface-area-volume-ratio\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio Calculator\",\"title\":\"Cell Surface Area to Volume Ratio Calculator — Formula & Resul\",\"desc\":\"A cell\'s surface area to volume ratio sets how fast it can exchange materials, and it falls as the cell grows — which is why cells divide instead of getting b\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cell-surface-area-volume-ratio\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"shape\",\"l\":\"Shape\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-shape\",\"opts\":[[\"sphere-coccus\",\"Sphere (coccus)\",\"1\"],[\"rod-2-1-bacillus\",\"Rod, 2:1 (bacillus)\",\"2\"],[\"cube\",\"Cube\",\"3\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"size\",\"l\":\"Size (diameter or length)\",\"suf\":\"µm\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"flux\",\"l\":\"Surface flux\",\"h\":\"Substrate uptake per area\",\"suf\":\"µmol/m²/s\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"area\",\"l\":\"Surface area\",\"suf\":\"µm²\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"volume\",\"l\":\"Volume\",\"suf\":\"µm³\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"ratio\",\"l\":\"Surface area to volume\",\"suf\":\"per µm\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"uptake\",\"l\":\"Total uptake capacity\",\"suf\":\"µmol/s\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"shape\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"sphere-coccus\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-shape\"},\"size\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"2\",\"c\":null}]},\"flux\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"1.0\",\"c\":null}]},\"area\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"volume\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"ratio\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"uptake\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-shape\":{\"name\":\"Shape\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"sphere-coccus\",\"label\":\"Sphere (coccus)\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"rod-2-1-bacillus\",\"label\":\"Rod, 2:1 (bacillus)\",\"value\":\"2\"},{\"uid\":\"cube\",\"label\":\"Cube\",\"value\":\"3\"}]}},\"eq\":[{\"formula\":\"Geometry\",\"var\":[{\"l\":\"area\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4*pi*pow(size/2,2),if_else(shape==2,2*pi*pow(size/2,2)+2*pi*size/2*size*2,6*size*size))\",\"r\":[\"shape\",\"size\"]},{\"l\":\"volume\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4/3*pi*pow(size/2,3),if_else(shape==2,pi*pow(size/2,2)*size*2,pow(size,3)))\",\"r\":[\"shape\",\"size\"]},{\"l\":\"ratio\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4*pi*pow(size/2,2),if_else(shape==2,2*pi*pow(size/2,2)+2*pi*size/2*size*2,6*size*size))/if_else(shape==1,4/3*pi*pow(size/2,3),if_else(shape==2,pi*pow(size/2,2)*size*2,pow(size,3)))\",\"r\":[\"shape\",\"size\"]},{\"l\":\"uptake\",\"f\":\"if_else(shape==1,4*pi*pow(size/2,2),if_else(shape==2,2*pi*pow(size/2,2)+2*pi*size/2*size*2,6*size*size))*flux\",\"r\":[\"shape\",\"size\",\"flux\"]}]}],\"js\":null,\"needsJs\":false}', 'cell', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, NULL, NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(155, 'reverse-complement', 'biology', 'Reverse Complement Calculator', 'Reverse Complement Calculator', 'Reverse Complement Calculator — Formula & Results', 'The reverse complement is the other strand of a DNA molecule written 5′ to 3′: read the sequence backwards and swap every base for its partner (A↔T, G↔C). Fre', 'The reverse complement is the other strand of a DNA molecule written 5′ to 3′: read the sequence backwards and swap every base for its partner (A↔T, G↔C). It returns length, gc content and melting temperature of the duplex, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is the first step of primer design, of checking that a sequencing read maps to the strand you expect, and of turning a gene\'s coding sequence into the template you will actually order. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Reverse Complement\",\"html\":\"<p>Reverse complement: read 3′→5′ and pair A·T, G·C.  GC% = (G + C) ÷ length × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/reverse-complement.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Reverse Complement Calculator — Hydrogen bonds per base pair\\\" title=\\\"Reverse Complement Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Hydrogen bonds per base pair</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>5′</code></td><td>the end with a free phosphate</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>3′</code></td><td>the end with a free hydroxyl</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>revcomp</code></td><td>the opposite strand, written 5′→3′</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Reverse Complement?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: base pairs per line of <strong>60</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A ↔ T</td><td><strong>DNA pairing</strong></td><td>two hydrogen bonds</td></tr><tr><td>G ↔ C</td><td><strong>DNA pairing</strong></td><td>three hydrogen bonds, which is why GC-rich duplexes melt higher</td></tr><tr><td>A ↔ U</td><td><strong>RNA pairing</strong></td><td>RNA has uracil where DNA has thymine</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>A reverse complement is not a palindrome: the reverse complement of ATGC is GCAT, and the reverse alone would be CGTA.</p></li><li><p>Ambiguity codes are handled properly — R (A or G) pairs with Y (C or T), S with S, W with W, K with M, B with V, D with H, and N with N.</p></li><li><p>The translation tools on this site take the reverse complement as input when you need the protein from the opposite strand.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Reversing without complementing (or the other way round) — both are common and both give a sequence that looks plausible.</li><li>Forgetting that a primer is written 5′→3′ and its template binding site is the reverse complement of the primer.</li><li>Pasting an alignment with gaps into a tool that does not strip them.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-calculator\\\">GC content</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\\\">Oligo Tm</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna\\\">Coding capacity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is the reverse complement of ATGGCC?\",\"a\":\"GGCCAT — read backwards (CCGGTA) and swap each base for its partner.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the reverse complement used instead of the reverse?\",\"a\":\"Because DNA is antiparallel: the other strand runs in the opposite direction, so its sequence must be both reversed and complemented to be written 5′→3′.\"},{\"q\":\"How do I get the protein from the opposite strand?\",\"a\":\"Take the reverse complement, then translate it in frame 1.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Reverse Complement actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns length, gc content and melting temperature of the duplex. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sequence\",\"label\":\"Sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAG\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Paste DNA; spaces, line breaks and FASTA headers are ignored\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"alphabet\",\"label\":\"Alphabet\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"dna-a-c-g-t\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"dna-a-c-g-t\",\"DNA (A, C, G, T)\",\"dna\"],[\"rna-a-c-g-u\",\"RNA (A, C, G, U)\",\"rna\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"width\",\"label\":\"Base pairs per line\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"60\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0 for one line\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gc\",\"label\":\"GC content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tm\",\"label\":\"Melting temperature of the duplex\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Wallace under 14 nt, GC rule to 40 nt, long-duplex form above\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/reverse-complement\",\"slug\":\"reverse-complement\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Reverse Complement Calculator\",\"title\":\"Reverse Complement Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"The reverse complement is the other strand of a DNA molecule written 5′ to 3′: read the sequence backwards and swap every base for its partner (A↔T, G↔C). Fre\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-reverse-complement\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sequence\",\"l\":\"Sequence\",\"h\":\"Paste DNA; spaces, line breaks and FASTA headers are ignored\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"alphabet\",\"l\":\"Alphabet\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-alphabet\",\"opts\":[[\"dna-a-c-g-t\",\"DNA (A, C, G, T)\",\"dna\"],[\"rna-a-c-g-u\",\"RNA (A, C, G, U)\",\"rna\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"width\",\"l\":\"Base pairs per line\",\"h\":\"0 for one line\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc\",\"l\":\"GC content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm\",\"l\":\"Melting temperature of the duplex\",\"h\":\"Wallace under 14 nt, GC rule to 40 nt, long-duplex form above\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sequence\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAG\",\"c\":null}]},\"alphabet\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"dna-a-c-g-t\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-alphabet\"},\"width\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"60\",\"c\":null}]},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tm\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-alphabet\":{\"name\":\"Alphabet\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"dna-a-c-g-t\",\"label\":\"DNA (A, C, G, T)\",\"value\":\"dna\"},{\"uid\":\"rna-a-c-g-u\",\"label\":\"RNA (A, C, G, U)\",\"value\":\"rna\"}]}},\"eq\":[],\"js\":\"function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\\n    // Everything that is not a residue goes: FASTA headers, digits (line\\n    // numbers pasted from a viewer), gaps, spaces and newlines.\\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n  }\\n  function wrap(s, width) {\\n    width = width || 60;\\n    var out = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\\n    return out.join(\'\\\\n\');\\n  }\\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\\n    // Which formula applies depends on the length, and using the wrong one is\\n    // not a small error: the GC rule gives 107 °C for a 39 bp duplex, because it\\n    // was fitted to oligos of 40 nt and below.\\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;                       // Wallace\\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;            // GC rule, G+C as a count\\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;   // long duplex, 50 mM Na+\\n  }\\n  function esc(s) {\\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\\n  }\\n  function block(title, body, note) {\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n\\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',\\n              R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\\n  function revcomp(s) {\\n    var out = \'\';\\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\\n      var c = s.charAt(i);\\n      out += COMP[c] || \'N\';\\n    }\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  function gcOf(s) {\\n    var gc = 0;\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\\n      var c = s.charAt(i);\\n      if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++;\\n    }\\n    return gc;\\n  }\\n\\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\'], function (ctx) {\\n    var raw = ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\');\\n    // selects return the option slug (\\\"rna-a-c-g-u\\\"), so match on the word\\n    var kind = /rna/i.test(String(ctx.getRawInput(\'alphabet\') || \'\')) ? \'rna\' : \'dna\';\\n    var width = Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'width\'));\\n    if (!isFinite(width) || width < 0) width = 60;\\n\\n    var seq = cleanSeq(raw, \'dna\');\\n    if (kind === \'rna\') seq = seq.replace(/T/g, \'U\');\\n    if (!seq) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Reverse complement\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to see the other strand.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var comp = revcomp(kind === \'rna\' ? seq.replace(/U/g, \'T\') : seq);\\n    if (kind === \'rna\') comp = comp.replace(/T/g, \'U\');\\n\\n    var gc = gcOf(kind === \'rna\' ? seq.replace(/U/g, \'T\') : seq);\\n    var pct = seq.length ? (gc / seq.length) * 100 : 0;\\n    var at = seq.length - gc;\\n    var tm = tmOf(seq.length, pct, at, gc);\\n\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', seq.length);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc\', Math.round(pct * 100) / 100);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'tm\', Math.round(tm * 10) / 10);\\n\\n    var w = width === 0 ? Math.max(seq.length, 1) : width;\\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Reverse complement (5′→3′)\', wrap(comp, w),\\n      seq.length + (kind === \'rna\' ? \' nt\' : \' bp\') + \' · GC \' + pct.toFixed(1) + \'%\'));\\n    if (seq.length <= 200) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Input, as read 5′→3′\', wrap(seq, w)));\\n    }\\n  });\",\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, 'function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\n    // Everything that is not a residue goes: FASTA headers, digits (line\n    // numbers pasted from a viewer), gaps, spaces and newlines.\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n  }\n  function wrap(s, width) {\n    width = width || 60;\n    var out = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\n    return out.join(\'\\n\');\n  }\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\n    // Which formula applies depends on the length, and using the wrong one is\n    // not a small error: the GC rule gives 107 °C for a 39 bp duplex, because it\n    // was fitted to oligos of 40 nt and below.\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;                       // Wallace\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;            // GC rule, G+C as a count\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;   // long duplex, 50 mM Na+\n  }\n  function esc(s) {\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\n  }\n  function block(title, body, note) {\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',\n              R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\n  function revcomp(s) {\n    var out = \'\';\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n      var c = s.charAt(i);\n      out += COMP[c] || \'N\';\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n  function gcOf(s) {\n    var gc = 0;\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\n      var c = s.charAt(i);\n      if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++;\n    }\n    return gc;\n  }\n\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\'], function (ctx) {\n    var raw = ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\');\n    // selects return the option slug (\"rna-a-c-g-u\"), so match on the word\n    var kind = /rna/i.test(String(ctx.getRawInput(\'alphabet\') || \'\')) ? \'rna\' : \'dna\';\n    var width = Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'width\'));\n    if (!isFinite(width) || width < 0) width = 60;\n\n    var seq = cleanSeq(raw, \'dna\');\n    if (kind === \'rna\') seq = seq.replace(/T/g, \'U\');\n    if (!seq) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Reverse complement\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to see the other strand.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var comp = revcomp(kind === \'rna\' ? seq.replace(/U/g, \'T\') : seq);\n    if (kind === \'rna\') comp = comp.replace(/T/g, \'U\');\n\n    var gc = gcOf(kind === \'rna\' ? seq.replace(/U/g, \'T\') : seq);\n    var pct = seq.length ? (gc / seq.length) * 100 : 0;\n    var at = seq.length - gc;\n    var tm = tmOf(seq.length, pct, at, gc);\n\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', seq.length);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc\', Math.round(pct * 100) / 100);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'tm\', Math.round(tm * 10) / 10);\n\n    var w = width === 0 ? Math.max(seq.length, 1) : width;\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Reverse complement (5′→3′)\', wrap(comp, w),\n      seq.length + (kind === \'rna\' ? \' nt\' : \' bp\') + \' · GC \' + pct.toFixed(1) + \'%\'));\n    if (seq.length <= 200) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Input, as read 5′→3′\', wrap(seq, w)));\n    }\n  });', NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(156, 'gc-content-sequence', 'biology', 'GC Content from a Sequence', 'GC Content from a Sequence', 'GC Content from a Sequence — Sliding Window', 'GC content is the share of a DNA molecule that is guanine or cytosine, and it decides how strongly two strands stick together. Free gc content from a sequence', 'GC content is the share of a DNA molecule that is guanine or cytosine, and it decides how strongly two strands stick together. It returns length, g + c bases, gc content, at content and melting temperature of the duplex, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nThis calculator reports GC% overall and in sliding windows, so a GC-rich island inside an otherwise AT-rich sequence is visible rather than averaged away. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the GC Content from a Sequence\",\"html\":\"<p>GC% = (G + C) ÷ N × 100;  Tm ≈ 64.9 + 41(G+C − 16.4)/N  (G+C as a count)</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/gc-content-sequence.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: GC Content from a Sequence — Typical GC content (%)\\\" title=\\\"GC Content from a Sequence\\\"><figcaption>Typical GC content (%)</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>sequence length</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>GC</code></td><td>number of guanine and cytosine bases</td><td>bases</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tm</code></td><td>melting temperature of the duplex</td><td>°C</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the GC Content from a Sequence?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: window size of <strong>50</strong> bp.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 40%</td><td><strong>AT-rich</strong></td><td>easier to melt; often regulatory or repeat DNA</td></tr><tr><td>40 – 60%</td><td><strong>Typical</strong></td><td>most vertebrate genomic DNA sits here</td></tr><tr><td>60 – 70%</td><td><strong>GC-rich</strong></td><td>more stable duplex, harder to denature</td></tr><tr><td>above 70%</td><td><strong>Very GC-rich</strong></td><td>CpG islands, ribosomal DNA, some bacterial genomes</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>GC pairs have three hydrogen bonds and AT pairs have two, which is why GC-rich DNA melts at a higher temperature and why GC-rich templates are harder to sequence through.</p></li><li><p>A single GC% figure hides structure. A 200 bp CpG island at 70% GC inside a 100 kb region at 41% GC changes the overall number by a fraction of a percent.</p></li><li><p>RNA has uracil instead of thymine; the GC fraction is calculated the same way.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Counting ambiguous bases (N, R, Y) as AT because they are not G or C.</li><li>Comparing GC% of an mRNA (no introns) with that of its gene.</li><li>Assuming high GC always means a high melting temperature in a cell — salt and length matter at least as much.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/reverse-complement\\\">Reverse complement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\\\">Oligo Tm</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna\\\">Coding capacity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a normal GC content?\",\"a\":\"About 41% for the human genome, 50% for many bacteria, and as low as 25% in some Plasmodium genomes.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does GC content matter for PCR?\",\"a\":\"GC-rich regions need higher denaturation temperatures and often additives such as betaine or DMSO.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the GC Content from a Sequence actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns length, g + c bases, gc content, at content and melting temperature of the duplex. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sequence\",\"label\":\"Sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Paste DNA or RNA\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"window\",\"label\":\"Window size\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"GC is computed in windows of this length\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gc_count\",\"label\":\"G + C bases\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gc_pct\",\"label\":\"GC content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"at_pct\",\"label\":\"AT content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"tm_gc\",\"label\":\"Melting temperature of the duplex\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"At 50 mM monovalent salt\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"°C\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/gc-content-sequence\",\"slug\":\"gc-content-sequence\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"GC Content from a Sequence\",\"title\":\"GC Content from a Sequence — Sliding Window\",\"desc\":\"GC content is the share of a DNA molecule that is guanine or cytosine, and it decides how strongly two strands stick together. Free gc content from a sequence\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-gc-content-sequence\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sequence\",\"l\":\"Sequence\",\"h\":\"Paste DNA or RNA\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"window\",\"l\":\"Window size\",\"h\":\"GC is computed in windows of this length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc_count\",\"l\":\"G + C bases\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc_pct\",\"l\":\"GC content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"at_pct\",\"l\":\"AT content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"tm_gc\",\"l\":\"Melting temperature of the duplex\",\"h\":\"At 50 mM monovalent salt\",\"suf\":\"°C\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sequence\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null},\"window\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gc_count\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gc_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"at_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"tm_gc\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[],\"js\":\"function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\\n    // Everything that is not a residue goes: FASTA headers, digits (line\\n    // numbers pasted from a viewer), gaps, spaces and newlines.\\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n  }\\n  function wrap(s, width) {\\n    width = width || 60;\\n    var out = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\\n    return out.join(\'\\\\n\');\\n  }\\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\\n    // Which formula applies depends on the length, and using the wrong one is\\n    // not a small error: the GC rule gives 107 °C for a 39 bp duplex, because it\\n    // was fitted to oligos of 40 nt and below.\\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;                       // Wallace\\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;            // GC rule, G+C as a count\\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;   // long duplex, 50 mM Na+\\n  }\\n  function esc(s) {\\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\\n  }\\n  function block(title, body, note) {\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n\\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\'], function (ctx) {\\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\\n    if (!seq) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'GC content\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to see its composition.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var n = seq.length, gc = 0, at = 0;\\n    for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n      var c = seq.charAt(i);\\n      if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++;\\n      else if (c === \'A\' || c === \'T\' || c === \'U\') at++;\\n    }\\n    var pct = gc / n * 100;\\n    var tm = tmOf(n, pct, at, gc);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', n);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_count\', gc);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_pct\', Math.round(pct * 100) / 100);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'at_pct\', Math.round((at / n * 100) * 100) / 100);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'tm_gc\', Math.round(tm * 10) / 10);\\n\\n    // A profile every 5% of the sequence, so structure is visible at a glance.\\n    var rows = [], steps = 20;\\n    for (var k = 0; k < steps; k++) {\\n      var a = Math.floor(n * k / steps), b = Math.floor(n * (k + 1) / steps);\\n      if (b <= a) continue;\\n      var g = 0;\\n      for (var j = a; j < b; j++) { var ch = seq.charAt(j); if (ch === \'G\' || ch === \'C\') g++; }\\n      var p = g / (b - a) * 100;\\n      var bar = \'\';\\n      for (var q = 0; q < 40; q++) bar += (q < Math.round(p / 2.5)) ? \'#\' : \'·\';\\n      rows.push(String(a + 1).padStart(7, \' \') + \'  \' + bar + \'  \' + p.toFixed(0) + \'%\');\\n    }\\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'GC content along the sequence\', rows.join(\'\\\\n\'),\\n      \'Each row is \' + (100 / steps).toFixed(0) + \'% of the sequence, \' + steps + \' windows in total.\'));\\n    var body = \'G+C  \' + gc + \' of \' + n + \'  (\' + pct.toFixed(1) + \'%)\\\\n\'\\n             + \'A+T  \' + at + \' of \' + n + \'  (\' + (at / n * 100).toFixed(1) + \'%)\\\\n\'\\n             + \'other \' + (n - gc - at) + \'  (ambiguous codes)\';\\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Composition\', body));\\n  });\",\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, 'function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\n    // Everything that is not a residue goes: FASTA headers, digits (line\n    // numbers pasted from a viewer), gaps, spaces and newlines.\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n  }\n  function wrap(s, width) {\n    width = width || 60;\n    var out = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\n    return out.join(\'\\n\');\n  }\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\n    // Which formula applies depends on the length, and using the wrong one is\n    // not a small error: the GC rule gives 107 °C for a 39 bp duplex, because it\n    // was fitted to oligos of 40 nt and below.\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;                       // Wallace\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;            // GC rule, G+C as a count\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;   // long duplex, 50 mM Na+\n  }\n  function esc(s) {\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\n  }\n  function block(title, body, note) {\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\'], function (ctx) {\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\n    if (!seq) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'GC content\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to see its composition.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var n = seq.length, gc = 0, at = 0;\n    for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n      var c = seq.charAt(i);\n      if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++;\n      else if (c === \'A\' || c === \'T\' || c === \'U\') at++;\n    }\n    var pct = gc / n * 100;\n    var tm = tmOf(n, pct, at, gc);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', n);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_count\', gc);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_pct\', Math.round(pct * 100) / 100);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'at_pct\', Math.round((at / n * 100) * 100) / 100);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'tm_gc\', Math.round(tm * 10) / 10);\n\n    // A profile every 5% of the sequence, so structure is visible at a glance.\n    var rows = [], steps = 20;\n    for (var k = 0; k < steps; k++) {\n      var a = Math.floor(n * k / steps), b = Math.floor(n * (k + 1) / steps);\n      if (b <= a) continue;\n      var g = 0;\n      for (var j = a; j < b; j++) { var ch = seq.charAt(j); if (ch === \'G\' || ch === \'C\') g++; }\n      var p = g / (b - a) * 100;\n      var bar = \'\';\n      for (var q = 0; q < 40; q++) bar += (q < Math.round(p / 2.5)) ? \'#\' : \'·\';\n      rows.push(String(a + 1).padStart(7, \' \') + \'  \' + bar + \'  \' + p.toFixed(0) + \'%\');\n    }\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'GC content along the sequence\', rows.join(\'\\n\'),\n      \'Each row is \' + (100 / steps).toFixed(0) + \'% of the sequence, \' + steps + \' windows in total.\'));\n    var body = \'G+C  \' + gc + \' of \' + n + \'  (\' + pct.toFixed(1) + \'%)\\n\'\n             + \'A+T  \' + at + \' of \' + n + \'  (\' + (at / n * 100).toFixed(1) + \'%)\\n\'\n             + \'other \' + (n - gc - at) + \'  (ambiguous codes)\';\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Composition\', body));\n  });', NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(157, 'dna-to-protein', 'biology', 'DNA to Protein Translation Calculator', 'DNA to Protein Translation Calculator', 'DNA to Protein Translation Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Translation reads a nucleotide sequence three bases at a time and writes the amino acids it encodes. Free dna to protein translation calculator: enter your va', 'Translation reads a nucleotide sequence three bases at a time and writes the amino acids it encodes. It returns coding length, amino acids in the longest frame, stop codons in frame 1 and mass of the frame-1 protein, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nBecause the reading frame is not marked in the sequence, the same DNA gives three completely different proteins — this calculator shows all six frames, three from each strand, which is how a real open reading frame is found. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the DNA to Protein Translation\",\"html\":\"<p>Each codon of three bases maps to one amino acid;  the standard genetic code is used</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/dna-to-protein.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: DNA to Protein Translation Calculator — Codons that mean something, and codons that stop\\\" title=\\\"DNA to Protein Translation Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Codons that mean something, and codons that stop</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>frame</code></td><td>which base the reading starts on (1, 2 or 3)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORF</code></td><td>open reading frame: start codon to stop codon</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>aa</code></td><td>amino acid</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the DNA to Protein Translation?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ATG</td><td><strong>Start</strong></td><td>methionine, and the usual start of an ORF</td></tr><tr><td>TAA, TAG, TGA</td><td><strong>Stop</strong></td><td>do not encode an amino acid</td></tr><tr><td>3 frames per strand</td><td><strong>Six in total</strong></td><td>only one is normally the real gene</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>The reverse strand is read as its reverse complement, which is why a gene can be on either strand of a chromosome.</p></li><li><p>A long open reading frame — a hundred codons or more with no stop — is the strongest sequence-level evidence that a region is protein-coding.</p></li><li><p>The mass uses average residue masses, so it matches what a mass spectrometer reports rather than the monoisotopic mass.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Translating a sequence that still contains introns and wondering why there are stops every few codons.</li><li>Assuming frame 1 is the gene: roughly half of human genes are on the reverse strand.</li><li>Pasting an alignment with gaps, which shifts every downstream codon.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/reverse-complement\\\">Reverse complement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-sequence\\\">GC content</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/coding-capacity-of-dna\\\">Coding capacity</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How do I find the reading frame of a gene?\",\"a\":\"Translate all six frames and look for the long open reading frame that starts with ATG and ends with a stop codon.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my translation have so many stops?\",\"a\":\"Almost always the wrong frame, or a genomic sequence where a cDNA was expected.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the DNA to Protein Translation actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns coding length, amino acids in the longest frame, stop codons in frame 1 and mass of the frame-1 protein. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I use it with the numbers straight from a datasheet?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Every box states its unit, and nothing is converted behind your back, so a figure copied from a datasheet, a kit insert or a protocol goes in as printed.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sequence\",\"label\":\"DNA sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Paste DNA; headers and numbers are removed\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"start\",\"label\":\"Frame handling\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"all-six-frames\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"all-six-frames\",\"All six frames\",\"six\"],[\"frame-1-only\",\"Frame 1 only\",\"one\"],[\"stop-at-the-first-stop-codon\",\"Stop at the first stop codon\",\"orf\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Coding length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"aa_count\",\"label\":\"Amino acids in the longest frame\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"aa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"stop_codons\",\"label\":\"Stop codons in frame 1\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mw\",\"label\":\"Mass of the frame-1 protein\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kDa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/dna-to-protein\",\"slug\":\"dna-to-protein\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"DNA to Protein Translation Calculator\",\"title\":\"DNA to Protein Translation Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Translation reads a nucleotide sequence three bases at a time and writes the amino acids it encodes. Free dna to protein translation calculator: enter your va\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-dna-to-protein\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sequence\",\"l\":\"DNA sequence\",\"h\":\"Paste DNA; headers and numbers are removed\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"start\",\"l\":\"Frame handling\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-start\",\"opts\":[[\"all-six-frames\",\"All six frames\",\"six\"],[\"frame-1-only\",\"Frame 1 only\",\"one\"],[\"stop-at-the-first-stop-codon\",\"Stop at the first stop codon\",\"orf\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Coding length\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"aa_count\",\"l\":\"Amino acids in the longest frame\",\"suf\":\"aa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stop_codons\",\"l\":\"Stop codons in frame 1\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw\",\"l\":\"Mass of the frame-1 protein\",\"suf\":\"kDa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sequence\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null},\"start\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"all-six-frames\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-start\"},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"aa_count\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"stop_codons\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-start\":{\"name\":\"Frame handling\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"all-six-frames\",\"label\":\"All six frames\",\"value\":\"six\"},{\"uid\":\"frame-1-only\",\"label\":\"Frame 1 only\",\"value\":\"one\"},{\"uid\":\"stop-at-the-first-stop-codon\",\"label\":\"Stop at the first stop codon\",\"value\":\"orf\"}]}},\"eq\":[],\"js\":\"function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\\n    // Everything that is not a residue goes: FASTA headers, digits (line\\n    // numbers pasted from a viewer), gaps, spaces and newlines.\\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n  }\\n  function wrap(s, width) {\\n    width = width || 60;\\n    var out = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\\n    return out.join(\'\\\\n\');\\n  }\\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\\n    // Which formula applies depends on the length, and using the wrong one is\\n    // not a small error: the GC rule gives 107 °C for a 39 bp duplex, because it\\n    // was fitted to oligos of 40 nt and below.\\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;                       // Wallace\\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;            // GC rule, G+C as a count\\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;   // long duplex, 50 mM Na+\\n  }\\n  function esc(s) {\\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\\n  }\\n  function block(title, body, note) {\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n\\n  var CODON = {\\n    TTT:\'F\',TTC:\'F\',TTA:\'L\',TTG:\'L\',CTT:\'L\',CTC:\'L\',CTA:\'L\',CTG:\'L\',\\n    ATT:\'I\',ATC:\'I\',ATA:\'I\',ATG:\'M\',GTT:\'V\',GTC:\'V\',GTA:\'V\',GTG:\'V\',\\n    TCT:\'S\',TCC:\'S\',TCA:\'S\',TCG:\'S\',CCT:\'P\',CCC:\'P\',CCA:\'P\',CCG:\'P\',\\n    ACT:\'T\',ACC:\'T\',ACA:\'T\',ACG:\'T\',GCT:\'A\',GCC:\'A\',GCA:\'A\',GCG:\'A\',\\n    TAT:\'Y\',TAC:\'Y\',TAA:\'*\',TAG:\'*\',CAT:\'H\',CAC:\'H\',CAA:\'Q\',CAG:\'Q\',\\n    AAT:\'N\',AAC:\'N\',AAA:\'K\',AAG:\'K\',GAT:\'D\',GAC:\'D\',GAA:\'E\',GAG:\'E\',\\n    TGT:\'C\',TGC:\'C\',TGA:\'*\',TGG:\'W\',CGT:\'R\',CGC:\'R\',CGA:\'R\',CGG:\'R\',\\n    AGT:\'S\',AGC:\'S\',AGA:\'R\',AGG:\'R\',GGT:\'G\',GGC:\'G\',GGA:\'G\',GGG:\'G\'\\n  };\\n  // Average residue masses, daltons. Water is lost per peptide bond.\\n  var MASS = {A:71.079,G:57.052,I:113.160,L:113.160,P:97.117,V:99.133,F:147.177,\\n              M:131.198,W:186.213,S:87.078,T:101.105,C:103.139,Y:163.180,N:114.104,\\n              Q:128.131,D:115.089,E:129.116,K:128.174,R:156.188,H:137.142};\\n  function translate(s, frame) {\\n    var out = \'\';\\n    for (var i = frame; i + 2 < s.length + 0; i += 3) {\\n      var c = s.substr(i, 3);\\n      if (c.length < 3) break;\\n      out += CODON[c] || \'X\';\\n    }\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  function massOf(prot) {\\n    var m = 18.0153;                     // the free termini\\n    for (var i = 0; i < prot.length; i++) {\\n      var aa = prot.charAt(i);\\n      if (aa === \'*\') continue;\\n      m += MASS[aa] || 110;\\n    }\\n    return m / 1000;\\n  }\\n  function longestORF(prot) {\\n    var best = \'\', cur = \'\';\\n    for (var i = 0; i < prot.length; i++) {\\n      if (prot.charAt(i) === \'*\') { if (cur.length > best.length) best = cur; cur = \'\'; }\\n      else cur += prot.charAt(i);\\n    }\\n    return cur.length > best.length ? cur : best;\\n  }\\n\\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\'], function (ctx) {\\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\\n    var startSlug = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'start\') || \'\');\\n    // slugs: all-six-frames / frame-1-only / stop-at-the-first-stop-codon\\n    var mode = /first-stop|stop-at/i.test(startSlug) ? \'orf\'\\n      : /frame-1-only|one/i.test(startSlug) ? \'one\' : \'six\';\\n    if (!seq) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Translation\', \'—\', \'Paste a DNA sequence to translate it in every frame.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var comp = \'\';\\n    var map = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',N:\'N\'};\\n    for (var i = seq.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) comp += map[seq.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\\n\\n    var f1 = translate(seq, 0);\\n    var stops = 0;\\n    for (var k = 0; k < f1.length; k++) if (f1.charAt(k) === \'*\') stops++;\\n\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', seq.length);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'aa_count\', f1.replace(/\\\\*/g, \'\').length);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'stop_codons\', stops);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw\', Math.round(massOf(f1.replace(/\\\\*/g, \'\')) * 100) / 100);\\n\\n    if (mode === \'one\') {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Frame 1\', wrap(f1, 60), f1.length + \' aa including stops\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (mode === \'orf\') {\\n      var orf = longestORF(f1);\\n      if (!orf) {\\n        ctx.addHtml(block(\'Longest open reading frame\', \'—\',\\n          \'Frame 1 has no stretch without a stop codon. Try the other frames or the reverse strand.\'));\\n      } else {\\n        var mw = massOf(orf);\\n        ctx.addHtml(block(\'Longest open reading frame in frame 1\', wrap(orf, 60),\\n          orf.length + \' aa · \' + orf.length * 3 + \' bp · \' + mw.toFixed(2) + \' kDa\'));\\n      }\\n      return;\\n    }\\n\\n    var frames = [\\n      [\'+1\', translate(seq, 0)], [\'+2\', translate(seq, 1)], [\'+3\', translate(seq, 2)],\\n      [\'−1\', translate(comp, 0)], [\'−2\', translate(comp, 1)], [\'−3\', translate(comp, 2)]\\n    ];\\n    var best = \'\';\\n    frames.forEach(function (f) {\\n      var orf = longestORF(f[1]);\\n      if (orf.length > best.length) best = orf;\\n    });\\n    frames.forEach(function (f) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Frame \' + f[0], wrap(f[1], 60),\\n        f[1].replace(/\\\\*/g, \'\').length + \' aa · longest ORF \' + longestORF(f[1]).length + \' aa\'));\\n    });\\n  });\",\"needsJs\":false}', 'protein', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, 'function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\n    // Everything that is not a residue goes: FASTA headers, digits (line\n    // numbers pasted from a viewer), gaps, spaces and newlines.\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n  }\n  function wrap(s, width) {\n    width = width || 60;\n    var out = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\n    return out.join(\'\\n\');\n  }\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\n    // Which formula applies depends on the length, and using the wrong one is\n    // not a small error: the GC rule gives 107 °C for a 39 bp duplex, because it\n    // was fitted to oligos of 40 nt and below.\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;                       // Wallace\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;            // GC rule, G+C as a count\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;   // long duplex, 50 mM Na+\n  }\n  function esc(s) {\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\n  }\n  function block(title, body, note) {\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n\n  var CODON = {\n    TTT:\'F\',TTC:\'F\',TTA:\'L\',TTG:\'L\',CTT:\'L\',CTC:\'L\',CTA:\'L\',CTG:\'L\',\n    ATT:\'I\',ATC:\'I\',ATA:\'I\',ATG:\'M\',GTT:\'V\',GTC:\'V\',GTA:\'V\',GTG:\'V\',\n    TCT:\'S\',TCC:\'S\',TCA:\'S\',TCG:\'S\',CCT:\'P\',CCC:\'P\',CCA:\'P\',CCG:\'P\',\n    ACT:\'T\',ACC:\'T\',ACA:\'T\',ACG:\'T\',GCT:\'A\',GCC:\'A\',GCA:\'A\',GCG:\'A\',\n    TAT:\'Y\',TAC:\'Y\',TAA:\'*\',TAG:\'*\',CAT:\'H\',CAC:\'H\',CAA:\'Q\',CAG:\'Q\',\n    AAT:\'N\',AAC:\'N\',AAA:\'K\',AAG:\'K\',GAT:\'D\',GAC:\'D\',GAA:\'E\',GAG:\'E\',\n    TGT:\'C\',TGC:\'C\',TGA:\'*\',TGG:\'W\',CGT:\'R\',CGC:\'R\',CGA:\'R\',CGG:\'R\',\n    AGT:\'S\',AGC:\'S\',AGA:\'R\',AGG:\'R\',GGT:\'G\',GGC:\'G\',GGA:\'G\',GGG:\'G\'\n  };\n  // Average residue masses, daltons. Water is lost per peptide bond.\n  var MASS = {A:71.079,G:57.052,I:113.160,L:113.160,P:97.117,V:99.133,F:147.177,\n              M:131.198,W:186.213,S:87.078,T:101.105,C:103.139,Y:163.180,N:114.104,\n              Q:128.131,D:115.089,E:129.116,K:128.174,R:156.188,H:137.142};\n  function translate(s, frame) {\n    var out = \'\';\n    for (var i = frame; i + 2 < s.length + 0; i += 3) {\n      var c = s.substr(i, 3);\n      if (c.length < 3) break;\n      out += CODON[c] || \'X\';\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n  function massOf(prot) {\n    var m = 18.0153;                     // the free termini\n    for (var i = 0; i < prot.length; i++) {\n      var aa = prot.charAt(i);\n      if (aa === \'*\') continue;\n      m += MASS[aa] || 110;\n    }\n    return m / 1000;\n  }\n  function longestORF(prot) {\n    var best = \'\', cur = \'\';\n    for (var i = 0; i < prot.length; i++) {\n      if (prot.charAt(i) === \'*\') { if (cur.length > best.length) best = cur; cur = \'\'; }\n      else cur += prot.charAt(i);\n    }\n    return cur.length > best.length ? cur : best;\n  }\n\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\'], function (ctx) {\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\n    var startSlug = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'start\') || \'\');\n    // slugs: all-six-frames / frame-1-only / stop-at-the-first-stop-codon\n    var mode = /first-stop|stop-at/i.test(startSlug) ? \'orf\'\n      : /frame-1-only|one/i.test(startSlug) ? \'one\' : \'six\';\n    if (!seq) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Translation\', \'—\', \'Paste a DNA sequence to translate it in every frame.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var comp = \'\';\n    var map = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',N:\'N\'};\n    for (var i = seq.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) comp += map[seq.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\n\n    var f1 = translate(seq, 0);\n    var stops = 0;\n    for (var k = 0; k < f1.length; k++) if (f1.charAt(k) === \'*\') stops++;\n\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', seq.length);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'aa_count\', f1.replace(/\\*/g, \'\').length);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'stop_codons\', stops);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw\', Math.round(massOf(f1.replace(/\\*/g, \'\')) * 100) / 100);\n\n    if (mode === \'one\') {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Frame 1\', wrap(f1, 60), f1.length + \' aa including stops\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    if (mode === \'orf\') {\n      var orf = longestORF(f1);\n      if (!orf) {\n        ctx.addHtml(block(\'Longest open reading frame\', \'—\',\n          \'Frame 1 has no stretch without a stop codon. Try the other frames or the reverse strand.\'));\n      } else {\n        var mw = massOf(orf);\n        ctx.addHtml(block(\'Longest open reading frame in frame 1\', wrap(orf, 60),\n          orf.length + \' aa · \' + orf.length * 3 + \' bp · \' + mw.toFixed(2) + \' kDa\'));\n      }\n      return;\n    }\n\n    var frames = [\n      [\'+1\', translate(seq, 0)], [\'+2\', translate(seq, 1)], [\'+3\', translate(seq, 2)],\n      [\'−1\', translate(comp, 0)], [\'−2\', translate(comp, 1)], [\'−3\', translate(comp, 2)]\n    ];\n    var best = \'\';\n    frames.forEach(function (f) {\n      var orf = longestORF(f[1]);\n      if (orf.length > best.length) best = orf;\n    });\n    frames.forEach(function (f) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Frame \' + f[0], wrap(f[1], 60),\n        f[1].replace(/\\*/g, \'\').length + \' aa · longest ORF \' + longestORF(f[1]).length + \' aa\'));\n    });\n  });', NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(158, 'codon-usage-calculator', 'biology', 'Codon Usage Calculator', 'Codon Usage Calculator', 'Codon Usage Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Codon usage is how often each of the 61 sense codons appears in a coding sequence. Free codon usage calculator: enter your values and get complete codons, gc', 'Codon usage is how often each of the 61 sense codons appears in a coding sequence. It returns complete codons, gc at the third position, stop codons, different codons used and rare codons, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nBecause several codons stand for the same amino acid, the pattern shows which synonyms a gene prefers — the first thing to check before expressing a gene in E. coli, yeast or CHO cells, where rare codons stall the ribosome. Every result is derived from the boxes you fill in, so changing one input updates the whole panel at once. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Codon Usage\",\"html\":\"<p>RSCU = observed count of a codon ÷ (count of its amino acid ÷ number of its synonyms).  GC3 = codons ending in G or C ÷ complete codons × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/codon-usage-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Codon Usage Calculator — Codons available for one amino acid\\\" title=\\\"Codon Usage Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Codons available for one amino acid</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>RSCU</code></td><td>relative synonymous codon usage; 1.0 = no preference</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>GC3</code></td><td>GC content at the third codon position</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td><code>CDS</code></td><td>coding sequence, in frame from the start codon</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Codon Usage?\",\"html\":\"<p>The form asks only for what the formula needs — nothing is optional decoration.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: reading frame set to <strong>0.0</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The arithmetic is the page\'s own: change one box and the result moves by the amount the formula implies, which is the quickest way to convince yourself the tool is honest.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>A raw figure only means something next to the bands it is normally judged against. Here is the scale that goes with this one:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RSCU &gt; 1.5</td><td><strong>Strongly preferred</strong></td><td>the codon is used far more than chance in this gene</td></tr><tr><td>RSCU 0.8 – 1.5</td><td><strong>Neutral</strong></td><td>no meaningful preference</td></tr><tr><td>RSCU &lt; 0.5</td><td><strong>Avoided</strong></td><td>a candidate rare codon if the host also dislikes it</td></tr><tr><td>GC3 &gt; 70%</td><td><strong>GC-rich coding sequence</strong></td><td>common in Streptomyces, Mycobacterium, some plants</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>RSCU normalises for family size: serine has six codons, methionine and tryptophan have one, so raw counts cannot be compared without it.</p></li><li><p>The rare-codon count only looks at families that appear at least five times. In a short sequence most amino acids occur once, so every codon is trivially 100% of its family and a plain 10% cut-off would report nothing.</p></li><li><p>Codon usage is species-specific. A codon that is common in humans can be rare in E. coli, which is why a human cDNA often expresses poorly in bacteria.</p></li><li><p>Rare codons do not always slow translation — clusters of them, especially at the 5′ end, are the ones that matter.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>The arithmetic is simple; the inputs are where things go wrong. The usual culprits:</p><ul><li>Analysing the sequence in the wrong frame, which turns every codon into noise.</li><li>Including the stop codon in the GC3 figure while excluding it from the codon table.</li><li>Comparing a gene\'s usage with a host table built from highly expressed genes rather than from the whole genome.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>If you need the rest of the working, these cover the adjacent quantities:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/reverse-translate\\\">Reverse translate</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dna-to-protein\\\">DNA to protein</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-sequence\\\">GC content</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What is a good GC3 value?\",\"a\":\"There is no good or bad value — it is a fingerprint of the organism. E. coli sits near 55%, human genes near 60%, and Plasmodium near 25%.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does codon usage matter for expression?\",\"a\":\"Rare codons can exhaust the matching tRNA, cause ribosome stalling and lower yield. Codon optimisation replaces them with common synonyms without changing the protein.\"},{\"q\":\"Should I include the stop codon?\",\"a\":\"Analyse it separately. Stops carry no amino acid, so they have no RSCU within a family.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Codon Usage actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns complete codons, gc at the third position, stop codons, different codons used and rare codons. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sequence\",\"label\":\"Coding sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAGAAATTTGGGCCCAAATTT\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Paste DNA; FASTA headers, spaces and digits are ignored\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"frame\",\"label\":\"Reading frame\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"frame-1\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Frame 1 starts at the first base\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"frame-1\",\"Frame 1\",\"0\"],[\"frame-2\",\"Frame 2\",\"1\"],[\"frame-3\",\"Frame 3\",\"2\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"codons\",\"label\":\"Complete codons\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gc3\",\"label\":\"GC at the third position\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"A high GC3 usually means a GC-rich organism\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"stops\",\"label\":\"Stop codons\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"distinct\",\"label\":\"Different codons used\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"of 64\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"rare\",\"label\":\"Rare codons\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Under 10% within a family that appears at least five times — candidates for codon optimisation\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/codon-usage-calculator\",\"slug\":\"codon-usage-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"Codon Usage Calculator\",\"title\":\"Codon Usage Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Codon usage is how often each of the 61 sense codons appears in a coding sequence. Free codon usage calculator: enter your values and get complete codons, gc\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-codon-usage-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sequence\",\"l\":\"Coding sequence\",\"h\":\"Paste DNA; FASTA headers, spaces and digits are ignored\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"frame\",\"l\":\"Reading frame\",\"h\":\"Frame 1 starts at the first base\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-frame\",\"opts\":[[\"frame-1\",\"Frame 1\",\"0\"],[\"frame-2\",\"Frame 2\",\"1\"],[\"frame-3\",\"Frame 3\",\"2\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"codons\",\"l\":\"Complete codons\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc3\",\"l\":\"GC at the third position\",\"h\":\"A high GC3 usually means a GC-rich organism\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"stops\",\"l\":\"Stop codons\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"distinct\",\"l\":\"Different codons used\",\"suf\":\"of 64\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"rare\",\"l\":\"Rare codons\",\"h\":\"Under 10% within a family that appears at least five times — candidates for codon optimisation\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sequence\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAGAAATTTGGGCCCAAATTT\",\"c\":null}]},\"frame\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"frame-1\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-frame\"},\"codons\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gc3\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"stops\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"distinct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"rare\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-frame\":{\"name\":\"Reading frame\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"frame-1\",\"label\":\"Frame 1\",\"value\":\"0\"},{\"uid\":\"frame-2\",\"label\":\"Frame 2\",\"value\":\"1\"},{\"uid\":\"frame-3\",\"label\":\"Frame 3\",\"value\":\"2\"}]}},\"eq\":[],\"js\":\"function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n  }\\n  function wrap(s, width) {\\n    width = width || 60;\\n    var out = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\\n    return out.join(\'\\\\n\');\\n  }\\n  function esc(s) {\\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\\n  }\\n  function block(title, body, note) {\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    }).join(\'\');\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\\n      + \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-scroll\\\"><table class=\\\"calc-script-table\\\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function gcOf(s) {\\n    var gc = 0;\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\\n    return gc;\\n  }\\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\\n  }\\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\\n  function revcomp(s) {\\n    var out = \'\';\\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\\n  function aaOf(codon) {\\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\\n  }\\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'frame\'], function (ctx) {\\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\\n    // the select\'s slug is \\\"frame-2\\\" for the second frame\\n    var frameSlug = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'frame\') || \'\');\\n    var frame = parseInt(frameSlug.replace(/\\\\D/g, \'\'), 10) - 1;\\n    if (!isFinite(frame) || frame < 0 || frame > 2) frame = 0;\\n    if (seq.length < 3) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Codon usage\', \'—\', \'Paste a coding sequence of at least three bases.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var counts = {}, total = 0, stops = 0, gc3 = 0, aaCount = {}, aaSyn = {};\\n    eachCodon(seq, frame, function (codon) {\\n      var aa = aaOf(codon);\\n      if (!aa) return;\\n      total++;\\n      if (aa === \'*\') { stops++; return; }\\n      counts[codon] = (counts[codon] || 0) + 1;\\n      aaCount[aa] = (aaCount[aa] || 0) + 1;\\n      if (codon.charAt(2) === \'G\' || codon.charAt(2) === \'C\') gc3++;\\n    });\\n    if (!total) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Codon usage\', \'—\', \'No complete codon in this frame.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    // Degeneracy of each amino acid in the standard code. It is a property of the\\n    // code, not of this sequence: counting the distinct codons *used* would give\\n    // a family of one for any amino acid the gene spells only one way, and RSCU\\n    // would then be wrong by the missing synonyms.\\n    var DEGEN = {F: 2, L: 6, S: 6, Y: 2, C: 2, W: 1, P: 4, H: 2, Q: 2, R: 6, I: 3,\\n                 T: 4, N: 2, K: 2, M: 1, V: 4, A: 4, D: 2, E: 2, G: 4};\\n    aaSyn = DEGEN;\\n\\n    var rare = 0;\\n    var rows = Object.keys(counts).sort(function (a, b) { return counts[b] - counts[a]; })\\n      .map(function (c) {\\n        var aa = aaOf(c);\\n        var family = aaCount[aa] || 1, syn = aaSyn[aa] || 1;\\n        var rscu = (counts[c] * syn) / family;\\n        var share = counts[c] / family;\\n        // Only meaningful where the amino acid appears often enough for a share\\n        // to mean anything: one occurrence in a family is always 100%.\\n        if (family >= 5 && share < 0.10 && syn > 1) rare++;\\n        return [c, aa, String(counts[c]), pct1(share * 100) + \'%\', rscu.toFixed(2)];\\n      });\\n\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'codons\', total - stops);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc3\', Math.round(gc3 / total * 1000) / 10);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'stops\', stops);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'distinct\', Object.keys(counts).length);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'rare\', rare);\\n\\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Summary\',\\n      (total - stops) + \' sense codons · \' + (stops ? stops + \' stop codon\' + (stops > 1 ? \'s\' : \'\')\\n        : \'no stop codon in frame \' + (frame + 1))\\n      + \'\\\\nGC3 \' + pct1(gc3 / total * 100) + \'% · \' + Object.keys(counts).length + \' different codons used\',\\n      \'RSCU above 1 means the codon is used more often than a flat distribution within its family.\'));\\n    ctx.addHtml(table(\'Codons in this sequence\', [\'Codon\', \'AA\', \'Count\', \'Share in family\', \'RSCU\'],\\n      rows.slice(0, 40),\\n      rows.length > 40 ? \'Showing the 40 most used of \' + rows.length + \' codons.\' : \'\'));\\n  });\",\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, 'function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n  }\n  function wrap(s, width) {\n    width = width || 60;\n    var out = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\n    return out.join(\'\\n\');\n  }\n  function esc(s) {\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\n  }\n  function block(title, body, note) {\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    }).join(\'\');\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\n      + \'<div class=\"calc-script-scroll\"><table class=\"calc-script-table\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function gcOf(s) {\n    var gc = 0;\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\n    return gc;\n  }\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\n  }\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\n  function revcomp(s) {\n    var out = \'\';\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\n    return out;\n  }\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\n  function aaOf(codon) {\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\n  }\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\n    }\n  }\n\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'frame\'], function (ctx) {\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\n    // the select\'s slug is \"frame-2\" for the second frame\n    var frameSlug = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'frame\') || \'\');\n    var frame = parseInt(frameSlug.replace(/\\D/g, \'\'), 10) - 1;\n    if (!isFinite(frame) || frame < 0 || frame > 2) frame = 0;\n    if (seq.length < 3) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Codon usage\', \'—\', \'Paste a coding sequence of at least three bases.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var counts = {}, total = 0, stops = 0, gc3 = 0, aaCount = {}, aaSyn = {};\n    eachCodon(seq, frame, function (codon) {\n      var aa = aaOf(codon);\n      if (!aa) return;\n      total++;\n      if (aa === \'*\') { stops++; return; }\n      counts[codon] = (counts[codon] || 0) + 1;\n      aaCount[aa] = (aaCount[aa] || 0) + 1;\n      if (codon.charAt(2) === \'G\' || codon.charAt(2) === \'C\') gc3++;\n    });\n    if (!total) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Codon usage\', \'—\', \'No complete codon in this frame.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    // Degeneracy of each amino acid in the standard code. It is a property of the\n    // code, not of this sequence: counting the distinct codons *used* would give\n    // a family of one for any amino acid the gene spells only one way, and RSCU\n    // would then be wrong by the missing synonyms.\n    var DEGEN = {F: 2, L: 6, S: 6, Y: 2, C: 2, W: 1, P: 4, H: 2, Q: 2, R: 6, I: 3,\n                 T: 4, N: 2, K: 2, M: 1, V: 4, A: 4, D: 2, E: 2, G: 4};\n    aaSyn = DEGEN;\n\n    var rare = 0;\n    var rows = Object.keys(counts).sort(function (a, b) { return counts[b] - counts[a]; })\n      .map(function (c) {\n        var aa = aaOf(c);\n        var family = aaCount[aa] || 1, syn = aaSyn[aa] || 1;\n        var rscu = (counts[c] * syn) / family;\n        var share = counts[c] / family;\n        // Only meaningful where the amino acid appears often enough for a share\n        // to mean anything: one occurrence in a family is always 100%.\n        if (family >= 5 && share < 0.10 && syn > 1) rare++;\n        return [c, aa, String(counts[c]), pct1(share * 100) + \'%\', rscu.toFixed(2)];\n      });\n\n    ctx.exportValue(\'codons\', total - stops);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc3\', Math.round(gc3 / total * 1000) / 10);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'stops\', stops);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'distinct\', Object.keys(counts).length);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'rare\', rare);\n\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Summary\',\n      (total - stops) + \' sense codons · \' + (stops ? stops + \' stop codon\' + (stops > 1 ? \'s\' : \'\')\n        : \'no stop codon in frame \' + (frame + 1))\n      + \'\\nGC3 \' + pct1(gc3 / total * 100) + \'% · \' + Object.keys(counts).length + \' different codons used\',\n      \'RSCU above 1 means the codon is used more often than a flat distribution within its family.\'));\n    ctx.addHtml(table(\'Codons in this sequence\', [\'Codon\', \'AA\', \'Count\', \'Share in family\', \'RSCU\'],\n      rows.slice(0, 40),\n      rows.length > 40 ? \'Showing the 40 most used of \' + rows.length + \' codons.\' : \'\'));\n  });', NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:48', '2026-10-29 14:08:48'),
(159, 'cpg-island-finder', 'biology', 'CpG Island Finder', 'CpG Island Finder', 'CpG Island Finder Calculator — Formula & Results', 'A CpG island finder scans a sequence for windows that are GC-rich and CpG-dense against expectation. Free cpg island finder calculator: enter your values and', 'A CpG island finder scans a sequence for windows that are GC-rich and CpG-dense against expectation. It returns gc content of the sequence, cpg dinucleotides observed, observed / expected cpg, cpg islands found and longest island, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nBy the classic definition an island is 200 bp or more with GC ≥ 50% and observed over expected CpG ≥ 0.6. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the CpG Island Finder\",\"html\":\"<p>Expected CpG = (C count × G count) ÷ N.  Observed/expected = CpG observed ÷ expected.  An island needs GC ≥ 50%, O/E ≥ 0.6 across a window of 200 bp</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/cpg-island-finder.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: CpG Island Finder — CpG observed / expected\\\" title=\\\"CpG Island Finder\\\"><figcaption>CpG observed / expected</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>CpG</code></td><td>a cytosine followed by a guanine on the same strand</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>O/E</code></td><td>observed divided by expected CpG count</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>number of bases examined</td><td>bp</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the CpG Island Finder?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Take the default values the page opens with: window of <strong>200</strong> bp, minimum gc in the window of <strong>50</strong>%, minimum observed/expected cpg of <strong>0.6</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>O/E &gt; 1.0</td><td><strong>CpG-rich</strong></td><td>unusual outside promoters and repeats</td></tr><tr><td>O/E 0.6 – 1.0</td><td><strong>Candidate island</strong></td><td>island criteria met if GC is also ≥ 50%</td></tr><tr><td>O/E &lt; 0.6</td><td><strong>Depleted</strong></td><td>the normal state of vertebrate DNA, where CpG is methylated and mutates to TpG</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>About 70–80% of human gene promoters sit in a CpG island, which is why a predicted island is used as a first pass at finding promoters in an unknown sequence.</p></li><li><p>Vertebrate genomes are CpG-depleted overall (O/E near 0.25) because methylated cytosine deaminates to thymine. Islands stay unmethylated and therefore keep their CpGs.</p></li><li><p>Methylation of an island silences the gene beneath it — the mechanism behind X inactivation and many tumour suppressors.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Running the finder on a sequence shorter than the window: every window then fails and the answer looks like \'no islands\' rather than \'sequence too short\'.</li><li>Treating a predicted island as a proven promoter — it is a hypothesis that needs a transcription start site or a ChIP signal.</li><li>Forgetting that GC-rich repeats also score as islands.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-sequence\\\">GC content</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/reverse-complement\\\">Reverse complement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dna-molecular-weight-calculator\\\">DNA molecular weight</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What defines a CpG island?\",\"a\":\"Historically 200 bp or more with GC ≥ 50% and observed over expected CpG ≥ 0.6. Modern genome annotations use slightly relaxed thresholds.\"},{\"q\":\"How many CpG islands does a human genome have?\",\"a\":\"About 28 000, roughly one per gene, plus many in repeats.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is CpG depleted in vertebrates?\",\"a\":\"Cytosine in a CpG is methylated, and methylcytosine deaminates to thymine, so CG slowly mutates away to TG.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the CpG Island Finder actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns gc content of the sequence, cpg dinucleotides observed, observed / expected cpg, cpg islands found and longest island. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sequence\",\"label\":\"Sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAGCGCGCGCGGCCGCGGATCGCGATCGCCGCG\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Paste DNA; 200 bp or more gives a meaningful answer\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"window\",\"label\":\"Window\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"200\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Gardiner-Garden uses 200 bp\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gc_min\",\"label\":\"Minimum GC in the window\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"50\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"oe_min\",\"label\":\"Minimum observed/expected CpG\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"0.6\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"0.6 is the classical cut-off\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"gc_pct\",\"label\":\"GC content of the sequence\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cpg_obs\",\"label\":\"CpG dinucleotides observed\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"cpg_oe\",\"label\":\"Observed / expected CpG\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Below 0.6 suggests the CG has been methylated and lost\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"islands\",\"label\":\"CpG islands found\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"longest\",\"label\":\"Longest island\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/cpg-island-finder\",\"slug\":\"cpg-island-finder\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"CpG Island Finder\",\"title\":\"CpG Island Finder Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"A CpG island finder scans a sequence for windows that are GC-rich and CpG-dense against expectation. Free cpg island finder calculator: enter your values and\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-cpg-island-finder\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sequence\",\"l\":\"Sequence\",\"h\":\"Paste DNA; 200 bp or more gives a meaningful answer\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"window\",\"l\":\"Window\",\"h\":\"Gardiner-Garden uses 200 bp\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc_min\",\"l\":\"Minimum GC in the window\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"oe_min\",\"l\":\"Minimum observed/expected CpG\",\"h\":\"0.6 is the classical cut-off\",\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc_pct\",\"l\":\"GC content of the sequence\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cpg_obs\",\"l\":\"CpG dinucleotides observed\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"cpg_oe\",\"l\":\"Observed / expected CpG\",\"h\":\"Below 0.6 suggests the CG has been methylated and lost\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"islands\",\"l\":\"CpG islands found\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"longest\",\"l\":\"Longest island\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sequence\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAGCGCGCGCGGCCGCGGATCGCGATCGCCGCG\",\"c\":null}]},\"window\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"200\",\"c\":null}]},\"gc_min\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"50\",\"c\":null}]},\"oe_min\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"],\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"0.6\",\"c\":null}]},\"gc_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cpg_obs\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"cpg_oe\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"islands\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"longest\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":[],\"eq\":[],\"js\":\"function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n  }\\n  function wrap(s, width) {\\n    width = width || 60;\\n    var out = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\\n    return out.join(\'\\\\n\');\\n  }\\n  function esc(s) {\\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\\n  }\\n  function block(title, body, note) {\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    }).join(\'\');\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\\n      + \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-scroll\\\"><table class=\\\"calc-script-table\\\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function gcOf(s) {\\n    var gc = 0;\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\\n    return gc;\\n  }\\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\\n  }\\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\\n  function revcomp(s) {\\n    var out = \'\';\\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\\n  function aaOf(codon) {\\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\\n  }\\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function oecpg(s) {\\n    var n = s.length, c = 0, g = 0, cpg = 0;\\n    for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n      var ch = s.charAt(i);\\n      if (ch === \'C\') { c++; if (s.charAt(i + 1) === \'G\') cpg++; }\\n      else if (ch === \'G\') g++;\\n    }\\n    return {n: n, c: c, g: g, cpg: cpg, oe: (c && g) ? cpg / ((c * g) / n) : 0};\\n  }\\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'window\', \'gc_min\', \'oe_min\'], function (ctx) {\\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\\n    var w = Math.max(20, Math.min(5000, Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'window\')) || 200));\\n    var gcMin = Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'gc_min\')); if (!isFinite(gcMin)) gcMin = 50;\\n    var oeMin = Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'oe_min\')); if (!isFinite(oeMin)) oeMin = 0.6;\\n\\n    if (seq.length < 20) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'CpG islands\', \'—\', \'Paste at least a few hundred bases to scan.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var whole = oecpg(seq);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_pct\', Math.round(gcOf(seq) / seq.length * 1000) / 10);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'cpg_obs\', whole.cpg);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'cpg_oe\', Math.round(whole.oe * 100) / 100);\\n\\n    // slide a window, keep every window that passes, then merge overlaps\\n    var step = Math.max(1, Math.floor(seq.length / 4000));\\n    var hits = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i + w <= seq.length; i += step) {\\n      var win = seq.substr(i, w);\\n      var gc = gcOf(win) / w * 100;\\n      if (gc < gcMin) continue;\\n      var o = oecpg(win);\\n      if (o.oe >= oeMin) hits.push({s: i, e: i + w, gc: gc, oe: o.oe});\\n    }\\n    var merged = [];\\n    hits.forEach(function (h) {\\n      var last = merged[merged.length - 1];\\n      if (last && h.s <= last.e) {\\n        last.e = Math.max(last.e, h.e);\\n        last.gc = Math.max(last.gc, h.gc);\\n        last.oe = Math.max(last.oe, h.oe);\\n      } else merged.push({s: h.s, e: h.e, gc: h.gc, oe: h.oe});\\n    });\\n    var longest = 0;\\n    merged.forEach(function (m) { longest = Math.max(longest, m.e - m.s); });\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'islands\', merged.length);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'longest\', longest);\\n\\n    if (!merged.length) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'CpG islands\', \'No island found\',\\n        \'GC \' + pct1(gcOf(seq) / seq.length * 100) + \'% over \' + seq.length + \' bp · O/E \'\\n        + pct2(whole.oe) + \'. Lower the thresholds if you expect a weak island, or paste a longer \'\\n        + \'region — a window of \' + w + \' bp needs at least that much sequence.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    ctx.addHtml(table(\'Islands found\',\\n      [\'From\', \'To\', \'Length\', \'GC\', \'CpG O/E\', \'Sequence\'],\\n      merged.map(function (m) {\\n        var sub = seq.substring(m.s, m.e);\\n        return [(m.s + 1) + \' bp\', m.e + \' bp\', (m.e - m.s) + \' bp\', pct1(m.gc) + \'%\',\\n                m.oe.toFixed(2), sub.length > 40 ? sub.slice(0, 37) + \'…\' : sub];\\n      }),\\n      \'Coordinates are 1-based on the pasted sequence. \' + merged.length + \' island\'\\n      + (merged.length > 1 ? \'s\' : \'\') + \', longest \' + longest + \' bp.\'));\\n  });\",\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, 'function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n  }\n  function wrap(s, width) {\n    width = width || 60;\n    var out = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\n    return out.join(\'\\n\');\n  }\n  function esc(s) {\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\n  }\n  function block(title, body, note) {\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    }).join(\'\');\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\n      + \'<div class=\"calc-script-scroll\"><table class=\"calc-script-table\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function gcOf(s) {\n    var gc = 0;\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\n    return gc;\n  }\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\n  }\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\n  function revcomp(s) {\n    var out = \'\';\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\n    return out;\n  }\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\n  function aaOf(codon) {\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\n  }\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\n    }\n  }\n\n  function oecpg(s) {\n    var n = s.length, c = 0, g = 0, cpg = 0;\n    for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n      var ch = s.charAt(i);\n      if (ch === \'C\') { c++; if (s.charAt(i + 1) === \'G\') cpg++; }\n      else if (ch === \'G\') g++;\n    }\n    return {n: n, c: c, g: g, cpg: cpg, oe: (c && g) ? cpg / ((c * g) / n) : 0};\n  }\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'window\', \'gc_min\', \'oe_min\'], function (ctx) {\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\n    var w = Math.max(20, Math.min(5000, Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'window\')) || 200));\n    var gcMin = Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'gc_min\')); if (!isFinite(gcMin)) gcMin = 50;\n    var oeMin = Number(ctx.getRawInput(\'oe_min\')); if (!isFinite(oeMin)) oeMin = 0.6;\n\n    if (seq.length < 20) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'CpG islands\', \'—\', \'Paste at least a few hundred bases to scan.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var whole = oecpg(seq);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_pct\', Math.round(gcOf(seq) / seq.length * 1000) / 10);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'cpg_obs\', whole.cpg);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'cpg_oe\', Math.round(whole.oe * 100) / 100);\n\n    // slide a window, keep every window that passes, then merge overlaps\n    var step = Math.max(1, Math.floor(seq.length / 4000));\n    var hits = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i + w <= seq.length; i += step) {\n      var win = seq.substr(i, w);\n      var gc = gcOf(win) / w * 100;\n      if (gc < gcMin) continue;\n      var o = oecpg(win);\n      if (o.oe >= oeMin) hits.push({s: i, e: i + w, gc: gc, oe: o.oe});\n    }\n    var merged = [];\n    hits.forEach(function (h) {\n      var last = merged[merged.length - 1];\n      if (last && h.s <= last.e) {\n        last.e = Math.max(last.e, h.e);\n        last.gc = Math.max(last.gc, h.gc);\n        last.oe = Math.max(last.oe, h.oe);\n      } else merged.push({s: h.s, e: h.e, gc: h.gc, oe: h.oe});\n    });\n    var longest = 0;\n    merged.forEach(function (m) { longest = Math.max(longest, m.e - m.s); });\n    ctx.exportValue(\'islands\', merged.length);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'longest\', longest);\n\n    if (!merged.length) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'CpG islands\', \'No island found\',\n        \'GC \' + pct1(gcOf(seq) / seq.length * 100) + \'% over \' + seq.length + \' bp · O/E \'\n        + pct2(whole.oe) + \'. Lower the thresholds if you expect a weak island, or paste a longer \'\n        + \'region — a window of \' + w + \' bp needs at least that much sequence.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    ctx.addHtml(table(\'Islands found\',\n      [\'From\', \'To\', \'Length\', \'GC\', \'CpG O/E\', \'Sequence\'],\n      merged.map(function (m) {\n        var sub = seq.substring(m.s, m.e);\n        return [(m.s + 1) + \' bp\', m.e + \' bp\', (m.e - m.s) + \' bp\', pct1(m.gc) + \'%\',\n                m.oe.toFixed(2), sub.length > 40 ? sub.slice(0, 37) + \'…\' : sub];\n      }),\n      \'Coordinates are 1-based on the pasted sequence. \' + merged.length + \' island\'\n      + (merged.length > 1 ? \'s\' : \'\') + \', longest \' + longest + \' bp.\'));\n  });', NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:49', '2026-10-29 14:08:49'),
(160, 'dna-pattern-finder', 'biology', 'DNA Pattern Finder', 'DNA Pattern Finder', 'DNA Pattern Finder Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Search a DNA sequence for a motif written in IUPAC code and get every position on both strands. Free dna pattern finder calculator: enter your values and get', 'Search a DNA sequence for a motif written in IUPAC code and get every position on both strands. It returns matches, forward strand, matches, reverse strand, total sites and average spacing, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is the quickest way to count a regulatory element or check whether a cloning site is unique. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the DNA Pattern Finder\",\"html\":\"<p>A pattern of length k has N − k + 1 possible start positions.  Each IUPAC letter is expanded to the set of bases it stands for</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/dna-pattern-finder.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: DNA Pattern Finder — Start positions a pattern can have\\\" title=\\\"DNA Pattern Finder\\\"><figcaption>Start positions a pattern can have</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>sequence length</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>k</code></td><td>pattern length</td><td>bp</td></tr><tr><td><code>IUPAC</code></td><td>the standard degenerate base alphabet</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the DNA Pattern Finder?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 site</td><td><strong>Unique</strong></td><td>usable as a single-cutter cloning site</td></tr><tr><td>2 – 5 sites</td><td><strong>A few</strong></td><td>check fragment sizes before digesting</td></tr><tr><td>more than 5</td><td><strong>Frequent</strong></td><td>poor choice for a unique site</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>Positions are 1-based and count from the first base of the pasted sequence, on the strand as written. Reverse-strand hits are reported at the position of the equivalent forward site so the two lists line up.</p></li><li><p>Palindromic patterns (GAATTC, most type II restriction sites) match both strands at the same coordinates and are therefore counted once, not twice.</p></li><li><p>Overlapping matches are counted, which matters for repeats such as ATATAT.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Forgetting that a reverse-strand hit means the motif is on the other strand — the protein or enzyme still binds there.</li><li>Typing a pattern with a space or a digit in it; both are stripped, which can silently change the motif.</li><li>Assuming a unique site is unique in the whole plasmid when only an insert was pasted.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/reverse-complement\\\">Reverse complement</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-sequence\\\">GC content</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/codon-usage-calculator\\\">Codon usage</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"What does N mean in a DNA pattern?\",\"a\":\"Any base. It is the IUPAC wildcard, so NNNN finds any four bases.\"},{\"q\":\"How do I find a restriction site?\",\"a\":\"Type the recognition sequence, e.g. GAATTC for EcoRI or AAGCTT for HindIII, and search both strands.\"},{\"q\":\"Does the search handle overlapping matches?\",\"a\":\"Yes — ATATAT contains ATATAT twice in an ATATATAT run, and both are reported.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the DNA Pattern Finder actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns matches, forward strand, matches, reverse strand, total sites and average spacing. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sequence\",\"label\":\"Sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"TTTGAATTCTTGGATCCAAGCTTAAAGAATTCAAGCTTGATATCGAATTCCCC\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Paste DNA; headers, spaces and digits are ignored\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"pattern\",\"label\":\"Pattern (IUPAC allowed)\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"GAATTC\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"N = any base, R = A/G, Y = C/T, W = A/T, S = G/C, K = G/T, M = A/C\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"strand\",\"label\":\"Strand\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"both-strands\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"both-strands\",\"Both strands\",\"both\"],[\"forward-only\",\"Forward only\",\"forward\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"matches_fwd\",\"label\":\"Matches, forward strand\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"matches_rev\",\"label\":\"Matches, reverse strand\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"total\",\"label\":\"Total sites\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"spacing\",\"label\":\"Average spacing\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Blank when there is only one site\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"bp\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/dna-pattern-finder\",\"slug\":\"dna-pattern-finder\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"DNA Pattern Finder\",\"title\":\"DNA Pattern Finder Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Search a DNA sequence for a motif written in IUPAC code and get every position on both strands. Free dna pattern finder calculator: enter your values and get\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-dna-pattern-finder\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sequence\",\"l\":\"Sequence\",\"h\":\"Paste DNA; headers, spaces and digits are ignored\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pattern\",\"l\":\"Pattern (IUPAC allowed)\",\"h\":\"N = any base, R = A/G, Y = C/T, W = A/T, S = G/C, K = G/T, M = A/C\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"strand\",\"l\":\"Strand\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-strand\",\"opts\":[[\"both-strands\",\"Both strands\",\"both\"],[\"forward-only\",\"Forward only\",\"forward\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"matches_fwd\",\"l\":\"Matches, forward strand\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"matches_rev\",\"l\":\"Matches, reverse strand\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"total\",\"l\":\"Total sites\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"spacing\",\"l\":\"Average spacing\",\"h\":\"Blank when there is only one site\",\"suf\":\"bp\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sequence\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"TTTGAATTCTTGGATCCAAGCTTAAAGAATTCAAGCTTGATATCGAATTCCCC\",\"c\":null}]},\"pattern\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"GAATTC\",\"c\":null}]},\"strand\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"both-strands\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-strand\"},\"matches_fwd\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"matches_rev\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"total\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"spacing\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-strand\":{\"name\":\"Strand\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"both-strands\",\"label\":\"Both strands\",\"value\":\"both\"},{\"uid\":\"forward-only\",\"label\":\"Forward only\",\"value\":\"forward\"}]}},\"eq\":[],\"js\":\"function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n  }\\n  function wrap(s, width) {\\n    width = width || 60;\\n    var out = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\\n    return out.join(\'\\\\n\');\\n  }\\n  function esc(s) {\\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\\n  }\\n  function block(title, body, note) {\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    }).join(\'\');\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\\n      + \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-scroll\\\"><table class=\\\"calc-script-table\\\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function gcOf(s) {\\n    var gc = 0;\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\\n    return gc;\\n  }\\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\\n  }\\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\\n  function revcomp(s) {\\n    var out = \'\';\\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\\n  function aaOf(codon) {\\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\\n  }\\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  var IUPAC = {A:\'A\', C:\'C\', G:\'G\', T:\'T\', U:\'T\', R:\'[AG]\', Y:\'[CT]\', S:\'[GC]\', W:\'[AT]\',\\n               K:\'[GT]\', M:\'[AC]\', B:\'[CGT]\', D:\'[AGT]\', H:\'[ACT]\', V:\'[ACG]\', N:\'[ACGT]\'};\\n  function patternToRegex(p) {\\n    var out = \'\';\\n    for (var i = 0; i < p.length; i++) {\\n      var c = p.charAt(i);\\n      if (!IUPAC[c]) return null;\\n      out += IUPAC[c];\\n    }\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  function findAll(hay, re) {\\n    var g = new RegExp(re, \'g\'), hits = [], m;\\n    while ((m = g.exec(hay)) !== null) {\\n      hits.push(m.index + 1);\\n      if (m.index === g.lastIndex) g.lastIndex++;\\n      if (hits.length > 5000) break;\\n    }\\n    return hits;\\n  }\\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'pattern\', \'strand\'], function (ctx) {\\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\\n    var raw = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'pattern\') || \'\').replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\').toUpperCase()\\n      .replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n    if (!seq) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern search\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to search.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (!raw) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern search\', \'—\', \'Type the motif to look for, for example GAATTC.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var re = patternToRegex(raw);\\n    if (!re) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern search\', \'Not a valid pattern\',\\n        \'Use only A, C, G, T and the IUPAC codes R Y S W K M B D H V N.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var fwd = findAll(seq, re);\\n    var rev = [];\\n    if (!/forward-only/i.test(String(ctx.getRawInput(\'strand\') || \'\'))) {\\n      var rc = revcomp(seq);\\n      findAll(rc, re).forEach(function (p) {\\n        var start = seq.length - (p - 1) - raw.length + 1;\\n        if (fwd.indexOf(start) < 0) rev.push(start);\\n      });\\n    }\\n    var all = fwd.concat(rev).sort(function (a, b) { return a - b; });\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'matches_fwd\', fwd.length);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'matches_rev\', rev.length);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'total\', all.length);\\n    if (all.length > 1 && all[all.length - 1] > all[0]) {\\n      ctx.exportValue(\'spacing\', Math.round((all[all.length - 1] - all[0]) / (all.length - 1)));\\n    } else {\\n      ctx.exportValue(\'spacing\', \'\');\\n    }\\n\\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern found\',\\n      all.length + \' site\' + (all.length === 1 ? \'\' : \'s\') + \' for \' + raw\\n      + (rev.length ? \' (\' + fwd.length + \' forward, \' + rev.length + \' reverse)\' : \'\')\\n      + \' in \' + seq.length + \' bp\\\\n\'\\n      + (all.length ? \'Positions: \' + all.slice(0, 60).join(\', \') + (all.length > 60 ? \' …\' : \'\') : \'—\'),\\n      all.length ? \'Positions are 1-based on the forward strand.\' : \'No site found.\'));\\n    if (all.length) {\\n      var rows = all.slice(0, 30).map(function (p) {\\n        var ctxs = seq.substr(Math.max(0, p - 6), raw.length + 10);\\n        return [String(p), p + raw.length - 1 > seq.length ? \'—\' : String(p + raw.length - 1),\\n                (fwd.indexOf(p) >= 0 ? \'forward\' : \'reverse\'), ctxs];\\n      });\\n      ctx.addHtml(table(\'First sites\', [\'Start\', \'End\', \'Strand\', \'Context ±5 bp\'], rows));\\n    }\\n  });\",\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, 'function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n  }\n  function wrap(s, width) {\n    width = width || 60;\n    var out = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\n    return out.join(\'\\n\');\n  }\n  function esc(s) {\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\n  }\n  function block(title, body, note) {\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    }).join(\'\');\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\n      + \'<div class=\"calc-script-scroll\"><table class=\"calc-script-table\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function gcOf(s) {\n    var gc = 0;\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\n    return gc;\n  }\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\n  }\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\n  function revcomp(s) {\n    var out = \'\';\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\n    return out;\n  }\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\n  function aaOf(codon) {\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\n  }\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\n    }\n  }\n\n  var IUPAC = {A:\'A\', C:\'C\', G:\'G\', T:\'T\', U:\'T\', R:\'[AG]\', Y:\'[CT]\', S:\'[GC]\', W:\'[AT]\',\n               K:\'[GT]\', M:\'[AC]\', B:\'[CGT]\', D:\'[AGT]\', H:\'[ACT]\', V:\'[ACG]\', N:\'[ACGT]\'};\n  function patternToRegex(p) {\n    var out = \'\';\n    for (var i = 0; i < p.length; i++) {\n      var c = p.charAt(i);\n      if (!IUPAC[c]) return null;\n      out += IUPAC[c];\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n  function findAll(hay, re) {\n    var g = new RegExp(re, \'g\'), hits = [], m;\n    while ((m = g.exec(hay)) !== null) {\n      hits.push(m.index + 1);\n      if (m.index === g.lastIndex) g.lastIndex++;\n      if (hits.length > 5000) break;\n    }\n    return hits;\n  }\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'pattern\', \'strand\'], function (ctx) {\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\n    var raw = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'pattern\') || \'\').replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\').toUpperCase()\n      .replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n    if (!seq) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern search\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to search.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    if (!raw) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern search\', \'—\', \'Type the motif to look for, for example GAATTC.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var re = patternToRegex(raw);\n    if (!re) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern search\', \'Not a valid pattern\',\n        \'Use only A, C, G, T and the IUPAC codes R Y S W K M B D H V N.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var fwd = findAll(seq, re);\n    var rev = [];\n    if (!/forward-only/i.test(String(ctx.getRawInput(\'strand\') || \'\'))) {\n      var rc = revcomp(seq);\n      findAll(rc, re).forEach(function (p) {\n        var start = seq.length - (p - 1) - raw.length + 1;\n        if (fwd.indexOf(start) < 0) rev.push(start);\n      });\n    }\n    var all = fwd.concat(rev).sort(function (a, b) { return a - b; });\n    ctx.exportValue(\'matches_fwd\', fwd.length);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'matches_rev\', rev.length);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'total\', all.length);\n    if (all.length > 1 && all[all.length - 1] > all[0]) {\n      ctx.exportValue(\'spacing\', Math.round((all[all.length - 1] - all[0]) / (all.length - 1)));\n    } else {\n      ctx.exportValue(\'spacing\', \'\');\n    }\n\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Pattern found\',\n      all.length + \' site\' + (all.length === 1 ? \'\' : \'s\') + \' for \' + raw\n      + (rev.length ? \' (\' + fwd.length + \' forward, \' + rev.length + \' reverse)\' : \'\')\n      + \' in \' + seq.length + \' bp\\n\'\n      + (all.length ? \'Positions: \' + all.slice(0, 60).join(\', \') + (all.length > 60 ? \' …\' : \'\') : \'—\'),\n      all.length ? \'Positions are 1-based on the forward strand.\' : \'No site found.\'));\n    if (all.length) {\n      var rows = all.slice(0, 30).map(function (p) {\n        var ctxs = seq.substr(Math.max(0, p - 6), raw.length + 10);\n        return [String(p), p + raw.length - 1 > seq.length ? \'—\' : String(p + raw.length - 1),\n                (fwd.indexOf(p) >= 0 ? \'forward\' : \'reverse\'), ctxs];\n      });\n      ctx.addHtml(table(\'First sites\', [\'Start\', \'End\', \'Strand\', \'Context ±5 bp\'], rows));\n    }\n  });', NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:49', '2026-10-29 14:08:49'),
(161, 'dna-molecular-weight-calculator', 'biology', 'DNA Molecular Weight Calculator', 'DNA Molecular Weight Calculator', 'DNA Molecular Weight Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Molecular weight of a DNA molecule, single- or double-stranded, from its base composition. Free dna molecular weight calculator: enter your values and get len', 'Molecular weight of a DNA molecule, single- or double-stranded, from its base composition. It returns length, gc content, molecular weight, one strand, molecular weight of the molecule, molecular weight and amount in 1 µg, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nIt is what you need to convert a mass in nanograms into picomoles for a ligation, and to work out how much of a PCR product is in a band on a gel. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the DNA Molecular Weight\",\"html\":\"<p>ssDNA = 313.21·A + 304.20·T + 289.18·C + 329.21·G − 61.96  (Da).  dsDNA = 2 × ssDNA.  pmol per µg = 10⁶ ÷ MW</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/dna-molecular-weight-calculator.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: DNA Molecular Weight Calculator — Residue mass of each base (Da)\\\" title=\\\"DNA Molecular Weight Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Residue mass of each base (Da)</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A, T, C, G</code></td><td>counts of each base</td><td>bases</td></tr><tr><td><code>MW</code></td><td>molecular weight</td><td>g/mol = Da</td></tr><tr><td><code>Da</code></td><td>dalton, one atomic mass unit</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the DNA Molecular Weight?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>under 50 bp</td><td><strong>Oligonucleotide</strong></td><td>order by scale, not by mass</td></tr><tr><td>100 – 3 000 bp</td><td><strong>Typical PCR product</strong></td><td>a 1 kb dsDNA is about 660 kDa</td></tr><tr><td>above 10 000 bp</td><td><strong>Plasmid or genomic fragment</strong></td><td>handle shear-sensitive</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>A base pair of double-stranded DNA weighs about 618–660 Da depending on GC content; the often quoted 660 Da per bp is the average over equal base frequencies.</p></li><li><p>Pairing does not consume mass: a duplex weighs the sum of its two strands. Because the complement has the same composition as the strand you pasted, the duplex is exactly twice the single strand.</p></li><li><p>RNA uses uracil, which is 306.17 Da, so an RNA strand of the same sequence weighs slightly more than the equivalent DNA.</p></li><li><p>The figure here is the free-acid mass, summed from the exact residue masses (about 618 Da per base pair at 50% GC). The rounded 660 Da per base pair used for quick bench arithmetic is the sodium salt, which adds a sodium counterion for every phosphate — that is why the µg-to-pmol converter, which uses 660, returns about 7% fewer picomoles per microgram.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Reporting the duplex mass for a single-stranded oligo, which doubles the number.</li><li>Using the 660 Da/bp shortcut for a GC-rich molecule; at 70% GC, a base pair is closer to 640 Da.</li><li>Forgetting that a 5′ modification (biotin, phosphate, a fluorophore) adds its own mass.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/dsdna-ug-to-pmol\\\">dsDNA µg to pmol</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/oligo-tm-calculator\\\">Oligo Tm</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-sequence\\\">GC content</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"How much does a 1 kb DNA fragment weigh?\",\"a\":\"About 660 kDa double-stranded, roughly 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁸ g per molecule.\"},{\"q\":\"How many pmol are in 100 ng of a 500 bp dsDNA?\",\"a\":\"100 ng ÷ 330 000 g/mol = 3 × 10⁻¹³ mol ≈ 0.30 pmol.\"},{\"q\":\"Does the calculator handle RNA?\",\"a\":\"Yes — choose RNA and uracil is counted at its own residue mass.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the DNA Molecular Weight actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns length, gc content, molecular weight, one strand, molecular weight of the molecule, molecular weight and amount in 1 µg. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"sequence\",\"label\":\"Sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAG\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Paste one strand; the other is assumed to be its exact complement\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"strands\",\"label\":\"Molecule\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"double-stranded-dna\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"double-stranded-dna\",\"Double-stranded DNA\",\"ds\"],[\"single-stranded-dna\",\"Single-stranded DNA\",\"ss\"],[\"single-stranded-rna\",\"Single-stranded RNA\",\"rna\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"Length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nt\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gc_pct\",\"label\":\"GC content\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mw_ss\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight, one strand\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mw\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight of the molecule\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"Da\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"mw_kda\",\"label\":\"Molecular weight\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"kDa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"pmol_per_ug\",\"label\":\"Amount in 1 µg\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Handy for setting up a ligation\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"pmol\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true}]', '[]', '{\"path\":\"/biology/dna-molecular-weight-calculator\",\"slug\":\"dna-molecular-weight-calculator\",\"cat\":\"biology\",\"name\":\"DNA Molecular Weight Calculator\",\"title\":\"DNA Molecular Weight Calculator — Formula & Results\",\"desc\":\"Molecular weight of a DNA molecule, single- or double-stranded, from its base composition. Free dna molecular weight calculator: enter your values and get len\",\"authors\":[],\"reviewers\":[],\"updated\":\"2026-10-29\",\"rate\":0,\"groups\":[{\"uid\":\"custom-dna-molecular-weight-calculator\",\"title\":\"\",\"desc\":\"\",\"m\":[[\"FR2\",[[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"sequence\",\"l\":\"Sequence\",\"h\":\"Paste one strand; the other is assumed to be its exact complement\",\"vt\":\"text\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"strands\",\"l\":\"Molecule\",\"vt\":\"valueSelect\",\"ds\":\"ds-strands\",\"opts\":[[\"double-stranded-dna\",\"Double-stranded DNA\",\"ds\"],[\"single-stranded-dna\",\"Single-stranded DNA\",\"ss\"],[\"single-stranded-rna\",\"Single-stranded RNA\",\"rna\"]]},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"length\",\"l\":\"Length\",\"suf\":\"nt\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"gc_pct\",\"l\":\"GC content\",\"suf\":\"%\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}],[{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw_ss\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight, one strand\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight of the molecule\",\"suf\":\"Da\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"mw_kda\",\"l\":\"Molecular weight\",\"suf\":\"kDa\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"},{\"t\":\"v\",\"n\":\"pmol_per_ug\",\"l\":\"Amount in 1 µg\",\"h\":\"Handy for setting up a ligation\",\"suf\":\"pmol\",\"oo\":1,\"vt\":\"numerical\"}]]]]}],\"vars\":{\"sequence\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGAAAGGGTGCCCGATAG\",\"c\":null}]},\"strands\":{\"type\":\"valueSelect\",\"rnd\":null,\"dfl\":[{\"v\":\"double-stranded-dna\",\"c\":null}],\"ds\":\"ds-strands\"},\"length\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"gc_pct\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mw_ss\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mw\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"mw_kda\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]},\"pmol_per_ug\":{\"type\":\"numerical\",\"rnd\":[\"omni\",\"0.0001\"]}},\"datasets\":{\"ds-strands\":{\"name\":\"Molecule\",\"uiType\":\"RADIO\",\"options\":[{\"uid\":\"double-stranded-dna\",\"label\":\"Double-stranded DNA\",\"value\":\"ds\"},{\"uid\":\"single-stranded-dna\",\"label\":\"Single-stranded DNA\",\"value\":\"ss\"},{\"uid\":\"single-stranded-rna\",\"label\":\"Single-stranded RNA\",\"value\":\"rna\"}]}},\"eq\":[],\"js\":\"function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\\n  }\\n  function wrap(s, width) {\\n    width = width || 60;\\n    var out = [];\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\\n    return out.join(\'\\\\n\');\\n  }\\n  function esc(s) {\\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\\n  }\\n  function block(title, body, note) {\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\\n    }).join(\'\');\\n    return \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-block\\\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\\n      + \'<div class=\\\"calc-script-scroll\\\"><table class=\\\"calc-script-table\\\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\\\"calc-script-note\\\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\\n  }\\n  function gcOf(s) {\\n    var gc = 0;\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\\n    return gc;\\n  }\\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\\n  }\\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\\n  function revcomp(s) {\\n    var out = \'\';\\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\\n  function aaOf(codon) {\\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\\n  }\\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'strands\'], function (ctx) {\\n    // A select hands back the option\'s slug (\\\"single-stranded-rna\\\"), not its\\n    // value, so the kind is matched on words rather than compared to a code.\\n    var pick = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'strands\') || \'\');\\n    var kind = /rna/i.test(pick) ? \'rna\' : /single/i.test(pick) ? \'ss\' : \'ds\';\\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\\n    if (kind === \'rna\') seq = seq.replace(/T/g, \'U\');\\n    if (!seq) {\\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Molecular weight\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to weigh it.\'));\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    var n = seq.length, c = {A: 0, T: 0, G: 0, C: 0, U: 0};\\n    for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n      var ch = seq.charAt(i);\\n      if (c.hasOwnProperty(ch)) c[ch]++;\\n    }\\n    var mass = 313.21 * c.A + 304.20 * c.T + 289.18 * c.C + 329.21 * c.G;\\n    if (kind === \'rna\') mass += 306.17 * c.U;\\n    var ss = mass - 61.96;\\n    var total = kind === \'ss\' || kind === \'rna\' ? ss : 2 * ss;\\n    var gc = c.G + c.C;\\n    var pctGC = n ? gc / n * 100 : 0;\\n\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', n);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_pct\', Math.round(pctGC * 100) / 100);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw_ss\', Math.round(ss));\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw\', Math.round(total));\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw_kda\', Math.round(total / 1000 * 10) / 10);\\n    ctx.exportValue(\'pmol_per_ug\', Math.round(1e6 / total * 1000) / 1000);\\n\\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Mass\',\\n      n + (kind === \'rna\' ? \' nt RNA\' : kind === \'ss\' ? \' nt ssDNA\' : \' bp dsDNA\')\\n      + \'\\\\nOne strand: \' + (Math.round(ss)).toLocaleString() + \' Da\'\\n      + \'\\\\nMolecule: \' + (Math.round(total)).toLocaleString() + \' Da = \'\\n      + (total / 1000).toFixed(1) + \' kDa\'\\n      + \'\\\\n1 µg = \' + (1e6 / total).toFixed(3) + \' pmol · 1 pmol = \'\\n      + (total / 1e6).toFixed(4) + \' µg\'\\n      + \'\\\\nMass per \' + (kind === \'rna\' ? \'base\' : \'base pair\') + \': \'\\n      + (total / n).toFixed(1) + \' Da\',\\n      kind === \'ds\' ? \'Double-stranded mass assumes the complement has the same composition.\'\\n                    : \'\'));\\n    ctx.addHtml(table(\'Composition\', [\'Base\', \'Count\', \'Share\', \'Residue mass (Da)\'],\\n      [[\'A\', String(c.A), pct1(n ? c.A / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'313.21\'],\\n       [\'T\', String(c.T), pct1(n ? c.T / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'304.20\'],\\n       [\'C\', String(c.C), pct1(n ? c.C / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'289.18\'],\\n       [\'G\', String(c.G), pct1(n ? c.G / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'329.21\'],\\n       [\'U\', String(c.U), pct1(n ? c.U / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'306.17\']],\\n      \'GC content \' + pct2(pctGC) + \'%. Residue masses are for the nucleotide monophosphate \'\\n      + \'in a chain, with one water subtracted per strand.\'));\\n  });\",\"needsJs\":false}', 'dna', '', 'published', 0, 1000, 0, 'function cleanSeq(raw, kind) {\n    var s = String(raw || \'\').replace(/^>.*$/gm, \'\');\n    s = s.replace(/[^A-Za-z]/g, \'\');\n    return kind === \'protein\' ? s.toUpperCase() : s.toUpperCase().replace(/U/g, \'T\');\n  }\n  function wrap(s, width) {\n    width = width || 60;\n    var out = [];\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i += width) out.push(s.slice(i, i + width));\n    return out.join(\'\\n\');\n  }\n  function esc(s) {\n    return String(s).replace(/&/g, \'&amp;\').replace(/</g, \'&lt;\').replace(/>/g, \'&gt;\');\n  }\n  function block(title, body, note) {\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3><pre>\' + esc(body) + \'</pre>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function table(title, head, rows, note) {\n    var h = \'<tr>\' + head.map(function (c) { return \'<th>\' + esc(c) + \'</th>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    var b = rows.map(function (r) {\n      return \'<tr>\' + r.map(function (c) { return \'<td>\' + esc(c) + \'</td>\'; }).join(\'\') + \'</tr>\';\n    }).join(\'\');\n    return \'<div class=\"calc-script-block\"><h3>\' + esc(title) + \'</h3>\'\n      + \'<div class=\"calc-script-scroll\"><table class=\"calc-script-table\">\' + h + b + \'</table></div>\'\n      + (note ? \'<p class=\"calc-script-note\">\' + esc(note) + \'</p>\' : \'\') + \'</div>\';\n  }\n  function gcOf(s) {\n    var gc = 0;\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { var c = s.charAt(i); if (c === \'G\' || c === \'C\') gc++; }\n    return gc;\n  }\n  function tmOf(n, pct, at, gc) {\n    if (n <= 14) return 2 * at + 4 * gc;\n    if (n <= 40) return 64.9 + 41 * (gc - 16.4) / n;\n    return 81.5 + 16.6 * Math.log10(0.05) + 0.41 * pct - 675 / n;\n  }\n  function pct1(x) { return (Math.round(x * 10) / 10).toFixed(1); }\n  function pct2(x) { return (Math.round(x * 100) / 100).toFixed(2); }\n  var COMP = {A:\'T\',T:\'A\',G:\'C\',C:\'G\',U:\'A\',R:\'Y\',Y:\'R\',S:\'S\',W:\'W\',K:\'M\',M:\'K\',\n              B:\'V\',V:\'B\',D:\'H\',H:\'D\',N:\'N\'};\n  function revcomp(s) {\n    var out = \'\';\n    for (var i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) out += COMP[s.charAt(i)] || \'N\';\n    return out;\n  }\n  // NCBI standard genetic code: bases ordered T, C, A, G for each position\n  var CODON_TABLE = \'FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\';\n  var BASES = \'TCAG\';\n  function aaOf(codon) {\n    var i1 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(0)), i2 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(1)),\n        i3 = BASES.indexOf(codon.charAt(2));\n    if (i1 < 0 || i2 < 0 || i3 < 0) return null;\n    return CODON_TABLE.charAt(16 * i1 + 4 * i2 + i3);\n  }\n  function eachCodon(seq, frame, fn) {\n    for (var i = (frame || 0); i + 3 <= seq.length; i += 3) {\n      fn(seq.substr(i, 3), i);\n    }\n  }\n\n  omni.onResult([\'sequence\', \'strands\'], function (ctx) {\n    // A select hands back the option\'s slug (\"single-stranded-rna\"), not its\n    // value, so the kind is matched on words rather than compared to a code.\n    var pick = String(ctx.getRawInput(\'strands\') || \'\');\n    var kind = /rna/i.test(pick) ? \'rna\' : /single/i.test(pick) ? \'ss\' : \'ds\';\n    var seq = cleanSeq(ctx.getRawInput(\'sequence\'), \'dna\');\n    if (kind === \'rna\') seq = seq.replace(/T/g, \'U\');\n    if (!seq) {\n      ctx.addHtml(block(\'Molecular weight\', \'—\', \'Paste a sequence to weigh it.\'));\n      return;\n    }\n    var n = seq.length, c = {A: 0, T: 0, G: 0, C: 0, U: 0};\n    for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n      var ch = seq.charAt(i);\n      if (c.hasOwnProperty(ch)) c[ch]++;\n    }\n    var mass = 313.21 * c.A + 304.20 * c.T + 289.18 * c.C + 329.21 * c.G;\n    if (kind === \'rna\') mass += 306.17 * c.U;\n    var ss = mass - 61.96;\n    var total = kind === \'ss\' || kind === \'rna\' ? ss : 2 * ss;\n    var gc = c.G + c.C;\n    var pctGC = n ? gc / n * 100 : 0;\n\n    ctx.exportValue(\'length\', n);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'gc_pct\', Math.round(pctGC * 100) / 100);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw_ss\', Math.round(ss));\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw\', Math.round(total));\n    ctx.exportValue(\'mw_kda\', Math.round(total / 1000 * 10) / 10);\n    ctx.exportValue(\'pmol_per_ug\', Math.round(1e6 / total * 1000) / 1000);\n\n    ctx.addHtml(block(\'Mass\',\n      n + (kind === \'rna\' ? \' nt RNA\' : kind === \'ss\' ? \' nt ssDNA\' : \' bp dsDNA\')\n      + \'\\nOne strand: \' + (Math.round(ss)).toLocaleString() + \' Da\'\n      + \'\\nMolecule: \' + (Math.round(total)).toLocaleString() + \' Da = \'\n      + (total / 1000).toFixed(1) + \' kDa\'\n      + \'\\n1 µg = \' + (1e6 / total).toFixed(3) + \' pmol · 1 pmol = \'\n      + (total / 1e6).toFixed(4) + \' µg\'\n      + \'\\nMass per \' + (kind === \'rna\' ? \'base\' : \'base pair\') + \': \'\n      + (total / n).toFixed(1) + \' Da\',\n      kind === \'ds\' ? \'Double-stranded mass assumes the complement has the same composition.\'\n                    : \'\'));\n    ctx.addHtml(table(\'Composition\', [\'Base\', \'Count\', \'Share\', \'Residue mass (Da)\'],\n      [[\'A\', String(c.A), pct1(n ? c.A / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'313.21\'],\n       [\'T\', String(c.T), pct1(n ? c.T / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'304.20\'],\n       [\'C\', String(c.C), pct1(n ? c.C / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'289.18\'],\n       [\'G\', String(c.G), pct1(n ? c.G / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'329.21\'],\n       [\'U\', String(c.U), pct1(n ? c.U / n * 100 : 0) + \'%\', \'306.17\']],\n      \'GC content \' + pct2(pctGC) + \'%. Residue masses are for the nucleotide monophosphate \'\n      + \'in a chain, with one water subtracted per strand.\'));\n  });', NULL, 0, 1, 1, '2026-10-29 14:08:49', '2026-10-29 14:08:49'),
(162, 'reverse-translate', 'biology', 'Reverse Translate Calculator', 'Reverse Translate Calculator', 'Reverse Translate Calculator — Formula & Results', 'Reverse translation turns a protein sequence back into a DNA sequence that would encode it. Free reverse translate calculator: enter your values and get prote', 'Reverse translation turns a protein sequence back into a DNA sequence that would encode it. It returns protein length, dna length, gc content of the dna, codons and melting temperature of the duplex, and the panel shows all of them at once.\n\nEvery amino acid except methionine and tryptophan has several codons, so there is no single right answer — this tool offers the codons a host actually uses, the GC-balanced choice, and the rare-codon-avoiding choice. The arithmetic keeps full precision internally and only rounds what it displays, so the number you copy out is the number the formula produced. Units are stated on every box and in every result, so a figure copied out of a protocol, a datasheet or a chart can be entered without converting it first.', '[{\"title\":\"The formula behind the Reverse Translate\",\"html\":\"<p>Each residue is replaced by a codon from the host\'s preferred set; DNA length = 3 × (residues + stop).  GC% = (G + C) ÷ length × 100</p>\\n<figure class=\\\"calc-figure\\\"><img src=\\\"/uploads/biology/reverse-translate.svg\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" alt=\\\"Animated diagram: Reverse Translate Calculator — Codons a residue can use\\\" title=\\\"Reverse Translate Calculator\\\"><figcaption>Codons a residue can use</figcaption></figure>\\n<table><caption>The symbols in the formula</caption><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>What it stands for</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>aa</code></td><td>amino acid residue</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>nt</code></td><td>nucleotide</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>codon</code></td><td>three bases that specify one residue</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use the Reverse Translate?\",\"html\":\"<p>Fill the boxes you have numbers for; the calculator decides what it can derive from them.</p>\\n<ol><li>Enter the values you already have. Every box accepts a decimal point and a minus sign; thousands separators are ignored.</li><li>The grey boxes are results: they are worked out from your inputs and cannot be typed into.</li><li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong>. The results fill in together, and from then on they follow every edit you make — there is no page to reload and nothing to submit.</li></ol>\"},{\"title\":\"A worked example\",\"html\":\"<p>Because the example uses the defaults, you can reproduce it by loading the page and reading the results panel before touching anything.</p>\"},{\"title\":\"What does the result mean?\",\"html\":\"<p>Interpretation is a convention, not part of the arithmetic. The usual bands are:</p>\\n<table><caption>How to read the result</caption><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Band</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>rare codons kept</td><td><strong>Risk of poor expression</strong></td><td>especially clusters near the 5′ end</td></tr><tr><td>GC 40 – 60%</td><td><strong>Comfortable range</strong></td><td>synthesis and PCR both behave well</td></tr><tr><td>GC above 70%</td><td><strong>Hard to synthesise</strong></td><td>secondary structure and mispriming become likely</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<ul><li><p>A back-translated sequence is a design proposal, not a recovered gene. Silent codons are lost forever when you translate forward, so the original DNA cannot be reconstructed.</p></li><li><p>The most-used codon is not always the best choice: runs of the same rare tRNA demand, strong secondary structure at the ribosome binding site, and hidden restriction sites all matter. Treat the output as a starting point for codon optimisation.</p></li><li><p>GC-balanced output is the safest default when the DNA will be chemically synthesised, because synthesis fails most often on extreme GC.</p></li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"What are the common mistakes?\",\"html\":\"<p>Before blaming the formula, check these — they explain nearly every mismatch:</p><ul><li>Assuming the reverse-translated sequence will match the original gene — it will not.</li><li>Ignoring the host: a codon set chosen for E. coli can be poor for Pichia or CHO.</li><li>Forgetting the stop codon, which truncates translation if it is missing in an expression construct.</li></ul>\"},{\"title\":\"Calculators that go with this one\",\"html\":\"<p>Most real jobs need two or three of these together:</p><p><a href=\\\"/biology/codon-usage-calculator\\\">Codon usage</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/dna-to-protein\\\">DNA to protein</a>. <a href=\\\"/biology/gc-content-sequence\\\">GC content</a>.</p>\"}]', '[{\"q\":\"Can I recover the original DNA from a protein?\",\"a\":\"No. Most amino acids map to several codons and that information is lost in translation; you can only propose a new sequence.\"},{\"q\":\"Which codon table should I use?\",\"a\":\"The host you will express in. E. coli for bacterial work, human for mammalian cells, GC-balanced if the sequence is being synthesised.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the tool avoid rare codons?\",\"a\":\"Clusters of rare codons deplete the matching tRNA and stall the ribosome, which lowers yield and can cause truncation.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the Reverse Translate actually calculate?\",\"a\":\"It returns protein length, dna length, gc content of the dna, codons and melting temperature of the duplex. Each one is derived by the formula on this page, so you can check any figure by hand.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I have to fill in every box?\",\"a\":\"Fill in the ones you know. The result boxes are computed from them, so you never enter a value the formula can derive — and if you change an input, every result updates with it.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the answer different from the one in my protocol?\",\"a\":\"Nearly always a unit or a rounding difference. This calculator works in the units printed next to each box, keeps full precision internally and only rounds for display, so recomputing from the rounded numbers on screen can differ in the last digit.\"}]', '[{\"key\":\"protein\",\"label\":\"Protein sequence\",\"type\":\"text\",\"default\":\"MKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFSRQLEERLGLIEVQAPILSRVGDGTQDNLSGAEKAVQV\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"Single-letter code; spaces, digits and FASTA headers are ignored\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"strategy\",\"label\":\"Codon choice\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"e-coli-most-used-codon\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"e-coli-most-used-codon\",\"E. coli, most used codon\",\"ecoli\"],[\"human-most-used-codon\",\"Human, most used codon\",\"human\"],[\"gc-balanced-50\",\"GC-balanced (50%)\",\"gc\"],[\"avoid-rare-codons-gc-rich-hosts\",\"Avoid rare codons (GC-rich hosts)\",\"gchost\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"tail\",\"label\":\"Add a stop codon\",\"type\":\"select\",\"default\":\"yes\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\",\"options\":[[\"yes\",\"Yes\",\"yes\"],[\"no\",\"No\",\"no\"]],\"output\":false},{\"key\":\"length_aa\",\"label\":\"Protein length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"aa\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"length\",\"label\":\"DNA length\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"nt\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"gc_pct\",\"label\":\"GC content of the DNA\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\",\"unit_group\":\"\",\"unit_default\":\"\",\"help\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"%\",\"options\":[],\"output\":true},{\"key\":\"codons\",\"label\":\"Codons\",\"type\":\"number\",\"default\":\"\"